{"parentUuid":"523fdde1-3efe-4ffb-8c5c-c20644cb5ff5","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"resp_007d7a42f80701a3016a7bdb0f33bc81918a9d72c1fede7900","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"codex-gpt-5-6-sol","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":845,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":92672,"output_tokens":55,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_fawVCouJjZe7vzUDIvAs9bAP","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\db\\schema.ts","offset":350,"limit":100}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_details":null},"attributionSkill":"windows-computer-use","type":"assistant","uuid":"dbc0b374-a0e1-4de9-a460-072f8293c9cb","timestamp":"2026-08-12T02:31:47.493Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"dbc0b374-a0e1-4de9-a460-072f8293c9cb","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"44611460-2829-462e-b2e9-af8e653be0db","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_fawVCouJjZe7vzUDIvAs9bAP","type":"tool_result","content":"350\t * A fence needs an atomic compare-and-set between the two paths, which needs a\n351\t * real transaction, which is what SQLite has and a read-modify-rewrite of a JSON\n352\t * file structurally does not.\n353\t *\n354\t * SECOND-ORDER WIN, worth stating because it changes an accepted failure. The\n355\t * JSON file was validated as a WHOLE: one incoherent record failed the file\n356\t * schema and quarantined all 24 h of everyone else's records. Rows validate\n357\t * individually here (`attempt-ledger.ts`), so one corrupt row costs exactly one\n358\t * requestId its status.\n359\t */\n360\texport const answerAttempts = sqliteTable(\n361\t\t\"answer_attempts\",\n362\t\t{\n363\t\t\t/**\n364\t\t\t * §11.4's idempotency key, and the CAS key. The PRIMARY KEY is doing real\n365\t\t\t * work: it is the uniqueness constraint that makes `INSERT .. ON CONFLICT DO\n366\t\t\t * NOTHING` a decision procedure rather than a race.\n367\t\t\t */\n368\t\t\trequestId: text(\"request_id\").primaryKey(),\n369\t\t\t/**\n370\t\t\t * Null for a `closed_not_received` tombstone and ONLY for one — a row that\n371\t\t\t * asserts nothing ever arrived has no attempt to describe. Storing a sentinel\n372\t\t\t * instead would be a lie the boundary could not catch, so the nullability is\n373\t\t\t * deliberate and the tombstone/attempt shapes are cross-checked on read.\n374\t\t\t */\n375\t\t\tquestionId: text(\"question_id\"),\n376\t\t\tdeviceId: text(\"device_id\"),\n377\t\t\tsurface: text().$type<\"phone\" | \"watch\">(),\n378\t\t\tleaseId: text(\"lease_id\"),\n379\t\t\t/** The attempt's own start. Null for a tombstone, exactly as above. */\n380\t\t\tstartedAtMs: integer(\"started_at_ms\"),\n381\t\t\t/**\n382\t\t\t * The row's creation instant, always set — including for a tombstone, which\n383\t\t\t * is why it is separate from `startedAtMs`.\n384\t\t\t *\n385\t\t\t * Retention ages on THIS column so pruning has one unambiguous clock for both\n386\t\t\t * row shapes. For a real attempt the two coincide (the claim happens at the\n387\t\t\t * attempt's start); they are still kept apart because a single column that\n388\t\t\t * means \"the attempt began\" for one shape and \"the negative was asserted\" for\n389\t\t\t * another is the kind of overload a later reader gets wrong.\n390\t\t\t */\n391\t\t\tcreatedAtMs: integer(\"created_at_ms\").notNull(),\n392\t\t\tstatus: text().notNull().$type<AnswerAttemptStatus>(),\n393\t\t\tresolvedAtMs: integer(\"resolved_at_ms\"),\n394\t\t\t/**\n395\t\t\t * Plain text, not `$type`d. The authoritative union is\n396\t\t\t * `AttemptFailureCode` in `src/companion/types.ts`, which this file must not\n397\t\t\t * import (see `answerAttemptStatuses`), and duplicating it here would create\n398\t\t\t * two lists to widen together. The ledger's own public write signature takes\n399\t\t\t * `AttemptFailureCode`, so the compile-time check lives at the call site where\n400\t\t\t * it is useful, and the runtime check lives at the read boundary.\n401\t\t\t */\n402\t\t\tfailureCode: text(\"failure_code\"),\n403\t\t\t/** The §11.3 guards this attempt passed, as a JSON array of guard names. */\n404\t\t\tguardsPassedJson: text(\"guards_passed_json\").notNull().default(\"[]\"),\n405\t\t\t/**\n406\t\t\t * The coverage epoch in force when this row was created.\n407\t\t\t *\n408\t\t\t * Not load-bearing for the status of a PRESENT row — §11.5 serves those\n409\t\t\t * regardless of coverage — so this is deliberately not consulted by the CAS.\n410\t\t\t * It is what makes a degraded window diagnosable after the fact: \"this row\n411\t\t\t * predates the rotation the client's stale epoch is complaining about\".\n412\t\t\t */\n413\t\t\tcoverageEpoch: text(\"coverage_epoch\").notNull(),\n414\t\t},\n415\t\t(table) => [\n416\t\t\t// Pruning scans by age; nothing else queries by anything but the key.\n417\t\t\tindex(\"answer_attempts_created_at_ms_idx\").on(table.createdAtMs),\n418\t\t],\n419\t);\n420\t\n421\t/**\n422\t * (ANSWER-LEDGER) The single-row coverage epoch (always `id = 1`).\n423\t *\n424\t * WHAT IT IS. An opaque random id, NOT a timestamp, that a client captures\n425\t * BEFORE it submits an answer, echoes back on a status read, and the server\n426\t * compares for EQUALITY. Nothing is subtracted, ordered, or aged.\n427\t *\n428\t * WHY IT REPLACED A WALL CLOCK. §11.5's original coverage proof had the client\n429\t * compute `serverTimeMs - recordsSinceMs > its own monotonic submitAgeMs`. That\n430\t * arithmetic mixes two clocks, and it fails in the one direction that matters: a\n431\t * server clock step FORWARD inflates the apparent coverage age, so a request the\n432\t * bridge cannot actually vouch for satisfies the inequality and the client\n433\t * renders the unrecoverable \"it was not sent\". An opaque token has no arithmetic\n434\t * to get wrong — equal or not equal, and unequal is never a terminal negative.\n435\t *\n436\t * WHY IT MUST ROTATE. The token means \"the coverage you captured is still the\n437\t * coverage I have\". Anything that loses continuity must rotate it in the SAME\n438\t * transaction, so a status read carrying the old token is answered with a degrade\n439\t * rather than a negative. In practice nothing does: `answer_attempts` is never\n440\t * pruned, because every row there is a fence and forgetting one un-decides\n441\t * something already announced to a phone. The token therefore changes only when a\n442\t * database is new or its continuity is genuinely lost — see\n443\t * `(LEDGER-KEEP-ATTEMPTS)` in `companion/attempt-ledger.ts`.\n444\t *\n445\t * WHY A DEDICATED TABLE AND NOT `host_settings`. `host_settings` is documented as\n446\t * host-wide user KNOBS that projects fall back to; every settings write path can\n447\t * touch it and every column there is nullable by design. This is not a knob, it\n448\t * is a safety token: it must be notNull, it must be rotated transactionally with\n449\t * a delete, and its lifecycle must be owned entirely by the ledger. Putting it in"}]},"uuid":"66954c63-1f97-4410-b6ae-16c70f8fb38c","timestamp":"2026-08-12T02:31:47.834Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\db\\schema.ts","content":" * A fence needs an atomic compare-and-set between the two paths, which needs a\n * real transaction, which is what SQLite has and a read-modify-rewrite of a JSON\n * file structurally does not.\n *\n * SECOND-ORDER WIN, worth stating because it changes an accepted failure. The\n * JSON file was validated as a WHOLE: one incoherent record failed the file\n * schema and quarantined all 24 h of everyone else's records. Rows validate\n * individually here (`attempt-ledger.ts`), so one corrupt row costs exactly one\n * requestId its status.\n */\nexport const answerAttempts = sqliteTable(\n\t\"answer_attempts\",\n\t{\n\t\t/**\n\t\t * §11.4's idempotency key, and the CAS key. The PRIMARY KEY is doing real\n\t\t * work: it is the uniqueness constraint that makes `INSERT .. ON CONFLICT DO\n\t\t * NOTHING` a decision procedure rather than a race.\n\t\t */\n\t\trequestId: text(\"request_id\").primaryKey(),\n\t\t/**\n\t\t * Null for a `closed_not_received` tombstone and ONLY for one — a row that\n\t\t * asserts nothing ever arrived has no attempt to describe. Storing a sentinel\n\t\t * instead would be a lie the boundary could not catch, so the nullability is\n\t\t * deliberate and the tombstone/attempt shapes are cross-checked on read.\n\t\t */\n\t\tquestionId: text(\"question_id\"),\n\t\tdeviceId: text(\"device_id\"),\n\t\tsurface: text().$type<\"phone\" | \"watch\">(),\n\t\tleaseId: text(\"lease_id\"),\n\t\t/** The attempt's own start. Null for a tombstone, exactly as above. */\n\t\tstartedAtMs: integer(\"started_at_ms\"),\n\t\t/**\n\t\t * The row's creation instant, always set — including for a tombstone, which\n\t\t * is why it is separate from `startedAtMs`.\n\t\t *\n\t\t * Retention ages on THIS column so pruning has one unambiguous clock for both\n\t\t * row shapes. For a real attempt the two coincide (the claim happens at the\n\t\t * attempt's start); they are still kept apart because a single column that\n\t\t * means \"the attempt began\" for one shape and \"the negative was asserted\" for\n\t\t * another is the kind of overload a later reader gets wrong.\n\t\t */\n\t\tcreatedAtMs: integer(\"created_at_ms\").notNull(),\n\t\tstatus: text().notNull().$type<AnswerAttemptStatus>(),\n\t\tresolvedAtMs: integer(\"resolved_at_ms\"),\n\t\t/**\n\t\t * Plain text, not `$type`d. The authoritative union is\n\t\t * `AttemptFailureCode` in `src/companion/types.ts`, which this file must not\n\t\t * import (see `answerAttemptStatuses`), and duplicating it here would create\n\t\t * two lists to widen together. The ledger's own public write signature takes\n\t\t * `AttemptFailureCode`, so the compile-time check lives at the call site where\n\t\t * it is useful, and the runtime check lives at the read boundary.\n\t\t */\n\t\tfailureCode: text(\"failure_code\"),\n\t\t/** The §11.3 guards this attempt passed, as a JSON array of guard names. */\n\t\tguardsPassedJson: text(\"guards_passed_json\").notNull().default(\"[]\"),\n\t\t/**\n\t\t * The coverage epoch in force when this row was created.\n\t\t *\n\t\t * Not load-bearing for the status of a PRESENT row — §11.5 serves those\n\t\t * regardless of coverage — so this is deliberately not consulted by the CAS.\n\t\t * It is what makes a degraded window diagnosable after the fact: \"this row\n\t\t * predates the rotation the client's stale epoch is complaining about\".\n\t\t */\n\t\tcoverageEpoch: text(\"coverage_epoch\").notNull(),\n\t},\n\t(table) => [\n\t\t// Pruning scans by age; nothing else queries by anything but the key.\n\t\tindex(\"answer_attempts_created_at_ms_idx\").on(table.createdAtMs),\n\t],\n);\n\n/**\n * (ANSWER-LEDGER) The single-row coverage epoch (always `id = 1`).\n *\n * WHAT IT IS. An opaque random id, NOT a timestamp, that a client captures\n * BEFORE it submits an answer, echoes back on a status read, and the server\n * compares for EQUALITY. Nothing is subtracted, ordered, or aged.\n *\n * WHY IT REPLACED A WALL CLOCK. §11.5's original coverage proof had the client\n * compute `serverTimeMs - recordsSinceMs > its own monotonic submitAgeMs`. That\n * arithmetic mixes two clocks, and it fails in the one direction that matters: a\n * server clock step FORWARD inflates the apparent coverage age, so a request the\n * bridge cannot actually vouch for satisfies the inequality and the client\n * renders the unrecoverable \"it was not sent\". An opaque token has no arithmetic\n * to get wrong — equal or not equal, and unequal is never a terminal negative.\n *\n * WHY IT MUST ROTATE. The token means \"the coverage you captured is still the\n * coverage I have\". Anything that loses continuity must rotate it in the SAME\n * transaction, so a status read carrying the old token is answered with a degrade\n * rather than a negative. In practice nothing does: `answer_attempts` is never\n * pruned, because every row there is a fence and forgetting one un-decides\n * something already announced to a phone. The token therefore changes only when a\n * database is new or its continuity is genuinely lost — see\n * `(LEDGER-KEEP-ATTEMPTS)` in `companion/attempt-ledger.ts`.\n *\n * WHY A DEDICATED TABLE AND NOT `host_settings`. `host_settings` is documented as\n * host-wide user KNOBS that projects fall back to; every settings write path can\n * touch it and every column there is nullable by design. This is not a knob, it\n * is a safety token: it must be notNull, it must be rotated transactionally with\n * a delete, and its lifecycle must be owned entirely by the ledger. Putting it in","numLines":100,"startLine":350,"totalLines":844}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"dbc0b374-a0e1-4de9-a460-072f8293c9cb","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
