{"parentUuid":"a3051889-ee24-4e9b-aca2-bb5ce9d9ceb0","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"resp_0ea805d884469ae5016a7c556fe3688191a37b55c142288401","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"codex-gpt-5-6-sol","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3447,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":109056,"output_tokens":58,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_hoZd3x95H3GCr3KdnPA7H7DR","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\keystrokes.ts","offset":700,"limit":300}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_details":null},"attributionSkill":"windows-computer-use","type":"assistant","uuid":"5c92a012-0e52-4173-a8c8-bc95427930bf","timestamp":"2026-08-12T11:13:54.051Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"5c92a012-0e52-4173-a8c8-bc95427930bf","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"44611460-2829-462e-b2e9-af8e653be0db","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_hoZd3x95H3GCr3KdnPA7H7DR","type":"tool_result","content":"700\t *    dangerous byte this validator can pass.\n701\t *  - `\\x1b` (0x1B) is the introducer of every control sequence the encoder emits\n702\t *    (`KEY_RIGHT_ARROW`); text carrying it could drive the TUI directly.\n703\t *  - `\\t` (0x09) moves focus between the picker's tabs, which is a navigation\n704\t *    act, not a character.\n705\t *  - `\\n` (0x0A) is unproven against the inline editor — see above.\n706\t *  - `\\0`, BEL, BS and the rest of C0 have no meaning as typed text and are\n707\t *    terminal control.\n708\t *  - DEL (0x7F) and C1 (0x80..0x9F) are refused by the scan itself and can never\n709\t *    be allow-listed: `U+009B` is CSI, the single-character `ESC [`, which a\n710\t *    UTF-8 terminal acts on exactly like the `\\x1b` above.\n711\t */\n712\tconst FREETEXT_ALLOWED_C0: ReadonlySet<number> = new Set<number>();\n713\t\n714\t/**\n715\t * The allow-list for a `/v1/message` body, which is a DIFFERENT writer with a\n716\t * different proof: `writeFramedInputToSession` frames the body as a bracketed\n717\t * paste, and a bracketed paste carries LF and TAB to the composer as literal\n718\t * characters rather than as Enter or as focus movement. That framing is what\n719\t * earns these two bytes; the picker path has no equivalent and so keeps the\n720\t * empty set above.\n721\t *\n722\t * Kept as a separate constant rather than by widening `FREETEXT_ALLOWED_C0`,\n723\t * because one writer's genuine need must never loosen the other writer's\n724\t * validator. §0.3 of PROTOCOL.md is the normative table both runtimes implement.\n725\t *\n726\t * Both members are C0. DEL and C1 are refused by `findForbiddenControlChar`\n727\t * itself on every path and are deliberately not expressible here: a bracketed\n728\t * paste carries `U+009B` to the composer as the CSI byte pair `c2 9b`, which is\n729\t * not a character in a sentence.\n730\t */\n731\texport const MESSAGE_ALLOWED_C0: ReadonlySet<number> = new Set([\n732\t\tCHAR_LF.charCodeAt(0),\n733\t\tCHAR_TAB.charCodeAt(0),\n734\t]);\n735\t\n736\t/**\n737\t * Reject strings that are not well-formed Unicode.\n738\t *\n739\t * A lone surrogate (an unpaired 0xD800..0xDFFF code unit) is a legal JS string\n740\t * but has no UTF-8 encoding. Node substitutes U+FFFD when it encodes one, so a\n741\t * value that passed validation reached the pty as `ef bf bd` — the user's text\n742\t * SILENTLY MUTATED on its way into a live terminal. Refusing is the only honest\n743\t * option: the bridge cannot know what the client meant, and guessing at it is\n744\t * the same class of mistake as normalising a duplicate option index.\n745\t *\n746\t * Implemented by hand rather than via `String.prototype.isWellFormed`: the\n747\t * workspace compiles against `lib: ES2022`, where that method does not exist.\n748\t *\n749\t * Exported because `/v1/message` types text into the same terminals through a\n750\t * different writer and must not have a second, drifting copy of this rule.\n751\t */\n752\texport function findUnpairedSurrogate(\n753\t\ttext: string,\n754\t): { index: number; unit: number } | null {\n755\t\tfor (let i = 0; i < text.length; i += 1) {\n756\t\t\tconst unit = text.charCodeAt(i);\n757\t\t\tif (unit < 0xd800 || unit > 0xdfff) continue;\n758\t\t\t// A high surrogate must be followed by a low surrogate; a low surrogate may\n759\t\t\t// never appear on its own.\n760\t\t\tconst isHigh = unit <= 0xdbff;\n761\t\t\tconst next = isHigh ? text.charCodeAt(i + 1) : Number.NaN;\n762\t\t\tif (isHigh && next >= 0xdc00 && next <= 0xdfff) {\n763\t\t\t\ti += 1;\n764\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n765\t\t\t}\n766\t\t\treturn { index: i, unit };\n767\t\t}\n768\t\treturn null;\n769\t}\n770\t\n771\t/**\n772\t * The first character that is a control byte this text may not carry, or `null`.\n773\t *\n774\t * \"Control\" is the full set PROTOCOL §0.3 names, and nothing narrower:\n775\t *\n776\t *  - C0, `U+0000`..`U+001F`;\n777\t *  - DEL, `U+007F`;\n778\t *  - **C1, `U+0080`..`U+009F`.**\n779\t *\n780\t * C1 is not decoration. `U+009B` is CSI — the single-character form of `ESC [`\n781\t * — and Node encodes it as the two UTF-8 bytes `c2 9b`, which a terminal in\n782\t * UTF-8 mode consumes as a control-sequence introducer. The picker's free-text\n783\t * body is written RAW (bracketed paste is inert against the picker), so a text\n784\t * carrying `U+009B` would drive the TUI exactly the way the refused `\\x1b` does,\n785\t * and `U+0085` (NEL) is a line break by another name. Scanning only `< 0x20`\n786\t * plus DEL admitted both into the one module whose whole policy is that an\n787\t * unproven byte is refused rather than guessed at. Neither is reachable from the\n788\t * Android client — `SemanticText.firstControlChar` has always rejected\n789\t * `0x80..0x9F` — so this closes daylight between the two runtimes rather than\n790\t * changing what any shipping client can do.\n791\t *\n792\t * The scan is over UTF-16 code units, the same unit both runtimes count length\n793\t * in, so an index reported here means the same position on both sides.\n794\t *\n795\t * `allowed` is REQUIRED and names the policy explicitly at each call site: the\n796\t * picker's free-text body passes `FREETEXT_ALLOWED_C0` (empty), `/v1/message`\n797\t * passes `MESSAGE_ALLOWED_C0` (`{LF, TAB}`). One implementation so the two\n798\t * writers cannot drift on what \"control byte\" means; two policies so one\n799\t * writer's proof can never silently license the other's bytes. An allow-list may\n800\t * only ever name C0 members — DEL and C1 have no proven meaning as typed text on\n801\t * either writer, so nothing is exempt from them.\n802\t *\n803\t * Exported for the same reason as `findUnpairedSurrogate`: `/v1/message` types\n804\t * into the same terminals through a different writer and must not carry a\n805\t * second, drifting copy of this scan.\n806\t */\n807\texport function findForbiddenControlChar(\n808\t\ttext: string,\n809\t\tallowed: ReadonlySet<number>,\n810\t): { index: number; code: number } | null {\n811\t\tfor (let i = 0; i < text.length; i += 1) {\n812\t\t\tconst code = text.charCodeAt(i);\n813\t\t\tconst isControl =\n814\t\t\t\tcode < 0x20 || code === 0x7f || (code >= 0x80 && code <= 0x9f);\n815\t\t\tif (!isControl) continue;\n816\t\t\tif (allowed.has(code)) continue;\n817\t\t\treturn { index: i, code };\n818\t\t}\n819\t\treturn null;\n820\t}\n821\t\n822\t/**\n823\t * §11.2 boundary validation of a free-text body, before any byte is encoded.\n824\t *\n825\t * Length, character set and Unicode well-formedness. Every failure refuses;\n826\t * nothing is stripped, normalised or substituted.\n827\t */\n828\tfunction assertFreeText(text: string, itemIndex: number): void {\n829\t\tif (text.length === 0) {\n830\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n831\t\t\t\t\"freetext_invalid\",\n832\t\t\t\t`item ${itemIndex} free text is empty`,\n833\t\t\t);\n834\t\t}\n835\t\tif (text.length > FREETEXT_MAX_CHARS) {\n836\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n837\t\t\t\t\"freetext_invalid\",\n838\t\t\t\t`item ${itemIndex} free text is ${text.length} chars, max ${FREETEXT_MAX_CHARS}`,\n839\t\t\t);\n840\t\t}\n841\t\tconst control = findForbiddenControlChar(text, FREETEXT_ALLOWED_C0);\n842\t\tif (control !== null) {\n843\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n844\t\t\t\t\"freetext_invalid\",\n845\t\t\t\t`item ${itemIndex} free text carries control byte 0x${control.code.toString(16)} at ${control.index}; refused, never stripped`,\n846\t\t\t);\n847\t\t}\n848\t\tconst surrogate = findUnpairedSurrogate(text);\n849\t\tif (surrogate !== null) {\n850\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n851\t\t\t\t\"freetext_invalid\",\n852\t\t\t\t`item ${itemIndex} free text carries an unpaired surrogate 0x${surrogate.unit.toString(16)} at ${surrogate.index}; refused rather than encoded as U+FFFD, which would type something other than what was written`,\n853\t\t\t);\n854\t\t}\n855\t}\n856\t\n857\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n858\t// shape classification\n859\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n860\t\n861\t/**\n862\t * Classifies a validated (questions, answers) pair into one of the PROVEN\n863\t * shapes, or throws `shape_unproven`.\n864\t *\n865\t * Call `validateAnswerItems` first — this assumes the pair is already\n866\t * structurally sound.\n867\t */\n868\texport function classifyAnswerShape(\n869\t\tquestions: readonly QuestionItem[],\n870\t\tanswers: readonly AnswerItem[],\n871\t): AnswerShape {\n872\t\tif (questions.length === 1) {\n873\t\t\tconst only = answers[0];\n874\t\t\tif (only === undefined) {\n875\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\"arity_mismatch\", \"no answer item\");\n876\t\t\t}\n877\t\t\tswitch (only.kind) {\n878\t\t\t\tcase \"select\":\n879\t\t\t\t\treturn \"single_select_one\";\n880\t\t\t\tcase \"multiselect\":\n881\t\t\t\t\treturn \"multiselect_one\";\n882\t\t\t\tcase \"freetext\":\n883\t\t\t\t\treturn \"freetext_one\";\n884\t\t\t\tdefault: {\n885\t\t\t\t\tconst unreachable: never = only;\n886\t\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n887\t\t\t\t\t\t\"kind_mismatch\",\n888\t\t\t\t\t\t`unknown answer kind: ${JSON.stringify(unreachable)}`,\n889\t\t\t\t\t);\n890\t\t\t\t}\n891\t\t\t}\n892\t\t}\n893\t\n894\t\t// N > 1. All three kinds compose freely here, because every per-question\n895\t\t// group leaves the picker on the NEXT question: a bare digit advances, the\n896\t\t// `\\r` that closes an inline editor advances (FREETEXT-N2-PROVEN), and a\n897\t\t// multi-select group's terminating `\\x1b[C` advances (MSEL-N2-PROVEN).\n898\t\t// The multi-select group is gated on its per-version proof: on a build\n899\t\t// where it was never driven, refusing is still the only honest option.\n900\t\tlet sawFreeText = false;\n901\t\tlet sawMultiSelect = false;\n902\t\tfor (const answer of answers) {\n903\t\t\tif (answer.kind === \"select\") continue;\n904\t\t\tif (answer.kind === \"freetext\") {\n905\t\t\t\tsawFreeText = true;\n906\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n907\t\t\t}\n908\t\t\tsawMultiSelect = true;\n909\t\t}\n910\t\tif (sawMultiSelect) {\n911\t\t\tif (provenPickerContract()?.multiSelectManyBytesProven !== true) {\n912\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n913\t\t\t\t\t\"shape_unproven\",\n914\t\t\t\t\t`a multi-select group inside an ${questions.length}-question prompt has no proven byte sequence on ${PROVEN_AGAINST}; refused rather than guessed`,\n915\t\t\t\t);\n916\t\t\t}\n917\t\t\treturn \"multiselect_many\";\n918\t\t}\n919\t\treturn sawFreeText ? \"freetext_many\" : \"single_select_many\";\n920\t}\n921\t\n922\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n923\t// encoding\n924\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n925\t\n926\t/**\n927\t * The picker numbers its rows from 1; the protocol indexes options from 0.\n928\t * A single bare digit can only address 1..9, so an option at index >= 9 has NO\n929\t * single-keystroke encoding. Emitting \"1\" then \"0\" for row 10 would select row 1\n930\t * and then press 0 — a different, wrong, irreversible answer. Refused.\n931\t */\n932\texport function digitFor(optionIndex: number, itemIndex: number): string {\n933\t\tif (optionIndex > MAX_DIGIT_ADDRESSABLE_INDEX) {\n934\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n935\t\t\t\t\"digit_out_of_range\",\n936\t\t\t\t`item ${itemIndex} option ${optionIndex} needs row ${optionIndex + 1}, which is not a single digit; refused (typing \"1\" then \"0\" would select row 1 and then press 0)`,\n937\t\t\t);\n938\t\t}\n939\t\treturn String(optionIndex + 1);\n940\t}\n941\t\n942\t/**\n943\t * (FREETEXT-CONTRACT-REPROVEN) The free-text sequence, re-driven on 2.1.226.\n944\t *\n945\t * This was briefly refused outright on my reading that a bare digit on the\n946\t * free-text row only MOVED THE CARET — the caret did move onto the row and the\n947\t * footer gained \"ctrl+g to edit in Notepad\", which I took as \"focused, not\n948\t * opened\". That screen does not actually distinguish the two: the option list\n949\t * stays rendered either way, and I never typed, so the reading rested on a\n950\t * placeholder string I expected and did not see. A later run drove the WHOLE\n951\t * sequence and the agent's `tool_result` came back with the typed text verbatim,\n952\t * which settles it — the original `[digit N+1, text, \"\\r\"]` shape is correct.\n953\t *\n954\t * The lesson worth keeping: a missing needle is not evidence of a missing\n955\t * behaviour. Only a `tool_result` proves a byte sequence.\n956\t */\n957\t\n958\t/**\n959\t * Intent -> the exact keystroke sequence. Pure: it touches no terminal, holds no\n960\t * lock and has no side effects, so every failure here is provably\n961\t * \"nothing was written\".\n962\t *\n963\t * The sequence is returned as DISCRETE keystrokes rather than one blob because\n964\t * guard 5 is re-evaluated between every one of them.\n965\t */\n966\texport function encodeAnswer(\n967\t\tquestions: readonly QuestionItem[],\n968\t\tanswers: readonly AnswerItem[],\n969\t): Keystroke[] {\n970\t\tvalidateAnswerItems(questions, answers);\n971\t\tconst shape = classifyAnswerShape(questions, answers);\n972\t\n973\t\tconst keystrokes: Keystroke[] =\n974\t\t\tshape === \"single_select_one\"\n975\t\t\t\t? encodeSingleSelectOne(questions, answers)\n976\t\t\t\t: shape === \"single_select_many\" ||\n977\t\t\t\t\t\tshape === \"freetext_many\" ||\n978\t\t\t\t\t\tshape === \"multiselect_many\"\n979\t\t\t\t\t? encodeMany(questions, answers)\n980\t\t\t\t\t: shape === \"multiselect_one\"\n981\t\t\t\t\t\t? encodeMultiSelectOne(questions, answers)\n982\t\t\t\t\t\t: encodeFreeTextOne(questions, answers);\n983\t\n984\t\tassertEmittedShape(shape, keystrokes);\n985\t\tassertNoReturnIntoMultiSelect(questions, keystrokes);\n986\t\treturn keystrokes;\n987\t}\n988\t\n989\t/** `[digit]` — one bare digit selects AND submits. No Enter, ever. */\n990\tfunction encodeSingleSelectOne(\n991\t\tquestions: readonly QuestionItem[],\n992\t\tanswers: readonly AnswerItem[],\n993\t): Keystroke[] {\n994\t\tconst answer = answers[0];\n995\t\tif (answer === undefined || answer.kind !== \"select\") {\n996\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\"kind_mismatch\", \"expected one select\");\n997\t\t}\n998\t\tif (questions[0] === undefined) {\n999\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\"arity_mismatch\", \"missing question item\");"}]},"uuid":"d0f01d34-503b-4ad0-b3f6-55e7c7b3332b","timestamp":"2026-08-12T11:13:54.363Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\keystrokes.ts","content":" *    dangerous byte this validator can pass.\n *  - `\\x1b` (0x1B) is the introducer of every control sequence the encoder emits\n *    (`KEY_RIGHT_ARROW`); text carrying it could drive the TUI directly.\n *  - `\\t` (0x09) moves focus between the picker's tabs, which is a navigation\n *    act, not a character.\n *  - `\\n` (0x0A) is unproven against the inline editor — see above.\n *  - `\\0`, BEL, BS and the rest of C0 have no meaning as typed text and are\n *    terminal control.\n *  - DEL (0x7F) and C1 (0x80..0x9F) are refused by the scan itself and can never\n *    be allow-listed: `U+009B` is CSI, the single-character `ESC [`, which a\n *    UTF-8 terminal acts on exactly like the `\\x1b` above.\n */\nconst FREETEXT_ALLOWED_C0: ReadonlySet<number> = new Set<number>();\n\n/**\n * The allow-list for a `/v1/message` body, which is a DIFFERENT writer with a\n * different proof: `writeFramedInputToSession` frames the body as a bracketed\n * paste, and a bracketed paste carries LF and TAB to the composer as literal\n * characters rather than as Enter or as focus movement. That framing is what\n * earns these two bytes; the picker path has no equivalent and so keeps the\n * empty set above.\n *\n * Kept as a separate constant rather than by widening `FREETEXT_ALLOWED_C0`,\n * because one writer's genuine need must never loosen the other writer's\n * validator. §0.3 of PROTOCOL.md is the normative table both runtimes implement.\n *\n * Both members are C0. DEL and C1 are refused by `findForbiddenControlChar`\n * itself on every path and are deliberately not expressible here: a bracketed\n * paste carries `U+009B` to the composer as the CSI byte pair `c2 9b`, which is\n * not a character in a sentence.\n */\nexport const MESSAGE_ALLOWED_C0: ReadonlySet<number> = new Set([\n\tCHAR_LF.charCodeAt(0),\n\tCHAR_TAB.charCodeAt(0),\n]);\n\n/**\n * Reject strings that are not well-formed Unicode.\n *\n * A lone surrogate (an unpaired 0xD800..0xDFFF code unit) is a legal JS string\n * but has no UTF-8 encoding. Node substitutes U+FFFD when it encodes one, so a\n * value that passed validation reached the pty as `ef bf bd` — the user's text\n * SILENTLY MUTATED on its way into a live terminal. Refusing is the only honest\n * option: the bridge cannot know what the client meant, and guessing at it is\n * the same class of mistake as normalising a duplicate option index.\n *\n * Implemented by hand rather than via `String.prototype.isWellFormed`: the\n * workspace compiles against `lib: ES2022`, where that method does not exist.\n *\n * Exported because `/v1/message` types text into the same terminals through a\n * different writer and must not have a second, drifting copy of this rule.\n */\nexport function findUnpairedSurrogate(\n\ttext: string,\n): { index: number; unit: number } | null {\n\tfor (let i = 0; i < text.length; i += 1) {\n\t\tconst unit = text.charCodeAt(i);\n\t\tif (unit < 0xd800 || unit > 0xdfff) continue;\n\t\t// A high surrogate must be followed by a low surrogate; a low surrogate may\n\t\t// never appear on its own.\n\t\tconst isHigh = unit <= 0xdbff;\n\t\tconst next = isHigh ? text.charCodeAt(i + 1) : Number.NaN;\n\t\tif (isHigh && next >= 0xdc00 && next <= 0xdfff) {\n\t\t\ti += 1;\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn { index: i, unit };\n\t}\n\treturn null;\n}\n\n/**\n * The first character that is a control byte this text may not carry, or `null`.\n *\n * \"Control\" is the full set PROTOCOL §0.3 names, and nothing narrower:\n *\n *  - C0, `U+0000`..`U+001F`;\n *  - DEL, `U+007F`;\n *  - **C1, `U+0080`..`U+009F`.**\n *\n * C1 is not decoration. `U+009B` is CSI — the single-character form of `ESC [`\n * — and Node encodes it as the two UTF-8 bytes `c2 9b`, which a terminal in\n * UTF-8 mode consumes as a control-sequence introducer. The picker's free-text\n * body is written RAW (bracketed paste is inert against the picker), so a text\n * carrying `U+009B` would drive the TUI exactly the way the refused `\\x1b` does,\n * and `U+0085` (NEL) is a line break by another name. Scanning only `< 0x20`\n * plus DEL admitted both into the one module whose whole policy is that an\n * unproven byte is refused rather than guessed at. Neither is reachable from the\n * Android client — `SemanticText.firstControlChar` has always rejected\n * `0x80..0x9F` — so this closes daylight between the two runtimes rather than\n * changing what any shipping client can do.\n *\n * The scan is over UTF-16 code units, the same unit both runtimes count length\n * in, so an index reported here means the same position on both sides.\n *\n * `allowed` is REQUIRED and names the policy explicitly at each call site: the\n * picker's free-text body passes `FREETEXT_ALLOWED_C0` (empty), `/v1/message`\n * passes `MESSAGE_ALLOWED_C0` (`{LF, TAB}`). One implementation so the two\n * writers cannot drift on what \"control byte\" means; two policies so one\n * writer's proof can never silently license the other's bytes. An allow-list may\n * only ever name C0 members — DEL and C1 have no proven meaning as typed text on\n * either writer, so nothing is exempt from them.\n *\n * Exported for the same reason as `findUnpairedSurrogate`: `/v1/message` types\n * into the same terminals through a different writer and must not carry a\n * second, drifting copy of this scan.\n */\nexport function findForbiddenControlChar(\n\ttext: string,\n\tallowed: ReadonlySet<number>,\n): { index: number; code: number } | null {\n\tfor (let i = 0; i < text.length; i += 1) {\n\t\tconst code = text.charCodeAt(i);\n\t\tconst isControl =\n\t\t\tcode < 0x20 || code === 0x7f || (code >= 0x80 && code <= 0x9f);\n\t\tif (!isControl) continue;\n\t\tif (allowed.has(code)) continue;\n\t\treturn { index: i, code };\n\t}\n\treturn null;\n}\n\n/**\n * §11.2 boundary validation of a free-text body, before any byte is encoded.\n *\n * Length, character set and Unicode well-formedness. Every failure refuses;\n * nothing is stripped, normalised or substituted.\n */\nfunction assertFreeText(text: string, itemIndex: number): void {\n\tif (text.length === 0) {\n\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\"freetext_invalid\",\n\t\t\t`item ${itemIndex} free text is empty`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (text.length > FREETEXT_MAX_CHARS) {\n\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\"freetext_invalid\",\n\t\t\t`item ${itemIndex} free text is ${text.length} chars, max ${FREETEXT_MAX_CHARS}`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tconst control = findForbiddenControlChar(text, FREETEXT_ALLOWED_C0);\n\tif (control !== null) {\n\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\"freetext_invalid\",\n\t\t\t`item ${itemIndex} free text carries control byte 0x${control.code.toString(16)} at ${control.index}; refused, never stripped`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tconst surrogate = findUnpairedSurrogate(text);\n\tif (surrogate !== null) {\n\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\"freetext_invalid\",\n\t\t\t`item ${itemIndex} free text carries an unpaired surrogate 0x${surrogate.unit.toString(16)} at ${surrogate.index}; refused rather than encoded as U+FFFD, which would type something other than what was written`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// shape classification\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n/**\n * Classifies a validated (questions, answers) pair into one of the PROVEN\n * shapes, or throws `shape_unproven`.\n *\n * Call `validateAnswerItems` first — this assumes the pair is already\n * structurally sound.\n */\nexport function classifyAnswerShape(\n\tquestions: readonly QuestionItem[],\n\tanswers: readonly AnswerItem[],\n): AnswerShape {\n\tif (questions.length === 1) {\n\t\tconst only = answers[0];\n\t\tif (only === undefined) {\n\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\"arity_mismatch\", \"no answer item\");\n\t\t}\n\t\tswitch (only.kind) {\n\t\t\tcase \"select\":\n\t\t\t\treturn \"single_select_one\";\n\t\t\tcase \"multiselect\":\n\t\t\t\treturn \"multiselect_one\";\n\t\t\tcase \"freetext\":\n\t\t\t\treturn \"freetext_one\";\n\t\t\tdefault: {\n\t\t\t\tconst unreachable: never = only;\n\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\t\t\"kind_mismatch\",\n\t\t\t\t\t`unknown answer kind: ${JSON.stringify(unreachable)}`,\n\t\t\t\t);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\t// N > 1. All three kinds compose freely here, because every per-question\n\t// group leaves the picker on the NEXT question: a bare digit advances, the\n\t// `\\r` that closes an inline editor advances (FREETEXT-N2-PROVEN), and a\n\t// multi-select group's terminating `\\x1b[C` advances (MSEL-N2-PROVEN).\n\t// The multi-select group is gated on its per-version proof: on a build\n\t// where it was never driven, refusing is still the only honest option.\n\tlet sawFreeText = false;\n\tlet sawMultiSelect = false;\n\tfor (const answer of answers) {\n\t\tif (answer.kind === \"select\") continue;\n\t\tif (answer.kind === \"freetext\") {\n\t\t\tsawFreeText = true;\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tsawMultiSelect = true;\n\t}\n\tif (sawMultiSelect) {\n\t\tif (provenPickerContract()?.multiSelectManyBytesProven !== true) {\n\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\t\"shape_unproven\",\n\t\t\t\t`a multi-select group inside an ${questions.length}-question prompt has no proven byte sequence on ${PROVEN_AGAINST}; refused rather than guessed`,\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn \"multiselect_many\";\n\t}\n\treturn sawFreeText ? \"freetext_many\" : \"single_select_many\";\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// encoding\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n/**\n * The picker numbers its rows from 1; the protocol indexes options from 0.\n * A single bare digit can only address 1..9, so an option at index >= 9 has NO\n * single-keystroke encoding. Emitting \"1\" then \"0\" for row 10 would select row 1\n * and then press 0 — a different, wrong, irreversible answer. Refused.\n */\nexport function digitFor(optionIndex: number, itemIndex: number): string {\n\tif (optionIndex > MAX_DIGIT_ADDRESSABLE_INDEX) {\n\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\"digit_out_of_range\",\n\t\t\t`item ${itemIndex} option ${optionIndex} needs row ${optionIndex + 1}, which is not a single digit; refused (typing \"1\" then \"0\" would select row 1 and then press 0)`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\treturn String(optionIndex + 1);\n}\n\n/**\n * (FREETEXT-CONTRACT-REPROVEN) The free-text sequence, re-driven on 2.1.226.\n *\n * This was briefly refused outright on my reading that a bare digit on the\n * free-text row only MOVED THE CARET — the caret did move onto the row and the\n * footer gained \"ctrl+g to edit in Notepad\", which I took as \"focused, not\n * opened\". That screen does not actually distinguish the two: the option list\n * stays rendered either way, and I never typed, so the reading rested on a\n * placeholder string I expected and did not see. A later run drove the WHOLE\n * sequence and the agent's `tool_result` came back with the typed text verbatim,\n * which settles it — the original `[digit N+1, text, \"\\r\"]` shape is correct.\n *\n * The lesson worth keeping: a missing needle is not evidence of a missing\n * behaviour. Only a `tool_result` proves a byte sequence.\n */\n\n/**\n * Intent -> the exact keystroke sequence. Pure: it touches no terminal, holds no\n * lock and has no side effects, so every failure here is provably\n * \"nothing was written\".\n *\n * The sequence is returned as DISCRETE keystrokes rather than one blob because\n * guard 5 is re-evaluated between every one of them.\n */\nexport function encodeAnswer(\n\tquestions: readonly QuestionItem[],\n\tanswers: readonly AnswerItem[],\n): Keystroke[] {\n\tvalidateAnswerItems(questions, answers);\n\tconst shape = classifyAnswerShape(questions, answers);\n\n\tconst keystrokes: Keystroke[] =\n\t\tshape === \"single_select_one\"\n\t\t\t? encodeSingleSelectOne(questions, answers)\n\t\t\t: shape === \"single_select_many\" ||\n\t\t\t\t\tshape === \"freetext_many\" ||\n\t\t\t\t\tshape === \"multiselect_many\"\n\t\t\t\t? encodeMany(questions, answers)\n\t\t\t\t: shape === \"multiselect_one\"\n\t\t\t\t\t? encodeMultiSelectOne(questions, answers)\n\t\t\t\t\t: encodeFreeTextOne(questions, answers);\n\n\tassertEmittedShape(shape, keystrokes);\n\tassertNoReturnIntoMultiSelect(questions, keystrokes);\n\treturn keystrokes;\n}\n\n/** `[digit]` — one bare digit selects AND submits. No Enter, ever. */\nfunction encodeSingleSelectOne(\n\tquestions: readonly QuestionItem[],\n\tanswers: readonly AnswerItem[],\n): Keystroke[] {\n\tconst answer = answers[0];\n\tif (answer === undefined || answer.kind !== \"select\") {\n\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\"kind_mismatch\", \"expected one select\");\n\t}\n\tif (questions[0] === undefined) {\n\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\"arity_mismatch\", \"missing question item\");","numLines":300,"startLine":700,"totalLines":1783}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5c92a012-0e52-4173-a8c8-bc95427930bf","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
