{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-12T13:49:41.484Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>aad3bdea00bc91122</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\083b7de7-951b-4059-a4aa-3dd81cb5a6cf\\tasks\\aad3bdea00bc91122.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review abstraction altitude\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Reviewed final settled tree (files were mid-edit by a concurrent agent for the first ~3 min; findings below are against current content).\n\n## Findings\n\n**1. `packages/host-service/src/companion/answer-guardless.test.ts:262` — new dep wired to the mark-only store method, so the composed behaviour is untested through the answer path.**\n`markRemoteAnsweredAndPublish: questions.markRemoteAnswered`. The dep exists precisely to pair the durable mark with the corrective frame; every test in this file substitutes the half that does no publishing. The only coverage of the composed function calls it directly (`answer-settlement-events.test.ts:91`), never through `handleAnswer`. Cost: the `createAnswerDeps` seam (`index.ts:2066`) is covered by nothing — a mis-wire there passes the whole suite. That exact mis-wire existed in the tree ten minutes ago and no test would have caught it.\n\n**2. `packages/host-service/src/companion/answer.ts:1319-1337` and `:1355-1370` — one policy, two encodings, 20 lines apart.**\nThe rule \"durable status `claimed` ⇒ nothing was typed ⇒ `failed`/`internal`; anything else ⇒ `unconfirmed`\" is written once as a const `outcome` object (`:1320`) and once as a three-branch ternary chain over `preWrite`/`SealedError` (`:1358-1367`). Right depth = one pure `abortOutcome(durable, error)` beside the ledger types, called from both catches. Cost: a new ledger status, or any change to what counts as proven-zero-write, needs two dissimilar edits; editing one silently splits the inner and outer error paths, and the ledger row and the audit entry then disagree about the same attempt.\n\n**3. `packages/host-service/src/companion/attempt-ledger.ts:139-140` — unknown sealed code collapses to `\"internal\"` silently.**\n`toLedgerFailureCode` is correctly placed (the layer owning `LEDGER_FAILURE_CODES` owns the narrowing, and it kills the old `as AttemptFailureCode` cast — good). But the widening-to-`internal` branch emits nothing. Cost: a genuinely-should-be-stored code added upstream never announces itself; the only symptom is a ledger where everything reads `internal`, diagnosable only by reading this function. Tension with the repo's fail-loud rule, which the sibling `reject(...)` path at `:552` honours.\n\n**4. `packages/host-service/src/companion/index.ts:1451` — `createNotifyingCaptureSink` widened from module-private to `export` for a test only.**\nConsumed exclusively by `answer-settlement-events.test.ts:3`. `index.ts` is the bridge composition root; anything exported there reads as supported API to the next refactorer, and no comment marks it test-only. Cost: future restructuring of the sink must preserve a public signature nothing in production imports.\n\n**5. `packages/host-service/src/companion/answer-settlement-events.test.ts:69, 72, 94` — `as never` casts fill three of five `NotifyingSinkDeps` members.**\nCost: the cast defeats the check the interface exists for. When `NotifyingSinkDeps` gains a member the test keeps compiling and silently exercises a half-built sink. `markRemoteAnsweredAndPublish` now takes its own `RemoteAnswerPublisherDeps` (`index.ts:1504`), so the second cast (`:94`) is avoidable with a real minimal double.\n\n**6. `packages/host-service/src/companion/answer-guardless.test.ts:254` and `:393` — monkey-patches a live registry instead of injecting a double.**\n`locks.runExclusive = options.lockRun`, then mid-test restore from a throwaway `createTerminalLockRegistry()`. Cost: the harness depends on the registry returning a writable-method object; if `createTerminalLockRegistry` ever returns a frozen object or class instance, both the setup and the restore break obscurely. Restore is also a second site that must know a swap happened — drop it and the \"retry succeeds\" assertion becomes vacuous.\n\n## Correct altitude (no change wanted)\n\n- `RemoteAnswerPublisherDeps` (`index.ts:1504`) as its own interface rather than `Pick&lt;NotifyingSinkDeps,…&gt;` — matches the documented `CurationGateDeps` precedent at `index.ts:1424`.\n- `publishQuestionResolved` (`index.ts:1429`) now backs all three publish sites (`:724`, `:1494`, `:1531`); the wire shape lives in one place.\n- Injecting the publisher into `AnswerDeps` rather than importing `EventStreamServer` into `answer.ts` keeps `answer.ts` as pure policy and `index.ts` as the composition root.\n- `toLedgerFailureCode`/`isLedgerFailureCode` placement — validation at the boundary that defines the accepted set, reused by row validation.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>80175</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>18</tool_uses><duration_ms>223024</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
