erbatim: its trailing `\\r` landed on the next question. The one shape still refused is refused on EVIDENCE rather than on caution: on a multi-select question the free-text row's digit toggles a checkbox, the body text is swallowed with no echo and the terminating `\\r` toggles whatever row the caret was on — a run that asked for free text came back answered with the first option instead. A `\\r` into a multi-select question is the single most dangerous byte this contract knows, so `assertNoReturnIntoMultiSelect` re-checks every emitted sequence against the prompt itself. The picker contract is per build AND per shape, and one build's proof can never backfill another's | `(COMPANION-BRIDGE)`, `(ANSWER-GUARDLESS)`, `(ANSWER-LEDGER)`, `(PUSH-PRESENCE)`, `(FREETEXT-N2-PROVEN)`, `(MSEL-N2-PROVEN)` |\n | Sidebar-state mirror | the desktop sidebar's CURATION — which projects and threads are in it, where they sit, and whether each is hidden, archived, snoozed, completed, soft-deleted, pinned or manually reordered — lives entirely in renderer `localStorage` collections (`v2WorkspaceLocalState`, `v2SidebarProjects`) that no other process can read, while `host.db` is a lifecycle-free append store of everything the machine has ever created. Anything outside the renderer that tries to show \"the user's sidebar\" therefore reads a structurally different, strictly larger set in which a thread binned last month is indistinguishable from one blocked on the user right now. This projects that curation into three `host.db` tables (`sidebar_workspace_state`, `sidebar_project_state`, `sidebar_mirror_meta`) via a fork-only `sidebarMirror.sync` tRPC mutation the renderer pushes on every curation change, debounced. It is a WHOLE-SNAPSHOT replace, not a delta: curation has no single choke point (context menus, bulk actions, kanban drags, the snooze submenu, drag-reorder, the CLI worktree placer), so the only correctness condition that survives new entry points is \"something changed\" — which also makes a failed sync, a host-service restart and a renderer reload all self-repair on the next push. The renderer stays the source of truth and the host-service never writes these tables; the mirror is a projection with ONE permitted failure direction — an ABSENT row means \"no opinion recorded\" and consumers must SHOW the row (`LEFT JOIN`, never `INNER`), because hiding on absence turns a transient miss into a blocked agent nobody sees. Staleness is deliberately NOT folded into that rule: a stale row still carrying `deleted_at`/`archived_at`/`is_hidden`/`snooze_until` hides a thread the user has since restored, which is the forbidden direction, so the writer bounds it instead — exactly one push in flight (two on separate loopback connections have no ordering guarantee, and an older one served last would leave host.db behind a signature already recorded as synced, freezing the mirror until the next curation change) and a retry that never gives up while the app runs (the bounded version had a terminal state that a host-service restart on the same port could never re-arm). Identity fields are normalized rather than trusted for the same reason a localStorage row cannot be: the collections validate on WRITE only, so a legacy row hands back `undefined` behind a `string` type, and the first `.length` on one would have thrown inside the render of a hook mounted with no error boundary nearer than the root route. With the renderer gone the mirror holds the user's last recorded curation; it never invents one. `(MIRROR-HEARTBEAT)` is what makes that statement CHECKABLE from outside: the signature gate that stops identical snapshots from being re-sent also made `last_full_sync_at_ms` measure the last curation CHANGE rather than renderer liveness, so an untouched sidebar was indistinguishable from a quit app and no consumer could age the mirror out toward showing. The same unchanged snapshot is therefore re-pushed every five minutes, and a push is additionally forced whenever the last PUSHED `app_launch_id` differs from the current one — the launch id travels in the meta row, not the snapshot, so a launch whose curation serialized identically to the previous one's would otherwise leave the mirror claiming the old launch and keep every thread snoozed-until-next-launch hidden downstream. `sidebar_mirror_meta` carries the two things an outside reader cannot otherwise derive: its presence distinguishes \"no renderer has ever synced\" (pass everything through) from a genuinely empty curated sidebar, and its `app_launch_id` is what makes an \"until next launch\" snooze evaluable at all | `(SIDEBAR-MIRROR)`, `(MIRROR-HEARTBEAT)` |\n | Companion tree mirrors the sidebar, not `host.db` | the bridge's three read statements were unqualified `SELECT`s, so the phone received a structurally different, strictly LARGER set than the desktop sidebar: 22/22 projects, 183/183 workspaces and 310 terminal rows, with binned, archived and snoozed threads rendering as ordinary live rows and 63 workspaces contributing an inert header with nothing under it. Two independent filters now stand between `host.db` and the wire. `(BRIDGE-SIDEBAR-FILTER)` consumes `(SIDEBAR-MIRROR)` and reproduces the renderer's own bucket classifier — deleted, completed, archived, snoozed and hidden are off the default sidebar and are therefore off the phone UNLESS they carry a pending captured question (a question pierces curation so it cannot disappear from every discovery surface), threads group under the project the user PLACED them in rather than `workspaces.project_id`, and a repo removed from the sidebar takes its threads with it. `(BRIDGE-LIVENESS)` answers the separate question of whether a `status='active'` row still names a pty that exists: it never did — a row leaves `active` only via an explicit dispose or a live in-process `onExit`, so a crash, a force-quit or a host-service restart strands it forever, and 403 such rows spanning 77 days were reaching the phone, 8 of them frozen on `PermissionRequest` and badging permanently-blocked agents that had not existed for weeks. Liveness is the same pair the host-service's own session listing joins (an in-process session, or an id the pty-daemon reports alive), snapshotted for read-side liveness filtering without daemon round trips, and the reap pass now walks row -> daemon as well as daemon -> row so `host.db` stops accumulating corpses (five independent conditions before a row is corrected, because being wrong costs a LIVE terminal its place in the desktop's own session list). `(REAPER-CORRECTION-CAP)` bounds that walk, because its two passes are not two independent observations: a degraded daemon — mid-restart, partially adopted, answering from a half-built registry — stays degraded for longer than the five minutes between them, so both listings can be the same wrong listing, and the corrective write has no undo. One pass may now correct at most `min(10, ceil(25% of active rows))` rows, oldest first, with everything over the cap keeping its confirmed two-pass standing for a later pass instead of restarting its clock; and \"an empty listing is never evidence\" is now asserted of BOTH observations rather than only the current one, by threading the previous pass's listed/not-listed fact in explicitly instead of leaving it to fall out of the caller clearing its set. `(CURATION-PROVENANCE)` makes the filter itself legible from the wire: every `/v1/tree` now carries an OPTIONAL `curation` object (enabled, mirror age, how many workspaces it removed), because an empty or short tree had two indistinguishable causes — nothing is running, or the mirror filtered it away — and only the second is a bug shape. Optional by contract, since both shipped clients must keep parsing a newer bridge. `(EMIT-OPTIONAL-FIELDS)` closes the same gap for three facts the bridge already held and never sent: a project/workspace `pinned` flag (pinning is the one act of curation that changes ORDER rather than membership, so a filter that only decides what to hide could not carry it, and `pinned_at`/`is_pinned` had been SELECTed and read by nothing; it is OMITTED rather than sent as `false` when curation is disabled, because §7.2 distinguishes a field the bridge does not report from one it reports as not-pinned, and a renderer that has never synced is not evidence that nothing is pinned), and `questionCount` + `multiSelect` on a pending-question ref — the headline is only the FIRST question's header, so a 3-question capture rendered exactly like a 1-question one, and a multi-select item has its own byte contract that single-select affordances would offer and the bridge would then refuse. The two filters have OPPOSITE failure directions from the guards they sit beside and that is deliberate: an unfilled mirror, an unmirrored workspace, an unreachable daemon or a stale snapshot all resolve to SHOWING the row, because hiding a real blocked agent is the one failure the feature cannot absorb — while the answer path consults neither liveness nor curation after resolving the captured terminal target, so a question captured before its thread was snoozed stays answerable. `(QUESTION-EXPIRY)` closes the same hole for questions: a pending question on a terminal that no longer exists is settled `stale` and its push retracted through the existing settled-ids wiring, instead of buzzing, counting toward `oldestUnansweredMs` and holding a slot forever. It is held to a HIGHER bar than the tree filter beside it, because `settle(stale)` is terminal and the retraction pulls a notification off the phone: it reads a strict `isProvablyGone` (never `!isLive`) that refuses to read anything into an empty daemon listing at any age — the reaper's rule, not the tree's — passes the row's own `last_attached_at ?? created_at` so a terminal born after the snapshot cannot lose that race, and requires the same verdict on two separate reconcile passes five minutes apart. A flap, an unreachable daemon or a stale snapshot resets the clock rather than condemning anything. The same strict predicate gates the push sender's fire-time re-check, whose `forget()` is equally one-shot: an uncertain verdict there keeps the buzz. `(BRIDGE-AGENT-KIND)` keeps read-side classification consistent: the kind falls back from nullable `definition_id` to the hook's exact `agent_id`. It is not an answer preflight — `(ANSWER-GUARDLESS)` deliberately writes to the captured Claude question's terminal without re-reading mutable binding evidence. Two whole-mirror gates sit in front of every per-row rule, both returning the SAME pass-through curation the bootstrap case does, because a mirror that is not evidence about this machine's sidebar right now must not be filtered with. `(MIRROR-AGE-OUT)` refuses a mirror whose last sync is over 20 minutes old — four of the writer's five-minute heartbeats, so only a genuinely absent renderer reaches it — because every hiding field in those tables is then an opinion from a session that has ended, and `snooze_launch_id` in particular keeps hiding threads the very next launch would have released, with nothing left to release them. `(MIRROR-ORG-GATE)` refuses a mirror written for a different organization: `host.db` is per machine and the mirror inside it is per org, the column had been written and read since the mirror shipped without ever being compared, and since ids do not collide across orgs the practical effect was the project gate hiding the ENTIRE tree. `(PUSH-CURATION-GATE)` extends the same curation to the one companion side effect that never passed through the read API: arming hangs off the capture sink, so a binned, archived, completed or snoozed thread still buzzed the WATCH and the notification then opened a tree that did not contain it — snooze worst of all, since \"not now\" is exactly a statement about being interrupted. It is asked at FIRE time, on every sweep, rather than once before `schedule` — and that is the correctness of the gate, not a detail of where it sits. Curation is REVOCABLE (a snooze expires by itself; an archive or a bin is undone by hand) while `armPush` runs exactly once, at capture, so declining to arm turned a twenty-minute snooze into six hours of si","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"persistedOutputPath":"C:\\Users\\khair\\.claude\\projects\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91\\tool-results\\bwwz21xzm.txt","persistedOutputSize":49894},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3e270ff0-d5d5-4d1a-a0ba-b4c5ad3ee602","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
