{"parentUuid":"c25e34d0-1f4e-4beb-b92e-8a69a8c33b72","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"5548f27b-2b1d-4e0e-84da-bd27822c3dc9","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_RZIiSg0OvI3Dzjxd6tB0B3lI","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (46.5KB). Full output saved to: C:\\Users\\khair\\.claude\\projects\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91\\tool-results\\ben05krsp.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\nwarning: in the working copy of 'packages/host-service/src/companion/answer-guardless.test.ts', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it\nwarning: in the working copy of 'packages/host-service/src/companion/answer.ts', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it\ndiff --git a/FEATURES.md b/FEATURES.md\nindex b202de796..a8e278a97 100644\n--- a/FEATURES.md\n+++ b/FEATURES.md\n@@ -77,7 +77,7 @@ in the merge that drops it (the only legitimate way a marker leaves this list).\n | Companion keep-awake | the phone/watch companion is worthless if the desktop sleeps mid-question: the answer path lives in host-service, which dies with the machine. The main process holds a power-save blocker (and suppresses app-initiated sleep) for exactly as long as the companion bridge is enabled AND running AND at least one device is paired, released the moment any of the three stops being true — it never pins the machine awake unconditionally, and it never blocks display sleep (screen off is fine; suspend is not) | `(KEEP-AWAKE)` |\n | Companion question capture | the `PreToolUse` AskUserQuestion hook payload already carries everything needed to DISCOVER and render a blocking question — `tool_name`, `tool_use_id`, `transcript_path`, `session_id`, `cwd`, `tool_input.questions[]` (`question`/`header`/`multiSelect`/`options[]`) plus `agent_id`/`agent_type` when a subagent asked — so the capture path forwards it to the bridge's question store instead of parsing transcripts (measured: 300 blocking questions over 7 days, 97.7% answerable, median 1 412 chars, 59% multi-question, ~10% multiSelect). Question text is captured VERBATIM and never truncated: a question longer than the terminal window was never retained in scrollback, so the screen can verify a picker but can never be its source | `(COMPANION-CAPTURE)` |\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"caa4ba37-1229-40c0-be6b-a164f95274b7","timestamp":"2026-08-12T17:45:46.862Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"warning: in the working copy of 'packages/host-service/src/companion/answer-guardless.test.ts', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it\nwarning: in the working copy of 'packages/host-service/src/companion/answer.ts', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it\ndiff --git a/FEATURES.md b/FEATURES.md\nindex b202de796..a8e278a97 100644\n--- a/FEATURES.md\n+++ b/FEATURES.md\n@@ -77,7 +77,7 @@ in the merge that drops it (the only legitimate way a marker leaves this list).\n | Companion keep-awake | the phone/watch companion is worthless if the desktop sleeps mid-question: the answer path lives in host-service, which dies with the machine. The main process holds a power-save blocker (and suppresses app-initiated sleep) for exactly as long as the companion bridge is enabled AND running AND at least one device is paired, released the moment any of the three stops being true — it never pins the machine awake unconditionally, and it never blocks display sleep (screen off is fine; suspend is not) | `(KEEP-AWAKE)` |\n | Companion question capture | the `PreToolUse` AskUserQuestion hook payload already carries everything needed to DISCOVER and render a blocking question — `tool_name`, `tool_use_id`, `transcript_path`, `session_id`, `cwd`, `tool_input.questions[]` (`question`/`header`/`multiSelect`/`options[]`) plus `agent_id`/`agent_type` when a subagent asked — so the capture path forwards it to the bridge's question store instead of parsing transcripts (measured: 300 blocking questions over 7 days, 97.7% answerable, median 1 412 chars, 59% multi-question, ~10% multiSelect). Question text is captured VERBATIM and never truncated: a question longer than the terminal window was never retained in scrollback, so the screen can verify a picker but can never be its source | `(COMPANION-CAPTURE)` |\n | Companion pairing surface (desktop) | the entry point the phone's own instructions name — \"open Superset and choose 'Pair a device'\" — without which the companion feature cannot be bootstrapped at all: `openPairing` is reachable only from the desktop's authenticated tRPC surface, so nothing else can ever open the one 120 s LAN window. A row in Experimental settings reads `companion.status` and derives its own availability (off / enabled-but-failed-to-start / running are three different messages, because they need three different actions from the user) rather than offering a button that can only fail; it opens a fork-only dialog that renders the returned URI as a QR and counts the window down. The pairing code lives in the URL FRAGMENT, so the dialog is the only thing that ever holds it: it is never logged (diagnostics use only the non-secret `pairingRef`), never persisted, never an attribute, never selectable text (a screenshot of a QR expires in 120 s; a screenshot of the string does not), and the flow component is unmounted on close so the value dies with the dialog. Dismissing takes the 0.0.0.0:47611 listener down instead of leaving it up for the rest of the window, and every window operation is serialised because the bridge allows exactly one process-wide (an unserialised close could land after a later open and silently kill the new window). States are honest and bounded — opening / open-with-time-remaining / expired / failed-with-the-server's-reason, never an unbounded spinner. It does NOT claim \"paired\": the bridge exposes no completion signal to the desktop, so the expiry copy points at the phone, which does know. QR is drawn by a dependency-free byte-mode encoder (level M, versions 1-40, full mask selection) — a QR library would move `package.json`/`bun.lock` under the `(LOCK-REGEN)` gate — always dark-on-white regardless of theme, since an inverted symbol is unreadable to some decoders | `(COMPANION-PAIRING-UI)`, `(COMPANION-PAIRING-ENTRY)`, `(COMPANION-PAIRING-SEARCH)` |\n-| Companion bridge (phone/watch) | fork-only host-service subsystem (`packages/host-service/src/companion/`) that lets a paired Android phone/watch read agent status and answer a blocked AskUserQuestion. Loopback listener on a fixed port behind a Cloudflare tunnel + Access service token, with the bridge validating the Access JWT itself and failing closed; every body is an end-to-end AES-256-GCM sealed envelope keyed on a per-device key that is DERIVED at pairing and never transmitted, with method/path/status/nonce bound into the AAD and a disk-persisted replay cache that outlives the freshness window. The client sends semantic intent only — questionId, fingerprint, option indices — so no wire field can express \"send these bytes to that terminal\"; the bridge owns the picker byte contract and injects the validated sequence inside ONE critical section holding the per-terminal lock in the same process as the pty writer, under an answer-wide lease. `(ANSWER-GUARDLESS)` means every pending captured Claude question with a terminal target is offered even before capability/session negotiation and even when sidebar curation hides its ordinary thread, and after fingerprint/shape validation, no transcript, screen, renderer, Windows login-session, agent-binding, permission-latch, marker, liveness or capability observation may veto or downgrade it; display-off and a Windows lock screen behave exactly like an unlocked desktop, and only daemon-acknowledged `pty.write` calls are counted as successful and confirmed; that acknowledgement never prematurely hides the question — positive matching PostToolUse/tool-result evidence performs settlement/retraction, while a best-effort PTY resize nudge asks Claude to repaint its consumed picker without becoming a write veto or outcome downgrade (the daemon protocol version is bumped; a detached v2 daemon remains adoptable and still receives the ENTIRE encoded companion sequence in one ordered legacy input frame, but because it cannot acknowledge that write the bridge returns durable `unconfirmed`, marks the question stale and retains the duplicate fence rather than lying about confirmation, refusing on negotiated capability state or stopping after the first keystroke) (per-keystroke locking interleaves two multi-select answers into a third one neither device chose). First-answer-wins, zero write retries, read-the-outcome instead. Push is data-only opaque ids with runtime assertions that make text leakage throw, and it is PRESENCE-GATED rather than delayed: a question buzzes IMMEDIATELY when the user is away from the desktop, and never while they are at it. Presence is two signals — human keystrokes into a terminal WebSocket (never the companion's own answer-injection path, or a phone answer would read as desk presence) and a 15 s Electron beacon carrying OS idle time plus lock state, with a locked screen overriding a recent keystroke. A held question is held with NO ceiling: it is cancelled if answered at the desk, and fired the moment presence lapses (walking away, locking, or the beacon going stale), with a 10 s settling window after a resume so a machine the user has just woken does not empty its queue into their wrist. Armed and sent states are rows in host.db, so a host-service restart neither loses a held push nor re-sends a sent one, and every send is serialised against its own retraction so a notification can never outlive its subject. Wire contract: `superset-companion/PROTOCOL.md`. The 24 h idempotency + status ledger is a TABLE in host.db, not a file, because the race it has to settle cannot be settled by a file: a status read can overtake an answer that was admitted but has not yet recorded itself, and absence then says nothing about whether it is about to be typed. `/v1/answer` and `/v1/answer/status` therefore race atomically for the right to decide — the answer compare-and-sets an `in_flight` claim BEFORE encoding or PTY injection begins, the status path compare-and-sets a `closed_not_received` tombstone that permanently forbids typing — and whichever wins, the loser is bound by it. Coverage is an opaque epoch the client echoes back and the server compares for equality, rotated transactionally with any prune, so no client-side clock arithmetic can manufacture a false \"it was not sent\"; the server returns one discriminated outcome and the client renders it rather than proving anything. Durability is asserted (`PRAGMA synchronous = FULL`) rather than inferred from NTFS rename ordering, and rows validate individually, so a corrupt row costs one requestId its status instead of quarantining every other record’s 24 hours. `(FREETEXT-N2-PROVEN)` widens WHERE a typed answer is accepted: free text now works on a multi-question prompt — 59% of real traffic — because the per-question sequence was driven in a pty and the agent's own `tool_result` confirmed it (the editor-closing `\\r` ADVANCES to the next question rather than submitting, so a free-text question and a digit question compose and the prompt is submitted by the same review-screen return that already ends an all-digit N>1 answer). `(MSEL-N2-PROVEN)` closes the last prompt shape: a multi-select question inside an N>1 prompt is a per-question group of toggle digits terminated by the SAME right-arrow that submits a lone multi-select — there it ADVANCES (next question mid-prompt, review screen on the last) — driven first, middle and last, twice each, mixed with free-text siblings and with the zero-toggle empty group (which the CLI accepts and then OMITS from the `tool_result`, exactly as a desk submit past the review warning does). What the earlier refutation actually refuted was the N=1 submit PAIR reused verbatim: its trailing `\\r` landed on the next question. The one shape still refused is refused on EVIDENCE rather than on caution: on a multi-select question the free-text row's digit toggles a checkbox, the body text is swallowed with no echo and the terminating `\\r` toggles whatever row the caret was on — a run that asked for free text came back answered with the first option instead. A `\\r` into a multi-select question is the single most dangerous byte this contract knows, so `assertNoReturnIntoMultiSelect` re-checks every emitted sequence against the prompt itself. The picker contract is per build AND per shape, and one build's proof can never backfill another's | `(COMPANION-BRIDGE)`, `(ANSWER-GUARDLESS)`, `(ANSWER-LEDGER)`, `(PUSH-PRESENCE)`, `(FREETEXT-N2-PROVEN)`, `(MSEL-N2-PROVEN)` |\n+| Companion bridge (phone/watch) | fork-only host-service subsystem (`packages/host-service/src/companion/`) that lets a paired Android phone/watch read agent status and answer a blocked AskUserQuestion. Loopback listener on a fixed port behind a Cloudflare tunnel + Access service token, with the bridge validating the Access JWT itself and failing closed; every body is an end-to-end AES-256-GCM sealed envelope keyed on a per-device key that is DERIVED at pairing and never transmitted, with method/path/status/nonce bound into the AAD and a disk-persisted replay cache that outlives the freshness window. The client sends semantic intent only — questionId, fingerprint, option indices — so no wire field can express \"send these bytes to that terminal\"; the bridge owns the picker byte contract and injects the validated sequence inside ONE critical section holding the per-terminal lock in the same process as the pty writer, under an answer-wide lease. `(ANSWER-GUARDLESS)` means every pending captured Claude question with a terminal target is offered even before capability/session negotiation and even