{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-16T01:45:19.302Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a65caaefbb128f1dd</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_019gTWh4mEvhL9LDnDsZhPJP</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\6e3543cc-ec14-446a-a214-ee9f89274f0a\\tasks\\a65caaefbb128f1dd.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Map companion alert composition\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I have the full pipeline. Findings:\n\n## 1. Where the alert payload is built\n\nTwo-stage, and **the payload carries no text at all**.\n\n- **Decision/mint:** `packages/host-service/src/companion/lifecycle-alerts.ts:448` (`mint`) → `:558` decides kind `\"e\"` (failed) vs `\"g\"` (ready). Held alert struct at `:51-69`: `alertId`, `kind`, `hostTerminalId`, `hostWorkspaceId`, `workspaceHandle`, `expiresAtMs`, `state`, `retryAtMs`, `failures`, `superseded`.\n- **Wire payload:** `packages/host-service/src/companion/push.ts:523` `buildLifecyclePushData`. Send call at `lifecycle-alerts.ts:388` passes only 4 fields.\n\nCurrent v2 FCM schema (`types.ts:1356` `PushDataV2`) — **5 keys, all opaque**:\n\n| key | value |\n|---|---|\n| `v` | `\"2\"` |\n| `k` | `\"g\"` ready-for-review \\| `\"e\"` agent failed |\n| `i` | alertId, 22-char base64url (SHA-256 of kind + terminalId + cycle-start) |\n| `w` | workspaceId **handle** (derived hash, not a name) |\n| `x` | expiresAtMs, 13 digits |\n\nNo title, no body, no terminal id, no names. `message.notification` is deliberately absent.\n\n**Exact user-visible text is composed locally on-device**, from `k` alone:\n\n- Phone (`superset-companion/app-phone/.../CompanionNotifications.kt:221-232`):\n  - ready → title `\"An agent finished\"`, body `\"Ready for review — open Superset to look over the work.\"`, channel `lifecycle_ready`\n  - error → title `\"An agent stopped on an error\"`, body `\"It will not continue on its own — open Superset to see what happened.\"`, channel `lifecycle_error`\n- Watch (`app-watch/.../LifecycleAlertPresentation.kt:50-68`):\n  - ready → `\"An agent finished\"` / `\"Ready for review\"`, no vibration\n  - error → `\"An agent stopped\"` / `\"It hit an error\"`, vibrates\n\nArming rule (`lifecycle-alerts.ts:548-556`): only fires when the previous terminal event was `Start`/`SubagentActive`/`BackgroundRunning` — or `PermissionRequest` for a failure. Outcomes are produced by the Python notify hook at `apps/desktop/src/main/lib/agent-jsonl-watcher/pane-map-hook.ts:2387-2405`, forwarded via `packages/host-service/src/trpc/router/notifications/companion-lifecycle-sink.ts:57` (`forwardCompanionLifecycle`), wired at `notifications.ts:298`.\n\n## 2. Identifiers available at that point\n\nAt mint time the manager holds only `hostTerminalId` + `hostWorkspaceId` (raw host ids) — **no names**. Names are reachable but not fetched:\n\n- `/v1/tree` names come from `host.db` in `companion/read-api.ts`:\n  - project `name` — `:1022`, from `projects.name`, falling back to `basename(repo_path)`\n  - workspace `name` — `:970`, `workspaces.name` falling back to `branch`; `branch` at `:971`\n  - terminal `title` — `:903` via `terminalTitle()` at `:699`, which is **NOT a tab/pane title**. Explicit comment at `:694`: *\"host.db carries no terminal title — the pane title is renderer state.\"* It returns `\"Shell\"` / `\"Agent\"` / capitalized agentId.\n- Field names on the wire: `Project.name`, `Workspace.name`, `Workspace.branch`, `Terminal.title` (`types.ts:593-665`).\n- Naming inversion warning (`read-api.ts:6-9`): in code, *project* = repo, *workspace* = branch/worktree; users say these the other way round.\n\nReal tab/pane titles exist **only in desktop main**, from renderer app state: `apps/desktop/src/main/lib/notifications/utils.ts:73` `getNotificationTitle` — priority `tab.userTitle &gt; tab.name &gt; pane.name &gt; \"Terminal\"`, sourced from `appState.data.tabsState` at `apps/desktop/src/main/windows/main.ts:360-365`.\n\n## 3. Delivery path\n\nPush, not poll. Host-service → FCM v1 HTTP (`push.ts:207`, `sendOnce` `:918`), broadcast to every registered device (`broadcast` `:1600`) → phone `CompanionMessagingService` parses via `core/.../LifecyclePush.kt:71` → phone posts its own notification → **phone relays to watch** over Wear Data Layer path `/companion/v1/lifecycle` (`WearProtocol.kt:77`), payload `WatchLifecycleAlert{kind, eventId, workspaceId, raisedAtMs, expiresAtMs}` (`:360`). Relay only happens after the phone's own post lands; phone notification is `setLocalOnly(true)` to avoid a bridged duplicate.\n\nGating before send (`lifecycle-alerts.ts:300-315` `claim`): held while user is present at desk, or while the thread is curated off the sidebar. TTL 6h, retry backoff 30s→15min. State is process-local, not durable (unlike the question path's DB fence).\n\n**Companion app is an external sibling repo:** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion` (Kotlin, `app-phone` / `app-watch` / `core`, with `PROTOCOL.md` as the normative spec). Not in this repo.\n\n## 4. terminal/session id → names mapping\n\n**No such mapping exists on the lifecycle path.** What exists:\n\n- host-service, per-terminal join: `read-api.ts:272` `terminalSourceStmt` — `terminal_sessions → workspaces` yielding `hostWorkspaceId`, `hostProjectId`, `agentId`. Ids only, no names.\n- host-service, names by id: `HostDbReader.findWorkspace(hostWorkspaceId)` (`read-api.ts:191`) returns `name` + `branch`; `listProjects()` (`:182`) returns project `name`. So **host-service can resolve project+workspace names from `hostWorkspaceId` synchronously today** — the lifecycle manager already holds a `HostDbReader` in the same composition root (`companion/index.ts:702-706`).\n- desktop main, tab/pane names: `notifications/utils.ts:73` (above), keyed on `tabId`/`paneId` from the lifecycle event, not on terminal id.\n- Handle derivation: `question-store.ts:205` `deriveHandle(kind, hostId)` — one-way; `read-api.ts:1333` does a reverse lookup by scanning rows.\n\n## 5. Constraints\n\nHard blockers for putting names in the push:\n\n- **Absolute privacy rule** — `push.ts:5-8`: *\"no question text, no option text, no workspace name, no branch name, no project name, no file path and no free text of any kind may EVER appear in an FCM message. Google is not in the trust boundary.\"* Enforced by `assertPushDataSafe` (`push.ts:345`), which **throws**, never logs-and-continues.\n- Every `data` value must match `PUSH_VALUE_PATTERN = /^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,43}$/` (`config.ts:288`) — no natural-language string can pass.\n- Closed key set **per version** (`push.ts:298-306`); adding a key to v2 fails the assertion. v1 is frozen.\n- Size: `PUSH_DATA_HARD_CAP_BYTES = 160` (`config.ts:286`); FCM absolute 4096 (`push.ts:205`).\n- `message.notification`/`apns`/`webpush` blocks structurally banned via closed envelope key set (`push.ts:307`).\n- Wear hop cap 60 KiB (`WearProtocol.kt:81`).\n- **No `green`/\"ready for review\" status in `/v1/tree`, deliberately** — `types.ts:524-529` and `read-api.ts:18-21`: the review axis lives in renderer storage the bridge cannot reach.\n- Capability negotiation exists (`types.ts:417-438`, `config.ts:119`) but there is **no lifecycle/alert capability token** — clients discriminate on payload `v` instead, and a pre-lifecycle client silently ignores `v:\"2\"` (PROTOCOL §13.4). Envelope version is separate and frozen at `1` (`types.ts:60`).\n- Logging discipline: values that fail assertions are never echoed (`push.ts:534-536`, `:383-386`).\n\n## Where a change would have to land\n\nIf you want alerts to name the project/workspace/tab:\n\n- **Names cannot ride the FCM push.** That is a deliberate trust-boundary rule with a throwing assertion, not an oversight.\n- **Viable path: keep push opaque, enrich on-device.** The push already carries `w` (workspace handle). The phone would resolve it against a sealed `/v1/tree` fetch and interpolate names locally. That is **host-service + companion app**, and it is mostly companion app — the tree already exposes `Project.name`, `Workspace.name`, `Workspace.branch`, `Terminal.title`. Note the alert carries **no terminal handle**, so per-tab naming would first need `t` added to `PushDataV2` (a v2 key-set change in `push.ts:298` + `buildLifecyclePushData` + the Kotlin `KEYS` set in `LifecyclePush.kt:63` + `WatchLifecycleAlert`) — an opaque handle is permissible under the rule, a name is not.\n- **Real tab/pane titles are a third change.** They live only in renderer state (`utils.ts:73`); `host.db` has no terminal title (`read-api.ts:694`). Exposing them on `/v1/tree` means desktop → host-service plumbing on top of the above.\n- **Host-service-only change** is sufficient if you just want better *kind* granularity or arming rules (`lifecycle-alerts.ts:530-562`) — no client change needed, since text is derived from `k` and both clients already handle `g`/`e` (watch ignores unknown kinds as of companion commit `e16c218`).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>146462</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>39</tool_uses><duration_ms>204587</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
