{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-16T04:48:50.741Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a4b4acd04792ec9a3</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\6e3543cc-ec14-446a-a214-ee9f89274f0a\\tasks\\a4b4acd04792ec9a3.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Implement fork host+renderer v3\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>All five fixed and verified. Changes remain uncommitted in the worktree.\n\n## Per-item\n\n**1. MAJOR — repair eligibility depended on a visibility-suppressed write** — Confirmed the mechanism at `statusTransitions.ts:101`: a turn-end on a **visible** target returns `clearSources` and `axes: null`, so the replayed source is never `review` exactly when the user is sitting on the pane. Eligibility is now two durable facts, neither read from the post-replay store:\n- `alreadyReadThrough` — `seenAt &gt;= row.lastEventAt` (sessionStorage, survives everything);\n- `wouldRaiseReview` — the **pure** `resolveV2AgentStatusTransition` asked with `targetVisible: false` and empty `statuses`, i.e. \"would this row's last event raise a green if nothing suppressed it\".\n\nThe clearing branch keeps reading the real store (it legitimately acts on actual state). `!seedColdStart` retained.\n\n**2. MAJOR — stale readiness retry could overwrite newer context** — Added `state.desired` (always the newest snapshot, recorded *before* the hash guard). `scheduleReadinessRetry` **takes no snapshot argument** any more and reads `desired` at fire time, so a retry can only push the host toward current truth. Retries are cancelled when a newer snapshot is queued, when the desired snapshot already equals `sentHash`, on host change, on reconnect (`forgetAlertContextSyncsForHost`), and on release. Also closed the adjacent window the same A/B ordering exposed: `sentHash` is now recorded only if the completed send is still the current intent, so a newer snapshot queued mid-flight can't be suppressed by its predecessor's hash.\n\n**3. MAJOR — layout changes stopped being observed** — Restored the layout-keyed effect: `syncAlertContexts()` **and** the title-listener subscriptions now run on `[workspacesById]`, so opening a 2nd/3rd tab moves `tabCount` and a newly opened terminal gets its listener. Register/release stays on the membership-only `[workspaceIds, hostUrl]`. This is cheap precisely because of the earlier fix — `releaseAlertContextSync` no longer runs on ticks, so the hash guard survives and a no-op tick costs one comparison. The now-unused `workspacesRef` was removed.\n\n**4. MAJOR — unconditional blind `c` broadcast** — The manager records `startedAtMs` at construction. In `markLifecycleSeen`, when `findLiveReadyAlert` returns null **and** `startedAtMs &lt;= seenThroughAt`, it returns without sending: this process has held alert state since before that instant, `sent`/`retracted` rows are retained until TTL, and the lookup accepts any state — so an empty answer is *proof of absence*, not ignorance. Only a host started **after** `seenThroughAt` broadcasts blind. Not logged (this is the ordinary warm-reconnect path). The `evictOldest` caveat is recorded in the comment. Four tests: the proof branch (silent), the restart branch (still broadcasts, now with `seenThroughAt` before manager start), proof-branch-doesn't-swallow-a-real-retraction, and 25 warm re-offers producing zero traffic.\n\n**5a. MINOR — starving batch + untrue comment** — Added a `REPAIR_COOLDOWN_MS` (30 min) skip-set keyed `${hostUrl}:${terminalId}`, pruned on write and bounded at 512. Recently-repaired rows are skipped **without** spending the cap, so each epoch advances to rows it has not covered — rows 11+ no longer starve. Counters split into `seenRepairsSent` / `seenRepairsSkipped` / `seenRepairsDeferred`, and the comment now states what the code actually does.\n\n**5b. MINOR — wrong privacy rationale in the doc** — Both the header and `resolveTabTitle` now say a non-terminal pane names the tab **only through an explicit rename**, because derived pane names (file basename, browser host/page title, comment author) are free text the 2026-08-16 waiver does not cover; recorded in FINAL.md §8. Both blocks stop pointing readers at the sidebar's `getPaneTitle`. Behaviour unchanged. The pinning test now uses a file pane named `secret-project-plan.ts` so the assertion demonstrates the leak it prevents.\n\n## Results\n\n- **Host companion suite: 342 pass / 0 fail** (23 files) — +3 from the blind-broadcast guard tests.\n- **Renderer: 49 pass / 0 fail** (5 files).\n- **Biome** (lint + format) on all touched paths: **0 diagnostics**, no suppressions.\n- **Marker gate** (bash): **All 160 present.**\n- **tsc**: desktop — no errors in any touched file; host-service — only the pre-existing `curation-gates.test.ts:341`.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: fixing #5a's docs-adjacency exposed that my inserted constants had orphaned the `(BUS-RESYNC)` module header onto them; I moved the block below `ResyncResult` so the header documents what it always did.\n\nConfirmed-resolved items were left untouched.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>577822</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>46</tool_uses><duration_ms>821434</duration_ms></usage>\n<worktree><worktreePath>C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a4b4acd04792ec9a3</worktreePath><worktreeBranch>worktree-agent-a4b4acd04792ec9a3</worktreeBranch></worktree>\n</task-notification>"}
