{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-16T05:51:38.230Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>afedf11751a4fe578</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_014DNMhAW1ySCu5Ju5Rc7tFM</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\6e3543cc-ec14-446a-a214-ee9f89274f0a\\tasks\\afedf11751a4fe578.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Simplify review: efficiency angle\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>All diffs gathered and read (fork worktree host-service + renderer incl. new untracked files; companion Kotlin core/phone/watch + PROTOCOL.md). Efficiency-only findings:\n\n## Findings\n\n**1. Renderer: full title-listener teardown/rebuild + full re-extraction on every layout tick**\n- File: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a4b4acd04792ec9a3\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\routes\\_authenticated\\components\\V2NotificationController\\components\\HostNotificationSubscriber\\HostNotificationSubscriber.tsx`, lines 324–335.\n- Summary: effect keyed on `workspacesById` — which its own sibling comment (line 280) documents as \"rebuilt on every layout live-query tick, several times a second\". Each run tears down and re-installs a title listener for EVERY `(terminalId, paneId)` pair across all workspaces (`subscribeTerminalTitleListeners` full rebuild: two Maps, `collectTerminalPaneRefs` walk, unsubscribe+resubscribe per pair, re-arm `onInstanceRegistered`), plus runs `syncAlertContexts()` = `extractAlertContexts` + `alertContextsHash` JSON.stringify for every hydrated workspace. The in-code claim \"a tick that changed nothing costs one hash comparison\" undercounts: it costs full listener churn + N extractions + N stringifys before the hash guard is consulted.\n- Cost: O(workspaces × panes) allocations + listener set mutations, several times/sec, continuously, in the renderer.\n- Cheaper: split the effect. (a) Key listener subscription on a stable fingerprint of the pane-ref set (`collectTerminalPaneRefs` pair keys joined, via `useMemo`) so churn happens only when the set changes — same pattern already used for `workspaceIds`/`workspacesKey`. (b) For per-tick sync, skip workspaces whose `paneLayout` reference is unchanged (cache last ref per workspace) so one workspace's tick doesn't re-extract all of them. Semantics (sync on layout change, listeners for late-arriving panes) preserved.\n\n**2. Renderer: extraction of ALL workspaces per terminal title change, upstream of the debounce**\n- File: same `HostNotificationSubscriber.tsx` lines 169–186 + `...\\lib\\companionAlertSync.ts` line 163 (debounce sits inside `queueAlertContextSync`, after extraction).\n- Summary: `onTitleChange` → `syncAlertContexts()` extracts + hashes every hydrated workspace on each title event; `alertContexts.ts` line 277 itself notes \"several a second\". The 250 ms debounce only suppresses the mutation, not the extraction/stringify burst.\n- Cost: N-workspace layout walks + JSON.stringify per prompt-redraw title event.\n- Cheaper: debounce first — one trailing 250 ms timer around `syncAlertContexts` itself (extraction at fire time is also fresher), and/or have `subscribeTerminalTitleListeners` pass the changed `terminalId` so only owning workspaces re-extract (the desired-pair map already knows the mapping).\n\n**3. Renderer: `wouldRaiseReview` + synthetic statuses computed for every row, used only on the repair branch**\n- File: `...\\lib\\resyncAgentStatus.ts` (diff hunk @ line ~490; `wouldRaiseReview` / `syntheticStatuses` construction).\n- Summary: `resolveV2AgentStatusTransition(...)` and the `syntheticStatuses` object are built unconditionally per snapshot row; results consumed only inside `if (alreadyReadThrough &amp;&amp; wouldRaiseReview &amp;&amp; !seedColdStart)`.\n- Cost: one resolver call + object allocation per terminal per resync (all terminals across all workspaces), wasted whenever `alreadyReadThrough` is false or `seedColdStart` — the majority of rows.\n- Cheaper: compute lazily under `alreadyReadThrough &amp;&amp; !seedColdStart`. Pure reorder, no semantic change.\n\n**4. Host: `listProjects().find()` full-table read per alert send**\n- File: `...\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\index.ts` line ~720 (`resolveAlertContext`).\n- Summary: resolving one project name loads every project row (`projectsStmt.all()`) then linear-scans; runs per question push, per lifecycle alert send attempt (each retry re-resolves — that freshness is deliberate, the scan isn't).\n- Cost: full `projects` materialization + scan per push.\n- Cheaper: prepared `SELECT ... FROM projects WHERE id = ?` (`findProject`) alongside the existing `findWorkspace`/`findTerminal` point statements in `read-api.ts`.\n\n**5. Host: N point queries per accepted tab-context snapshot**\n- File: `...\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\push-context.ts` lines 238–248 (`sync`).\n- Summary: `deps.db.findTerminal(terminalId)` per terminal to validate placement — up to 256 prepared-statement round trips per snapshot; snapshots arrive per debounced title burst per workspace.\n- Cost: N SQLite point lookups per sync mutation instead of 1 query.\n- Cheaper: one `SELECT id FROM terminal_sessions WHERE origin_workspace_id = ?` (or `id IN (...)`) then Set membership; same rejection semantics.\n\n**6. Renderer: snapshot hash recomputed up to 3× per sync cycle**\n- File: `...\\lib\\companionAlertSync.ts` lines 153, 168, 190.\n- Summary: `alertContextsHash` (JSON.stringify) computed in the fast path, again in the timer callback, again for `desiredHash` — same or overlapping snapshots.\n- Cost: redundant stringify per debounce cycle (per workspace).\n- Cheaper: compute once per snapshot at `queueAlertContextSync` entry and carry `{snapshot, hash}` through `desired`/`pending`.\n\n**Trivial (noting, not pressing):** `sanitizePushName` (`push.ts`) runs a regex test per code point; a single global-flag `.replace()` does the strip in one pass — ≤3 names ≤256 bytes per send, so negligible. `assertPushDataSafe` sorts `Object.keys(keysByKind)` per call though it's only needed for the error message. `companionAlertSync.ts` lines 320–345 carry a fully duplicated JSDoc block (dead text, not runtime cost).\n\n## Clean areas\n- No blocking fs/sync work added to desktop main-thread startup (renderer/host-service only; test-setup is test-only). The repo's startup footgun rule is not violated.\n- Closures: `resolveAlertContext`, sinks, and `HeldAlert` records copy scalar fields; no long-lived closure captures a large scope.\n- Companion (Kotlin): clean on this angle. `SeenLifecycleEvents.suppress` / `PendingLifecycleDismissals` `commit()` writes are per-retraction (rare, user-action-rate), documented as durability-required on the FCM thread, and write-before-send ordering is a PROTOCOL §13.5.5 ruling — the common-case double write of the pending file (enqueue then clear) is the design, not waste. `enqueue` already skips the write when the id is present and nothing expired. No repeated JSON serialization introduced (the `encodedSize`/send double-encode predates this diff). Watch/phone composition is a handful of small string builds per push.\n\nFrozen wire schema, golden vectors, and FINAL.md §8 rulings untouched by every suggestion above — all six findings are implementation-cost changes with identical observable behavior.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>188466</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>29</tool_uses><duration_ms>280988</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
