{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-16T06:49:05.193Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a4b4acd04792ec9a3</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\6e3543cc-ec14-446a-a214-ee9f89274f0a\\tasks\\a4b4acd04792ec9a3.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Implement fork host+renderer v3\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>All green. Changes remain uncommitted in the worktree.\n\n## Incident during this task — recovered\n\nWhile applying item 18 I used PowerShell `Set-Content -NoNewline` on a line array, which concatenated `lifecycle-alerts.ts` into a single 37 KB line. No Entire checkpoints existed on this branch.\n\nI recovered it rather than losing the work: the damaged file is exactly `lines.join(\"\")`, so I reconstructed newlines with a dictionary of the file's last committed lines (byte-exact for unedited regions) plus heuristics for lines added this session, then re-indented block-comment interiors and restored blank lines. Every pass was verified by re-joining the result and byte-comparing against the damaged file, so nothing could be invented or lost. Final state: **345/345 tests pass, tsc clean, biome clean, diff vs HEAD is 454 added / 27 removed with zero blank-line loss.** Recovery scripts were deleted. I did not use that PowerShell idiom again.\n\n## Applied (16 of 18)\n\n| # | Item | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `hasLoneSurrogate` → shared `findUnpairedSurrogate` | golden + surrogate vectors byte-identical |\n| 2 | Deleted stale duplicate JSDoc (wrong stamp) | |\n| 3 | Removed doubled `/**` | |\n| 4 | `createMockStorage()` factory | both storages |\n| 5 | `markTerminalSeenAndReportRead` | both intent sites call the one helper |\n| 6 | `retireAlert(alert, reason)` | `supersede` loops it; `retractById` uses it for known ids |\n| 7 | Flat `PUSH_VALUE_RULES` | rule-undefined throw + exempt cross-check kept |\n| 9 | `createSinkSlot` factory | presence/bridge slots untouched |\n| 10 | `sanitizeTerminalHandle` + `typeof` narrowing | both copies replaced |\n| 11 | Dropped the promise chain | registry `sync` is synchronous; ~25 lines |\n| 12 | Subscriber efficiency | fingerprint-keyed listeners, layout-ref skip, 250 ms title debounce |\n| 13 | Hash once, carried as `{snapshot, hash}` | |\n| 14 | Lazy `wouldRaiseReview` + single `retryEligibleAtMs` | |\n| 15 | Prepared `findProject(id)` | replaces `listProjects().find()` per send |\n| 16 | One `listTerminalIdsForWorkspace` query | replaces the per-terminal loop |\n| 17 | Ids derived first, key built from them | no more `split(\"|\")` |\n| 18 | `hasEvictedUnexpired` boolean | |\n\n## Skipped (1) — reverted per the rules\n\n**Item 8** (wrap `resolveContextSafely` once at the composition root, delete both consumer wrappers). `push-armed-context.test.ts` → *\"still fires when the resolver THROWS\"* passes a throwing resolver **directly into `createPushSender`** and asserts the entry still lands in `sent`. That is real behavior — a broken resolver must cost the name, never the buzz — and item 8 would require deleting or weakening it, since `resolveAlertContext` is an injection point any caller supplies. I reverted all three edits (push.ts, lifecycle-alerts.ts, index.ts) and left a comment at the push.ts guard explaining why it stays.\n\nTwo consequential notes on applied items:\n- **#9** required the lifecycle-seen sink to return `boolean` (the slot's `call` returns the sink's value). I made it return `true` on **every** path — including the DB-revalidation drops — so `accepted` keeps meaning \"a running bridge received it\", exactly as before. Returning `false` there would have changed behavior: the resync would classify a permanent drop as a transient failure and retry it on the short cooldown.\n- **#14**'s test seam had to change shape: the map now stores a deadline, and a deadline minus a clock the test doesn't share isn't a duration. `__peekRepairCooldownForTest` became `__peekRepairOutcomeForTest` returning `\"repaired\" | \"failed\" | null` — the distinction the branch actually makes, asserted exactly rather than approximately. No test was weakened.\n\n## Final results\n\n- **Host companion suite: 345 pass / 0 fail** (23 files)\n- **Renderer: 103 pass / 0 fail** (8 files)\n- **Biome** (lint + format) across all touched paths: **0 diagnostics**, one precise suppression on the fingerprint-keyed effect (biome cannot express \"re-run key, not a read value\"; matches the existing `cacheVersion` precedent in this codebase)\n- **Marker gate**: All 160 present\n- **tsc**: desktop — no errors in any touched file; host-service — only the pre-existing `curation-gates.test.ts:341`</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>890174</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>211</tool_uses><duration_ms>3282489</duration_ms></usage>\n<worktree><worktreePath>C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a4b4acd04792ec9a3</worktreePath><worktreeBranch>worktree-agent-a4b4acd04792ec9a3</worktreeBranch></worktree>\n</task-notification>"}
