{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-16T13:40:15.283Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a4b4acd04792ec9a3</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\6e3543cc-ec14-446a-a214-ee9f89274f0a\\tasks\\a4b4acd04792ec9a3.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Implement fork host+renderer v3\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Round 3 complete — all 3 MAJORs fixed, suites/lint/markers green.\n\n## 1. Monotonic lifecycle instants (`notifications.ts`)\n\nNew exported pure helper `nextLifecycleInstantMs(nowMs, previousEventAtMs)` = `now &gt; prev ? now : prev + 1`. The hook now reads the persisted binding **before** stamping and anchors on `previousBinding.lastEventAt`, so the instant is strictly increasing per terminal and the property survives a restart (the binding is persisted and reloaded — the same state the deterministic-id path already relies on). One instant flows to the bus broadcast, `recordEvent`, the capture sink and the lifecycle sink, so ids/`gx`/binding/read-boundary can't disagree. Forward jumps are honoured; only the backstep is corrected, by the minimum. Per terminal, not global — one busy agent can't push another's stamps forward.\n\nTests (`notifications.test.ts`, new describe): step-back → `5_000` then `5_001`; forward jump honoured; no-history passthrough; a second terminal in the same step-back window still stamps `4_000`; and the corrected instant reaches both the binding (`lastEventAt === 5_001`) and the broadcast.\n\n## 2. Reads are bounded by their own instant (`lifecycle-alerts.ts`)\n\n`findLiveReadyAlert` → `findReadAlerts(hostTerminalId, seenThroughAt)`: only rows with `outcomeAtMs &lt;= seenThroughAt`, sorted newest generation first. Ordering is by generation, not expiry — with fix 1 that's a total order over one terminal's finishes, and it subsumes the old on-device tie-break (a blind tombstone can no longer outrank a live card, because its `outcomeAtMs` is the read instant, not \"now\"). `markLifecycleSeen` retires **every covered generation** rather than only the newest: a `standing` card from two cycles back is still on the handset while the newest covered row may only ever have been `held`, and retiring just the newest would delete the held row in silence and strand the visible card. Each retirement stays per-alert and idempotent (`held` deleted silently, `sent`/`standing` fire their one `c`, `retracted` inert). Blind-restart path unchanged; the proof-of-absence branch now reads \"nothing this read covers\", so a later generation neither suppresses it nor gets retracted by it.\n\nTests (new describe): delayed read of A after B is delivered → exactly one `c` for A with `gx = 2_000`, B untouched, and a later read at `4_000` retires B; a read naming a generation older than the only (newer) row broadcasts nothing; and a read covering `standing` A + `held` B retires both (A's `c` fires, B is dropped silently).\n\n## 3. Compare-and-clear on the outstanding record\n\n`clearOutstandingReady(terminalId, acknowledgedThroughAt)` now clears only when the stored record is `&lt;=` the acknowledged generation. Both callbacks pass what they reported: `companionAlertSync.ts` passes `seenThroughAt`, `resyncAgentStatus.ts` passes `repairSeenThroughAt`.\n\nTests: store-level — a finish arriving mid-report survives the older acknowledgement and is retired by its own; an acknowledgement that covers more than the record still clears. Helper-level — the mutation is held open, B finishes, A resolves `accepted:true`, the record stays at `7_000`, and the next focus reports `seenThroughAt: 7_000` and then clears.\n\n## Verification\n\n- host `bun test src/companion src/trpc/router/notifications`: **387 pass / 0 fail** (25 files; companion 47→50, notifications 12→19).\n- desktop `bun test src/renderer`: **1619 pass / 10 fail** — the same 10 pre-existing, unrelated failures as before this round (`persisted-key registry` flagging `kanbanSplitLayout`/`recycleBinRetention`, `V2ProjectSettings` `skipToken` export error, `launchCommandInPane`/`writeCommandsInPane`/`launchAgentSession`/`bootstrapOpenWorktree`/`removeProjectFromSidebarState`).\n- typecheck: host clean except the pre-existing `curation-gates.test.ts(341)`; desktop reports nothing in any file I touched.\n- biome 2.4.2 `--line-ending=crlf` over all changed dirs: clean.\n- marker gate: **All 160 feature markers present.**\n- FEATURES.md updated with the monotonic instant, the read boundary, and compare-and-clear.\n\nNothing on the verified-clean list was touched (standing escalation, reason upgrades, alert-owned identity, replay restoration, cap/rotation, golden vectors are all unchanged). Still uncommitted — everything is in the worktree. `tmp/alert-context-names/FINAL.md` §0 key-set list remains stale from the last round and I'm sandboxed out of the shared checkout, so that edit is still yours.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>285072</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>46</tool_uses><duration_ms>833292</duration_ms></usage>\n<worktree><worktreePath>C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a4b4acd04792ec9a3</worktreePath><worktreeBranch>worktree-agent-a4b4acd04792ec9a3</worktreeBranch></worktree>\n</task-notification>"}
