ransmitted; no secret rendered as text; nothing secret reaches a server; CF token bound to device during the exchange), `§4.1:566-578` LAN is a **hard requirement, no degraded path**, `§4.3:617-620` *\"`pc` is 256 bits, not a human-typed short code … no PAKE is required. The code is short-lived, not short.\"*, `§4.6:708-728` private-IP check, `§4.7:730-739`. **A 6-digit code contradicts §4.3 explicitly — PROTOCOL.md §4 must be amended, not worked around.**\n\n**No PAKE/SPAKE2/SRP/OPAQUE/CPace exists in either repo** (greps clean). Available reusable primitives:\n\n- Bridge `companion/crypto.ts`: `hkdfExtract:205`, `hkdfExpandLabel:220`, `hkdfExpandInfo:238`, `hmacSha256:291`, `x25519:326` (rejects all-zero Z), `generateX25519KeyPair:353`, `seal:644` / `openSealed:600`, `constantTimeEquals:708`, `randomBytes:813`, durable-write helpers `:1232-1257`.\n- Phone `core/crypto/`: `Hkdf.kt`, `KeyDerivation.kt`, `X25519.kt`, `SealedEnvelope.kt`, `ConstantTime.kt`, `Base64Url.kt`; cross-runtime differential vectors already pinned (`core/src/test/.../CrossRuntimeDifferentialVectors20260729Test.kt`).\n- Missing for a real PAKE: hash-to-curve / X25519 point addition on both sides. Node exposes neither for X25519 → a true SPAKE2 needs a curve library (`@noble/curves`) on the bridge and BouncyCastle/noble-equivalent on Android. This is the single largest cost driver below.\n\n## 5. Where a 6-digit flow plugs in\n\n| Layer | File:line | Change |\n|---|---|---|\n| Desktop dialog | `apps/desktop/src/renderer/components/PairDeviceDialog/PairDeviceDialog.tsx:363-379` | render 6 digits beside/instead of `&lt;QrCode&gt;`; phase machine `:64-82` and countdown `:185-198` reusable as-is |\n| Desktop entry row | `.../CompanionPairingSetting/CompanionPairingSetting.tsx:29-38` | copy text says \"never leaves your Wi-Fi\" — must change |\n| tRPC | `packages/host-service/src/trpc/router/companion/companion.ts:409,441,458` | add `openRemotePairing` returning `{code, expiresAtMs}`; `pairingState` needs no change |\n| Bridge window | `companion/pairing.ts:950-1097` | second window variant: 6-digit code, no LAN listener, routes served on the **loopback** listener so the tunnel carries them |\n| Bridge routing | `companion/http.ts:1173-1188` + `ROUTES:820` | new unsealed `/v1/pair/*` paths; must sit *outside* `handleSealed` |\n| Phone UI | `app-phone/.../ui/pairing/PairingScreen.kt:78-196` | **no manual-entry field exists today — camera-only**; add a digit field in the `Unpaired/Scanning` branch `:165-192` |\n| Phone wiring | `ui/shell/CompanionShell.kt:177-186` (`session::pairFromQr`), `data/BridgeCompanionRepository.kt:890-910` | add `pairFromCode` sibling |\n| Phone client | `core/.../pairing/PairingClient.kt:50-208` | second client on the TLS origin (not `ConnectionSpec.CLEARTEXT`); `PairingUri.kt:122` host check bypassed for this path |\n| Phone errors | `phone/model/PairingState.kt:47-91` | new codes (`CODE_WRONG`, `CODE_BURNED`) |\n| PROTOCOL | `§4` `:554-748` (rewrite `§4.1`, `§4.3`, `§4.6`, `§4.7`), `§2` `:270-313`, `§12` rate limits | normative amendment required |\n\n---\n\n## Viable architectures\n\n### A. PAKE (SPAKE2 or CPace) over the tunnel hostname, behind a path-scoped Access **Bypass** policy\nAdd a second Cloudflare Access policy on `superset.khaira.family/v1/pair/*` with action **Bypass** (no service token). Bridge serves `/v1/pair/*` unsealed on 47610; desktop shows 6 digits; both run SPAKE2 with the digits as the password → session key → sealed step-4 delivers the CF secret exactly as today.\n- **Security**: correct construction — a 6-digit password is only safe inside a PAKE, where each online guess costs one round and the window burns after 3. Offline dictionary attack is impossible by PAKE definition. Requires: strict per-source rate limit + burn on the Bypass path (that path is now *internet-reachable and unauthenticated* — it is the whole new attack surface, and it sits in the same process as the pty writer). Keep the 120 s window, single-use, and 3-strike burn from `pairing.ts:818-847`.\n- **Trade-offs**: new unauthenticated internet-facing endpoint (PROTOCOL `§1.1:202-204` \"loopback only\" must be amended); needs a hash-to-curve-capable curve lib on **both** runtimes plus new cross-runtime differential vectors; CF policy is manual config the code can't verify.\n- **Size**: large. ~600-900 LOC bridge + ~500 Kotlin + PAKE dependency on both sides + test vectors + PROTOCOL §4 rewrite. 2-3 weeks equivalent.\n\n### B. Make the origin reachable pre-pairing, keep the existing X25519+HKDF exchange, harden with the 6 digits (no PAKE)\nShip the CF service token in the app (or a second, pairing-only Access token scoped to `/v1/pair/*`), so a fresh phone can reach the origin. Then run the **existing** kex/confirm over TLS, with the 6-digit code replacing `pc` as the HKDF input, plus a server-side burn.\n- **Security**: weaker in principle — a 20-bit code without a PAKE means the confirm MAC is a verifier an attacker who reaches the endpoint can grind. Mitigated *only* by the online burn (3 strikes / 120 s / single use), which is genuinely the same posture as a bank OTP but no longer the \"no dictionary to attack\" property `§4.3:617` sells. Also spends goal 4 (`§4:563`): the token stops being device-bound. Note `§2.2:305-312` already says the token authenticates nothing — a stolen token yields only `unknown_device`.\n- **Trade-offs**: cheapest by far, reuses `derivePairingKeys`/transcript/MAC/seal verbatim; but embeds a shared credential in a sideloaded APK, and the burn is the *only* thing between 10⁶ guesses and a device key. Consider 8-10 digits to buy 27-33 bits.\n- **Size**: small. ~200 LOC bridge (new window variant + route), ~250 Kotlin (TLS pairing client + digit UI), zero new crypto, zero new test vectors. Days.\n\n### C. Cloud rendezvous relay\nBroker the two halves through a server neither has to reach directly: either a tiny Cloudflare Worker/KV keyed by the 6 digits, or Superset's existing relay (`tunnel-client-v2.ts:55,139` — desktop already holds an authenticated `/v2/control` socket). Desktop posts its ephemeral `dPub` + salt under `hash(code)`; phone fetches, posts `pPub`; sealed step-4 flows through the same slot.\n- **Security**: the relay sees only public values *if* combined with a PAKE (A) — with a plain 6-digit code the relay operator or anyone who guesses the slot can MITM the kex outright. So C is a **transport** for A, not an alternative to it. Adds a third party to a design whose §4 goal 3 (`:560-562`) is \"nothing secret ever reaches a server\".\n- **Trade-offs**: works when the tunnel itself is down or the hostname is unknown; no CF policy edit; but new deployed infrastructure to own, and the phone still needs an identity to use Superset's relay (it has none).\n- **Size**: medium-large. Worker + KV + both clients ≈ A's cost plus deployment; using Superset's relay is larger still (new phone-side auth).\n\n**Recommendation shape**: B is the honest minimum-change path if the 6-digit entropy is compensated (longer code + hard burn + audit). A is the only option that preserves the security *claims* PROTOCOL.md currently makes, and its Bypass-path exposure is the thing to review hardest. C only as a fallback transport layered on A.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>199090</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>38</tool_uses><duration_ms>247010</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"},"uuid":"ac9c2ddf-25a6-4b88-9096-2bf44bc254e6","timestamp":"2026-08-16T20:23:25.828Z","permissionMode":"bypassPermissions","origin":{"kind":"task-notification"},"promptSource":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.233","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
