{"parentUuid":"5cee566e-72e6-4795-a95b-c188b3f48157","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_011Ce74Ekr7wAbzfWP9ajZoY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YbD6ymhz1ogAd6NRW8fSQM","name":"PowerShell","input":{"command":"$s = Get-Content \"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\SETUP.md\" -TotalCount 120 | Out-String; $s.Substring(0, [Math]::Min(3500, $s.Length)); Test-Path \"$env:USERPROFILE\\.config\\cf\"; Get-ChildItem \"$env:USERPROFILE\\.cloudflared\" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Select-Object Name","description":"Read companion SETUP.md and check CF credentials"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7120,"cache_read_input_tokens":459576,"output_tokens":385,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":117},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7120,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":385,"cache_read_input_tokens":459576,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7120,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7120},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"59c98406-a8c3-48c6-8f53-923ddae1a19a","timestamp":"2026-08-16T20:27:05.384Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.233","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"59c98406-a8c3-48c6-8f53-923ddae1a19a","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"45ca7d09-fa99-4b80-ba22-acddca00d9ae","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YbD6ymhz1ogAd6NRW8fSQM","type":"tool_result","content":"# Infrastructure setup — done 2026-07-28\r\n\r\nEverything here was provisioned before any app code exists. No secrets are in this repo.\r\n\r\n## Firebase / FCM — DONE and VERIFIED\r\n\r\nReused the existing **Home-Projects** Google Cloud project rather than creating a new one.\r\n\r\n| Thing | Value |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| GCP / Firebase project | `metal-complex-352812` (\"Home-Projects\") |\r\n| Project number | `681443047156` |\r\n| Android app id | `1:681443047156:android:00f5e5e087e9eccc48d0a1` |\r\n| Package name | `family.khaira.supersetcompanion` |\r\n| Sender service account | `superset-companion-fcm@metal-complex-352812.iam.gserviceaccount.com` |\r\n| Role granted | `roles/firebasecloudmessaging.admin` (send only — not project-wide Firebase admin) |\r\n\r\n**Files:**\r\n- `app-phone/google-services.json` — committed deliberately. Contains no secrets; it ships inside the APK.\r\n- `~/.superset/companion/fcm-service-account.json` — **the sender credential.** Lives outside this repo, ACL-restricted to your user account only. Never copy it in here.\r\n\r\n**Verified working**, not assumed: minted an OAuth token from the service account and called FCM v1\r\n`messages:send` with a deliberately fake device token. It returned `INVALID_ARGUMENT — the registration\r\ntoken is not a valid FCM registration token`, which is the correct rejection and proves auth and\r\npermissions are right. A first attempt returned `PERMISSION_DENIED` purely because IAM had not yet\r\npropagated; it passed ~45s later.\r\n\r\nSet up entirely through `gcloud` plus the Firebase Management REST API — no console clicks, and the\r\n`firebase` CLI login was not needed. Two gotchas worth remembering: the REST calls need an\r\n`x-goog-user-project` header or they fail with a confusing quota-project error, and the role you want\r\nis `firebasecloudmessaging.admin` (`firebasemessaging.admin` does not exist and\r\n`firebasenotifications.admin` lacks `cloudmessaging.messages.create`).\r\n\r\n## Cloudflare — DONE except the service install\r\n\r\n`cloudflared` **2026.7.3** installed at `C:\\Program Files (x86)\\cloudflared\\cloudflared.exe`.\r\n\r\n| Thing | Value |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Tunnel name | `superset-companion` |\r\n| Tunnel id | `a829d34c-eed9-4f6a-aca7-859099f0da05` |\r\n| Hostname | `superset.khaira.family` (CNAME added) |\r\n| Bridge port | `127.0.0.1:47610` |\r\n| Config | `~/.cloudflared/config.yml` |\r\n| Credential | `~/.cloudflared/a829d34c-….json` — **secret**, same class as the FCM key |\r\n\r\nPort `47610` is deliberately **outside** `48000-48999`. host-service picks its port by hashing the org\r\nid into that range, and falls back to any free port if that one is taken — so sharing the range risks\r\na collision that would silently point the tunnel at the wrong service.\r\n\r\n**Verified working, not assumed.** Ran the tunnel in the foreground: it registered four QUIC\r\nconnections to Cloudflare's London edge (lhr13/lhr18), and a live HTTPS request through it returned\r\n**502** — meaning the edge reached this machine and found nothing listening on 47610, which is exactly\r\nright before the bridge exists.\r\n\r\n### That test confirmed the app's error taxonomy\r\n\r\nThe client design distinguishes \"desktop is asleep or off\" from \"desktop is up but Superset isn't\r\nrunning\". Both states are now empirically confirmed and distinguishable from the phone:\r\n\r\n| Response | Means | What the phone should say |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| **530** | Tunnel not connected | Desktop asleep or off — wake it |\r\n| **502** | Tunnel up, nothing on 47610 | Superset isn't running — open it |\r\n| **20\r\nTrue\r\n\r\nName\r\n----\r\na829d34c-eed9-4f6a-aca7-859099f0da05.json\r\ncert.pem\r\nconfig.yml","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"bc900cad-0b61-4114-9164-b1cdbbc787c2","timestamp":"2026-08-16T20:27:10.478Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Infrastructure setup — done 2026-07-28\r\n\r\nEverything here was provisioned before any app code exists. No secrets are in this repo.\r\n\r\n## Firebase / FCM — DONE and VERIFIED\r\n\r\nReused the existing **Home-Projects** Google Cloud project rather than creating a new one.\r\n\r\n| Thing | Value |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| GCP / Firebase project | `metal-complex-352812` (\"Home-Projects\") |\r\n| Project number | `681443047156` |\r\n| Android app id | `1:681443047156:android:00f5e5e087e9eccc48d0a1` |\r\n| Package name | `family.khaira.supersetcompanion` |\r\n| Sender service account | `superset-companion-fcm@metal-complex-352812.iam.gserviceaccount.com` |\r\n| Role granted | `roles/firebasecloudmessaging.admin` (send only — not project-wide Firebase admin) |\r\n\r\n**Files:**\r\n- `app-phone/google-services.json` — committed deliberately. Contains no secrets; it ships inside the APK.\r\n- `~/.superset/companion/fcm-service-account.json` — **the sender credential.** Lives outside this repo, ACL-restricted to your user account only. Never copy it in here.\r\n\r\n**Verified working**, not assumed: minted an OAuth token from the service account and called FCM v1\r\n`messages:send` with a deliberately fake device token. It returned `INVALID_ARGUMENT — the registration\r\ntoken is not a valid FCM registration token`, which is the correct rejection and proves auth and\r\npermissions are right. A first attempt returned `PERMISSION_DENIED` purely because IAM had not yet\r\npropagated; it passed ~45s later.\r\n\r\nSet up entirely through `gcloud` plus the Firebase Management REST API — no console clicks, and the\r\n`firebase` CLI login was not needed. Two gotchas worth remembering: the REST calls need an\r\n`x-goog-user-project` header or they fail with a confusing quota-project error, and the role you want\r\nis `firebasecloudmessaging.admin` (`firebasemessaging.admin` does not exist and\r\n`firebasenotifications.admin` lacks `cloudmessaging.messages.create`).\r\n\r\n## Cloudflare — DONE except the service install\r\n\r\n`cloudflared` **2026.7.3** installed at `C:\\Program Files (x86)\\cloudflared\\cloudflared.exe`.\r\n\r\n| Thing | Value |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Tunnel name | `superset-companion` |\r\n| Tunnel id | `a829d34c-eed9-4f6a-aca7-859099f0da05` |\r\n| Hostname | `superset.khaira.family` (CNAME added) |\r\n| Bridge port | `127.0.0.1:47610` |\r\n| Config | `~/.cloudflared/config.yml` |\r\n| Credential | `~/.cloudflared/a829d34c-….json` — **secret**, same class as the FCM key |\r\n\r\nPort `47610` is deliberately **outside** `48000-48999`. host-service picks its port by hashing the org\r\nid into that range, and falls back to any free port if that one is taken — so sharing the range risks\r\na collision that would silently point the tunnel at the wrong service.\r\n\r\n**Verified working, not assumed.** Ran the tunnel in the foreground: it registered four QUIC\r\nconnections to Cloudflare's London edge (lhr13/lhr18), and a live HTTPS request through it returned\r\n**502** — meaning the edge reached this machine and found nothing listening on 47610, which is exactly\r\nright before the bridge exists.\r\n\r\n### That test confirmed the app's error taxonomy\r\n\r\nThe client design distinguishes \"desktop is asleep or off\" from \"desktop is up but Superset isn't\r\nrunning\". Both states are now empirically confirmed and distinguishable from the phone:\r\n\r\n| Response | Means | What the phone should say |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| **530** | Tunnel not connected | Desktop asleep or off — wake it |\r\n| **502** | Tunnel up, nothing on 47610 | Superset isn't running — open it |\r\n| **20\r\nTrue\r\n\r\nName\r\n----\r\na829d34c-eed9-4f6a-aca7-859099f0da05.json\r\ncert.pem\r\nconfig.yml","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"59c98406-a8c3-48c6-8f53-923ddae1a19a","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.233","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
