}]},"uuid":"6e7f6893-cd27-4bd9-b353-03b00655aa53","timestamp":"2026-08-17T00:25:44.841Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"warning: in the working copy of 'apps/desktop/src/renderer/components/PairDeviceDialog/PairDeviceDialog.tsx', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it\nwarning: in the working copy of 'packages/host-service/src/companion/http.ts', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it\nwarning: in the working copy of 'packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it\ndiff --git a/FEATURES.md b/FEATURES.md\nindex 9023d7e762..eb0403b8b8 100644\n--- a/FEATURES.md\n+++ b/FEATURES.md\n@@ -83,6 +83,7 @@ in the merge that drops it (the only legitimate way a marker leaves this list).\n | Companion question capture | the `PreToolUse` AskUserQuestion hook payload already carries everything needed to DISCOVER and render a blocking question — `tool_name`, `tool_use_id`, `transcript_path`, `session_id`, `cwd`, `tool_input.questions[]` (`question`/`header`/`multiSelect`/`options[]`) plus `agent_id`/`agent_type` when a subagent asked — so the capture path forwards it to the bridge's question store instead of parsing transcripts (measured: 300 blocking questions over 7 days, 97.7% answerable, median 1 412 chars, 59% multi-question, ~10% multiSelect). Question text is captured VERBATIM and never truncated: a question longer than the terminal window was never retained in scrollback, so the screen can verify a picker but can never be its source | `(COMPANION-CAPTURE)` |\n | Companion pairing surface (desktop) | the entry point the phone's own instructions name — \"open Superset and choose 'Pair a device'\" — without which the companion feature cannot be bootstrapped at all: `openPairing` is reachable only from the desktop's authenticated tRPC surface, so nothing else can ever open the one 120 s LAN window. A row in Experimental settings reads `companion.status` and derives its own availability (off / enabled-but-failed-to-start / running are three different messages, because they need three different actions from the user) rather than offering a button that can only fail; it opens a fork-only dialog that renders the returned URI as a QR and counts the window down. The pairing code lives in the URL FRAGMENT, so the dialog is the only thing that ever holds it: it is never logged (diagnostics use only the non-secret `pairingRef`), never persisted, never an attribute, never selectable text (a screenshot of a QR expires in 120 s; a screenshot of the string does not), and the flow component is unmounted on close so the value dies with the dialog. Dismissing takes the 0.0.0.0:47611 listener down instead of leaving it up for the rest of the window, and every window operation is serialised because the bridge allows exactly one process-wide (an unserialised close could land after a later open and silently kill the new window). States are honest and bounded — opening / open-with-time-remaining / expired / failed-with-the-server's-reason, never an unbounded spinner. It does NOT claim \"paired\": the bridge exposes no completion signal to the desktop, so the expiry copy points at the phone, which does know. QR is drawn by a dependency-free byte-mode encoder (level M, versions 1-40, full mask selection) — a QR library would move `package.json`/`bun.lock` under the `(LOCK-REGEN)` gate — always dark-on-white regardless of theme, since an inverted symbol is unreadable to some decoders | `(COMPANION-PAIRING-UI)`, `(COMPANION-PAIRING-ENTRY)`, `(COMPANION-PAIRING-SEARCH)` |\n | Companion bridge (phone/watch) | fork-only host-service subsystem (`packages/host-service/src/companion/`) that lets a paired Android phone/watch read agent status and answer a blocked AskUserQuestion. Loopback listener on a fixed port behind a Cloudflare tunnel + Access service token, with the bridge validating the Access JWT itself and failing closed; every body is an end-to-end AES-256-GCM sealed envelope keyed on a per-device key that is DERIVED at pairing and never transmitted, with method/path/status/nonce bound into the AAD and a disk-persisted replay cache that outlives the freshness window. The client sends semantic intent only — questionId, fingerprint, option indices — so no wire field can express \"send these bytes to that terminal\"; the bridge owns the picker byte contract and injects the validated sequence inside ONE critical section holding the per-terminal lock in the same process as the pty writer, under an answer-wide lease. `(ANSWER-GUARDLESS)` means every pending captured Claude question with a terminal target is offered even before capability/session negotiation and even when sidebar curation hides its ordinary thread, and after fingerprint/shape validation, no transcript, screen, renderer, Windows login-session, agent-binding, permission-latch, marker, liveness or capability observation may veto or downgrade it; display-off and a Windows lock screen behave exactly like an unlocked desktop, and only daemon-acknowledged `pty.write` calls are counted as successful and confirmed; `(ANSWER-FRAME-PACING)` keeps protocol-v3 keystrokes as discrete frames with a fixed 500 ms settling gap after each acknowledged non-final frame (renewing the answer lease immediately before every following write), because a daemon acknowledgement precedes Claude's picker transition and burst N-question digits can land on the wrong item — measured on Claude Code 2.1.228 the boundary failure is a SILENTLY submitted wrong answer set (a 250 ms run misattributed every answer one question over), while 500 ms passed 3/3 plus a final clean shipping-value proof; the dynamic sequence deadline scales from the actual encoded frame count rather than duplicating the encoder's picker-shape limits, while protocol v2 remains one complete unpaced legacy frame with its honest durable `unconfirmed` fence; pacing observes no screen/renderer/transcript/display/lock/agent state and can never veto or downgrade an acknowledged write; that acknowledgement never prematurely hides the question — positive matching PostToolUse/tool-result evidence performs settlement/retraction, while a best-effort PTY resize nudge asks Claude to repaint its consumed picker without becoming a write veto or outcome downgrade (the daemon protocol version is bumped; a detached v2 daemon remains adoptable and still receives the ENTIRE encoded companion sequence in one ordered legacy input frame, but because it cannot acknowledge that write the bridge returns durable `unconfirmed`, marks the question stale and retains the duplicate fence rather than lying about confirmation, refusing on negotiated capability state or stopping after the first keystroke) (per-keystroke locking interleaves two multi-select answers into a third one neither device chose). First-answer-wins, zero write retries, read-the-outcome instead. Push is data-only opaque ids with runtime assertions that make text leakage throw — with ONE narrow, explicitly-waived exception since 2026-08-16: `(ALERT-CONTEXT-NAMES)` lets the project, workspace and tab names ride a v3 push in plaintext so an alert can say which chat it is about, while every other free string stays banned and the exempt key set is enumerated by a test; v1/v2 are frozen, keep their 160-byte leak tripwire, and still carry nothing but ids. Push is PRESENCE-GATED rather than delayed: a question buzzes IMMEDIATELY when the user is away from the desktop, and never while they are at it. Presence is two signals — human keystrokes into a terminal WebSocket (never the companion's own answer-injection path, or a phone answer would read as desk presence) and a 15 s Electron beacon carrying OS idle time plus lock state, with a locked screen overriding a recent keystroke. A held question is held with NO ceiling: it is cancelled if answered at the desk, and fired the moment presence lapses (walking away, locking, or the beacon going stale), with a 10 s settling window after a resume so a machine the user has just woken does not empty its queue into their wrist. Armed and sent states are rows in host.db, so a host-service restart neither loses a held push nor re-sends a sent one, and every send is serialised against its own retraction so a notification can never outlive its subject. Wire contract: `superset-companion/PROTOCOL.md`. The 24 h idempotency + status ledger is a TABLE in host.db, not a file, because the race it has to settle cannot be settled by a file: a status read can overtake an answer that was admitted but has not yet recorded itself, and absence then says nothing about whether it is about to be typed. `/v1/answer` and `/v1/answer/status` therefore race atomically for the right to decide — the answer compare-and-sets an `in_flight` claim BEFORE encoding or PTY injection begins, the status path compare-and-sets a `closed_not_received` tombstone that permanently forbids typing — and whichever wins, the loser is bound by it. Coverage is an opaque epoch the client echoes back and the server compares for equality, rotated transactionally with any prune, so no client-side clock arithmetic can manufacture a false \"it was not sent\"; the server returns one discriminated outcome and the client renders it rather than proving anything. Durability is asserted (`PRAGMA synchronous = FULL`) rather than inferred from NTFS rename ordering, and rows validate individually, so a corrupt row costs one requestId its status instead of quarantining every other record’s 24 hours. `(FREETEXT-N2-PROVEN)` widens WHERE a typed answer is accepted: free text now works on a multi-question prompt — 59% of real traffic — because the per-question sequence was driven in a pty and the agent's own `tool_result` confirmed it (the editor-closing `\\r` ADVANCES to the next question rather than submitting, so a free-text question and a digit question compose and the prompt is submitted by the same review-screen return that already ends an all-digit N>1 answer). `(MSEL-N2-PROVEN)` closes the last prompt shape: a multi-select question inside an N>1 prompt is a per-question group of toggle digits terminated by the SAME right-arrow that submits a lone multi-select — there it ADVANCES (next question mid-prompt, review screen on the last) — driven first, middle and last, twice each, mixed with free-text siblings and with the zero-toggle empty group (which the CLI accepts and then OMITS from the `tool_result`, exactly as a desk submit past the review warning does). What the earlier refutation actually refuted was the N=1 submit PAIR reused verbatim: its trailing `\\r` landed on the next question. The one shape still refused is refused on EVIDENCE rather than on caution: on a multi-select question the free-text row's digit toggles a checkbox, the body text is swallowed with no echo and the terminating `\\r` toggles whatever row the caret was on — a run that asked for free text came back answered with the first option instead. A `\\r` into a multi-select question is the single most dangerous byte this contract knows, so `assertNoReturnIntoMultiSelect` re-checks every emitted sequence against the prompt itself. The picker contract is per build AND per shape, and one build's proof can never backfill another's | `(COMPANION-BRIDGE)`, `(ANSWER-GUARDLESS)`, `(ANSWER-LEDGER)`, `(PUSH-PRESENCE)`, `(FREETEXT-N2-PROVEN)`, `(MSEL-N2-PROVEN)` |\n+| Companion pairing by 8-digit code (works off-LAN) | pairing used to have ONE way in — scan a QR and finish the exchange on 0.0.0.0:47611 — which a network that blocks phone -> PC traffic makes physically impossible, and a fresh install is then stuck forever because the ONE secret it is missing (the main Cloudflare service token) is delivered only by that exchange. `(REMOTE-CODE-PAIRING)` adds a second, additive way in: the desktop shows 8 CSPRNG digits grouped `1234-5678`, the user types them into the phone, and an SRP-6a (RFC 5054, 3072-bit group, SHA-256, `g=5`) exchange runs over the tunnel across three unsealed POSTs — `/v1/pair/begin` (metadata in, `pid` + a fresh 16-byte `pairSalt` out), `/v1/pair/kex` (`A` in, `B` out) and `/v1/pair/confirm` (`macPhone` in, the sealed step-4 envelope out). SRP, not the QR flow's X25519-plus-a-short-code: 8 typed digits are ~26.6 bits, so ANY scheme in which a passive observer can test a guess offline is broken by a laptop in minutes — mixing the code into HKDF alongside an X25519 secret does exactly that, because the observer holds both public keys and can score a guess without ever talking to the desktop again. A PAKE is the only construction that removes the offline test: the desktop stores `v = g^x mod N` where `x = H(pairSalt ‖ H(\"sc/v3 remote-pair\":code))`, and NEITHER the code, `x`, `v`, nor any other offline-checkable derivative is ever transmitted, so every guess costs one online request against a window that is counting. Secret exponentiations (`g^x`, `g^b`, `(A·v^u)^b`) run in OpenSSL through `node:crypto`, never in `BigInt`; `A` is refused unless it decodes to EXACTLY 384 canonical base64url bytes with `1 < A < N-1` and is never reduced mod `N`; `u == 0` is refused; all of it fails as `pair_bad_key_agreement` rather than as a wrong code. Everything is domain-separated from the QR flow (salt `superset-companion/pair/v3-srp`, labels `sc/v3-srp device `/`confirm-phone`/`confirm-desktop`, an 825-byte `sc/v3-srp` transcript binding `pid ‖ deviceId ‖ PAD(A) ‖ PAD(B) ‖ pairSalt` — deliberately NOT RFC 2945's `M1`/`M2`, which would leave `pid` and `deviceId` unauthenticated — and `/v1/pair/confirm` bound into the step-4 AAD so a captured envelope cannot be replayed across flows), while the QR/LAN flow's wire bytes are unchanged BYTE FOR BYTE with pinned goldens proving it, and two cross-runtime differential vector cases (one with a leading-zero code) are frozen jointly with the Android client after both sides derived them independently — TypeScript/OpenSSL here, Java/BouncyCastle there — and agreed on every value. Grinding is ONLINE-only and bounded by, in order, the endpoint answering a bodyless 404 (byte-identical to an unknown path) unless a window is open, ONE window at a time process-wide across BOTH kinds, opened only by the person at the desktop, 120 s, single use, three bad MACs burning it, five confirms per window, a per-source request cap keyed on `cf-connecting-ip` and per-candidate ownership by that source — at most three guesses out of 10^8 per window a human deliberately opened. Reachability is HOST-scoped and fails closed: the three pairing paths are served ONLY on the dedicated public host `pair-superset.khaira.family` and by POST only (anything else there, trailing slash included, is a bare 404), those same paths are a bare 404 on the main host, an unknown or missing `Host` reaches nothing, and every ordinary route keeps its unchanged main Cloudflare Access JWT validation — the host/method/path gate is registered BEFORE the Access gate, which is what lets a phone with no Access token pair at all. There is no pairing-scoped Access token any more: one that ships inside a published APK is public by construction and was never a security boundary, so the PAKE is the boundary and the residual exposure — an unauthenticated public endpoint that a distributed flood can make expensive — is accepted and documented rather than papered over. Absent `~/.superset/companion/public-pair-host.json` the code flow is simply off (the QR flow is untouched) and `openRemotePairing` refuses with a message naming the file to create; a file that is present but malformed — or that names the MAIN application host — throws at start-up rather than silently falling back. A burned window is remembered for 60 s so the phone can be told \"you burned it\" instead of the bare 404 a never-opened window produces. Every derived key a candidate holds is OVERWRITTEN on every exit — close, expiry, eviction, burn and successful consumption alike — because dropping a map entry only makes key material unreachable, which is the one state nothing can ever clean up; the honest limits are stated in code, namely that JS `BigInt`s and strings (the displayed code, the Access secret) cannot be zeroed and that `BigInt` is not constant time. The desktop dialog gains a mode toggle, renders the grouped digits (never selectable, never logged, never persisted, dropped with the dialog) and counts the same window down for either kind, with `pairingState` carrying the window's kind so the two modes cannot read each other's verdict | `(REMOTE-CODE-PAIRING)` |\n | Sidebar-state mirror | the desktop sidebar's CURATION — which projects and threads are in it, where they sit, and whether each is hidden, archived, snoozed, completed, soft-deleted, pinned or manually reordered — lives entirely in renderer `localStorage` collections (`v2WorkspaceLocalState`, `v2SidebarProjects`) that no other process can read, while `host.db` is a lifecycle-free append store of everything the machine has ever created. Anything outside the renderer that tries to show \"the user's sidebar\" therefore reads a structurally different, strictly larger set in which a thread binned last month is indistinguishable from one blocked on the user right now. This projects that curation into three `host.db` tables (`sidebar_workspace_state`, `sidebar_project_state`, `sidebar_mirror_meta`) via a fork-only `sidebarMirror.sync` tRPC mutation the renderer pushes on every curation change, debounced. It is a WHOLE-SNAPSHOT replace, not a delta: curation has no single choke point (context menus, bulk actions, kanban drags, the snooze submenu, drag-reorder, the CLI worktree placer), so the only correctness condition that survives new entry points is \"something changed\" — which also makes a failed sync, a host-service restart and a renderer reload all self-repair on the next push. The renderer stays the source of truth and the host-service never writes these tables; the mirror is a projection with ONE permitted failure direction — an ABSENT row means \"no opinion recorded\" and consumers must SHOW the row (`LEFT JOIN`, never `INNER`), because hiding on absence turns a transient miss into a blocked agent nobody sees. Staleness is deliberately NOT folded into that rule: a stale row still carrying `deleted_at`/`archived_at`/`is_hidden`/`snooze_until` hides a thread the user has since restored, which is the forbidden direction, so the writer bounds it instead — exactly one push in flight (two on separate loopback connections have no ordering guarantee, and an older one served last would leave host.db behind a signature already recorded as synced, freezing the mirror until the next curation change) and a retry that never gives up while the app runs (the bounded version had a terminal state that a host-service restart on the same port could never re-arm). Identity fields are normalized rather than trusted for the same reason a localStorage row cannot be: the collections validate on WRITE only, so a legacy row hands back `undefined` behind a `string` type, and the first `.length` on one would have thrown inside the render of a hook mounted with no error boundary nearer than the root route. With the renderer gone the mirror holds the user's last recorded curation; it never invents one. `(MIRROR-HEARTBEAT)` is what makes that statement CHECKABLE from outside: the signature gate that stops identical snapshots from being re-sent also made `last_full_sync_at_ms` measure the last curation CHANGE rather than renderer liveness, so an untouched sidebar was indistinguishable from a quit app and no consumer could age the mirror out toward showing. The same unchanged snapshot is therefore re-pushed every five minutes, and a push is additionally forced whenever the last PUSHED `app_launch_id` differs from the current one — the launch id travels in the meta row, not the snapshot, so a launch whose curation serialized identically to the previous one's would otherwise leave the mirror claiming the old launch and keep every thread snoozed-until-next-launch hidden downstream. `sidebar_mirror_meta` carries the two things an outside reader cannot otherwise derive: its presence distinguishes \"no renderer has ever synced\" (pass everything through) from a genuinely empty curated sidebar, and its `app_launch_id` is what makes an \"until next launch\" snooze evaluable at all | `(SIDEBAR-MIRROR)`, `(MIRROR-HEARTBEAT)` |\n | Companion tree mirrors the sidebar, not `host.db` | the bridge's three read statements were unqualified `SELECT`s, so the phone received a structurally different, strictly LARGER set than the desktop sidebar: 22/22 projects, 183/183 workspaces and 310 terminal rows, with binned, archived and snoozed threads rendering as ordinary live rows and 63 workspaces contributing an inert header with nothing under it. Two independent filters now stand between `host.db` and the wire. `(BRIDGE-SIDEBAR-FILTER)` consumes `(SIDEBAR-MIRROR)` and reproduces the renderer's own bucket classifier — deleted, completed, archived, snoozed and hidden are off the default sidebar and are therefore off the phone UNLESS they carry a pending captured question (a question pierces curation so it cannot disappear from every discovery surface), threads group under the project the user PLACED them in rather than `workspaces.project_id`, and a repo removed from the sidebar takes its threads with it. `(BRIDGE-LIVENESS)` answers the separate question of whether a `status='active'` row still names a pty that exists: it never did — a row leaves `active` only via an explicit dispose or a live in-process `onExit`, so a crash, a force-quit or a host-service restart strands it forever, and 403 such rows spanning 77 days were reaching the phone, 8 of them frozen on `PermissionRequest` and badging permanently-blocked agents that had not existed for weeks. Liveness is the same pair the host-service's own session listing joins (an in-process session, or an id the pty-daemon reports alive), snapshotted for read-side liveness filtering without daemon round trips, and the reap pass now walks row -> daemon as well as daemon -> row so `host.db` stops accumulating corpses (five independent conditions before a row is corrected, because being wrong costs a LIVE terminal its place in the desktop's own session list). `(REAPER-CORRECTION-CAP)` bounds that walk, because its two passes are not two independent observations: a degraded daemon — mid-restart, partially adopted, answering from a half-built registry — stays degraded for longer than the five minutes between them, so both listings can be the same wrong listing, and the corrective write has no undo. One pass may now correct at most `min(10, ceil(25% of active rows))` rows, oldest first, with everything over the cap keeping its confirmed two-pass standing for a later pass instead of restarting its clock; and \"an empty listing is never evidence\" is now asserted of BOTH observations rather than only the current one, by threading the previous pass's listed/not-listed fact in explicitly instead of leaving it to fall out of the caller clearing its set. `(CURATION-PROVENANCE)` makes the filter itself legible from the wire: every `/v1/tree` now carries an OPTIONAL `curation` object (enabled, mirror age, how many workspaces it removed), because an empty or short tree had two indistinguishable causes — nothing is running, or the mirror filtered it away — and only the second is a bug shape. Optional by contract, since both shipped clients must keep parsing a newer bridge. `(EMIT-OPTIONAL-FIELDS)` closes the same gap for three facts the bridge already held and never sent: a project/workspace `pinned` flag (pinning is the one act of curation that changes ORDER rather than membership, so a filter that only decides what to hide could not carry it, and `pinned_at`/`is_pinned` had been SELECTed and read by nothing; it is OMITTED rather than sent as `false` when curation is disabled, because §7.2 distinguishes a field the bridge does not report from one it reports as not-pinned, and a renderer that has never synced is not evidence that nothing is pinned), and `questionCount` + `multiSelect` on a pending-question ref — the headline is only the FIRST question's header, so a 3-question capture rendered exactly like a 1-question one, and a multi-select item has its own byte contract that single-select affordances would offer and the bridge would then refuse. The two filters have OPPOSITE failure directions from the guards they sit beside and that is deliberate: an unfilled mirror, an unmirrored workspace, an unreachable daemon or a stale snapshot all resolve to SHOWING the row, because hiding a real blocked agent is the one failure the feature cannot absorb — while the answer path consults neither liveness nor curation after resolving the captured terminal target, so a question captured before its thread was snoozed stays answerable. `(QUESTION-EXPIRY)` closes the same hole for questions: a pending question on a terminal that no longer exists is settled `stale` and its push retracted through the existing settled-ids wiring, instead of buzzing, counting toward `oldestUnansweredMs` and holding a slot forever. It is held to a HIGHER bar than the tree filter beside it, because `settle(stale)` is terminal and the retraction pulls a notification off the phone: it reads a strict `isProvablyGone` (never `!isLive`) that refuses to read anything into an empty daemon listing at any age — the reaper's rule, not the tree's — passes the row's own `last_attached_at ?? created_at` so a terminal born after the snapshot cannot lose that race, and requires the same verdict on two separate reconcile passes five minutes apart. A flap, an unreachable daemon or a stale snapshot resets the clock rather than condemning anything. The same strict predicate gates the push sender's fire-time re-check, whose `forget()` is equally one-shot: an uncertain verdict there keeps the buzz. `(BRIDGE-AGENT-KIND)` keeps read-side classification consistent: the kind falls back from nullable `definition_id` to the hook's exact `agent_id`. It is not an answer preflight — `(ANSWER-GUARDLESS)` deliberately writes to the captured Claude question's terminal without re-reading mutable binding evidence. Two whole-mirror gates sit in front of every per-row rule, both returning the SAME pass-through curation the bootstrap case does, because a mirror that is not evidence about this machine's sidebar right now must not be filtered with. `(MIRROR-AGE-OUT)` refuses a mirror whose last sync is over 20 minutes old — four of the writer's five-minute heartbeats, so only a genuinely absent renderer reaches it — because every hiding field in those tables is then an opinion from a session that has ended, and `snooze_launch_id` in particular keeps hiding threads the very next launch would have released, with nothing left to release them. `(MIRROR-ORG-GATE)` refuses a mirror written for a different organization: `host.db` is per machine and the mirror inside it is per org, the column had been written and read since the mirror shipped without ever being compared, and since ids do not collide across orgs the practical effect was the project gate hiding the ENTIRE tree. `(PUSH-CURATION-GATE)` extends the same curation to the one companion side effect that never passed through the read API: arming hangs off the capture sink, so a binned, archived, completed or snoozed thread still buzzed the WATCH and the notification then opened a tree that did not contain it — snooze worst of all, since \"not now\" is exactly a statement about being interrupted. It is asked at FIRE time, on every sweep, rather than once before `schedule` — and that is the correctness of the gate, not a detail of where it sits. Curation is REVOCABLE (a snooze expires by itself; an archive or a bin is undone by hand) while `armPush` runs exactly once, at capture, so declining to arm turned a twenty-minute snooze into six hours of silence for a question the sidebar had long since put back, with no fence row left for anything to reconsider and the tree still showing the question the whole time. Every capture is armed now, restart reconstruction covers all of them unchanged, and the sweep decides only whether THIS sweep may fire, so the buzz lands on the first sweep after the curation lapses. The curation hold and the presence hold are independent `continue`s over the same armed entry — whichever holds, holds, and neither can release what the other is still holding — and both sit ABOVE the one-shot `isStillUnanswered` branch, so the one input that is EXPECTED to change its mind is never answered at the point where the only outcomes are fire-now or forget-forever. Answering is still the only permanent disarm. Every uncertain answer BUZZES: curation disabled, no `workspaces` row, a question the memory-only store has never seen (the restart case, which `isStillUnanswered` owns and logs), or anything thrown while asking, and only a positive non-`show` verdict from an enabled curation holds — cached for `CURATION_RECHECK_MS` and logged once per HOLD EPISODE rather than 1,800 times an hour, because a 2 s sweep over a 6 h hold would otherwise mean ~10,800 synchronous reads of the whole mirror per question and as many identical log lines | `(BRIDGE-SIDEBAR-FILTER)`, `(BRIDGE-LIVENESS)`, `(QUESTION-EXPIRY)`, `(BRIDGE-AGENT-KIND)`, `(MIRROR-AGE-OUT)`, `(MIRROR-ORG-GATE)`, `(PUSH-CURATION-GATE)`, `(REAPER-CORRECTION-CAP)`, `(CURATION-PROVENANCE)`, `(EMIT-OPTIONAL-FIELDS)` |\n | Tree freshness the phone can trust | the phone stamps its list 'updated just now' when a heartbeat comes back with `treeStale == false` and counts matching the tree it holds — the desktop asserting, live, that a refetch would return what is already on screen. `treeStale` is derived from `gseq`, and `gseq` moved for exactly ONE reason: a question RESOLVING. So a NEW question was invisible to it, and the counts beside it do not always move either, because `deriveSessionStatus` alre","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"persistedOutputPath":"C:\\Users\\khair\\.claude\\projects\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91\\tool-results\\bftwxp2is.txt","persistedOutputSize":185190},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"05e4e537-7f8a-4aed-95f7-e6d3c9569c0a","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.233","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
