{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:03:14.021Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>abc4dbe1a407c7511</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_uvB0hUBqxRGkxBLdAvZE5vQL</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\abc4dbe1a407c7511.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Simplify pairing implementation\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Tooling caveat (must read)\n\nBash tool is dead in this environment — every invocation, including `pwd`, fails with `/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching `''` (broken shell profile). So **no `git diff`, no `git status`, no test/lint runs.** Scope was reconstructed by reading the pairing/SRP file set in both trees. Findings below are read-verified against current file contents; I could not confirm which hunks are uncommitted vs. baseline. Treat items touching pre-existing helpers (candidate 5, 6) as lower confidence on \"in-diff\".\n\n---\n\n## Simplification candidates\n\n**1**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\index.ts`\n- line: `1451`\n- summary: `current.pairing` is a third mirror of the one-window slot (authoritative `openWindow` in `pairing.ts:1889`, plus `trackedPairingWindow` in `companion.ts:199`), and it is nulled *before* the open that may throw — simpler form is deleting the field and exporting `closeCurrentPairingWindow()` from `pairing.ts` so `closePairing()` acts on the authoritative slot.\n- failure_scenario: Remote window open. Second `openRemotePairing` arrives (two desktop windows, or a retry after a dropped IPC reply). Line 1451 sets `current.pairing = null`; `openRemotePairingWindow` then throws `ONE_WINDOW_MESSAGE` because `pairing.ts`'s `openWindow` is still live. Bridge is now wedged: `closePairing()` (line 1466-1470) sees `open === null`, returns `false`, closes nothing; every subsequent open throws \"a pairing window is already open\"; `PairDeviceDialog`'s pre-open `closePairing` (`PairDeviceDialog.tsx:249`) cannot clear it either. Pairing is unavailable until the 120 s timer fires. Same defect at line `1423` for the LAN path.\n\n**2**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\n- line: `1907`\n- summary: `remoteBurnMemo` is a second, free-running 60 s lifecycle overlapping the window slot; it is cleared in exactly one place (`openRemotePairing`, line 2237) and nowhere else — simpler form is a terminal outcome recorded on the last window (`\"burned\" | \"expired\" | \"consumed\"`) read through the same slot, so open/close of *any* window kind reconciles it in one step.\n- failure_scenario: User burns a remote code (3 wrong entries) → memo armed for 60 s. User immediately opens a **QR** window instead. `openPairingWindow` never touches `remoteBurnMemo`, so `currentRemotePairing()` returns `null` (kind `lan`) and `handleRemotePairRequest` (`http.ts:821`) answers `403 pair_code_burned`. Phone renders `PairingError.CODE_BURNED` — \"Too many tries, so that code is dead. Open a new pairing window on your desktop\" — to a user who is standing in front of a freshly opened pairing window. Same stale 403 after `closePairing()`, which also never clears the memo.\n\n**3**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt`\n- line: `346`\n- summary: A wire code now traverses five hand-maintained tables (`FAILURE_BY_WIRE` 376, `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` 399, `messageFor` 354, `remotePairingError` in `RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt:20`, `PairingError.userText` in `PairingState.kt:113`) that duplicate the QR path's single `when` block in `PairingClient.kt:343` — simpler form is one `Map&lt;String, PairingFailure&gt;` keyed on the desktop's `PairingErrorCode` union plus an explicit `UNKNOWN` member, with status used only when the body is unparseable.\n- failure_scenario: Pairing DNS/tunnel not proxied through Cloudflare → `cf-connecting-ip` absent → `http.ts:808` answers `400 {\"code\":\"unknown\"}`. `\"unknown\"` is absent from `FAILURE_BY_WIRE`, `400` is absent from `FAILURE_BY_STATUS`, so line 346's `?: PairingFailure.BAD_MAC` fires → `PairingError.BAD_MAC` → \"Pairing failed to verify. Nothing was stored.\" User retypes correct digits indefinitely against a configuration fault the message actively points away from. The QR path's equivalent (`PairingClient.kt:393`) defaults the *same* wire response to `UNREACHABLE` — two flows, two different diagnoses of one byte-identical reply.\n\n**4**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\components\\PairDeviceDialog\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx`\n- line: `194`\n- summary: Module-global mutable `pairingOperations` promise chain plus an unconditional `closePairing.mutate()` before every open (line 249) is a fourth guard over an invariant the host already owns, and its comment claims the race is removed \"by construction\" when the chain is per-renderer-module only — simpler form is dropping both and surfacing the host's typed one-window refusal.\n- failure_scenario: Two desktop windows (or a renderer reload mid-open) each hold their own `pairingOperations`. Window A opens a code window; window B's dialog runs its pre-open `closePairing`, silently killing A's live 120 s code while A's dialog keeps counting it down and rendering digits the bridge no longer honours. A's poll only notices on the next `pairingState` tick, and reports it as `closed-early` — the same verdict a *successful pairing* produces (`companion.ts:439`), so A tells the user their phone may have paired when nothing did.\n\n**5**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\n- line: `1329`\n- summary: `evictOldestKexSessionsIfFull` re-derives insertion order with an O(n) `createdAtMs` min-scan, while `chargePerSource` (line 1314) evicts from the *same* structure using `Map` insertion order — two eviction idioms for one invariant; simpler form is `state.kexSessions.keys().next()`, which also lets `KexSessionCommon.createdAtMs` (line 376, read nowhere else) be deleted.\n- failure_scenario: `Date.now()` is millisecond-granular and a flood introduces many candidates inside one tick, so `&lt;` at line 1334 ties and the scan silently degrades to insertion order anyway — the timestamp buys nothing but reads as an LRU policy. A future edit that adds candidate refresh (touching `createdAtMs`) turns this into a genuine LRU while `chargePerSource` stays FIFO, and the `(PAIR-JUNK-SURVIVAL)` argument at line 228 — \"evicting the real phone's candidate requires `MAX_KEX_SESSIONS` introductions inside its kex→confirm gap\" — becomes false for one of the two tables with no test catching the divergence.\n\n**6**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\n- line: `301`\n- summary: `unattributedAttempts` is write-only state on every window — incremented at line 1795 and read only to interpolate itself into its own log line at 1797, never compared to any limit (its own doc says so) — simpler form is deleting the field and logging the event without a counter, or making it a real diagnostics read.\n- failure_scenario: The counter's stated purpose is turning \"the phone can't find the desktop\" from unattributable into diagnosable, but nothing surfaces it: it is absent from `PairingWindowHandleBase.toJSON()` (line 450), from `CompanionPairingState` (`companion.ts:163`), and from the pairing-window log line at 2240. During a real incident the operator must grep 120 s of `console.warn` and count lines by hand, which is exactly what the field was added to avoid — meanwhile it sits on the wire-adjacent session type inviting a future serialiser to publish per-window attacker telemetry.\n\n---\n\nChecked and found clean: SRP-6a arithmetic (`srp.ts` group definition, `PAD()` widths, `srpAssertClientPublic` degenerate-value rejection, fixed-window secret ladder loop bounds), transcript byte-length assertions and prefix domain separation on both sides, key-zeroization paths (`wipeAllPairingKeys` / `completePairing` outer `finally` / Kotlin `ConstantTime.zeroize` in `RemotePairingHandshake.run`'s `finally`), pairing-response nonce counter-0 reservation, `routeByHost` two-host isolation matrix, `remotePairSourceKey` comma/IP-literal refusal, `readBoundedBody` content-length + streaming caps, and the `chargeConfirmAttempt` / `burnMac` / `chargeKexComputation` ordering relative to the MAC check.\n\n**FIX-FIRST** — candidates 1 and 2 are observable protocol-lockstep failures, not style.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>182233</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>35</tool_uses><duration_ms>308837</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
