{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:03:37.399Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>abc4dbe1a407c7511</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_uvB0hUBqxRGkxBLdAvZE5vQL</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\abc4dbe1a407c7511.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Simplify pairing implementation\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Tooling caveat (must read)\n\nBash tool is dead in this environment — every invocation, including `pwd`, fails with `/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching `''` (broken shell profile). So **no `git diff`, no `git status`, no test/lint runs.** Scope was reconstructed by reading the pairing/SRP file set in both trees. Findings below are read-verified against current file contents; I could not confirm which hunks are uncommitted vs. baseline. Treat items touching pre-existing helpers (candidate 5, 6) as lower confidence on \"in-diff\".\n\n---\n\n## Simplification candidates\n\n**1**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\index.ts`\n- line: `1451`\n- summary: `current.pairing` is a third mirror of the one-window slot (authoritative `openWindow` in `pairing.ts:1889`, plus `trackedPairingWindow` in `companion.ts:199`), and it is nulled *before* the open that may throw — simpler form is deleting the field and exporting `closeCurrentPairingWindow()` from `pairing.ts` so `closePairing()` acts on the authoritative slot.\n- failure_scenario: Remote window open. Second `openRemotePairing` arrives (two desktop windows, or a retry after a dropped IPC reply). Line 1451 sets `current.pairing = null`; `openRemotePairingWindow` then throws `ONE_WINDOW_MESSAGE` because `pairing.ts`'s `openWindow` is still live. Bridge is now wedged: `closePairing()` (line 1466-1470) sees `open === null`, returns `false`, closes nothing; every subsequent open throws \"a pairing window is already open\"; `PairDeviceDialog`'s pre-open `closePairing` (`PairDeviceDialog.tsx:249`) cannot clear it either. Pairing is unavailable until the 120 s timer fires. Same defect at line `1423` for the LAN path.\n\n**2**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\n- line: `1907`\n- summary: `remoteBurnMemo` is a second, free-running 60 s lifecycle overlapping the window slot; it is cleared in exactly one place (`openRemotePairing`, line 2237) and nowhere else — simpler form is a terminal outcome recorded on the last window (`\"burned\" | \"expired\" | \"consumed\"`) read through the same slot, so open/close of *any* window kind reconciles it in one step.\n- failure_scenario: User burns a remote code (3 wrong entries) → memo armed for 60 s. User immediately opens a **QR** window instead. `openPairingWindow` never touches `remoteBurnMemo`, so `currentRemotePairing()` returns `null` (kind `lan`) and `handleRemotePairRequest` (`http.ts:821`) answers `403 pair_code_burned`. Phone renders `PairingError.CODE_BURNED` — \"Too many tries, so that code is dead. Open a new pairing window on your desktop\" — to a user who is standing in front of a freshly opened pairing window. Same stale 403 after `closePairing()`, which also never clears the memo.\n\n**3**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt`\n- line: `346`\n- summary: A w