log keeps counting it down and rendering digits the bridge no longer honours. A's poll only notices on the next `pairingState` tick, and reports it as `closed-early` — the same verdict a *successful pairing* produces (`companion.ts:439`), so A tells the user their phone may have paired when nothing did.\n\n**5**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\n- line: `1329`\n- summary: `evictOldestKexSessionsIfFull` re-derives insertion order with an O(n) `createdAtMs` min-scan, while `chargePerSource` (line 1314) evicts from the *same* structure using `Map` insertion order — two eviction idioms for one invariant; simpler form is `state.kexSessions.keys().next()`, which also lets `KexSessionCommon.createdAtMs` (line 376, read nowhere else) be deleted.\n- failure_scenario: `Date.now()` is millisecond-granular and a flood introduces many candidates inside one tick, so `&lt;` at line 1334 ties and the scan silently degrades to insertion order anyway — the timestamp buys nothing but reads as an LRU policy. A future edit that adds candidate refresh (touching `createdAtMs`) turns this into a genuine LRU while `chargePerSource` stays FIFO, and the `(PAIR-JUNK-SURVIVAL)` argument at line 228 — \"evicting the real phone's candidate requires `MAX_KEX_SESSIONS` introductions inside its kex→confirm gap\" — becomes false for one of the two tables with no test catching the divergence.\n\n**6**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\n- line: `301`\n- summary: `unattributedAttempts` is write-only state on every window — incremented at line 1795 and read only to interpolate itself into its own log line at 1797, never compared to any limit (its own doc says so) — simpler form is deleting the field and logging the event without a counter