{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:05:14.372Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a26db4c7a07499fb6</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_7RvCpzufccwvKkkJYI4c2rHc</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a26db4c7a07499fb6.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Find pairing code reuse\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Reuse-finder findings — secure remote pairing batch\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:272\nDefect: `openConfirmEnvelope`, `postJson`, `post`, the OkHttp builder and the error-body→`PairingFailure` mapper are a second copy of `PairingClient.kt`'s (lines 256, 284, 298, 57, 343); the existing abstraction is `PairingClient` itself, which already owns the step-4 envelope opener and the pairing HTTP client.\nFailure: The two copies have already diverged — `PairingClient.openConfirmEnvelope:257` refuses a body shorter than `MIN_SEALED_BODY_BYTES` before parsing; the remote copy does not. Any §4.4 step-4 change (AAD path/method, envelope kind, deviceId binding, `PairConfirmPayload` shape) must be edited twice; editing one leaves the other flow failing GCM-tag validation and reporting `pair_code_wrong`/`BAD_MAC`, which is indistinguishable from a mistyped code. Two error-body types (`PairingErrorBody`, private in PairingClient.kt:409, vs `protocol.PairErrorBody`) parse the same wire object.\nFix: Extract one `PairingTransport` (client construction + `post`/`postJson` + status/wire failure table) and one `openPairConfirmEnvelope(body, deviceKey, deviceIdBytes, confirmPath)`; parameterise the two flows by path + failure mapper. One error-body type.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\http.ts:851\nDefect: The three `/v1/pair/*` routes cast raw JSON (`parsed as RemotePairBeginRequest`) instead of going through this module's zod schema layer (`ROUTES[path].schema.safeParse` in `parseBody`:1363), so `pairing.ts` re-implements the same five field validations by hand (`assertLabel`:1817, `assertSurface`:1828, `assertAppVersion`:1835, `assertProtocolRange`:1846, `decodeExact`:1801) that `helloSchema`:976 / `id22`:963 / `surfaceSchema`:967 / `protocolRangeSchema`:970 already express.\nFailure: The two already disagree — `protocolRangeSchema` has no upper bound on `protocol.max`; `assertProtocolRange` caps it at `0xff`. A phone advertising `{min:0,max:300}` is accepted at `/v1/session/hello` and refused at `/v1/pair/begin` with a bare `unknown`. This is the exact \"legal at one boundary, refused at another\" failure `crypto.ts:764` documents having already been paid for once with wire ids. Every future field rule (label cap, surface value) now has two homes and the pairing home has no `satisfies z.ZodType&lt;...&gt;` compile check.\nFix: Add `remotePairBeginSchema`/`remotePairKexSchema`/`remotePairConfirmSchema` built from the existing `id22`/`surfaceSchema`/`protocolRangeSchema`/`MAX_LABEL_CHARS` primitives, `safeParse` in `handleRemotePairRequest` before `steps.*`, and delete the hand-rolled asserts from `pairing.ts`'s remote path.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\crypto\\KeyDerivation.kt:138\nDefect: `deriveRemotePairingKeys`:138 and `remotePairingTranscript`:166 are line-for-line copies of `derivePairingKeys`:67 and `pairingTranscript`:98 with the five constants swapped — while the desktop half factored the identical difference into `PairingProfile` + shared `expandPairingKeys`/`buildHkdfSalt` (pairing.ts:539, 800, 783).\nFailure: One key schedule now exists in four independently editable places across two repos (2 shared on desktop, 2 duplicated here). A change to the shared shape — e.g. adding a byte to the `K_dev` `info` or reordering the transcript — landing in `derivePairingKeys` but not `deriveRemotePairingKeys` produces a different PRK on one flow only; the symptom is `pair_code_wrong` with no diagnostic on either side (the file's own header calls this out for the raw-bytes-vs-base64url interop bug). The desktop's `PairingProfile` structure is the reviewable proof that only the labels differ; the Kotlin side has no such proof.\nFix: Mirror the desktop: one `PairingProfile` value type (salt prefix, device/confirm labels, transcript prefix, expected transcript length), one `expandPairingKeys(prk, profile, pid, deviceId)` and one `transcript(profile, ...)`; the LAN and remote entry points become two profile constants.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\data\\BridgeCompanionRepository.kt:948\nDefect: `pairFromCode` duplicates `pairFromQr`:891 in full — same five `catch` clauses (`DeviceLockedException`/`SecureStoreException`/`CompanionException` → identical states), same `STORING` → `publishPairing` → `discardSession` → `refresh` tail — differing only by the failure mapper and a `withTimeout` wrapper.\nFailure: A new exception type from the store layer, or a change to the post-pair tail (e.g. a step that must run before `refresh`), has to be added to both. Missing the second copy is silent: the state machine sticks on `Exchanging(STORING)` or leaves a stale session, with no exception and no log.\nFix: One `private suspend fun completePairing(map: (PairingFailure) -&gt; PairingError, run: suspend () -&gt; PairedDeviceRecord)` holding the catch ladder and the tail; both entry points become a mapper + a lambda.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\components\\PairDeviceDialog\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx:196\nDefect: `serialize` is a byte-identical tail-promise mutex — third copy in this repo, after `createSerialiser` (packages/host-service/src/companion/crypto.ts:864, whose docblock records consolidating three earlier copies) and `serializeAuthWrite` (apps/desktop/src/lib/trpc/routers/auth/utils/auth-functions.ts:196).\nFailure: The subtlety the canonical version documents — passing the SAME `work` to both `then` arms so a rejected predecessor cannot wedge the chain, plus the `.then(noop, noop)` that stops the swallow becoming an unhandled rejection — is now hand-maintained in three files. A fix applied to one silently misses the others; here the wedged chain means the pairing dialog's open/close operations stop running and the user sees a permanent \"Opening a pairing window…\".\nFix: Promote one `createSerialiser()` into a shared package (`@superset/shared` or renderer `lib/`) and have all three call sites use it.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:783\nDefect: `buildHkdfSalt`:783, the `IKM = Z || pairingCode` build at :726 and `concatFixed`:664 each hand-roll a byte concatenation, while `crypto.ts` already owns `hkdfInfoWithSuffix`:275 (\"the single place the label-plus-raw-id concatenation is built\") and a `concatBytes`:573 that is not exported.\nFailure: `crypto.ts:279` and `pairing.ts:787` build `utf8(prefix) || bytes` two different ways (`concatBytes` vs manual `set`/offset). `concatBytes` staying private is why every consumer re-rolls it — `srp.ts` and `pairing.ts` both did. An offset arithmetic slip in one of the hand-rolled copies produces wrong key material with no exception (`concatFixed` catches only a total-length mismatch, not a wrong offset), surfacing as an unexplainable `pair_bad_mac`.\nFix: Export `concatBytes` from `crypto.ts` and route `buildHkdfSalt`, the IKM build and `concatFixed`'s body through it (keeping `concatFixed`'s expected-total assertion as a wrapper).\n```\n\nChecked and found clean for this angle: `srp.ts` (BigInt/PAD/ladder helpers have no existing counterpart to reuse; `SrpGroup` parameterisation is the right shape), `config.ts` (uses `readJsonFileIfPresent`), `index.ts` (`openPairing`/`openRemotePairing` share `pairingDeps`, one window slot, no duplicated wiring), `companion.ts` tRPC router (one `trackedPairingWindow`), `pairing.ts` per-source throttling (`chargePerSource` correctly shared by both ceilings), `PairingCode.kt`, `RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt` (separation from the QR mapping is documented and justified), `Srp.kt` (delegates to BouncyCastle rather than re-implementing SRP).\n\nNot assessed (out of angle): correctness of the SRP arithmetic, the desktop/Android wire-version constants, rate-limit bounds, secret-lifetime claims.\n\n**FIX-FIRST**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>226790</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>54</tool_uses><duration_ms>429592</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
