{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:05:25.251Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ad447cf674c86c046</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\ad447cf674c86c046.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Audit pairing efficiency bounds\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Bash is unusable this session (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF` on every command, including `true`) — no test/lint runs. Review done from the materialized diffs plus current-file reads.\n\n## Efficiency findings — secure remote pairing (up to 6)\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1313\nsummary: `chargePerSource` EVICTS the oldest bucket to admit any unseen source, so the per-source ceilings impose no global bound on requests per window — REMOTE_PAIR_REQUESTS_PER_SOURCE(32) x REMOTE_PAIR_MAX_SOURCES(32) reads like a 1024-request cap but is unbounded under source rotation.\nfailure_scenario: Window open 120 s. Attacker sends /v1/pair/begin from N distinct CF-Connecting-IP values. Each new IP: `counts.get` -&gt; undefined, `used(0) &lt; 32` passes, then `!counts.has &amp;&amp; size&gt;=32` deletes the oldest entry and inserts. Every request is admitted. Per request the desktop pays a 4 KiB bounded read + JSON.parse + `parseKexMetadata` + Map insert + `evictOldestKexSessionsIfFull`'s O(16) linear scan, on the host-service's single thread, for the whole window. Only `kexComputations` (64) and `confirmAttempts` (5) are genuinely global; nothing else is. Cheaper/bounded alternative: a monotone per-window TOTAL request counter alongside the per-source table (the same shape `chargeKexComputation` already uses), and refuse rather than evict once `maxSources` is reached.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1061\nsummary: `srpServerHandshake` runs three 3072-bit modular exponentiations synchronously on the host-service event loop, from an unauthenticated public endpoint.\nfailure_scenario: srp.ts's own header measures ~2.8-3.3 ms per exponentiation and ~10 ms per kex on the shipped Electron/win-arm64 runtime. `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE=4` caps one IP at 4 candidates, so 16 distinct source IPs x 4 kex each reaches `MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW=64` -&gt; ~640 ms of cumulative event-loop stall per 120 s window, delivered in indivisible ~10 ms blocks. During each block every other bridge activity on that single thread — /v1/heartbeat, answer leases (15 s TTL), the 2 s push sweep, event publishes — is queued, not merely slowed. Cheaper/bounded alternative: run the kex on a `node:worker_threads` pool (one worker, serialized queue) so the exponentiation never occupies the request thread, and/or lower the per-window ceiling now that eviction (finding above) lets a flood walk to it.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\PairingClient.kt:223\nsummary: `handshake: RemotePairingHandshake = RemotePairingHandshake()` is a default argument, so every `pairWithCode` call constructs a fresh `OkHttpClient()` — discarding the shared client `CompanionCore` already threads into `PairingClient` (CompanionCore.kt:31/49) and that `PairingClient` stores unused for this path.\nfailure_scenario: User mistypes the code twice, then succeeds. Three `pairWithCode` calls -&gt; three `OkHttpClient` instances, each owning its own `Dispatcher` ExecutorService, `ConnectionPool` (5 idle conns, 5 min keep-alive), TaskRunner thread and `RouteDatabase`. None is ever shut down (`dispatcher.executorService.shutdown()` / `connectionPool.evictAll()` are never called), so each attempt strands threads and idle TLS sockets to pair-superset.khaira.family for minutes past the 120 s window, and no attempt reuses the previous attempt's TLS session — a full handshake per retry on a possibly-cellular link, right when latency matters most against a 120 s deadline. Cheaper/bounded alternative: make `RemotePairingHandshake` take the injected `baseClient` (`PairingClient`'s existing field) and drop the no-arg default, or make the default a lazily-created process singleton.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1796\nsummary: `noteUnattributedAttempt` emits one `console.warn` per unattributable request, with no sampling, no cap and no per-window ceiling above it — log I/O amplification driven by remote unauthenticated callers.\nfailure_scenario: Composes with the eviction finding: since the per-source table admits any rotated IP, a distributed flood sending `/v1/pair/kex` or `/v1/pair/confirm` with a wrong pid or an unknown deviceId produces one formatted warn line per request for the full 120 s window, each a synchronous-ish write into the host-service log sink on the same thread the SRP kex runs on. `refuseCandidateRewrite` (pairing.ts:1198) and `chargePerSource`'s flood message (pairing.ts:1308) have the same shape. Cheaper/bounded alternative: log the first occurrence and then only on a count threshold/interval (e.g. powers of two, or once per window at close with the final `unattributedAttempts` total) — the counter is already maintained and is the diagnostic that matters.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\srp.ts:505\nsummary: `srpComputeK(group)` is recomputed inside every `srpServerHandshake`, and `srpSecretModPow` rebuilds the 16-entry `base^i` window table on every call (srp.ts:336-343) even when `base` is the group generator — both are constants of `SRP_3072_SHA256`.\nfailure_scenario: Per handshake this repeats: `srpPad(N)` (a 768-char `BigInt.toString(16)` + `padStart` + `Buffer.from(hex)`), `srpPad(g)`, one SHA-256, and 14 wasted 3072-bit modmuls building the g-table for `g^b`. At the window ceiling of 64 derivations that is ~900 redundant 3072-bit modmuls plus ~128 large transient hex strings, all on the blocking path of finding #2. Cheaper alternative: memoize `k` per `SrpGroup` at module load (it is a pure function of `N` and `g`), and cache the generator window table per group; leave the per-call table only for the non-constant base in `(A*v^u)^b`.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\components\\PairDeviceDialog\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx:313\nsummary: Two independent intervals run for the window's life — a 250 ms countdown tick that `setState`s at 4 Hz to drive a display rounded to whole seconds, plus a separate 1 Hz `pairingState` poll (line 385).\nfailure_scenario: Open the dialog for a full window: ~480 `setRemainingMs` state updates and full `PairingFlow` re-renders, three of every four rendering a byte-identical \"Expires in m:ss\", on top of 120 tRPC round-trips to the host-service. The QR path is saved by `QrCode`'s `useMemo` on `value`, so the cost is React reconciliation rather than re-encoding — but it is still 4x the renders the UI needs, in a dialog that holds the live secret. Cheaper alternative: tick at 1000 ms (or keep 250 ms and only `setState` when `Math.ceil(left/1000)` changes), and derive the countdown from the same 1 Hz poll effect rather than running a second timer.\n```\n\n**Checked and clean (efficiency angle):** `handleRemotePairRequest` gate ordering in `http.ts` (host -&gt; source -&gt; window -&gt; rate -&gt; 4 KiB bounded read, all before any crypto; a closed window allocates nothing and answers 404 without logging); `MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW` is genuinely monotone (never decremented, evicted or reset) and the exact-retry short-circuit at `pairing.ts:1043` prevents a repeated `A` from paying twice; `readBoundedBody` cap; `evictOldestKexSessionsIfFull` table bounded at 16; key/secret zeroization on every candidate-drop path; window `setTimeout` cleared in both `close()` paths; `remoteBurnMemo` self-expiring; phone-side SRP correctly off the main thread via `Dispatchers.Default` (RemotePairingHandshake.kt:164/190) with `retryOnConnectionFailure(false)` and a `withTimeout(PAIRING_WINDOW_MS)` backstop, so no client-side retry amplification; `QrCode` memoized on `value`.\n\n**FIX-FIRST** — findings 1-3 are real bounds/resource defects on a public, unauthenticated surface.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>181520</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>44</tool_uses><duration_ms>440486</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:05:33.937Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ad447cf674c86c046</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\ad447cf674c86c046.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Audit pairing efficiency bounds\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Bash is unusable this session (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF` on every command, including `true`) — no test/lint runs. Review done from the materialized diffs plus current-file reads.\n\n## Efficiency findings — secure remote pairing (up to 6)\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1313\nsummary: `chargePerSource` EVICTS the oldest bucket to admit any unseen source, so the per-source ceilings impose no global bound on requests per window — REMOTE_PAIR_REQUESTS_PER_SOURCE(32) x REMOTE_PAIR_MAX_SOURCES(32) reads like a 1024-request cap but is unbounded under source rotation.\nfailure_scenario: Window open 120 s. Attacker sends /v1/pair/begin from N distinct CF-Connecting-IP values. Each new IP: `counts.get` -&gt; undefined, `used(0) &lt; 32` passes, then `!counts.has &amp;&amp; size&gt;=32` deletes the oldest entry and inserts. Every request is admitted. Per request the desktop pays a 4 KiB bounded read + JSON.parse + `parseKexMetadata` + Map insert + `evictOldestKexSessionsIfFull`'s O(16) linear scan, on the host-service's single thread, for the whole window. Only `kexComputations` (64) and `confirmAttempts` (5) are genuinely global; nothing else is. Cheaper/bounded alternative: a monotone per-window TOTAL request counter alongside the per-source table (the same shape `chargeKexComputation` already uses), and refuse rather than evict once `maxSources` is reached.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1061\nsummary: `srpServerHandshake` runs three 3072-bit modular exponentiations synchronously on the host-service event loop, from an unauthenticated public endpoint.\nfailure_scenario: srp.ts's own header measures ~2.8-3.3 ms per exponentiation and ~10 ms per kex on the shipped Electron/win-arm64 runtime. `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE=4` caps one IP at 4 candidates, so 16 distinct source IPs x 4 kex each reaches `MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW=64` -&gt; ~640 ms of cumulative event-loop stall per 120 s window, delivered in indivisible ~10 ms blocks. During each block every other bridge activity on that single thread — /v1/heartbeat, answer leases (15 s TTL), the 2 s push sweep, event publishes — is queued, not merely slowed. Cheaper/bounded alternative: run the kex on a `node:worker_threads` pool (one worker, serialized queue) so the exponentiation never occupies the request thread, and/or lower the per-window ceiling now that eviction (finding above) lets a flood walk to it.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\PairingClient.kt:223\nsummary: `handshake: RemotePairingHandshake = RemotePairingHandshake()` is a default argument, so every `pairWithCode` call constructs a fresh `OkHttpClient()` — discarding the shared client `CompanionCore` already threads into `PairingClient` (CompanionCore.kt:31/49) and that `PairingClient` stores unused for this path.\nfailure_scenario: User mistypes the code twice, then succeeds. Three `pairWithCode` calls -&gt; three `OkHttpClient` instances, each owning its own `Dispatcher` ExecutorService, `ConnectionPool` (5 idle conns, 5 min keep-alive), TaskRunner thread and `RouteDatabase`. None is ever shut down (`dispatcher.executorService.shutdown()` / `connectionPool.evictAll()` are never called), so each attempt strands threads and idle TLS sockets to pair-superset.khaira.family for minutes past the 120 s window, and no attempt reuses the previous attempt's TLS session — a full handshake per retry on a possibly-cellular link, right when latency matters most against a 120 s deadline. Cheaper/bounded alternative: make `RemotePairingHandshake` take the injected `baseClient` (`PairingClient`'s existing field) and drop the no-arg default, or make the default a lazily-created process singleton.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1796\nsummary: `noteUnattributedAttempt` emits one `console.warn` per unattributable request, with no sampling, no cap and no per-window ceiling above it — log I/O amplification driven by remote unauthenticated callers.\nfailure_scenario: Composes with the eviction finding: since the per-source table admits any rotated IP, a distributed flood sending `/v1/pair/kex` or `/v1/pair/confirm` with a wrong pid or an unknown deviceId produces one formatted warn line per request for the full 120 s window, each a synchronous-ish write into the host-service log sink on the same thread the SRP kex runs on. `refuseCandidateRewrite` (pairing.ts:1198) and `chargePerSource`'s flood message (pairing.ts:1308) have the same shape. Cheaper/bounded alternative: log the first occurrence and then only on a count threshold/interval (e.g. powers of two, or once per window at close with the final `unattributedAttempts` total) — the counter is already maintained and is the diagnostic that matters.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\srp.ts:505\nsummary: `srpComputeK(group)` is recomputed inside every `srpServerHandshake`, and `srpSecretModPow` rebuilds the 16-entry `base^i` window table on every call (srp.ts:336-343) even when `base` is the group generator — both are constants of `SRP_3072_SHA256`.\nfailure_scenario: Per handshake this repeats: `srpPad(N)` (a 768-char `BigInt.toString(16)` + `padStart` + `Buffer.from(hex)`), `srpPad(g)`, one SHA-256, and 14 wasted 3072-bit modmuls building the g-table for `g^b`. At the window ceiling of 64 derivations that is ~900 redundant 3072-bit modmuls plus ~128 large transient hex strings, all on the blocking path of finding #2. Cheaper alternative: memoize `k` per `SrpGroup` at module load (it is a pure function of `N` and `g`), and cache the generator window table per group; leave the per-call table only for the non-constant base in `(A*v^u)^b`.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\components\\PairDeviceDialog\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx:313\nsummary: Two independent intervals run for the window's life — a 250 ms countdown tick that `setState`s at 4 Hz to drive a display rounded to whole seconds, plus a separate 1 Hz `pairingState` poll (line 385).\nfailure_scenario: Open the dialog for a full window: ~480 `setRemainingMs` state updates and full `PairingFlow` re-renders, three of every four rendering a byte-identical \"Expires in m:ss\", on top of 120 tRPC round-trips to the host-service. The QR path is saved by `QrCode`'s `useMemo` on `value`, so the cost is React reconciliation rather than re-encoding — but it is still 4x the renders the UI needs, in a dialog that holds the live secret. Cheaper alternative: tick at 1000 ms (or keep 250 ms and only `setState` when `Math.ceil(left/1000)` changes), and derive the countdown from the same 1 Hz poll effect rather than running a second timer.\n```\n\n**Checked and clean (efficiency angle):** `handleRemotePairRequest` gate ordering in `http.ts` (host -&gt; source -&gt; window -&gt; rate -&gt; 4 KiB bounded read, all before any crypto; a closed window allocates nothing and answers 404 without logging); `MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW` is genuinely monotone (never decremented, evicted or reset) and the exact-retry short-circuit at `pairing.ts:1043` prevents a repeated `A` from paying twice; `readBoundedBody` cap; `evictOldestKexSessionsIfFull` table bounded at 16; key/secret zeroization on every candidate-drop path; window `setTimeout` cleared in both `close()` paths; `remoteBurnMemo` self-expiring; phone-side SRP correctly off the main thread via `Dispatchers.Default` (RemotePairingHandshake.kt:164/190) with `retryOnConnectionFailure(false)` and a `withTimeout(PAIRING_WINDOW_MS)` backstop, so no client-side retry amplification; `QrCode` memoized on `value`.\n\n**FIX-FIRST** — findings 1-3 are real bounds/resource defects on a public, unauthenticated surface.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>181520</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>44</tool_uses><duration_ms>440486</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
