n the 120 s window (`burnMac`, PAIRING_MAX_BAD_MACS). The user is told \"the desktop did not accept that code\" and can never pair, at any code, from that keyboard. Fix direction: restrict to `in '0'..'9'` in `digitsOrNull`/`takeDigits`, or normalize Unicode digits to ASCII before `passwordBytes`.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1215\nsummary: `assertCandidateSource` pins a remote candidate to the exact `CF-Connecting-IP` of its `begin`, but the phone makes three separate HTTPS calls whose egress address can legitimately change.\nfailure_scenario: Phone does `begin` over cellular IPv6, then OkHttp (`retryOnConnectionFailure(false)`, three discrete `newCall`s in `RemotePairingHandshake.run`) opens a new connection for `kex` after a Wi-Fi/cellular handoff, a CGNAT pool rotation, or Happy-Eyeballs picking IPv4. `cf-connecting-ip` differs → `refuseCandidateRewrite(\"a different source address\")` → 400 `pair_wrong_peer`. The Kotlin client maps that to a terminal `WRONG_PEER` (\"the desktop does not recognise this exchange\") with no re-`begin`, so pairing fails for a user doing nothing wrong — exactly the mobile-network case the remote flow exists to serve.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\index.ts:1423\nsummary: `current.pairing = null` is executed BEFORE `openPairingWindow`/`openRemotePairingWindow` (same at line 1451), so an open that throws orphans a still-live window from `closePairing()` and from shutdown teardown.\nfailure_scenario: A window is already open (module-level `openWindow` set) — e.g. a second desktop window, or any caller that reaches `openPairing` without the dialog's preceding `closePairing`. Line 1423 nulls the remembered handle, then `pairing.ts` throws `ONE_WINDOW_MESSAGE`. `closePairing()` now sees `current.pairing === null`, returns `false` and closes nothing; the teardown step `close: () =&gt; state?.pairing?.close()` (line 1142) is a no-op. The LAN window keeps 0.0.0.0:47611 bound and a live QR/code answering for the rest of its 120 s, every retry fails, and the user has no UI path to stop it. Fix direction: null the field only after a successful open, or close the tracked handle first.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1313\nsummary: `chargePerSource` evicts the oldest bucket when the table is full and `Map.set` on an existing key does not refresh insertion order, so a heavy spender's own counter is the first thing evicted — the per-source cap is resettable.\nfailure_scenario: Attacker sends 32 requests from IP_A (hits `REMOTE_PAIR_REQUESTS_PER_SOURCE`), then sends one request each from 32 fresh IPs. Each new IP triggers the `counts.size &gt;= maxSources` eviction, oldest-first, and IP_A's bucket is the oldest — it is deleted. IP_A now has a fresh 32-request allowance. The same holds for `kexSourceCounts`/`MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE`, so a single host with a modest address pool defeats both per-source ceilings the §4.9 security argument enumerates (\"per-source request caps\"). Only `MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW` still bounds CPU.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1330\nsummary: `evictOldestKexSessionsIfFull` justifies eviction with \"it re-kexes and tries again\", but the Android client has no such retry — an evicted candidate is a terminal pairing failure.\nfailure_scenario: Attacker (4 addresses × `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE` = 16 introductions) fills the candidate table inside the sub-second gap between the phone's `begin` and its `kex`. The phone's candidate is evicted. Its `kex` hits `requireRemoteCandidate` → 400 `pair_wrong_peer`. `RemotePairingHandshake.run` (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:170) is strictly linear begin→kex→confirm with no re-`begin`, and `remotePairingError(WRONG_PEER)` is terminal, so the eviction the desktop treats as harmless is a repeatable, cheap, unauthenticated denial of pairing for the whole window.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\http.ts:821\nsummary: The burn-memo 403 `pair_code_burned` is answered before `endpoint.admit`, so it is returned to any unauthenticated internet caller and is charged against no per-source counter.\nfailure_scenario: With no window open, `endpoint === null`, so `chargeRemoteRequest` never runs. Any host on the internet can POST `/v1/pair/begin` to the public pairing host in a tight loop and distinguish \"403 pair_code_burned\" from the bare 404 for 60 s (`PAIRING_REMOTE_BURN_MEMO_MS`) after any burn. That leaks that a pairing was attempted and failed three times on this machine, to callers who were never given the code, and the polling itself is un-throttled by any of the caps documented for this surface.\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>193293</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>55</tool_uses><duration_ms>481819</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
