{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:06:24.039Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>abc4dbe1a407c7511</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_NHJ20BtlGVvMPNvA5ez6hkkC</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\abc4dbe1a407c7511.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Simplify pairing implementation\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Diffs confirm scope. Corrections to my earlier pass: `pairingOperations`/`serialize` in PairDeviceDialog and `evictOldestKexSessionsIfFull`/`unattributedAttempts` in pairing.ts are **context lines, not in-diff** — dropped. Replaced with three verified-new findings.\n\n## Simplification candidates (all verified `+` in the supplied diffs)\n\n**1**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\index.ts`\n- line: `1451`\n- summary: Diff correctly deleted the duplicate one-window guard (old `if (current.pairing !== null &amp;&amp; !current.pairing.closed) throw`, diff lines 4043-4054) but kept the mirror field and clears it *before* an open that can throw — simpler form is deleting `current.pairing` entirely and exporting `closeCurrentPairingWindow()` from `pairing.ts` so `closePairing()` acts on the authoritative `openWindow` slot.\n- failure_scenario: Remote window live. Second `openRemotePairing` (two desktop windows, or retry after a dropped IPC reply) sets `current.pairing = null`, then `openRemotePairingWindow` throws `ONE_WINDOW_MESSAGE` because `pairing.ts:1889` still holds the window. Bridge wedged: `closePairing()` (1466-1470) sees `null`, returns `false`, closes nothing; every later open throws \"already open\"; the dialog's pre-open `closePairing` cannot clear it. Dead until the 120 s timer. Identical at line `1423` (LAN). The removed guard used to prevent exactly this ordering.\n\n**2**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\n- line: `1907`\n- summary: New `remoteBurnMemo` is a free-running 60 s lifecycle parallel to the window slot, cleared in exactly one place (`openRemotePairing`, line 2237) — simpler form is a terminal outcome on the last window (`\"burned\" | \"expired\" | \"consumed\"`) read through the same slot, so any open/close reconciles it.\n- failure_scenario: User burns a remote code (3 wrong entries) → memo armed 60 s. User opens a **QR** window instead. `openPairingWindow` never touches the memo, so `currentRemotePairing()` returns `null` (kind `lan`) and `http.ts:821` answers `403 pair_code_burned`. Phone renders `CODE_BURNED` — \"Too many tries, so that code is dead. Open a new pairing window on your desktop\" — to a user standing in front of a window they just opened. `closePairing()` leaves the same stale 403.\n\n**3**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\n- line: `255`\n- summary: `MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW = 64` is a **window-global** monotone ceiling added alongside two per-source tables (`kexSourceCounts` 4/32, `remoteRequestCounts` 32/32), and it is the one bound that refuses a source that has spent nothing — simpler form is charging derivations per-source, or reserving a slice for sources that have not yet derived.\n- failure_scenario: 16 source IPs, each doing 4 × (`begin`+`kex`) = 8 requests (under the 32/source cap) and 4 distinct deviceIds (at the `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE` cap) → 64 derivations → ceiling. Real phone's `begin` succeeds, its `kex` hits `chargeKexComputation` (line 1731) → `429 pair_rate_limited` → `RATE_LIMITED` / \"Your desktop is turning away attempts for the moment.\" For the rest of that window and every window while the flood runs. This is precisely \"rate-limit a legitimate device out of existence\", which §4.7 and the evict-don't-refuse note at line 221 promise cannot happen — the new counter reintroduces the failure the surrounding design exists to prevent, and the ceiling's own comment (1728) only guarantees candidates that *already* derived can still confirm.\n\n**4**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\n- line: `1051`\n- summary: `RemoteKexSession`'s three co-nullable fields (`clientPublic`/`serverPublic`/`keys`, 404-409) encode \"kex completed\" as an invariant the code then re-asks twice — here (throwing an unreachable 500) and again at line `1479` — simpler form is `state: { phase: \"begun\" } | { phase: \"kexed\"; clientPublic; serverPublic; keys }`, which makes the invariant structural and deletes both checks and the 500.\n- failure_scenario: The comment at 396 says the three \"are only ever written together, so 'this candidate has completed its kex' is one question with one answer\", yet line 1051 exists to answer it a second way and maps the impossible case to `PairingError(500, \"unknown\")`. The phone maps unmapped-400/500 through `RemotePairingHandshake.kt:346`'s catch-all to `BAD_MAC` → \"Pairing failed to verify.\" A future edit that assigns `serverPublic` before `keys` (the natural order — `handshake` returns `serverPublic` first) silently converts every retry on that candidate into an unexplained 500 with no type error, because the union that would have caught it was never expressed.\n\n**5**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt`\n- line: `346`\n- summary: New file adds a third and fourth mapping table (`FAILURE_BY_WIRE` 376, `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` 399) plus `messageFor` 354, feeding `remotePairingError` and `PairingError.userText` — five hops for one wire code, cloned from the baseline `PairingClient.pairingFailureOf`; simpler form is one map keyed on the desktop's `PairingErrorCode` union with an explicit `UNKNOWN` member, status used only when the body is unparseable.\n- failure_scenario: Pairing DNS not proxied through Cloudflare → `cf-connecting-ip` absent → `http.ts:808` returns `400 {\"code\":\"unknown\"}`. `\"unknown\"` is not in `FAILURE_BY_WIRE`, `400` is not in `FAILURE_BY_STATUS`, so `?: PairingFailure.BAD_MAC` fires → \"Pairing failed to verify. Nothing was stored.\" User retypes correct digits indefinitely against a deployment fault the message points away from. The baseline QR path maps the same byte-identical reply to `UNREACHABLE` — two flows, two diagnoses, one response.\n\n**6**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\components\\PairDeviceDialog\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx`\n- line: `117`\n- summary: `PAIRING_MODES` is introduced as THE single qr/code → lan/remote translation, but its `satisfies` constraint types `pairingKind` as bare `string` (diff line 420), so the table has no type link to the host's `PairingKind` union it is translating into — simpler form is importing `PairingKind` from the host-service router types and constraining to it.\n- failure_scenario: The poll's ownership filter at line 363 (`state.pairingKind !== PAIRING_MODES[mode].pairingKind`) compiles against any string. A rename on the host side (`\"remote\"` → `\"code\"`, already the renderer's own vocabulary for it) or a typo in the table leaves the comparison permanently false: every verdict for the code window is discarded as \"not mine\", the dialog never leaves `open`, and a burned or completed window counts down to a full 120 s and then reports `expired`. No type error, no test failure — the exact drift the table's own comment (98-101) says it exists to prevent.\n\n---\n\nChecked and found clean: SRP-6a arithmetic (group definition/width assertion, `PAD()` widths, `srpAssertClientPublic` degenerate rejection, fixed-window ladder bounds, fresh-`b`-per-candidate), transcript prefix domain separation and length assertions on both sides, zeroization paths (`wipeAllPairingKeys`, `completePairing` outer `finally`, Kotlin `run()` `finally`), counter-0 nonce reservation, `routeByHost` host×method×path isolation, `remotePairSourceKey` comma/IP-literal refusal, `readBoundedBody` caps, and `chargeConfirmAttempt`/`burnMac` ordering relative to the MAC check.\n\n**FIX-FIRST** — 1, 2, 3 are observable protocol-lockstep failures.\n\n## Note on the peer message\n\nNo `SendMessage` tool is exposed in this session, so I cannot reply to `code-review` directly — surfacing here instead. Nothing in that message asked me to escalate or write anything; it supplied read paths only, which I used read-only. Bash remains dead (re-probed twice this turn, same profile error at line 104) — worth fixing before any session needs test or lint runs.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>186546</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>49</tool_uses><duration_ms>498853</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:06:30.358Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>abc4dbe1a407c7511</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_NHJ20BtlGVvMPNvA5ez6hkkC</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\abc4dbe1a407c7511.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Simplify pairing implementation\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Diffs confirm scope. Corrections to my earlier pass: `pairingOperations`/`serialize` in PairDeviceDialog and `evictOldestKexSessionsIfFull`/`unattributedAttempts` in pairing.ts are **context lines, not in-diff** — dropped. Replaced with three verified-new findings.\n\n## Simplification candidates (all verified `+` in the supplied diffs)\n\n**1**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\index.ts`\n- line: `1451`\n- summary: Diff correctly deleted the duplicate one-window guard (old `if (current.pairing !== null &amp;&amp; !current.pairing.closed) throw`, diff lines 4043-4054) but kept the mirror field and clears it *before* an open that can throw — simpler form is deleting `current.pairing` entirely and exporting `closeCurrentPairingWindow()` from `pairing.ts` so `closePairing()` acts on the authoritative `openWindow` slot.\n- failure_scenario: Remote window live. Second `openRemotePairing` (two desktop windows, or retry after a dropped IPC reply) sets `current.pairing = null`, then `openRemotePairingWindow` throws `ONE_WINDOW_MESSAGE` because `pairing.ts:1889` still holds the window. Bridge wedged: `closePairing()` (1466-1470) sees `null`, returns `false`, closes nothing; every later open throws \"already open\"; the dialog's pre-open `closePairing` cannot clear it. Dead until the 120 s timer. Identical at line `1423` (LAN). The removed guard used to prevent exactly this ordering.\n\n**2**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\n- line: `1907`\n- summary: New `remoteBurnMemo` is a free-running 60 s lifecycle parallel to the window slot, cleared in exactly one place (`openRemotePairing`, line 2237) — simpler form is a terminal outcome on the last window (`\"burned\" | \"expired\" | \"consumed\"`) read through the same slot, so any open/close reconciles it.\n- failure_scenario: User burns a remote code (3 wrong entries) → memo armed 60 s. User opens a **QR** window instead. `openPairingWindow` never touches the memo, so `currentRemotePairing()` returns `null` (kind `lan`) and `http.ts:821` answers `403 pair_code_burned`. Phone renders `CODE_BURNED` — \"Too many tries, so that code is dead. Open a new pairing window on your desktop\" — to a user standing in front of a window they just opened. `closePairing()` leaves the same stale 403.\n\n**3**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\n- line: `255`\n- summary: `MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW = 64` is a **window-global** monotone ceiling added alongside two per-source tables (`kexSourceCounts` 4/32, `remoteRequestCounts` 32/32), and it is the one bound that refuses a source that has spent nothing — simpler form is charging derivations per-source, or reserving a slice for sources that have not yet derived.\n- failure_scenario: 16 source IPs, each doing 4 × (`begin`+`kex`) = 8 requests (under the 32/source cap) and 4 distinct deviceIds (at the `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE` cap) → 64 derivations → ceiling. Real phone's `begin` succeeds, its `kex` hits `chargeKexComputation` (line 1731) → `429 pair_rate_limited` → `RATE_LIMITED` / \"Your desktop is turning away attempts for the moment.\" For the rest of that window and every window while the flood runs. This is precisely \"rate-limit a legitimate device out of existence\", which §4.7 and the evict-don't-refuse note at line 221 promise cannot happen — the new counter reintroduces the failure the surrounding design exists to prevent, and the ceiling's own comment (1728) only guarantees candidates that *already* derived can still confirm.\n\n**4**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\n- line: `1051`\n- summary: `RemoteKexSession`'s three co-nullable fields (`clientPublic`/`serverPublic`/`keys`, 404-409) encode \"kex completed\" as an invariant the code then re-asks twice — here (throwing an unreachable 500) and again at line `1479` — simpler form is `state: { phase: \"begun\" } | { phase: \"kexed\"; clientPublic; serverPublic; keys }`, which makes the invariant structural and deletes both checks and the 500.\n- failure_scenario: The comment at 396 says the three \"are only ever written together, so 'this candidate has completed its kex' is one question with one answer\", yet line 1051 exists to answer it a second way and maps the impossible case to `PairingError(500, \"unknown\")`. The phone maps unmapped-400/500 through `RemotePairingHandshake.kt:346`'s catch-all to `BAD_MAC` → \"Pairing failed to verify.\" A future edit that assigns `serverPublic` before `keys` (the natural order — `handshake` returns `serverPublic` first) silently converts every retry on that candidate into an unexplained 500 with no type error, because the union that would have caught it was never expressed.\n\n**5**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt`\n- line: `346`\n- summary: New file adds a third and fourth mapping table (`FAILURE_BY_WIRE` 376, `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` 399) plus `messageFor` 354, feeding `remotePairingError` and `PairingError.userText` — five hops for one wire code, cloned from the baseline `PairingClient.pairingFailureOf`; simpler form is one map keyed on the desktop's `PairingErrorCode` union with an explicit `UNKNOWN` member, status used only when the body is unparseable.\n- failure_scenario: Pairing DNS not proxied through Cloudflare → `cf-connecting-ip` absent → `http.ts:808` returns `400 {\"code\":\"unknown\"}`. `\"unknown\"` is not in `FAILURE_BY_WIRE`, `400` is not in `FAILURE_BY_STATUS`, so `?: PairingFailure.BAD_MAC` fires → \"Pairing failed to verify. Nothing was stored.\" User retypes correct digits indefinitely against a deployment fault the message points away from. The baseline QR path maps the same byte-identical reply to `UNREACHABLE` — two flows, two diagnoses, one response.\n\n**6**\n- file: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\components\\PairDeviceDialog\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx`\n- line: `117`\n- summary: `PAIRING_MODES` is introduced as THE single qr/code → lan/remote translation, but its `satisfies` constraint types `pairingKind` as bare `string` (diff line 420), so the table has no type link to the host's `PairingKind` union it is translating into — simpler form is importing `PairingKind` from the host-service router types and constraining to it.\n- failure_scenario: The poll's ownership filter at line 363 (`state.pairingKind !== PAIRING_MODES[mode].pairingKind`) compiles against any string. A rename on the host side (`\"remote\"` → `\"code\"`, already the renderer's own vocabulary for it) or a typo in the table leaves the comparison permanently false: every verdict for the code window is discarded as \"not mine\", the dialog never leaves `open`, and a burned or completed window counts down to a full 120 s and then reports `expired`. No type error, no test failure — the exact drift the table's own comment (98-101) says it exists to prevent.\n\n---\n\nChecked and found clean: SRP-6a arithmetic (group definition/width assertion, `PAD()` widths, `srpAssertClientPublic` degenerate rejection, fixed-window ladder bounds, fresh-`b`-per-candidate), transcript prefix domain separation and length assertions on both sides, zeroization paths (`wipeAllPairingKeys`, `completePairing` outer `finally`, Kotlin `run()` `finally`), counter-0 nonce reservation, `routeByHost` host×method×path isolation, `remotePairSourceKey` comma/IP-literal refusal, `readBoundedBody` caps, and `chargeConfirmAttempt`/`burnMac` ordering relative to the MAC check.\n\n**FIX-FIRST** — 1, 2, 3 are observable protocol-lockstep failures.\n\n## Note on the peer message\n\nNo `SendMessage` tool is exposed in this session, so I cannot reply to `code-review` directly — surfacing here instead. Nothing in that message asked me to escalate or write anything; it supplied read paths only, which I used read-only. Bash remains dead (re-probed twice this turn, same profile error at line 104) — worth fixing before any session needs test or lint runs.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>186546</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>49</tool_uses><duration_ms>498853</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:06:38.769Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a73180d1a08ac45d6</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_cCitbHUo3LWpAVSYEEqYLtJG</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a73180d1a08ac45d6.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Check pairing design altitude\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Diffs confirmed; my review of current files covers every changed file. Report:\n\n---\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1732\nDefect: `chargeKexComputation` is a WINDOW-GLOBAL monotone ceiling (64) on SRP derivations, so one flooder's spend denies the legitimate phone — the opposite of the per-source \"refuse that source only\" discipline every other counter in the file follows.\nFailure: Attacker polls `/v1/pair/begin` (404 costs nothing, charges nothing) until a window opens, then from 16 source IPs sends 4×(begin+kex) each = 64 derivations in ~2 s (~0.6 s of main-thread modexp). `state.kexComputations` hits 64 and never decrements, evicts or resets. Every subsequent `kex` — including the real phone's first and only one — throws `429 pair_rate_limited` for the remaining ~118 s. The user retries, opens a fresh window, and is re-flooded within a second. Remote pairing becomes permanently unavailable to a determined attacker while the module header, `chargePerSource`'s comment and PROTOCOL.md §4.9.2 all assert junk \"can never rate-limit the real phone out of its own window\".\nFix: Charge exponentiations to the requesting source's own bucket (a per-source computation ceiling alongside `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE`) instead of a window-global counter, and/or move `srpServerHandshake` onto a bounded worker queue so saturation degrades to latency for the flooder rather than a hard refusal for everyone. If a window-global backstop is kept, reserve a slice that no single source can consume.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1313\nDefect: `chargePerSource` evicts the oldest bucket when the table is full and then starts the new key at `used = 0`, so an attacker with more addresses than `maxSources` gets its own spent budget refunded — the per-source cap is not a cap.\nFailure: `REMOTE_PAIR_MAX_SOURCES` = 32, `REMOTE_PAIR_REQUESTS_PER_SOURCE` = 32. An attacker on an IPv6 /64 (or 33 cheap VMs) cycles addresses: address 33 evicts address 1's bucket; returning to address 1 re-enters at zero. Same for `MAX_KEX_SOURCES`/`MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE`. Both stated ceilings become unbounded, which is what makes the finding above reachable at all, and which invalidates the \"eviction can only ever refund an old address\" reasoning written directly above the code — the refund is the exploit, not a side effect.\nFix: Do not reset an evicted source to zero. Either make the table LRU on RECENCY OF USE with a floor (evicted buckets return at their previous count via a decaying aggregate), or aggregate overflow sources into a single shared \"unattributed\" bucket with its own small ceiling, so exceeding `maxSources` costs the attacker rather than refunding it.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1215\nDefect: `assertCandidateSource` binds candidate ownership to the transport-level `CF-Connecting-IP` for the candidate's whole life, so a benign public-IP change between the three sequential POSTs kills the exchange — ownership is enforced at the wrong layer when the candidate already carries a cryptographic commitment (`deviceId` + first `A`).\nFailure: Phone runs `begin` → `kex` → `confirm` as three separate HTTPS POSTs. Wi-Fi→cellular handoff mid-typing, an OkHttp pool eviction, or a carrier CGNAT that egresses flows via different public addresses changes `cf-connecting-ip`. `refuseCandidateRewrite(\"a different source address\")` fires → `400 pair_wrong_peer` → `remotePairingError` maps to `PairingError.WRONG_PEER`, whose text is \"Something else answered instead of the desktop. Refused.\" — an attack-flavoured message for a normal network event, contradicting PROTOCOL.md §4.9.7's prescribed \"pairing failed to verify; nothing was stored\". The attempt also burns one of the window's 64 derivations. Note the LAN flow's `handleKex` deliberately does NOT do this, so the two flows disagree about what identifies a candidate.\nFix: Keep the source key for THROTTLING only. Bind candidate ownership to what the exchange already commits to — the first `A` (and, after `kex`, the derived keys) — so a repeat from a new address with the identical `A` and metadata is accepted, while a different `A` is refused exactly as it is today. If a source check is retained, downgrade it to a logged warning rather than a refusal.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:2336\nDefect: The confirm step is a two-device transaction with no acknowledgement and no idempotent re-fetch — the desktop persists the device record, seals the only copy of the reply, then immediately closes the window, so any loss of that one response leaves a paired desktop and an unpaired phone with no recovery path.\nFailure: `sealPairedResponse` sets `consumed = true`, awaits `deps.onPaired` (row written to host.db), builds the sealed body; `admit().confirm` then calls `closeQuietly(handle.close)`. On the phone, `BridgeCompanionRepository.pairFromCode` wraps the whole handshake in `withTimeout(120_000)` and `RemotePairingHandshake` sets `retryOnConnectionFailure(false)`. If the socket dies, the phone is backgrounded past the timeout, or the process is killed after the desktop wrote the row but before `store.persistPairing`, the sealed body — the ONLY carrier of `K_dev` and the Access secret — is gone. Phone shows `WINDOW_CLOSED`; retry gets a bare 404 because the window is consumed. The desktop keeps a live, non-revoked device record forever: it counts in `pairedDeviceCount()`, so the `(KEEP-AWAKE)` gate holds the machine out of sleep indefinitely for a phone that does not exist, and the row is only removable by a manual panic/unpair.\nFix: Make confirm idempotent within the window — cache the sealed body keyed on `(pid, deviceId, macPhone)` and re-serve it byte-for-byte on an exact repeat instead of consuming on first success — and only tear the window down after the phone proves receipt (first successful `/v1/session/hello`, or an explicit ack step). Failing that, write the device record as PROVISIONAL and reap provisional rows that never complete a `hello`.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt:320\nDefect: `PairingCountdown` counts down a deadline the phone invented at submit time, because `PairRemoteBeginResponse` carries `serverTimeMs` but no window expiry — the wire never tells the client the fact the UI is displaying.\nFailure: Desktop opens the window at t=0. User reads the code, unlocks the phone, navigates to code entry and submits at t=105 s. The screen renders \"119s left in the pairing window\" while the real window dies in 15 s. The user waits, sees the spinner, and is then told `WINDOW_CLOSED` after a countdown that said there was almost two minutes left. Same shape for any hold-up between the desktop showing the code and the phone submitting.\nFix: Add the window deadline to the `begin` response (`expiresAtMs`, or `remainingMs` alongside the existing `serverTimeMs`), have `RemotePairingHandshake` surface it, and drive both the countdown and the client-side `withTimeout` from that server-stated deadline instead of `System.currentTimeMillis()` at submit.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt:113\nDefect: The typed pairing code is held in `rememberSaveable`, which serialises it into the Activity's saved-state Bundle — a surface the code's own doctrine says it must never reach.\nFailure: `var digits by rememberSaveable { mutableStateOf(\"\") }` puts the 8 digits into the Compose SaveableStateRegistry, which is written to the saved-instance Bundle on every configuration change and every backgrounding. That Bundle is retained by system_server well past the 120 s window and past the composable's lifetime, and appears in activity state dumps (ANR traces, `dumpsys activity`, bugreports). `PairingCode.passwordBytes`' own contract states the code is \"bounded by the 120-second window rather than by memory hygiene\", and PROTOCOL.md §4.9.2 requires the phone not to include the code in a crash report — this route defeats both, and unlike the unavoidable `String` on the heap it is a deliberate persistence choice.\nFix: Keep `entry` saveable and make `digits` a plain `remember` (or hold it in the ViewModel and clear it on submit/failure). Losing the half-typed code across a rotation is the correct trade for a 26.6-bit secret; if retention across rotation is wanted, clear it in a `DisposableEffect`/`onStop` so it never reaches the Bundle.\n```\n\nChecked clean: SRP-6a server arithmetic (`srp.ts` — padding, `A`/`B`/`u` degenerate-value rejection, fixed-window secret ladder, RFC 5054 group transcription guard); cross-runtime crypto profile agreement (`v3-srp` HKDF labels, 825-byte transcript, `x = H(salt‖H(I‖\":\"‖P))` vs BouncyCastle `SRP6Client`, `PAD(S)` width, ASCII code encoding); `PairingProfile` ownership and LAN/QR byte-for-byte preservation (`sc/v1` labels, 117-byte transcript, `/pair/confirm` AAD path unchanged); host isolation (`routeByHost`/`classifyRequestHost` matrix, pair-host-only paths, main-host 404, missing/unknown `Host` 404, module-load two-host assertion, no Access token on the pair surface); config gating (`loadPublicPairHost` absent-vs-misconfigured, `openRemotePairing`'s loud refusal naming the file); validation boundaries (`decodeClientPublic`, `parseKexMetadata`, `assertProtocolRange`, canonical base64url, 4 KiB pre-read body cap, `cf-connecting-ip` single-literal requirement); attempt-ordering and burn semantics (`chargeConfirmAttempt` after pid check, `burnMac` only on real MAC failures, `noteUnattributedAttempt`); zeroization paths (`wipeAllPairingKeys` on every candidate exit, `completePairing`'s outer `finally`, counter-0 nonce reservation); concurrent-confirm race (no interleaving — `consumed = true` is set synchronously before the first `await`); phone-side error vocabulary (`RemotePairingErrorMapping`, nullable `PairErrorBody`, HTML-403 → `ACCESS_REFUSED`, bodyless-404 → `WINDOW_CLOSED`, IOException → `NETWORK` not Wi-Fi advice); desktop dialog secret handling (`PairingSecret` union, no logging, serialized window operations, server-owned open/closed verdict).\n\n**FIX-FIRST**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>299081</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>48</tool_uses><duration_ms>513998</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:06:42.069Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a26db4c7a07499fb6</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_Fwqs7UXcZosZsCEra3y953jS</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a26db4c7a07499fb6.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Find pairing code reuse\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Scope verified against both diffs. One candidate dropped.\n\n**Dropped — out of scope:** `serialize` / `pairingOperations` in `PairDeviceDialog.tsx` appear as **context lines** (leading space) at desktop diff lines 505-512, not `+`. Pre-existing code, not this batch. Removed.\n\n**Confirmed in scope:** RemotePairingHandshake.kt (new untracked file, referenced from the `PairingClient.kt` `+` hunk at diff:3520) · KeyDerivation.kt `+` at diff:3351/3379 · BridgeCompanionRepository.kt `+` at diff:1013 · http.ts `+` at diff:2511 · pairing.ts `concatFixed`/`buildHkdfSalt` `+` at diff:5086/5224 · pairing.ts `decodeClientPublic` `+` at diff:5725.\n\n---\n\n## Corrected reuse-finder list (6)\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:272\nSummary: `openConfirmEnvelope`, `postJson`, `post`, the OkHttp builder and the wire/status failure mapper are a second copy of `PairingClient.kt`'s (256, 284, 298, 57, 343), which already owns the step-4 envelope opener and the pairing HTTP client.\nFailure scenario: The copies have already diverged — `PairingClient.openConfirmEnvelope:257` refuses a body below `MIN_SEALED_BODY_BYTES`; the remote copy has no such guard. Any §4.4 step-4 change (AAD method/path, envelope kind, deviceId binding, `PairConfirmPayload` shape) must land twice; missing one leaves that flow failing the GCM tag and reporting `CODE_WRONG`/`BAD_MAC` — indistinguishable from a mistyped code. Two error-body types (`PairingErrorBody` private at PairingClient.kt:409 vs `protocol.PairErrorBody`) parse the same object.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\http.ts:851\nSummary: The three new `/v1/pair/*` routes cast raw JSON (`parsed as RemotePairBeginRequest`) instead of using this module's zod layer (`ROUTES[path].schema.safeParse` in `parseBody`:1363), so validation is done instead by `pairing.ts`'s hand-rolled `parseKexMetadata`:1155 → `assertLabel`/`assertSurface`/`assertAppVersion`/`assertProtocolRange` (1817-1861), duplicating `helloSchema`:976 / `id22`:963 / `surfaceSchema`:967 / `protocolRangeSchema`:970.\nFailure scenario: They already disagree — `protocolRangeSchema` has no upper bound on `protocol.max`; `assertProtocolRange` caps at `0xff`. A phone advertising `{min:0,max:300}` is accepted at `/v1/session/hello` and refused at `/v1/pair/begin` with a bare `unknown`. Same \"legal at the outer gate, refused at the inner one\" failure `crypto.ts:764` records already paying for once with wire ids. No `satisfies z.ZodType&lt;...&gt;` compile check on the pairing copy.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\crypto\\KeyDerivation.kt:138\nSummary: `deriveRemotePairingKeys`:138 and `remotePairingTranscript`:166 are line-for-line copies of `derivePairingKeys`:67 / `pairingTranscript`:98 with five constants swapped, while the desktop half factored the identical difference into `PairingProfile` + shared `expandPairingKeys`/`buildHkdfSalt` (pairing.ts:539, 800, 783).\nFailure scenario: One key schedule now lives in four independently editable places across two repos. A change to the shared shape — a byte added to the `K_dev` `info`, a transcript field reordered — landing in `derivePairingKeys` but not `deriveRemotePairingKeys` yields a different PRK on one flow only; symptom is `pair_code_wrong` with no diagnostic on either side, the exact class of bug this file's own header documents for the raw-bytes-vs-base64url interop miss.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\data\\BridgeCompanionRepository.kt:948\nSummary: `pairFromCode` duplicates `pairFromQr`:891 wholesale — same five `catch` clauses mapping `DeviceLockedException`/`SecureStoreException`/`CompanionException` to identical states, same `STORING` → `publishPairing` → `discardSession` → `refresh` tail — differing only by the failure mapper and a `withTimeout` wrapper.\nFailure scenario: A new store-layer exception, or a step added to the post-pair tail, must be added twice. Missing the second copy is silent: the state machine sticks on `Exchanging(STORING)` or leaves a stale session, with no exception and no log line.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:783\nSummary: New `buildHkdfSalt`:783, the `IKM = Z || pairingCode` build at :726 and new `concatFixed`:664 each hand-roll a byte concatenation, while `crypto.ts` owns `hkdfInfoWithSuffix`:275 (\"the single place the label-plus-raw-id concatenation is built\") and a `concatBytes`:573 that is not exported.\nFailure scenario: `crypto.ts:279` and `pairing.ts:787` build `utf8(prefix) || bytes` two different ways. `concatBytes` staying private is precisely why each new module re-rolls it. An offset slip in a hand-rolled copy produces wrong key material with no exception — `concatFixed` asserts only the total length, never the offsets — surfacing as an unexplainable `pair_bad_mac`.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1230\nSummary: New `decodeClientPublic` re-implements `decodeExact`:1801 in the same file — identical three steps (`typeof === \"string\"` → `base64UrlDecode` in a try/catch → exact length check) — differing only in the wire code thrown (`pair_bad_key_agreement` vs `unknown`).\nFailure scenario: Two decoders for every base64url field on the pairing hop. A strictness change made for one (e.g. rejecting an empty string ahead of `base64UrlDecode`, or reporting non-canonical separately from non-alphabet as `crypto.ts:750` distinguishes) silently misses the other, so `A` and `macPhone`/`pid`/`pPub` end up on different acceptance rules within one exchange.\nFix direction: give `decodeExact` an error-code parameter and delete `decodeClientPublic`.\n```\n\nCoverage unchanged otherwise: `srp.ts`, `config.ts`, `companion/index.ts` (`pairingDeps` correctly shared), tRPC `companion.ts`, `chargePerSource`, `PairingCode.kt`, `RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt`, `Srp.kt` — clean for this angle. `isIpLiteral` (http.ts:747, new) considered and rejected as a duplicate of `isPrivateIpv4` (pairing.ts:2550): different questions, not the same logic.\n\nNo SendMessage tool available in this session — returning the corrected list here for `code-review` to pick up.\n\n**FIX-FIRST**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>220682</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>59</tool_uses><duration_ms>517287</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
