{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:06:44.351Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a73180d1a08ac45d6</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_cCitbHUo3LWpAVSYEEqYLtJG</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a73180d1a08ac45d6.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Check pairing design altitude\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Diffs confirmed; my review of current files covers every changed file. Report:\n\n---\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1732\nDefect: `chargeKexComputation` is a WINDOW-GLOBAL monotone ceiling (64) on SRP derivations, so one flooder's spend denies the legitimate phone — the opposite of the per-source \"refuse that source only\" discipline every other counter in the file follows.\nFailure: Attacker polls `/v1/pair/begin` (404 costs nothing, charges nothing) until a window opens, then from 16 source IPs sends 4×(begin+kex) each = 64 derivations in ~2 s (~0.6 s of main-thread modexp). `state.kexComputations` hits 64 and never decrements, evicts or resets. Every subsequent `kex` — including the real phone's first and only one — throws `429 pair_rate_limited` for the remaining ~118 s. The user retries, opens a fresh window, and is re-flooded within a second. Remote pairing becomes permanently unavailable to a determined attacker while the module header, `chargePerSource`'s comment and PROTOCOL.md §4.9.2 all assert junk \"can never rate-limit the real phone out of its own window\".\nFix: Charge exponentiations to the requesting source's own bucket (a per-source computation ceiling alongside `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE`) instead of a window-global counter, and/or move `srpServerHandshake` onto a bounded worker queue so saturation degrades to latency for the flooder rather than a hard refusal for everyone. If a window-global backstop is kept, reserve a slice that no single source can consume.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1313\nDefect: `chargePerSource` evicts the oldest bucket when the table is full and then starts the new key at `used = 0`, so an attacker with more addresses than `maxSources` gets its own spent budget refunded — the per-source cap is not a cap.\nFailure: `REMOTE_PAIR_MAX_SOURCES` = 32, `REMOTE_PAIR_REQUESTS_PER_SOURCE` = 32. An attacker on an IPv6 /64 (or 33 cheap VMs) cycles addresses: address 33 evicts address 1's bucket; returning to address 1 re-enters at zero. Same for `MAX_KEX_SOURCES`/`MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE`. Both stated ceilings become unbounded, which is what makes the finding above reachable at all, and which invalidates the \"eviction can only ever refund an old address\" reasoning written directly above the code — the refund is the exploit, not a side effect.\nFix: Do not reset an evicted source to zero. Either make the table LRU on RECENCY OF USE with a floor (evicted buckets return at their previous count via a decaying aggregate), or aggregate overflow sources into a single shared \"unattributed\" bucket with its own small ceiling, so exceeding `maxSources` costs the attacker rather than refunding it.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1215\nDefect: `assertCandidateSource` binds candidate ownership to the transport-level `CF-Connecting-IP` for the candidate's whole life, so a benign public-IP change between the three sequential POSTs kills the exchange — ownership is enforced at the wrong layer when the candidate already carries a cryptographic commitment (`deviceId` + first `A`).\nFailure: Phone runs `begin` → `kex` → `confirm` as three separate HTTPS POSTs. Wi-Fi→cellular handoff mid-typing, an OkHttp pool eviction, or a carrier CGNAT that egresses flows via different public addresses changes `cf-connecting-ip`. `refuseCandidateRewrite(\"a different source address\")` fires → `400 pair_wrong_peer` → `remotePairingError` maps to `PairingError.WRONG_PEER`, whose text is \"Something else answered instead of the desktop. Refused.\" — an attack-flavoured message for a normal network event, contradicting PROTOCOL.md §4.9.7's prescribed \"pairing failed to verify; nothing was stored\". The attempt also burns one of the window's 64 derivations. Note the LAN flow's `handleKex` deliberately does NOT do this, so the two flows disagree about what identifies a candidate.\nFix: Keep the source key for THROTTLING only. Bind candidate ownership to what the exchange already commits to — the first `A` (and, after `kex`, the derived keys) — so a repeat from a new address with the identical `A` and metadata is accepted, while a different `A` is refused exactly as it is today. If a source check is retained, downgrade it to a logged warning rather than a refusal.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:2336\nDefect: The confirm step is a two-device transaction with no acknowledgement and no idempotent re-fetch — the desktop persists the device record, seals the only copy of the reply, then immediately closes the window, so any loss of that one response leaves a paired desktop and an unpaired phone with no recovery path.\nFailure: `sealPairedResponse` sets `consumed = true`, awaits `deps.onPaired` (row written to host.db), builds the sealed body; `admit().confirm` then calls `closeQuietly(handle.close)`. On the phone, `BridgeCompanionRepository.pairFromCode` wraps the whole handshake in `withTimeout(120_000)` and `RemotePairingHandshake` sets `retryOnConnectionFailure(false)`. If the socket dies, the phone is backgrounded past the timeout, or the process is killed after the desktop wrote the row but before `store.persistPairing`, the sealed body — the ONLY carrier of `K_dev` and the Access secret — is gone. Phone shows `WINDOW_CLOSED`; retry gets a bare 404 because the window is consumed. The desktop keeps a live, non-revoked device record forever: it counts in `pairedDeviceCount()`, so the `(KEEP-AWAKE)` gate holds the machine out of sleep indefinitely for a phone that does not exist, and the row is only removable by a manual panic/unpair.\nFix: Make confirm idempotent within the window — cache the sealed body keyed on `(pid, deviceId, macPhone)` and re-serve it byte-for-byte on an exact repeat instead of consuming on first success — and only tear the window down after the phone proves receipt (first successful `/v1/session/hello`, or an explicit ack step). Failing that, write the device record as PROVISIONAL and reap provisional rows that never complete a `hello`.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt:320\nDefect: `PairingCountdown` counts down a deadline the phone invented at submit time, because `PairRemoteBeginResponse` carries `serverTimeMs` but no window expiry — the wire never tells the client the fact the UI is displaying.\nFailure: Desktop opens the window at t=0. User reads the code, unlocks the phone, navigates to code entry and submits at t=105 s. The screen renders \"119s left in the pairing window\" while the real window dies in 15 s. The user waits, sees the spinner, and is then told `WINDOW_CLOSED` after a countdown that said there was almost two minutes left. Same shape for any hold-up between the desktop showing the code and the phone submitting.\nFix: Add the window deadline to the `begin` response (`expiresAtMs`, or `remainingMs` alongside the existing `serverTimeMs`), have `RemotePairingHandshake` surface it, and drive both the countdown and the client-side `withTimeout` from that server-stated deadline instead of `System.currentTimeMillis()` at submit.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt:113\nDefect: The typed pairing code is held in `rememberSaveable`, which serialises it into the Activity's saved-state Bundle — a surface the code's own doctrine says it must never reach.\nFailure: `var digits by rememberSaveable { mutableStateOf(\"\") }` puts the 8 digits into the Compose SaveableStateRegistry, which is written to the saved-instance Bundle on every configuration change and every backgrounding. That Bundle is retained by system_server well past the 120 s window and past the composable's lifetime, and appears in activity state dumps (ANR traces, `dumpsys activity`, bugreports). `PairingCode.passwordBytes`' own contract states the code is \"bounded by the 120-second window rather than by memory hygiene\", and PROTOCOL.md §4.9.2 requires the phone not to include the code in a crash report — this route defeats both, and unlike the unavoidable `String` on the heap it is a deliberate persistence choice.\nFix: Keep `entry` saveable and make `digits` a plain `remember` (or hold it in the ViewModel and clear it on submit/failure). Losing the half-typed code across a rotation is the correct trade for a 26.6-bit secret; if retention across rotation is wanted, clear it in a `DisposableEffect`/`onStop` so it never reaches the Bundle.\n```\n\nChecked clean: SRP-6a server arithmetic (`srp.ts` — padding, `A`/`B`/`u` degenerate-value rejection, fixed-window secret ladder, RFC 5054 group transcription guard); cross-runtime crypto profile agreement (`v3-srp` HKDF labels, 825-byte transcript, `x = H(salt‖H(I‖\":\"‖P))` vs BouncyCastle `SRP6Client`, `PAD(S)` width, ASCII code encoding); `PairingProfile` ownership and LAN/QR byte-for-byte preservation (`sc/v1` labels, 117-byte transcript, `/pair/confirm` AAD path unchanged); host isolation (`routeByHost`/`classifyRequestHost` matrix, pair-host-only paths, main-host 404, missing/unknown `Host` 404, module-load two-host assertion, no Access token on the pair surface); config gating (`loadPublicPairHost` absent-vs-misconfigured, `openRemotePairing`'s loud refusal naming the file); validation boundaries (`decodeClientPublic`, `parseKexMetadata`, `assertProtocolRange`, canonical base64url, 4 KiB pre-read body cap, `cf-connecting-ip` single-literal requirement); attempt-ordering and burn semantics (`chargeConfirmAttempt` after pid check, `burnMac` only on real MAC failures, `noteUnattributedAttempt`); zeroization paths (`wipeAllPairingKeys` on every candidate exit, `completePairing`'s outer `finally`, counter-0 nonce reservation); concurrent-confirm race (no interleaving — `consumed = true` is set synchronously before the first `await`); phone-side error vocabulary (`RemotePairingErrorMapping`, nullable `PairErrorBody`, HTML-403 → `ACCESS_REFUSED`, bodyless-404 → `WINDOW_CLOSED`, IOException → `NETWORK` not Wi-Fi advice); desktop dialog secret handling (`PairingSecret` union, no logging, serialized window operations, server-owned open/closed verdict).\n\n**FIX-FIRST**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>299081</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>48</tool_uses><duration_ms>513998</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
