{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:10:06.243Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>adb4097c6fbaf514a</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_B7PpT24ljPLWpaEeu4Eoj6o3</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\adb4097c6fbaf514a.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Audit removed pairing behavior\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Finder Angle B (removed-behavior auditor) — 6 candidates.\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:2097\nDefect: The \"one pairing window, process-wide, across BOTH kinds\" slot is claimed only AFTER `openPairingWindow` awaits `server.listen`, while `openRemotePairing` (pairing.ts:2234) runs to completion with no await at all — and the mirror guard that used to stand in front of it in index.ts was deleted in this diff (desktop diff lines 4043-4054).\nFailure: Two renderer instances (two desktop windows, or a reload racing the settings page — each has its own module-level `serialize` chain in PairDeviceDialog). A calls `openPairing`; inside the `await` on 0.0.0.0:47611, B calls `openRemotePairing`, whose guard sees an empty slot, mints a code, sets `openWindow = {remote}`. A's listen resolves and overwrites `openWindow = {lan}`. The remote window is now live (timer, verifier, 120 s) but `currentRemotePairing()` returns null because the slot says `\"lan\"`, so B's displayed 8-digit code answers every phone request with a bare 404 for its whole life. `BridgeState.pairing` is likewise the LAN handle, so B pressing Cancel tears down A's QR instead. Aggravated by index.ts:1423 / index.ts:1451, which now set `current.pairing = null` BEFORE the open that can throw — the old code threw first and kept the field — so a refused open strands a live window that `closePairing` then reports as `{closed:false}` and never closes.\nFix: Reserve the slot synchronously at the top of both openers (set a placeholder/`openWindow` sentinel before any await, release it on failure), and restore the \"check, then clear\" order in index.ts so `current.pairing` is only nulled after the new handle is in hand.\n\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\http.ts:739\nDefect: `remotePairSourceKey` derives the ONLY identity the unauthenticated pairing surface has from the client-suppliable `CF-Connecting-IP` header, on a listener that binds 127.0.0.1:47610 — the header is trustworthy only for traffic that actually transited Cloudflare.\nFailure: Any process on the machine (or anything that can reach loopback:47610 — a compromised renderer, a local dev tool, a browser extension proxying) POSTs to `http://127.0.0.1:47610/v1/pair/begin` with `Host: pair-superset.khaira.family` and a fresh `CF-Connecting-IP` per request. `chargeRemoteRequest`, `chargeKexIntroduction` and — critically — `assertCandidateSource` (pairing.ts:1215), which exists specifically to stop a second caller driving somebody else's candidate, are all keyed on that chosen string, so every one of them is defeated at will: the attacker can race the real phone's `begin`, spend its confirm attempts, and burn the user's window on demand. The comment at http.ts:724 asserts \"a client cannot forge it through the tunnel\", which is true and is not the reachable path.\nFix: Only accept `CF-Connecting-IP` when the request provably arrived via the tunnel (a cloudflared-only shared secret header, a dedicated loopback port bound for tunnel ingress, or an allowlist on `req.socket.remoteAddress` plus a tunnel token), and refuse the pairing surface for any request that cannot prove it.\n\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1306\nDefect: `chargePerSource` evicts the oldest-INSERTED bucket when the table is full, and `counts.set(existingKey, ...)` does not reorder a Map key — so an exhausted source's counter is recoverable rather than sticky, and the \"per-window ceiling\" the anti-grind argument rests on is not a ceiling.\nFailure: An attacker with ≥33 source addresses (trivial over IPv6, or via the forged header above) spends its full `REMOTE_PAIR_REQUESTS_PER_SOURCE` (32) from address X, then issues one request each from 32 fresh addresses. X was inserted first, so X's bucket is the one evicted; the attacker returns to X with a full fresh allowance and repeats indefinitely. The same applies to `kexSourceCounts` / `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE`. What actually survives is only `MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW` and the 3-strike burn; the module header at pairing.ts:64-72 lists \"per-source request caps\" as one of the bounds the 8-digit code's security argument depends on. The comment at pairing.ts:1296-1299 states the eviction \"can only ever refund an old address\" as if refunding were safe.\nFix: Make eviction least-recently-USED (re-insert on every charge) or, better, never evict a bucket that is at or over its cap — bound the table by refusing new sources once full rather than by forgetting old ones.\n\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\http.ts:38\nDefect: The module header still declares, as a normative security property, that the pairing paths \"accept ONLY the second, pairing-scoped Access identity (and every other path accepts only the main one — fail closed in both directions)\"; that control was removed in this batch and `handleRemotePairRequest` (http.ts:794) validates no Access token whatsoever. `index.ts:249-252` repeats it, telling the caller `openRemotePairing` throws \"when the pairing-scoped Cloudflare Access application is not configured\" — the actual condition is a missing `public-pair-host.json` (index.ts:1446, config.ts:817). There is no `aud` for a pairing identity anywhere left in the tree.\nFailure: A maintainer (or the nightly `(MERGE-SEMANTIC-GATE)` reviewer, which reads these headers as the specification) audits the unauthenticated surface, reads line 38, and concludes an Access gate stands in front of `/v1/pair/*`. Every future change to that surface is then reasoned about under a control that does not exist — including the two findings above, whose severity is entirely a function of \"there is no token in front of this\".\nFix: Delete the pairing-scoped-Access claim from http.ts:35-40 and index.ts:249-252 and replace it with what actually gates the surface (host isolation + open window + per-source cap + SRP), matching the honest text already at http.ts:787-792.\n\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\http.ts:885\nDefect: The pairing hop's `CleartextError` branch answers with `cleartextResponse`, which is the SEALED protocol's error shape and vocabulary and sets no `cache-control: no-store` — bypassing `pairingJsonResponse` (http.ts:698), the single constructor whose stated reason for existing is that \"there is nowhere else to forget it\".\nFailure: `readBoundedBody` (http.ts:910) is the only caller that throws these, so a `content-length` that is non-numeric or &gt;4 KiB on `/v1/pair/*` returns `{code:\"envelope_invalid\"|\"body_too_large\", serverTimeMs, retryAfterMs}` with no `no-store`. On the phone, `remoteFailureOf` (RemotePairingHandshake.kt:339) finds neither code in `FAILURE_BY_WIRE` nor status 400/413 in `FAILURE_BY_STATUS`, so it defaults to `PairingFailure.BAD_MAC` and shows the generic \"rejected the exchange with 413\" — and an intermediary is free to cache the refusal for the pairing host, which is exactly the rule the one-constructor comment is defending.\nFix: Map `CleartextError` to a `PairingErrorCode` inside `handleRemotePairRequest` (413 → a pairing-vocabulary code via `pairingErrorResponse`), or have `readBoundedBody` throw `PairingError` when the caller is the pairing hop.\n\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:250\nDefect: `macPhone`, `macDesktopExpected`, `macDesktopReceived` and the 825-byte `transcript` are zeroized only on the success path; the `finally` at line 260 covers `sharedSecret`, `prk` and the two confirm keys but not these.\nFailure: Any failure after the MACs are computed — the confirm POST throwing `IOException`/non-200 (line 324), `openConfirmEnvelope` failing (line 229), the `macDesktop` comparison failing (line 240), the deviceId mismatch (line 243), or the blank-client-secret check (line 246) — returns through the `finally` leaving both confirmation MACs and the full transcript (which contains `PAD(A)`, `PAD(B)`, `pid`, `deviceId`, `pairSalt`) as live heap arrays with no reference and no wipe, on the one path that runs most often in practice: a mistyped code. Every other secret in the same function is wiped unconditionally, so this is an inconsistency in the zeroization discipline rather than a stated limit.\nFix: Move the four arrays into `var`s declared before the `try` and add them to the existing `finally` zeroize call, deleting the success-path-only line 250.\n```\n\nAreas checked and found clean: SRP-6a arithmetic and interop against BouncyCastle (`srpComputeX`/`K`/`U`, `PAD()` width discipline, `A` range and degenerate-value rejection, `u == 0`, fixed-window secret ladder); every cross-runtime constant (`sc/v3-srp` transcript prefix and 825-byte layout, `superset-companion/pair/v3-srp` HKDF salt, the three `sc/v3-srp` labels, `PAIRING_SRP_IDENTITY`, wire version 2, `PROTOCOL_CURRENT`/`PROTOCOL_VERSION_AT_PAIRING` = 1, the three `/v1/pair/*` paths, step-4 request-form AAD); `base64UrlDecode` canonicality (the non-canonical-deviceId map-key hazard is genuinely closed); nonce counter 0 reservation; candidate key wiping on every eviction/replace/burn/close path; `routeByHost` host×method×path isolation and its registration order; `EnvelopeHeader.parse` minimum-length guard on the phone; `pairFromCode`'s state machine and exception coverage against `pairFromQr`; `PairingClient.pairWithCode`'s `use{}` key wipe; the two-host boot assertion in config.ts.\n\n**FIX-FIRST**\n\nNote: the Bash tool is unusable in this session (the shell profile fails to parse — every command, including `true`, exits 2 with `unexpected EOF while looking for matching '`), so no test, lint or `git` run was possible; all verification above is by reading source and the materialized diffs. Findings 1, 3 and 4 are reasoned from the code paths, not executed.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>250457</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>64</tool_uses><duration_ms>721531</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
