open that can throw (old code threw first, kept the field) → a refused open strands a live window that `closePairing` reports `{closed:false}` and never closes.\nFix: Reserve the slot synchronously at the top of both openers before any await, release on failure; restore check-then-clear order in index.ts.\n\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\http.ts:739\nDefect: `remotePairSourceKey` derives the ONLY identity of the unauthenticated pairing surface from client-suppliable `CF-Connecting-IP`, on a listener bound to 127.0.0.1:47610. Header is trustworthy only for traffic that actually transited Cloudflare.\nFailure: Any local process (compromised renderer, dev tool, extension) POSTs `http://127.0.0.1:47610/v1/pair/begin` with `Host: pair-superset.khaira.family` + fresh `CF-Connecting-IP` per request. `chargeRemoteRequest`, `chargeKexIntroduction` and `assertCandidateSource` (pairing.ts:1215) — the last existing specifically to stop a second caller driving someone else's candidate — are all keyed on that chosen string. Attacker races the real phone's `begin`, spends its confirm attempts, burns the user's window on demand. Comment at http.ts:724 defends only the through-tunnel path.\nFix: Accept the header only when the request provably arrived via the tunnel (cloudflared-only shared secret header, dedicated tunnel-ingress port, or socket-address allowlist); refuse the surface otherwise.\n\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1306\nDefect: `chargePerSource` evicts oldest-INSERTED bucket; `Map.set` on an existing key does not reorder → an exhausted source's counter is refundable, so the per-source cap is not a per-window ceiling.\nFailure: Attacker with ≥33 addresses (trivial over IPv6, or via the forged header above) spends all 32 of `REMOTE_PAIR_REQUESTS_PER_SOURCE` from X, then one request each from 32 fresh addresses. X was inserted first → X's bucket evicted → attacker returns to X with a full fresh allowance, repeats indefinitely. Same for `kexSourceCounts`/`MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE`. Only `MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW` and the 3-strike burn survive, yet pairing.ts:64-72 lists \"per-source request caps\" among the bounds the 8-digit code's whole security argument rests on. pairing.ts:1296-1299 states the refund as if it were safe.\nFix: LRU eviction (re-insert on every charge), or never evict a bucket at/over its cap — bound the table by refusing new sources, not by forgetting old ones.\n\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\http.ts:38\nDefect: Module header still declares as normative that pairing paths \"accept ONLY the second, pairing-scoped Access identity (and every other path accepts only the main one — fail closed in both directions)\". That control was removed in this batch; `handleRemotePairRequest` (http.ts:794) validates no token at all. index.ts:249-252 repeats it, claiming `openRemotePairing` throws when \"the pairing-scoped Cloudflare Access application is not configured\" — actual condition is a missing `public-pair-host.json` (index.ts:1446, config.ts:817). No pairing `aud` remains anywhere in the tree.\nFailure: A maintainer — or the nightly `(MERGE-SEMANTIC-GATE)` reviewer, which reads these headers as the spec — audits the unauthenticated surface, reads line 38, concludes an Access gate stands in front of `/v1/pair/*`. Every later change to that surface is reasoned about under a control that does not exist, including findings 2 and 3 above, whose severity is entirely a function of \"there is no token in front of this\".\nFix: Delete the pairing-scoped-Access claim at http.ts:35-40 and index.ts:249-252; state what actually gates the surface, matching the honest text already at http.ts:787-792.\n\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\http.ts:885\nDefect: Pairing hop's `CleartextError` branch answers via `cleartextResponse` — the SEALED protocol's error shape/vocabulary, and with no `cache-control: no-store` — bypassing `pairingJsonResponse` (http.ts:698), the single constructor whose stated reason to exist is \"there is nowhere else to forget it\".\nFailure: `readBoundedBody` (http.ts:910) is its only thrower, so a non-numeric or &gt;4 KiB `content-length` on `/v1/pair/*` returns `{code:\"envelope_invalid\"|\"body_too_large\", serverTimeMs, retryAfterMs}` uncached-uncontrolled. Phone's `remoteFailureOf` (RemotePairingHandshake.kt:339) finds neither code in `FAILURE_BY_WIRE` nor 400/413 in `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` → defaults to `PairingFailure.BAD_MAC`. An intermediary may cache the pairing-host refusal — the exact rule the one-constructor comment defends.\nFix: Map `CleartextError` to a `PairingErrorCode` inside `handleRemotePairRequest`, or have `readBoundedBody` throw `PairingError` on the pairing hop.\n\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:250\nDefect: `macPhone`, `macDesktopExpected`, `macDesktopReceived`, `transcript` zeroized only on the success path; the `finally` at line 260 covers `sharedSecret`, `prk` and both confirm keys but not these.\nFailure: Any post-MAC failure — confirm POST throwing IOException/non-200 (line 324), `openConfirmEnvelope` failing (229), macDesktop mismatch (240), deviceId mismatch (243), blank client secret (246) — exits through the `finally` leaving both confirmation MACs and the 825-byte transcript (PAD(A), PAD(B), pid, deviceId, pairSalt) as unreachable, unwiped heap arrays. Hits on the most common real path: a mistyped code. Every other secret in the function is wiped unconditionally.\nFix: Hoist the four arrays to `var`s before the `try`, add to the existing `finally` zeroize, delete success-only line 250.\n```\n\nChecked clean: SRP-6a arithmetic + BouncyCastle interop (`x`/`k`/`u`, PAD width discipline, `A` range + degenerate rejection, `u==0`, fixed-window ladder); all cross-runtime constants (`sc/v3-srp` prefix + 825-byte layout, `superset-companion/pair/v3-srp` salt, three `sc/v3-srp` labels, `PAIRING_SRP_IDENTITY`, wire v2, protocol 1, three `/v1/pair/*` paths, step-4 request-form AAD); `base64UrlDecode` canonicality (non-canonical-deviceId map-key hazard genuinely closed); nonce counter-0 reservation; candidate key wiping on every eviction/replace/burn/close path; `routeByHost` isolation + registration order; `EnvelopeHeader.parse` min-length guard; `pairFromCode` vs `pairFromQr` state machine and exception coverage; `PairingClient.pairWithCode` `use{}` key wipe; two-host boot assertion in config.ts.\n\nCaveat for the parent: Bash was unusable this session (shell profile parse error — every command, incl. `true`, exits 2 with `unexpected EOF while looking for matching '`). No test/lint/`git` run. All verification by reading source plus the materialized diffs at `C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\secure-pairing-desktop.diff` and `C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\secure-pairing-companion.diff`. Findings 1, 2, 3 are reasoned from code paths, not executed.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>242769</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>64</tool_uses><duration_ms>768202</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
