{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:20:52.122Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ac74c2f20466f36c9</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\ac74c2f20466f36c9.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify OkHttp client leak\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"candidate\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\pairing\\\\PairingClient.kt:223\",\n  \"verdict\": \"CONFIRMED_DOWNGRADED\",\n  \"severity\": \"MINOR\",\n  \"mechanism_verified\": true,\n  \"harm_claim_verified\": false,\n  \"reason\": \"Mechanically true. Kotlin default args are evaluated per invocation, so every `pairWithCode` call with no explicit `handshake` builds a new `RemotePairingHandshake()`, whose own default `baseClient: OkHttpClient = OkHttpClient()` (RemotePairingHandshake.kt:80) is a brand-new client with its own ConnectionPool/Dispatcher/TaskRunner. The client injected into PairingClient (CompanionCore.kt:49 passes the shared `httpClient`) is derived at PairingClient.kt:57 into `client`, which is used ONLY by the QR path `pair()`; `pairWithCode` never touches it, so the shared client is never threaded into the remote path. Sole production call site (BridgeCompanionRepository.kt:962) omits `handshake`, so the default is always taken, and `core.pairingClient()` allocates a fresh PairingClient per attempt too. Harm is overstated, though: (a) OkHttp reclaims — pooled sockets evict after the 5-min keepAlive via daemon TaskRunner threads that idle-exit, and the Dispatcher's ExecutorService is never even started because both handshake and pairing posts use synchronous `execute()`, so no non-daemon 'OkHttp Dispatcher' thread is created; the abandoned client is then GC-able. Nothing is permanently stranded and there is no shutdown/evictAll requirement in this codebase's other clients either. (b) Connection reuse WITHIN a handshake (begin/kex/confirm, three POSTs to the same origin) is preserved since they share the one handshake client; only cross-attempt reuse is lost — an extra TLS handshake per user retry on a user-driven, server-rate-limited flow (PairingFailure.RATE_LIMITED/CODE_BURNED bound attempt count). (c) Production's 'shared' client is itself a plain `OkHttpClient()` (CompanionCoreFactory.kt:30 → CompanionCore.kt:31 default), carrying no interceptors, DNS, proxy, cache or TLS config, so nothing configured is lost by not inheriting it — only pool/dispatcher sharing. No correctness, security, data-integrity or failure-handling impact; SRP/MAC/envelope logic unaffected. Real finding is a convention/resource-hygiene divergence: every other transport type (SealedTransport.kt:89, EventStreamClient.kt:67, PairingClient.kt:52) receives the shared client from CompanionCore, and this path silently opts out. Fix (not applied): thread the core client through — give PairingClient a `RemotePairingHandshake` (or an OkHttpClient) constructed once from the injected `baseClient` and drop the per-call default-arg construction.\",\n  \"files_read\": [\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\pairing\\\\PairingClient.kt\",\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\pairing\\\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt\",\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\CompanionCore.kt\",\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\CompanionCoreFactory.kt\",\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\app-phone\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\phone\\\\data\\\\BridgeCompanionRepository.kt\"\n  ]\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>40854</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>7</tool_uses><duration_ms>67122</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:20:54.343Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ab6e740c9ec4e16cf</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_85dZwpjLQwmiBuqHRfcZkxQQ</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\ab6e740c9ec4e16cf.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify Unicode code input\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"CONFIRMED\",\n  \"severity\": \"MINOR\",\n  \"file\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\pairing\\\\PairingCode.kt\",\n  \"lines\": \"20, 30, 46\",\n  \"reason\": \"Charset mismatch is real. Line 20/30 `it.isDigit()` = JVM `Character.isDigit` → true for every Unicode Nd (U+0660 Arabic-Indic, U+06F0 extended Arabic-Indic, U+0966 Devanagari, U+FF10 fullwidth), so 8 such chars pass `digitsOrNull` length check and render via `grouped()` as a normal-looking code. Line 46 `digits.toByteArray(Charsets.US_ASCII)` = `java.lang.String.getBytes(Charset)`, which per spec replaces unmappable chars with the charset replacement byte ('?' 0x3F) and never throws → password becomes 0x3F repeated, still 8 bytes, silently wrong. No ASCII normalization or filtering exists on any path: PairingScreen.kt:297-310 sets only `KeyboardType.Number` (an IME hint, not enforcement) and pipes raw input through `PairingCode.takeDigits` (same isDigit), paste is explicitly unfiltered by design, `GroupedCodeTransformation` is presentation-only, and `RemotePairingHandshake.run` (lines 116-121, 165) re-validates with the same `digitsOrNull` before feeding `passwordBytes` to `Srp.startClient`. Consequence chain is real: mismatched P → SRP/MAC failure → server CODE_WRONG, and the desktop tracks a bounded attempt budget (`PairingFailure.CODE_BURNED`, 'the pairing window was closed after too many attempts', mapped in FAILURE_BY_WIRE) → non-ASCII digits consume attempts of the 120s window with a 'wrong code' message the user cannot act on. Also violates the file's own doc contract ('the digits as ASCII') and the fail-fast rule: the lossy replacement is silent. Trigger frequency is the only mitigating factor — desktop renders ASCII so paste is normally ASCII; realistic trigger is a localized IME/keyboard emitting native digits or a paste from a localized message, hence MINOR not MAJOR. PairingCodeTest.kt covers only ASCII/latin cases (no Nd case), so no test would catch it. Fix (not applied): restrict acceptance to ASCII `in '0'..'9'` in both `digitsOrNull` and `takeDigits`, or fold Nd digits to their ASCII equivalent via `Character.digit` before encoding; add a test asserting Arabic-Indic input is rejected or normalized, never '?'-substituted.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>41226</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>9</tool_uses><duration_ms>76011</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:21:16.419Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a29a2020999719241</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_Y6F8tYaok3z2D9lYXWeODCtH</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a29a2020999719241.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify confirm transaction loss\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"candidate\": \"packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:2336\",\n  \"verdict\": \"REJECT\",\n  \"severity\": \"NONE_TO_MINOR\",\n  \"reason\": \"Ordering is deliberate, documented, and the safe direction. completePairing (pairing.ts:1525-1533) states persist-before-seal explicitly: a crash between the two leaves the desktop holding a record the phone never confirmed, 'recoverable by re-pairing'; the reverse order leaves the phone holding a key the desktop has no record of, which is unrecoverable. Recovery verified: §4.8 forces a NEW deviceId per pairing (device-store.ts:1290-1296 create() throws on duplicate; index.ts:1528-1533 pre-checks), so a fresh re-pair is never blocked by the stale record and mints a clean one — no overwrite needed, no collision. Key material is not orphaned: the stale record still references its keyRef, so ORPHAN-SWEEP (device-store.ts:628-731) correctly leaves it and refuses to wipe live-referenced keys; user revoke/purge path is intact. No idempotent replay exists (state.consumed set at sealPairedResponse:1569, window closed at 2336, /v1/pair/* then 404s per 2294-2301) — confirmed, but the lost message is macDesktop + Access client secret, not K_dev (phone derives K_dev locally), so a phone that loses the response simply cannot reach the bridge and must re-pair. Impact is therefore: one stale, revocable, non-authorising device row inflating pairedDeviceCount and flipping anyPaired(), requiring manual revoke. No corruption, no partial write (persist() is one transaction + anchor commit), no lost data, no auth bypass. Claimed 'data-integrity impact' unsupported; at most MINOR (no auto-reap of never-acknowledged records).\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>76546</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>11</tool_uses><duration_ms>98100</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:21:18.384Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a122ca31319726584</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_rN70zXxokI5sYkcjwdqxXn9d</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a122ca31319726584.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify saved pairing secret\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"NOT_A_DEFECT\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"file\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\app-phone\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\phone\\\\ui\\\\pairing\\\\PairingScreen.kt:113\",\n  \"reason\": \"Claim overstates persistence and impact on every axis. (1) No disk persistence: MainActivity in app-phone/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml has no android:persistableMode, so the rememberSaveable value lands in the Activity icicle Bundle held in system_server RAM only — TaskPersister writes PersistableBundle persistentState, not the icicle. allowBackup=false + fullBackupContent=false + dataExtractionRules block backup/transfer exfil. (2) Bounded lifetime: CompanionShell.kt:130 dispatches routes via when(route) with no SaveableStateHolder, so leaving CompanionRoute.PAIRING (onDone/onCancel/BackHandler at CompanionShell.kt:116) removes PairingScreen from composition, unregisters its SaveableStateRegistry entry and drops digits from all later saves. digits is also explicitly reset to \\\"\\\" on Try again (PairingScreen.kt:181) and on Scan a QR instead (PairingScreen.kt:232). Retention is limited to the user sitting on the pairing screen. (3) No exploitable value: host-service enforces the secret's life independently — packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:971 sets expiresAtMs = openedAtMs + PAIRING_WINDOW_MS (120_000, core CompanionConstants.kt:83) and :1085-1087 closes the window on a timer, with single-use consumed state. A restored stale code authenticates nothing. (4) Zero marginal exposure: the same 8 digits already exist as an unclearable immutable Kotlin String on the app heap, in the OutlinedTextField/IME buffer and in TextField's own default saver; reading system_server memory requires root, a strictly higher privilege than reading the app heap. (5) No functional bug: restore only refills the text field — nothing auto-submits, and the only side effect is submittedAtMs restoring an already-elapsed countdown (PairingCountdown coerces to 0).\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>51665</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>11</tool_uses><duration_ms>89605</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:21:52.613Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ad3c84c8c02c6699b</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_cLXJ0hWucjBOGXZmeO9q11qs</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\ad3c84c8c02c6699b.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify global kex starvation\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>{\n  \"verdict\": \"INVALID\",\n  \"candidate\": \"packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:1732 — window-global monotone 64 SRP-derivation ceiling exhaustible by 16 sources x 4, locking out legitimate phone kex with 429\",\n  \"mechanism_confirmed\": true,\n  \"reason\": \"Mechanism arithmetic is exact but the behavior is intended, documented and test-locked, not a defect. Verified: MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW=64 (pairing.ts:255), MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE=4 (:236), MAX_KEX_SESSIONS=16 (:228), MAX_KEX_SOURCES=32 (:238). chargeKexComputation (:1731-1739) is charged once per GENUINELY NEW derivation only — cached exact retry returns stored B before it (:1043-1055), 'A' rewrite is refused (:1049), begin/confirm never reach it — and it is never decremented or reset for the window's life (state init :2177, window 120 s, PAIRING_WINDOW_MS=120_000). So 16 distinct cf-connecting-ip sources x 4 introductions each = 64 charges, and any later NEW candidate (including the real phone) gets 429 pair_rate_limited for the window remainder. That exact outcome is the stated design: the :239-255 and :1720-1730 comments say the counter is 'MONOTONE BY DESIGN' and that at the ceiling 'every further NEW derivation