o honest parties is the typed code') is false at that point in the flow. (3) Server-side ordering confirmed: consumed=true then onPaired(persist) then seal (pairing.ts:678-753, header comment 604-614 states persist-before-seal is non-negotiable), and PROTOCOL.md:966 makes remote step 7 identical, so the phantom-record claim holds: window consumed, device record persisted, phone zeroizes deviceKey in finally (RemotePairingHandshake.kt:257-262). (4) No rollback or recovery exists anywhere: no unpair/abort call on the phone path, and the desktop cannot observe the phone's open failure. (5) User impact chain: PairingError.CODE_WRONG ('check the code, and check the window is still up', PROTOCOL.md:1132) tells the user to retype into a window that is already consumed; the retry then fails 410/404 -&gt; 'open a new pairing window'. Downgrade drivers: the orphan-record window is inherent and explicitly accepted by the protocol (pairing.ts:609-613, 'recoverable by re-pairing'), not created by this mapping; the record is removable via desktop revoke (PROTOCOL.md:1195-1202); and the mapping is deliberate and documented (app-phone/.../RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt:14-18 names 'an envelope that will not open' as CODE_WRONG). Residual real defect: no failure code tells the truth here - CODE_WRONG blames the code, and BAD_MAC's user text asserts 'nothing was stored', which is also false post-persist; a distinct failure ('the desktop may have recorded this device - remove it and pair again') is the correct fix. Correction to candidate wording: wrong envelope kind and wrong addressed deviceId throw PairingException, not EnvelopeException (RemotePairingHandshake.kt:278-283), so only header-parse/AEAD-tag/AAD failures and payload decode reach this catch.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>60866</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>19</tool_uses><duration_ms>148182</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
