{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:24:06.118Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a05dfe32a356d7ed9</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_slJJRba4Ej3OplXUlCiOQk1t</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a05dfe32a356d7ed9.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify mobile source pin\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"candidate\": \"packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:1215 — assertCandidateSource pins a pairing candidate to its first CF-Connecting-IP\",\n  \"verdict\": \"REJECT\",\n  \"severity_if_confirmed\": \"MINOR\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.82,\n  \"reason\": \"Server behavior verified: sourceKey = single well-formed cf-connecting-ip (http.ts:738-744), and the pin is enforced at all three remote steps — begin (pairing.ts:983), kex (pairing.ts:1040), confirm (pairing.ts:1478) — with no re-pin, grace, or fallback; a source change throws PairingError(400,'pair_wrong_peer') via refuseCandidateRewrite (pairing.ts:1197-1202). But the impact claim is overstated and partly unverifiable. (1) Exposure is bounded by PAIRING_WINDOW_MS = 120_000 (config.ts:62) — begin/kex/confirm are a single short human-driven burst, and a phone's CF-visible public IP (Wi-Fi NAT, CGNAT binding, IPv6 temporary address) is stable far longer than ~1-2 minutes in the ordinary case; a mid-window handoff requires the user to physically drop Wi-Fi while standing at the desktop pairing UI, which is not the default realistic state. (2) Failure is not data-damaging and not window-destroying: refuseCandidateRewrite charges nothing — it is outside the 3-strike MAC budget (only requireRemoteCandidate calls noteUnattributedAttempt) and outside the confirm budget, so no burn, no key wipe; recovery is a retry (fresh deviceId → fresh candidate under the new source's own full per-source allowance, chargePerSource is per-key) or reopening the 120 s window. (3) The tradeoff is explicitly documented and deliberate (pairing.ts:1204-1213): without the pin, anyone who learns the cleartext deviceId can spend another device's confirm attempts, burn the window on wrong MACs, or race begin with rewritten metadata on an unauthenticated public endpoint — a real DoS against a window a human is standing in front of. (4) The client-side half of the claim could not be verified: no RemotePairingHandshake.kt, and no Kotlin sources at all, exist in this repo/worktree (glob + rg over *.kt found nothing), so 'OkHttp connection reuse', 'no retry', and 'terminal failure' are unsubstantiated — the phone may well re-begin with a fresh deviceId, which succeeds. Net: correct, intentional, bounded, recoverable hardening with an unproven client-side failure story — a MINOR robustness note at most (e.g. allow re-pin on an otherwise-identical candidate before any MAC is charged), not a BLOCKER/MAJOR finding.\",\n  \"verified_by\": [\n    \"packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:970-1003 (begin pin)\",\n    \"packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:1029-1055 (kex pin)\",\n    \"packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:1466-1490 (confirm pin)\",\n    \"packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:1197-1218 (refusal path, no budget charge)\",\n    \"packages/host-service/src/companion/http.ts:721-833 (source derivation, single value, refuse-on-absence)\",\n    \"packages/host-service/src/companion/config.ts:60-63 (120 s window, 3 bad MACs)\",\n    \"packages/host-service/src/companion/remote-pairing.test.ts (no test asserts a mid-flow source change; pin is untested but documented)\"\n  ],\n  \"unverifiable\": [\n    \"companion RemotePairingHandshake.kt absent from this checkout — OkHttp connection reuse, retry policy, and deviceId stability/rotation across attempts could not be read\"\n  ]\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>52916</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>21</tool_uses><duration_ms>242687</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:24:13.159Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a8198e5b2bc6984f6</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_4u41023N4H8t0wpeWhfVw2t7</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a8198e5b2bc6984f6.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify pairing slot race\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"CONFIRMED\",\n  \"reason\": \"Real check-then-claim TOCTOU on the process-wide slot. pairing.ts:1954 tests `openWindow` (LAN), then yields at the `await` over `server.listen` (2077-2091), and claims the slot UNCONDITIONALLY at 2097 (`openWindow = { handle, state, deps }`) with no re-check and no reservation. `openRemotePairing` (2138-2244) has zero awaits between its own guard at 2141 and its claim at 2234 (mintRemotePairingCode/randomBytes/srpComputeX/srpComputeVerifier are all sync), so once its body starts it wins the slot atomically. Nothing serializes the two on the server: bridge index.ts:1415-1463 has no mutex (its comment at 1417-1422 explicitly delegates the one-window rule to pairing.ts, and it even clears `current.pairing = null` at 1423 BEFORE awaiting), and companion.ts:454-485 exposes both as plain concurrent protectedProcedure mutations. Same-tick concurrency is reachable: the renderer uses httpBatchStreamLink (host-service-client.ts:29, 'same-tick calls share one HTTP request') and tRPC executes batched calls concurrently, so a batch containing openPairing+openRemotePairing starts both in one tick — LAN suspends on listen (nextTick, bind host is the literal '0.0.0.0', config.ts:26, so no DNS but still a yield) while remote runs its guard+claim to completion. Damage matches the candidate: LAN overwrites the remote entry, currentRemotePairing() (2302-2308) reads `openWindow` and returns null for a lan-kind slot, so the 8 digits already returned to the dialog count down while /v1/pair/* 404s; the orphaned remote handle's close() no-ops on the slot (2212 `openWindow?.handle === handle` is false) and lives until its 120s timer; and cancellation targets the wrong window — both bridge `current.pairing` (1425 after 1455) and router `trackedPairingWindow` (461 after 482) end holding the LAN handle, so closePairing (index.ts:1464-1479) tears down LAN and leaves the remote state up. Only mitigation found is renderer-side, not a guard: the module-level promise chain `pairingOperations`/`serialize` in PairDeviceDialog.tsx:194-204 + the awaited closePairing at 249, which serializes only within one renderer process and is bypassed by any second local tRPC client or a crafted batch — a client-side lock protecting a documented process-wide security invariant (1867-1882). Fix: claim the slot (or a reservation marker) synchronously before the listen await, and release it on the error path.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>64726</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>18</tool_uses><duration_ms>247356</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:24:28.077Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ac74c2f20466f36c9</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\ac74c2f20466f36c9.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify OkHttp client leak\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"candidate\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\pairing\\\\PairingClient.kt:223\",\n  \"verdict\": \"CONFIRMED_DOWNGRADED\",\n  \"severity\": \"MINOR\",\n  \"mechanism_verified\": true,\n  \"harm_claim_verified\": false,\n  \"reason\": \"Mechanically true. Kotlin default args are evaluated per invocation, so every `pairWithCode` call with no explicit `handshake` builds a new `RemotePairingHandshake()`, whose own default `baseClient: OkHttpClient = OkHttpClient()` (RemotePairingHandshake.kt:80) is a brand-new client with its own ConnectionPool/Dispatcher/TaskRunner. The client injected into PairingClient (CompanionCore.kt:49 passes the shared `httpClient`) is derived at PairingClient.kt:57 into `client`, which is used ONLY by the QR path `pair()`; `pairWithCode` never touches it, so the shared client is never threaded into the remote path. Sole production call site (BridgeCompanionRepository.kt:962) omits `handshake`, so the default is always taken, and `core.pairingClient()` allocates a fresh PairingClient per attempt too. Harm is overstated, though: (a) OkHttp reclaims — pooled sockets evict after the 5-min keepAlive via daemon TaskRunner threads that idle-exit, and the Dispatcher's ExecutorService is never even started because both handshake and pairing posts use synchronous `execute()`, so no non-daemon 'OkHttp Dispatcher' thread is created; the abandoned client is then GC-able. Nothing is permanently stranded and there is no shutdown/evictAll requirement in this codebase's other clients either. (b) Connection reuse WITHIN a handshake (begin/kex/confirm, three POSTs to the same origin) is preserved since they share the one handshake client; only cross-attempt reuse is lost — an extra TLS handshake per user retry on a user-driven, server-rate-limited flow (PairingFailure.RATE_LIMITED/CODE_BURNED bound attempt count). (c) Production's 'shared' client is itself a plain `OkHttpClient()` (CompanionCoreFactory.kt:30 → CompanionCore.kt:31 default), carrying no interceptors, DNS, proxy, cache or TLS config, so nothing configured is lost by not inheriting it — only pool/dispatcher sharing. No correctness, security, data-integrity or failure-handling impact; SRP/MAC/envelope logic unaffected. Real finding is a convention/resource-hygiene divergence: every other transport type (SealedTransport.kt:89, EventStreamClient.kt:67, PairingClient.kt:52) receives the shared client from CompanionCore, and this path silently opts out. Fix (not applied): thread the core client through — give PairingClient a `RemotePairingHandshake` (or an OkHttpClient) constructed once from the injected `baseClient` and drop the per-call default-arg construction.\",\n  \"files_read\": [\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\pairing\\\\PairingClient.kt\",\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\pairing\\\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt\",\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\CompanionCore.kt\",\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\CompanionCoreFactory.kt\",\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\app-phone\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\phone\\\\data\\\\BridgeCompanionRepository.kt\"\n  ]\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>40854</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>7</tool_uses><duration_ms>67122</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:24:28.077Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ab6e740c9ec4e16cf</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_85dZwpjLQwmiBuqHRfcZkxQQ</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\ab6e740c9ec4e16cf.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify Unicode code input\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"CONFIRMED\",\n  \"severity\": \"MINOR\",\n  \"file\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\pairing\\\\PairingCode.kt\",\n  \"lines\": \"20, 30, 46\",\n  \"reason\": \"Charset mismatch is real. Line 20/30 `it.isDigit()` = JVM `Character.isDigit` → true for every Unicode Nd (U+0660 Arabic-Indic, U+06F0 extended Arabic-Indic, U+0966 Devanagari, U+FF10 fullwidth), so 8 such chars pass `digitsOrNull` length check and render via `grouped()` as a normal-looking code. Line 46 `digits.toByteArray(Charsets.US_ASCII)` = `java.lang.String.getBytes(Charset)`, which per spec replaces unmappable chars with the charset replacement byte ('?' 0x3F) and never throws → password becomes 0x3F repeated, still 8 bytes, silently wrong. No ASCII normalization or filtering exists on any path: PairingScreen.kt:297-310 sets only `KeyboardType.Number` (an IME hint, not enforcement) and pipes raw input through `PairingCode.takeDigits` (same isDigit), paste is explicitly unfiltered by design, `GroupedCodeTransformation` is presentation-only, and `RemotePairingHandshake.run` (lines 116-121, 165) re-validates with the same `digitsOrNull` before feeding `passwordBytes` to `Srp.startClient`. Consequence chain is real: mismatched P → SRP/MAC failure → server CODE_WRONG, and the desktop tracks a bounded attempt budget (`PairingFailure.CODE_BURNED`, 'the pairing window was closed after too many attempts', mapped in FAILURE_BY_WIRE) → non-ASCII digits consume attempts of the 120s window with a 'wrong code' message the user cannot act on. Also violates the file's own doc contract ('the digits as ASCII') and the fail-fast rule: the lossy replacement is silent. Trigger frequency is the only mitigating factor — desktop renders ASCII so paste is normally ASCII; realistic trigger is a localized IME/keyboard emitting native digits or a paste from a localized message, hence MINOR not MAJOR. PairingCodeTest.kt covers only ASCII/latin cases (no Nd case), so no test would catch it. Fix (not applied): restrict acceptance to ASCII `in '0'..'9'` in both `digitsOrNull` and `takeDigits`, or fold Nd digits to their ASCII equivalent via `Character.digit` before encoding; add a test asserting Arabic-Indic input is rejected or normalized, never '?'-substituted.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>41226</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>9</tool_uses><duration_ms>76011</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
