dev (phone derives K_dev locally), so a phone that loses the response simply cannot reach the bridge and must re-pair. Impact is therefore: one stale, revocable, non-authorising device row inflating pairedDeviceCount and flipping anyPaired(), requiring manual revoke. No corruption, no partial write (persist() is one transaction + anchor commit), no lost data, no auth bypass. Claimed 'data-integrity impact' unsupported; at most MINOR (no auto-reap of never-acknowledged records).\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>76546</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>11</tool_uses><duration_ms>98100</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:24:28.077Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a122ca31319726584</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_rN70zXxokI5sYkcjwdqxXn9d</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a122ca31319726584.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify saved pairing secret\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"NOT_A_DEFECT\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"file\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\app-phone\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\phone\\\\ui\\\\pairing\\\\PairingScreen.kt:113\",\n  \"reason\": \"Claim overstates persistence and impact on every axis. (1) No disk persistence: MainActivity in app-phone/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml has no android:persistableMode, so the rememberSaveable value lands in the Activity icicle Bundle held in system_server RAM only — TaskPersister writes PersistableBundle persistentState, not the icicle. allowBackup=false + fullBackupContent=false + dataExtractionRules block backup/transfer exfil. (2) Bounded lifetime: CompanionShell.kt:130 dispatches routes via when(route) with no SaveableStateHolder, so leaving CompanionRoute.PAIRING (onDone/onCancel/BackHandler at CompanionShell.kt:116) removes PairingScreen from composition, unregisters its SaveableStateRegistry entry and drops digits from all later saves. digits is also explicitly reset to \\\"\\\" on Try again (PairingScreen.kt:181) and on Scan a QR instead (PairingScreen.kt:232). Retention is limited to the user sitting on the pairing screen. (3) No exploitable value: host-service enforces the secret's life independently — packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:971 sets expiresAtMs = openedAtMs + PAIRING_WINDOW_MS (120_000, core CompanionConstants.kt:83) and :1085-1087 closes the window on a timer, with single-use consumed state. A restored stale code authenticates nothing. (4) Zero marginal exposure: the same 8 digits already exist as an unclearable immutable Kotlin String on the app heap, in the OutlinedTextField/IME buffer and in TextField's own default saver; reading system_server memory requires root, a strictly higher privilege than reading the app heap. (5) No functional bug: restore only refills the text field — nothing auto-submits, and the only side effect is submittedAtMs restoring an already-elapsed countdown (PairingCountdown coerces to 0).\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>51665</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>11</tool_uses><duration_ms>89605</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:24:28.077Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ad3c84c8c02c6699b</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_cLXJ0hWucjBOGXZmeO9q11qs</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\ad3c84c8c02c6699b.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify global kex starvation\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>{\n  \"verdict\": \"INVALID\",\n  \"candidate\": \"packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:1732 — window-global monotone 64 SRP-derivation ceiling exhaustible by 16 sources x 4, locking out legitimate phone kex with 429\",\n  \"mechanism_confirmed\": true,\n  \"reason\": \"Mechanism arithmetic is exact but the behavior is intended, documented and test-locked, not a defect. Verified: MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW=64 (pairing.ts:255), MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE=4 (:236), MAX_KEX_SESSIONS=16 (:228), MAX_KEX_SOURCES=32 (:238). chargeKexComputation (:1731-1739) is charged once per GENUINELY NEW derivation only — cached exact retry returns stored B before it (:1043-1055), 'A' rewrite is refused (:1049), begin/confirm never reach it — and it is never decremented or reset for the window's life (state init :2177, window 120 s, PAIRING_WINDOW_MS=120_000). So 16 distinct cf-connecting-ip sources x 4 introductions each = 64 charges, and any later NEW candidate (including the real phone) gets 429 pair_rate_limited for the window remainder. That exact outcome is the stated design: the :239-255 and :1720-1730 comments say the counter is 'MONOTONE BY DESIGN' and that at the ceiling 'every further NEW derivation is refused for the rest of the window while candidates that already derived can still confirm', with the rationale that MAX_KEX_SESSIONS evicts rather than refuses so nothing else bounds unauthenticated 3072-bit modexp on the main thread. remote-pairing.test.ts:1199-1315 asserts precisely this — 64 fillers spread 4-per-source, then #65 begin=200 / kex=429 pair_rate_limited, with already-derived candidates still confirming. The claimed contradiction does not exist in the source: the per-source isolation promise (:1275-1279) is scoped to chargeKexIntroduction only, and http.ts:787-792 already records the accepted limitation that an unauthenticated internet flood can spend this budget. Residual is availability-only on a human-opened 120 s window that the user can simply reopen; no data, secret or integrity impact, and the alternative (uncapped exponentiation) is a strictly worse main-thread DoS.\",\n  \"files\": [\n    \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/.claude/worktrees/agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f/packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts\",\n    \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/.claude/worktrees/agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f/packages/host-service/src/companion/remote-pairing.test.ts\",\n    \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/.claude/worktrees/agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f/packages/host-service/src/companion/http.ts\",\n    \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/.claude/worktrees/agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f/packages/host-service/src/companion/config.ts\"\n  ]\n}</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>55159</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>12</tool_uses><duration_ms>126670</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:24:28.077Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a719e11bc77d66746</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_jMHFgKi3PEKHHzI1jWd8mWnE</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a719e11bc77d66746.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify resettable rate cap\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>{\"verdict\":\"PLAUSIBLE\",\"reason\":\"Map semantics confirmed: iteration/keys() is insertion-ordered and set() on an existing key does not reorder, so chargePerSource (pairing.ts:1313-1317) evicts the oldest-INSERTED bucket — including a still-flooding source — and its counter restarts at 0. So both per-source ceilings (REMOTE_PAIR_REQUESTS_PER_SOURCE=32 via chargeRemoteRequest, MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE=4 via chargeKexIntroduction) are defeatable by cycling &gt;REMOTE_PAIR_MAX_SOURCES/MAX_KEX_SOURCES=32 distinct source keys. Mechanism real; impact overstated. sourceKey is Cloudflare's cf-connecting-ip, validated as a single IP literal and unforgeable through the tunnel (http.ts:738-744), so the refund needs &gt;=33 genuinely distinct client addresses — an attacker who already has 33 addresses already holds 33*32=1056 requests and 33*4=132 candidate introductions inside a 120 s window, far past the 16 introductions needed to churn the candidate table, so no new attack class is unlocked, only removal of the long-run ceiling. Crypto is NOT unbounded: new SRP derivations are gated by chargeKexComputation against MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW=64, explicitly monotone, never reset or evicted, charged immediately before srpServerHandshake (pairing.ts:239-255, 1057), with exact-retry short-circuit; MAC grinding is bounded per-window by confirmAttempts(5)/PAIRING_MAX_BAD_MACS(3); memory is bounded by MAX_KEX_SESSIONS=16 evict-with-zeroing. Log-spam half of the claim is inverted: the console.warn fires only on refusal at cap, so the refund produces FEWER warn lines, not unbounded ones. Residual exposure is cheap request handling (&lt;=4 KiB body, JSON parse, map lookups) inside a 120 s human-opened window, which the module header already declares an accepted limitation ('A distributed flood can still make this process busy'), and the refund itself is documented as deliberate at pairing.ts:1296-1299. Verified by reading pairing.ts (1275-1347, 210-363, 960-1140, 2270-2373) and http.ts (713-893); no independent global request counter exists, which is the one real gap, but it does not reach a shipping defect.\"}</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>51679</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>10</tool_uses><duration_ms>115915</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:24:28.077Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a5af19671d4d99163</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_AgRxAnAgMR9YOlslrQqo4rLH</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a5af19671d4d99163.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify spoofed source header\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"REJECT\",\n  \"severity\": \"none\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"file\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\\\packages\\\\host-service\\\\src\\\\companion\\\\http.ts\",\n  \"line\": 739,\n  \"reason\": \"Mechanism half-real, impact false. Real part: listener binds 127.0.0.1:47610 (config.ts:23-24, http.ts:2075), so any local process can connect directly, set Host=&lt;publicPairHost&gt; to hit routeByHost -&gt; kind:'pair' (http.ts:624-680), and forge cf-connecting-ip; the doc claim at http.ts:724-736 only asserts unforgeability THROUGH the tunnel (Cloudflare overwrites the header), which holds. No security delta: (1) the three pairing paths are unauthenticated by design and already reachable by any internet caller on the public pair host, so a forged local caller gains no route it does not already have; (2) every abuse the rotating source key unlocks sits under window-GLOBAL ceilings that the source key does not gate - PAIRING_MAX_BAD_MACS=3 on state.failedMacAttempts (pairing.ts:2342, config.ts:63), MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW=64 monotone (pairing.ts:255,1732), MAX_KEX_SESSIONS evicting rather than growing (pairing.ts:1330-1342); (3) burn specifically is NOT amplified - 3 wrong-code confirms from ONE source burn the window, well inside REMOTE_PAIR_REQUESTS_PER_SOURCE=32, so IP rotation buys nothing; (4) candidate ownership (assertCandidateSource, pairing.ts:983,1040,1214) is not bypassed by rotation - it refuses mismatched sources, and hijacking a real candidate needs the phone's deviceId, not an IP; (5) confidentiality is unaffected - the 8-digit code is only defeated by MACs, capped globally at 3; (6) main-host routes remain Access-JWT-validated with no loopback exemption (http.ts:606-612), so the loopback path yields no authenticated surface. Residual capability is a same-user local process DoSing pairing - a process that can already kill the host-service or read its memory, i.e. inside the trust boundary. At most a MINOR comment nit that the header's honesty is tunnel-conditional; not a defect.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>54374</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>16</tool_uses><duration_ms>140392</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:24:28.077Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a4e6ef69427e9e1db</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_chnJ79EcBVwaAOo8A1os6VZl</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a4e6ef69427e9e1db.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify envelope error mapping\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"CONFIRMED_MINOR\",\n  \"candidate\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\pairing\\\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:229-237\",\n  \"reason\": \"Mapping is provably a misdiagnosis, but damage is a wrong message plus an already-accepted orphan record, not corruption. Verified: (1) post() throws on any non-200, so openConfirmEnvelope is reached only after HTTP 200 (RemotePairingHandshake.kt:311-336). (2) A 200 confirm body is only produced after the desktop recomputed macPhone in constant time and matched it; a mismatch returns 401 pair_code_wrong and stores nothing (PROTOCOL.md:944-949 step 5; LAN reference implementation packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:665-672). macPhone derives from K_conf_p, which comes from the same PRK/S as K_dev, so MAC-verified =&gt; code correct =&gt; K_dev identical on both sides. Therefore an EnvelopeException at step 6 can never mean a wrong typed code; it means body/AAD corruption, an edge tampering with the sealed reply, or an implementation mismatch. The in-code rationale at :230-231 ('the only thing that varies between two honest parties is the typed code') is false at that point in the flow. (3) Server-side ordering confirmed: consumed=true then onPaired(persist) then seal (pairing.ts:678-753, header comment 604-614 states persist-before-seal is non-negotiable), and PROTOCOL.md:966 makes remote step 7 identical, so the phantom-record claim holds: window consumed, device record persisted, phone zeroizes deviceKey in finally (RemotePairingHandshake.kt:257-262). (4) No rollback or recovery exists anywhere: no unpair/abort call on the phone path, and the desktop cannot observe the phone's open failure. (5) User impact chain: PairingError.CODE_WRONG ('check the code, and check the window is still up', PROTOCOL.md:1132) tells the user to retype into a window that is already consumed; the retry then fails 410/404 -&gt; 'open a new pairing window'. Downgrade drivers: the orphan-record window is inherent and explicitly accepted by the protocol (pairing.ts:609-613, 'recoverable by re-pairing'), not created by this mapping; the record is removable via desktop revoke (PROTOCOL.md:1195-1202); and the mapping is deliberate and documented (app-phone/.../RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt:14-18 names 'an envelope that will not open' as CODE_WRONG). Residual real defect: no failure code tells the truth here - CODE_WRONG blames the code, and BAD_MAC's user text asserts 'nothing was stored', which is also false post-persist; a distinct failure ('the desktop may have recorded this device - remove it and pair again') is the correct fix. Correction to candidate wording: wrong envelope kind and wrong addressed deviceId throw PairingException, not EnvelopeException (RemotePairingHandshake.kt:278-283), so only header-parse/AEAD-tag/AAD failures and payload decode reach this catch.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>60866</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>19</tool_uses><duration_ms>148182</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:24:28.077Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a616a573b93e2247c</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_OwLwlrUlzy2ciWLPu5SYSSnW</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a616a573b93e2247c.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify orphan pairing handle\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>{\"verdict\":\"CONFIRMED\",\"reason\":\"Verified end-to-end. index.ts:1423 (openPairing) and :1451 (openRemotePairing) both clear `current.pairing = null` on a straight-line path BEFORE awaiting the open, with no try/catch/restore (lines 1415-1434, 1443-1463 read verbatim; 1417-1422 comment states the memo is dropped because pairing.ts is authoritative). pairing.ts is authoritative AND throws: `openPairingWindow` (pairing.ts:1951-1959) and `openRemotePairing` (pairing.ts:2138-2146) both do `if (openWindow) { console.warn(...); throw new Error(ONE_WINDOW_MESSAGE) }`, and `openWindow` is only cleared by each handle's own `close()` (pairing.ts:2033, 2212), by the 120s timer (2093, 2230), or by a successful/burned confirm (2336, 2348). Both bridge methods are exposed as tRPC mutations (companion.ts:454, 478) reachable by the renderer, so a second open (double-clicked QR button, or QR-then-code) is trivially reachable; the second call throws AFTER the memo has been nulled. `current.pairing` is the ONLY reference index.ts holds (all `.pairing` sites: 1142, 1423, 1425, 1451, 1455, 1466, 1468, 1476), so after the refusal: closePairing (1464-1479) sees `open === null`, returns false and closes nothing while the LAN window is still live and 0.0.0.0:47611 still bound; and the stop() teardown step `close: () =&gt; state?.pairing?.close()` (index.ts:1142) also no-ops, leaving a listening pairing socket plus its timer alive past bridge stop, and pairing.ts's module-level `openWindow` non-null so a restarted bridge's first openPairing also refuses until the 120s timer fires. companion.ts's `trackedPairingWindow` (companion.ts:199, 422, 461, 482, 498) is read-only in pairingState and never closes the handle, so it is not a mitigating path. Nothing catches, retries, or restores the memo; nothing else can close the window early.\"}</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>91911</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>24</tool_uses><duration_ms>161030</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:24:28.077Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a5885efb646cc54d0</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_ThB4ASSGgoQkY7R40LSx6dNG</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a5885efb646cc54d0.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify candidate eviction failure\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"PLAUSIBLE\",\n  \"reason\": \"Server-side race is real and reachable: C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\\\packages\\\\host-service\\\\src\\\\companion\\\\pairing.ts:1329 evicts strictly oldest createdAtMs, and handleRemoteKex (line 1039) -&gt; requireRemoteCandidate (1111) returns 400 pair_wrong_peer for an evicted deviceId. Bounds verified: MAX_KEX_SESSIONS=16 (line 228), MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE=4 distinct deviceIds per source per window (236), MAX_KEX_SOURCES=32 (238); sourceKey = cf-connecting-ip literal, unforgeable through the tunnel but one per client IP (http.ts:738). Phone's candidate is NEWEST after its begin, so evicting it needs 16 fresh introductions after begin, i.e. &gt;=4 distinct attacker IPs (trivial on IPv6) landing inside the sub-second begin-&gt;kex gap on a known public pairing host. Cost is DoS of one 120s window only; no key/code exposure. Server explicitly permits recovery: the failed kex charges noteUnattributedAttempt (1794) which is log/counter only - no confirm budget, no MAC strike, no window burn - and a re-begin with the same deviceId is accepted, charging one of the phone IP's 4 introductions. So the code comment's claim 'can simply begin again' is correct AT THE SERVER. The escalation to terminal failure rests entirely on the Android client being linear with no re-begin on pair_wrong_peer, and that is UNVERIFIABLE here: no RemotePairingHandshake.kt and zero .kt files exist anywhere in the worktree (companion client lives outside this repo); no test covers eviction (remote-pairing.test.ts has no 'evict' case). Verdict stays PLAUSIBLE, not confirmed: mechanism proven, client terminal-failure and severity unproven.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>53478</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>17</tool_uses><duration_ms>192645</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:24:28.077Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a05dfe32a356d7ed9</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_slJJRba4Ej3OplXUlCiOQk1t</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a05dfe32a356d7ed9.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify mobile source pin\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"candidate\": \"packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:1215 — assertCandidateSource pins a pairing candidate to its first CF-Connecting-IP\",\n  \"verdict\": \"REJECT\",\n  \"severity_if_confirmed\": \"MINOR\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.82,\n  \"reason\": \"Server behavior verified: sourceKey = single well-formed cf-connecting-ip (http.ts:738-744), and the pin is enforced at all three remote steps — begin (pairing.ts:983), kex (pairing.ts:1040), confirm (pairing.ts:1478) — with no re-pin, grace, or fallback; a source change throws PairingError(400,'pair_wrong_peer') via refuseCandidateRewrite (pairing.ts:1197-1202). But the impact claim is overstated and partly unverifiable. (1) Exposure is bounded by PAIRING_WINDOW_MS = 120_000 (config.ts:62) — begin/kex/confirm are a single short human-driven burst, and a phone's CF-visible public IP (Wi-Fi NAT, CGNAT binding, IPv6 temporary address) is stable far longer than ~1-2 minutes in the ordinary case; a mid-window handoff requires the user to physically drop Wi-Fi while standing at the desktop pairing UI, which is not the default realistic state. (2) Failure is not data-damaging and not window-destroying: refuseCandidateRewrite charges nothing — it is outside the 3-strike MAC budget (only requireRemoteCandidate calls noteUnattributedAttempt) and outside the confirm budget, so no burn, no key wipe; recovery is a retry (fresh deviceId → fresh candidate under the new source's own full per-source allowance, chargePerSource is per-key) or reopening the 120 s window. (3) The tradeoff is explicitly documented and deliberate (pairing.ts:1204-1213): without the pin, anyone who learns the cleartext deviceId can spend another device's confirm attempts, burn the window on wrong MACs, or race begin with rewritten metadata on an unauthenticated public endpoint — a real DoS against a window a human is standing in front of. (4) The client-side half of the claim could not be verified: no RemotePairingHandshake.kt, and no Kotlin sources at all, exist in this repo/worktree (glob + rg over *.kt found nothing), so 'OkHttp connection reuse', 'no retry', and 'terminal failure' are unsubstantiated — the phone may well re-begin with a fresh deviceId, which succeeds. Net: correct, intentional, bounded, recoverable hardening with an unproven client-side failure story — a MINOR robustness note at most (e.g. allow re-pin on an otherwise-identical candidate before any MAC is charged), not a BLOCKER/MAJOR finding.\",\n  \"verified_by\": [\n    \"packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:970-1003 (begin pin)\",\n    \"packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:1029-1055 (kex pin)\",\n    \"packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:1466-1490 (confirm pin)\",\n    \"packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:1197-1218 (refusal path, no budget charge)\",\n    \"packages/host-service/src/companion/http.ts:721-833 (source derivation, single value, refuse-on-absence)\",\n    \"packages/host-service/src/companion/config.ts:60-63 (120 s window, 3 bad MACs)\",\n    \"packages/host-service/src/companion/remote-pairing.test.ts (no test asserts a mid-flow source change; pin is untested but documented)\"\n  ],\n  \"unverifiable\": [\n    \"companion RemotePairingHandshake.kt absent from this checkout — OkHttp connection reuse, retry policy, and deviceId stability/rotation across attempts could not be read\"\n  ]\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>52916</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>21</tool_uses><duration_ms>242687</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:24:28.077Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a8198e5b2bc6984f6</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_4u41023N4H8t0wpeWhfVw2t7</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a8198e5b2bc6984f6.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify pairing slot race\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"CONFIRMED\",\n  \"reason\": \"Real check-then-claim TOCTOU on the process-wide slot. pairing.ts:1954 tests `openWindow` (LAN), then yields at the `await` over `server.listen` (2077-2091), and claims the slot UNCONDITIONALLY at 2097 (`openWindow = { handle, state, deps }`) with no re-check and no reservation. `openRemotePairing` (2138-2244) has zero awaits between its own guard at 2141 and its claim at 2234 (mintRemotePairingCode/randomBytes/srpComputeX/srpComputeVerifier are all sync), so once its body starts it wins the slot atomically. Nothing serializes the two on the server: bridge index.ts:1415-1463 has no mutex (its comment at 1417-1422 explicitly delegates the one-window rule to pairing.ts, and it even clears `current.pairing = null` at 1423 BEFORE awaiting), and companion.ts:454-485 exposes both as plain concurrent protectedProcedure mutations. Same-tick concurrency is reachable: the renderer uses httpBatchStreamLink (host-service-client.ts:29, 'same-tick calls share one HTTP request') and tRPC executes batched calls concurrently, so a batch containing openPairing+openRemotePairing starts both in one tick — LAN suspends on listen (nextTick, bind host is the literal '0.0.0.0', config.ts:26, so no DNS but still a yield) while remote runs its guard+claim to completion. Damage matches the candidate: LAN overwrites the remote entry, currentRemotePairing() (2302-2308) reads `openWindow` and returns null for a lan-kind slot, so the 8 digits already returned to the dialog count down while /v1/pair/* 404s; the orphaned remote handle's close() no-ops on the slot (2212 `openWindow?.handle === handle` is false) and lives until its 120s timer; and cancellation targets the wrong window — both bridge `current.pairing` (1425 after 1455) and router `trackedPairingWindow` (461 after 482) end holding the LAN handle, so closePairing (index.ts:1464-1479) tears down LAN and leaves the remote state up. Only mitigation found is renderer-side, not a guard: the module-level promise chain `pairingOperations`/`serialize` in PairDeviceDialog.tsx:194-204 + the awaited closePairing at 249, which serializes only within one renderer process and is bypassed by any second local tRPC client or a crafted batch — a client-side lock protecting a documented process-wide security invariant (1867-1882). Fix: claim the slot (or a reservation marker) synchronously before the listen await, and release it on the error path.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>64726</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>18</tool_uses><duration_ms>247356</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
