URLs come from a fixed origin constant vs invitation.baseUrl; the error mappers encode different taxonomies (remote adds ACCESS_DENIED/EDGE_UNVERIFIABLE wire codes and 403/429/503, defaults BAD_MAC, classifies MAC failures as CODE_WRONG because the secret is typed digits; LAN defaults UNREACHABLE with Wi-Fi-specific wording); AAD path constants differ; PairingClient is store-coupled while RemotePairingHandshake returns an AutoCloseable result consumed by pairWithCode (PairingClient.kt:218-241). Factoring these together needs ~5 injected knobs (client config, base URL, path, failure mapper, opener failure enum) to save roughly 30 lines, and would couple the security-critical TLS-pinned remote path to the intentionally cleartext LAN path. (3) No concrete current effect and no demonstrated future effect beyond generic duplication aesthetics; the only asserted behavioural consequence is disproven. Not a defect.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>43989</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>20</tool_uses><duration_ms>213424</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
