{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:33:11.507Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a83dd3475ea376ad7</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a83dd3475ea376ad7.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify schema duplication mismatch\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"REJECT\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"reason\": \"Premise is factually wrong and the residual point is cost-free. (1) No `/v1/pair/*` route exists. C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/packages/host-service/src/companion/http.ts:820-918 `ROUTES: Record&lt;SealedPath, SealedRoute&gt;` contains only /v1/session/hello, /v1/tree, /v1/transcript, /v1/question, /v1/answer, /v1/answer/status, /v1/message, /v1/device/register, /v1/heartbeat, /v1/panic, /v1/events/ticket. Line 851 cited by the candidate is `handler: (h, ctx, body) =&gt; h.question(ctx, body as QuestionRequest)` — the /v1/question entry, not pairing. (2) The `body as XRequest` casts are not raw-JSON casts bypassing zod: every entry carries `schema` validated at the boundary before dispatch (parseBody + per-route zod, `satisfies z.ZodType&lt;XRequest&gt;` gives compile-time schema/type agreement), and the table's doc comment states the cast happens HERE and nowhere else by design. (3) Pairing is intentionally a separate host: http.ts:4-5 documents the sealed server binds 127.0.0.1:47610 while pairing.ts is a different LAN socket (47611, 120 s window, cleartext JSON) with paths `/pair/kex` + `/pair/confirm` (pairing.ts:162-163); manual assert helpers there (assertLabel/assertSurface/assertAppVersion/assertProtocolRange) are deliberate for the unencrypted pre-key hop, and http.ts:599-611 explicitly documents keeping caps aligned with pairing.ts/limits.ts. (4) The boundary asymmetry is real but has no observable cost: pairing.ts:922-937 rejects max &gt; 0xff, while http.ts:590-594 protocolRangeSchema bounds only min&gt;=0 and min&lt;=max — but read-api.ts:592-595 clamps `hi = Math.min(clientRange.max, BRIDGE_PROTOCOL_RANGE.max)` and yields `protocol: ProtocolVersion = degraded ? 0 : hi`, so an oversized client max negotiates to the bridge max; no byte overflow into the AAD, no state stored from the hello range, no error path difference. Worst case is a redundant unbounded integer in a schema whose value is immediately clamped — MINOR-style consistency nit at best, not the claimed validation bypass.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>40902</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>23</tool_uses><duration_ms>171398</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:33:11.507Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a35f008ee66deda18</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a35f008ee66deda18.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify transport duplication drift\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"REFUTED\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"reason\": \"Cited evidence is factually wrong, and the residual claim is pure preference. (1) The differentiator the candidate relies on does not exist: RemotePairingHandshake.openConfirmEnvelope (RemotePairingHandshake.kt:277) calls EnvelopeHeader.parse, and EnvelopeHeader.parse (crypto/SealedEnvelope.kt:68-73) itself throws EnvelopeException when body.size &lt; CompanionConstants.MIN_SEALED_BODY_BYTES; SealedEnvelope.open re-checks it at line 239. So the remote opener does reject short bodies, and RemotePairingHandshake.kt:227-237 maps that EnvelopeException to PairingException(CODE_WRONG). PairingClient.kt:257 is a redundant pre-check that only changes the thrown failure enum/message, not acceptance. No drift, no missed guard, no security or correctness gap. (2) The remaining overlap is deliberate divergence, not copy-paste drift: remote transport pins TLS via connectionSpecsFor(origin) and hard-refuses non-TLS origins (RemotePairingHandshake.kt:415-433), sets followSslRedirects(false) + retryOnConnectionFailure(false) and constant-driven timeouts, vs PairingClient's CLEARTEXT LAN client with hardcoded 5s/10s (PairingClient.kt:57-63); URLs come from a fixed origin constant vs invitation.baseUrl; the error mappers encode different taxonomies (remote adds ACCESS_DENIED/EDGE_UNVERIFIABLE wire codes and 403/429/503, defaults BAD_MAC, classifies MAC failures as CODE_WRONG because the secret is typed digits; LAN defaults UNREACHABLE with Wi-Fi-specific wording); AAD path constants differ; PairingClient is store-coupled while RemotePairingHandshake returns an AutoCloseable result consumed by pairWithCode (PairingClient.kt:218-241). Factoring these together needs ~5 injected knobs (client config, base URL, path, failure mapper, opener failure enum) to save roughly 30 lines, and would couple the security-critical TLS-pinned remote path to the intentionally cleartext LAN path. (3) No concrete current effect and no demonstrated future effect beyond generic duplication aesthetics; the only asserted behavioural consequence is disproven. Not a defect.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>43989</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>20</tool_uses><duration_ms>213424</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
