xposure: run() takes codeDigits as an ARGUMENT (:110-116) — the user types the code BEFORE begin — so begin (:131), kex (:170) and confirm (:212) fire back-to-back with only local SRP compute between them (Srp.startClient :163-166, session.agree :190). Real exposure to a source change is ~1-3 s, not the window's 120 s. (2) NOT terminal: deviceIdBytes = Ids.randomDeviceIdBytes() is minted FRESH per run() (:123), so the deviceId is per-attempt, not a stable install identity. A retap re-runs with a brand-new deviceId → brand-new candidate → the pin cannot cause a persistent lockout, and the new source IP gets its own full per-source allowance (chargePerSource is keyed per address, pairing.ts:1301-1318). (3) The refusal costs nothing recoverable: confirm's assertCandidateSource (:1478) runs BEFORE chargeConfirmAttempt (:1493) and before the MAC compare/burnMac, and kex's (:1040) runs before chargeKexComputation (:1057) — so a roam-refused attempt spends no confirm attempt, no bad-MAC strike, and no modexp; the window is not burned and the same code stays valid. Client retry behavior confirmed as claimed but harmless: retryOnConnectionFailure(false) (:97), one execute() per step (:319), non-200 → throw (:324) aborting run(); a mid-handshake handoff that kills the pooled connection surfaces as IOException → PairingFailure.UNREACHABLE (:328-335), not WRONG_PEER. Reaching pair_wrong_peer requires the pooled connection to die AND a new one to be established from a different CF-visible address inside a ~1-3 s non-interactive burst — a narrow race, not 'ordinary Wi-Fi/cellular handoff'. Finally the pin is a documented deliberate tradeoff (pairing.ts:1204-1213): without it, anyone who learns the cleartext deviceId can drive another device's candidate on an unauthenticated public endpoint. Residual real-but-small issue: the WRONG_PEER user string 'the desktop does not recognise this exchange' (:362) gives the roamed user no retry instruction — MINOR UX wording, not the claimed BLOCKER/MAJOR data/production defect.\",\n  \"verified_by\": [\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\pairing\\\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:110-124 (code supplied up front; fresh random deviceId per run)\",\n    \"...RemotePairingHandshake.kt:131-224 (single linear begin→kex→confirm, no re-begin, no in-flow retry)\",\n    \"...RemotePairingHandshake.kt:86-100 (single OkHttpClient, pooled/keep-alive, retryOnConnectionFailure(false))\",\n    \"...RemotePairingHandshake.kt:311-347 + 376-407 (error map: pair_wrong_peer → WRONG_PEER, IOException → UNREACHABLE, terminal throw)\",\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\\\packages\\\\host-service\\\\src\\\\companion\\\\pairing.ts:983,1040,1057,1197-1218,1466-1493 (pin placement precedes every budget charge)\",\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\\\packages\\\\host-service\\\\src\\\\companion\\\\http.ts:721-833 (source derivation, refuse-on-absence, per-request admit)\",\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\\\packages\\\\host-service\\\\src\\\\companion\\\\config.ts:60-63 (PAIRING_WINDOW_MS 120_000, PAIRING_MAX_BAD_MACS 3)\"\n  ],\n  \"unverifiable\": [\n    \"Whether the companion UI auto-retries run() on WRONG_PEER or requires a user retap — caller/ViewModel not read; either way recovery succeeds because deviceId is re-minted per run()\"\n  ]\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>52663</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>22</tool_uses><duration_ms>890871</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:34:58.180Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ab1d1323403c42aac</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_hweeI3aC9Mgh7KvlH7DY2pYr</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\ab1d1323403c42aac.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify stale burn memo\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"REJECT\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"reason\": \"Facts confirmed in worktree file C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/.claude/worktrees/agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f/packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts: remoteBurnMemo declared L1907, set only at L2343 (three bad MACs during remote confirm), cleared only at L2237 (openRemotePairing) or by its own 60s TTL at L1918-1919; openPairingWindow (LAN/QR, L1951) does not touch it. http.ts L819-824 returns 403 pair_code_burned only when currentRemotePairing() is null AND memo live. So the mechanical claim is accurate, but it is not a defect: a QR window serves the LAN flow (separate endpoints/port), never the remote-code endpoints, so while a QR window is open there is genuinely no remote window for a remote-code phone to reach. 403 'burned, ask for a new code' is the correct instruction in that state — the user must open a new REMOTE window; a QR window does not mint a code. Clearing the memo on QR open would regress the documented intent (L1891-1902: distinguish burned from never-existed) by degrading a genuinely burned window to a bare 404. No fresh remote window can be poisoned (openRemotePairing clears memo before returning, and the 403 branch is unreachable while endpoint is non-null). Memo holds no code/secret, self-forgets in 60s, and cannot persist a pairing record. Damage: none; worst case a strictly-more-informative status for &lt;=60s on a flow that is unavailable anyway. Recall-biased re-check found no adjacent real bug (no stale 403 on a live window, no leak, no unbounded retention). Cosmetic at most, below MINOR.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>40558</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>16</tool_uses><duration_ms>357989</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
