680) and classifyRequestHost (http.ts:624-633) gate solely on the Host header vs PAIRING_PUBLIC_HOST/BRIDGE_PUBLIC_HOST, handleRemotePairRequest's own documented gate order (http.ts:766-779) is host -&gt; CF-Connecting-IP -&gt; open window -&gt; rate, with no JWT/aud check anywhere, and loadPublicPairHost (config.ts:817-826) reads only public-pair-host.json's `host` field. Same header also says 'TWO PATHS' and names only /v1/pair/kex and /v1/pair/confirm while REMOTE_PAIR_PATHS (http.ts:545-549) has three, including /v1/pair/begin. index.ts:254 doc claims openRemotePairing throws 'when the pairing-scoped Cloudflare Access application is not configured'; the implementation (index.ts:1443-1449) throws only when state.publicPairHost is null, with an error message about DNS/tunnel ingress and public-pair-host.json — no Access application. Concrete cost is real, not stylistic: the file-head comment is the entry point a future security review reads, and it asserts an authentication control on the ONLY unauthenticated internet-reachable surface in the bridge, inviting a reviewer or later editor to assume token-based protection that does not exist and to weaken the host isolation / SRP / window gates that actually carry the boundary. Fix: rewrite both blocks to describe the real controls (dedicated public pairing hostname, three POST-only paths, SRP-6a PAKE, CF-Connecting-IP per-source caps, bare 404 outside an operator-opened window) and restate the index.ts throw condition as missing public-pair-host.json / unconfigured public pairing hostname.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>69388</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>25</tool_uses><duration_ms>371542</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
