airing\\\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:110-263): confirmed strictly linear, one begin/kex/confirm, OkHttp retryOnConnectionFailure(false), no re-begin; non-200 -&gt; remoteFailureOf:339 -&gt; FAILURE_BY_WIRE maps pair_wrong_peer -&gt; PairingFailure.WRONG_PEER, thrown out of run(). BridgeCompanionRepository.pairFromCode:948-988 has no retry loop: PairingException -&gt; PairingState.Failed(remotePairingError(...)) (RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt:30 -&gt; PairingError.WRONG_PEER). So 'no automatic re-begin' is CONFIRMED. But 'terminally fails' is REFUTED: PairingScreen.kt:173-192 renders Failed on the CODE route with a 'Try again' button that clears digits and calls onRetry(), and each pairFromCode mints a fresh deviceId (RemotePairingHandshake.kt:123), so a user-driven retry is a clean new begin - allowed up to the phone IP's 4 introductions per 120s window, after which its own source gets 429 pair_rate_limited. Residual real defect is UX/diagnostic, not availability-critical: PairingState.kt:117 shows WRONG_PEER as 'Something else answered instead of the desktop. Refused.' - an on-path-attacker accusation for a benign eviction, which is the wrong instruction to the user (correct advice is 'try again'), and repeated evictions can burn the phone's 4 begins and close the window. No key, code or secret exposure on this path. Net: mechanism verified, client no-auto-retry verified, terminal-failure claim wrong, impact = timing-precise multi-IP DoS of one 120s window plus misleading error copy. MINOR.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>97082</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>25</tool_uses><duration_ms>933278</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
