alt and never reads a deadline, and nothing else in the exchange feeds a corrected deadline back — a stale window surfaces only as an HTTP failure at the moment the phone posts (PairingFailure.WINDOW_CLOSED, RemotePairingHandshake.kt:358). withTimeout half also confirmed: BridgeCompanionRepository.kt:961 wraps pairWithCode in withTimeout(PAIRING_WINDOW_MS) started at submit, so its backstop likewise over-runs the server window by the scan-to-submit offset. Correction to the candidate's severity/framing: (a) the countdown renders ONLY on the typed-code route (PairingScreen.kt:205, entry == CODE), not the QR route; (b) the skew is a misleading label, not a functional break — the server still refuses on time and the UI shows PairingError.WINDOW_CLOSED, so no wrong pairing is accepted and no state is corrupted; (c) the divergence is deliberate and documented in-place (PairingScreen.kt:115-118: 'the phone cannot see that moment ... it counts its own deadline'), so it is a knowingly accepted UX gap, not an oversight. Real defect worth reporting: the string asserts server-window semantics the number does not have. Closing it requires the begin response to carry expiresAtMs (or remainingMs) — a wire/protocol change on both sides — or the label to be reworded to a client-side timeout.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>55640</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>30</tool_uses><duration_ms>408308</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
