alves are still two separate functions — derivePairingKeys (L711, IKM = Z || pairingCode) and deriveSrpPairingKeys (L754, IKM = PAD(S)) — and the TRANSCRIPTS are deliberately two builders (buildPairingTranscript L584, buildSrpPairingTranscript L622) with the rationale written in: widening would relax the fixed-width assertions that make separator-free concatenation safe, and the prefix is hardcoded 'so the domain separation between the two flows is a property of these two functions, not of whoever calls them'. So the Kotlin transcript duplication MIRRORS a deliberate desktop decision, not a divergence; the only genuine asymmetry is the 3-expand + salt block (~8 lines, one extra edit site). DAMAGE CLAIM FAILS: (a) both key schedules are wire-frozen protocol (v1 pinned by golden vectors, v3-srp a distinct domain), so a schedule change is a cross-repo, cross-spec version change that a Kotlin-local helper could not have kept in sync with TypeScript anyway; (b) the flows cannot be conflated — every constant is prefixed v1 vs v3-srp, giving full domain separation, and the SRP path has a different IKM and different required sizes (SRP_GROUP_BYTES 384 vs X25519 32), so a shared Kotlin function would need the same branch desktop keeps; (c) 'silently break interoperability' is wrong — drift surfaces as a failed key confirmation (pair_bad_mac / pair_code_wrong) plus explicit size checks (requireSize, PAIRING_TRANSCRIPT_BYTES 117 / REMOTE_PAIRING_TRANSCRIPT_BYTES 825, both asserted). NO CURRENT EFFECT: every constant matches the desktop byte-for-byte — 'superset-companion/pair/v1'/'superset-companion/pair/v3-srp' (config.ts L64/L130-131), 'sc/v1 device '/'sc/v3-srp device ' (L65/L134), confirm-phone/confirm-desktop pairs (L66-67/L135-136), transcript prefixes 'sc/v1'/'sc/v3-srp' (L71/L132), and 825 = 9 + 816 matches SRP_TRANSCRIPT_BODY_BYTES (pairing.ts L216). Net: a real but small maintenance cost (one duplicated 8-line expand block on the client), correctly reported as MINOR/