ean an unnamed rejection') and RemotePairingHandshakeTest.kt:368-403, which asserts 400 {\\\"code\\\":\\\"unknown\\\"}, 500 {\\\"code\\\":\\\"unknown\\\"} and an unknown future code all land on BAD_MAC for forward compatibility. The LAN asymmetry is also intentional, not an oversight: PairingClient.kt:384-398 falls back to UNREACHABLE, and RemotePairingErrorMappingTest.kt:55-61 documents that the LAN 'join the same Wi-Fi' advice is wrong on the remote route. At most a MINOR naming nit (enum reuse of BAD_MAC for 'unnamed rejection'), not a defect.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>86174</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>34</tool_uses><duration_ms>412151</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:35:54.488Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a0770e41a4e692136</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_SNR2aXQyq7H6sT6a3EckQqzZ</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a0770e41a4e692136.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify burn status oracle\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"REJECT\",\n  \"candidate\": \"http.ts:821 (worktree agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f) — 60s unauthenticated, uncharged 403 pair_code_burned vs 404 burn oracle\",\n  \"premise_confirmed\": true,\n  \"reason\": \"Re-verified in C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/.claude/worktrees/agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f/packages/host-service/src/companion/. The mechanism exists exactly as described — http.ts:819-825 does `const endpoint = (deps.lookup ?? currentRemotePairing)(); if (endpoint === null) { if ((deps.burned ?? remoteWindowWasBurned)()) return pairingErrorResponse(403, \\\"pair_code_burned\\\"); return notFound(); }`, i.e. before `endpoint.admit(sourceKey)` (line 832), before `readBoundedBody` (835), on the unauthenticated public pairing host (no Access JWT; identity is CF-Connecting-IP only), so the branch is genuinely uncharged and unauthenticated for PAIRING_REMOTE_BURN_MEMO_MS = 60_000 (config.ts:144). But it is not a defect: (1) INTENDED AND DOCUMENTED — pairing.ts:1891-1923 states the memo's purpose (tell a user who mistyped three times WHY, instead of a bare 404), it is process-global, holds only `forgetAtMs` (no code, no verifier — both zeroed at burn, pairing.ts:2223-2227), self-expires on read, and is cleared when a new window opens (pairing.ts:2237); rememberRemoteBurn() fires only on the remote confirm path at failedMacAttempts &gt;= PAIRING_MAX_BAD_MACS (pairing.ts:2342-2343). (2) NO INCREMENTAL SECRET — the same unauthenticated audience already learns far more from the open-window state: during the 120s window POST /v1/pair/begin returns 200 with salt/B. Learning 'a window burned in the last 60s' after it is gone grants no grinding surface: window closed, code zeroed, verifier discarded, so no guess can be spent. (3) NO RESOURCE GAP — the 403 path and the 404 path are both O(1), pre-body, allocation-free; the 403 costs a boolean read of a single module-level object, so polling it is exactly as cheap as polling for the catch-all 404. No amplification, no table growth (unlike admit(), which is why the charge sits behind a live window). (4) NO CACHE ISSUE — pairingErrorResponse routes through pairingJsonResponse (http.ts:698-714) which sets `cache-control: no-store`; a 403 with no-store is not stored by Cloudflare or the phone, so the memo cannot be replayed past its deadline. Only observation worth passing back is cosmetic doc drift, not a finding: the comment at pairing.ts:1898-1899 and config.ts:141 say the memo 'holds the pairing REFERENCE and a deadline', but `interface RemoteBurnMemo` (pairing.ts:1903-1905) holds only `forgetAtMs` — the code leaks strictly less than the comment claims.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>63325</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>26</tool_uses><duration_ms>435175</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
