{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:39:44.355Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a05dfe32a356d7ed9</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_b1SeyGb3xAb0lvtZimXmNF4o</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a05dfe32a356d7ed9.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify mobile source pin\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"candidate\": \"packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:1215 — assertCandidateSource pins a remote pairing candidate to its first CF-Connecting-IP for begin/kex/confirm\",\n  \"verdict\": \"REJECT\",\n  \"severity_if_confirmed\": \"MINOR\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.93,\n  \"recall_check\": \"Re-verified against the exact companion client the first pass could not locate. The client evidence weakens the claim rather than confirming it; no additional defect surfaced that would flip this to CONFIRM.\",\n  \"reason\": \"Server pin is real and enforced at all three stages — begin C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\\\packages\\\\host-service\\\\src\\\\companion\\\\pairing.ts:983, kex :1040, confirm :1478, all routing to refuseCandidateRewrite (:1197-1202) → 400 pair_wrong_peer, with sourceKey = single well-formed cf-connecting-ip (http.ts:738-744). Three client facts read from C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\pairing\\\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt kill the impact claim. (1) NOT a 120 s human-paced exposure: run() takes codeDigits as an ARGUMENT (:110-116) — the user types the code BEFORE begin — so begin (:131), kex (:170) and confirm (:212) fire back-to-back with only local SRP compute between them (Srp.startClient :163-166, session.agree :190). Real exposure to a source change is ~1-3 s, not the window's 120 s. (2) NOT terminal: deviceIdBytes = Ids.randomDeviceIdBytes() is minted FRESH per run() (:123), so the deviceId is per-attempt, not a stable install identity. A retap re-runs with a brand-new deviceId → brand-new candidate → the pin cannot cause a persistent lockout, and the new source IP gets its own full per-source allowance (chargePerSource is keyed per address, pairing.ts:1301-1318). (3) The refusal costs nothing recoverable: confirm's assertCandidateSource (:1478) runs BEFORE chargeConfirmAttempt (:1493) and before the MAC compare/burnMac, and kex's (:1040) runs before chargeKexComputation (:1057) — so a roam-refused attempt spends no confirm attempt, no bad-MAC strike, and no modexp; the window is not burned and the same code stays valid. Client retry behavior confirmed as claimed but harmless: retryOnConnectionFailure(false) (:97), one execute() per step (:319), non-200 → throw (:324) aborting run(); a mid-handshake handoff that kills the pooled connection surfaces as IOException → PairingFailure.UNREACHABLE (:328-335), not WRONG_PEER. Reaching pair_wrong_peer requires the pooled connection to die AND a new one to be established from a different CF-visible address inside a ~1-3 s non-interactive burst — a narrow race, not 'ordinary Wi-Fi/cellular handoff'. Finally the pin is a documented deliberate tradeoff (pairing.ts:1204-1213): without it, anyone who learns the cleartext deviceId can drive another device's candidate on an unauthenticated public endpoint. Residual real-but-small issue: the WRONG_PEER user string 'the desktop does not recognise this exchange' (:362) gives the roamed user no retry instruction — MINOR UX wording, not the claimed BLOCKER/MAJOR data/production defect.\",\n  \"verified_by\": [\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\pairing\\\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:110-124 (code supplied up front; fresh random deviceId per run)\",\n    \"...RemotePairingHandshake.kt:131-224 (single linear begin→kex→confirm, no re-begin, no in-flow retry)\",\n    \"...RemotePairingHandshake.kt:86-100 (single OkHttpClient, pooled/keep-alive, retryOnConnectionFailure(false))\",\n    \"...RemotePairingHandshake.kt:311-347 + 376-407 (error map: pair_wrong_peer → WRONG_PEER, IOException → UNREACHABLE, terminal throw)\",\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\\\packages\\\\host-service\\\\src\\\\companion\\\\pairing.ts:983,1040,1057,1197-1218,1466-1493 (pin placement precedes every budget charge)\",\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\\\packages\\\\host-service\\\\src\\\\companion\\\\http.ts:721-833 (source derivation, refuse-on-absence, per-request admit)\",\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\\\packages\\\\host-service\\\\src\\\\companion\\\\config.ts:60-63 (PAIRING_WINDOW_MS 120_000, PAIRING_MAX_BAD_MACS 3)\"\n  ],\n  \"unverifiable\": [\n    \"Whether the companion UI auto-retries run() on WRONG_PEER or requires a user retap — caller/ViewModel not read; either way recovery succeeds because deviceId is re-minted per run()\"\n  ]\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>52663</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>22</tool_uses><duration_ms>890871</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:39:44.355Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ab1d1323403c42aac</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_hweeI3aC9Mgh7KvlH7DY2pYr</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\ab1d1323403c42aac.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify stale burn memo\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"REJECT\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"reason\": \"Facts confirmed in worktree file C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/.claude/worktrees/agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f/packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts: remoteBurnMemo declared L1907, set only at L2343 (three bad MACs during remote confirm), cleared only at L2237 (openRemotePairing) or by its own 60s TTL at L1918-1919; openPairingWindow (LAN/QR, L1951) does not touch it. http.ts L819-824 returns 403 pair_code_burned only when currentRemotePairing() is null AND memo live. So the mechanical claim is accurate, but it is not a defect: a QR window serves the LAN flow (separate endpoints/port), never the remote-code endpoints, so while a QR window is open there is genuinely no remote window for a remote-code phone to reach. 403 'burned, ask for a new code' is the correct instruction in that state — the user must open a new REMOTE window; a QR window does not mint a code. Clearing the memo on QR open would regress the documented intent (L1891-1902: distinguish burned from never-existed) by degrading a genuinely burned window to a bare 404. No fresh remote window can be poisoned (openRemotePairing clears memo before returning, and the 403 branch is unreachable while endpoint is non-null). Memo holds no code/secret, self-forgets in 60s, and cannot persist a pairing record. Damage: none; worst case a strictly-more-informative status for &lt;=60s on a flow that is unavailable anyway. Recall-biased re-check found no adjacent real bug (no stale 403 on a live window, no leak, no unbounded retention). Cosmetic at most, below MINOR.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>40558</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>16</tool_uses><duration_ms>357989</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:39:44.355Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a9c76f52ff17e2261</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_CqqYqtT3rGPLtPX3xbp7swpC</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a9c76f52ff17e2261.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify stale access claims\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"CONFIRMED\",\n  \"severity\": \"MINOR\",\n  \"in_scope\": true,\n  \"files\": [\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\\\packages\\\\host-service\\\\src\\\\companion\\\\http.ts:35-41\",\n    \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\\\packages\\\\host-service\\\\src\\\\companion\\\\index.ts:244-254\"\n  ],\n  \"reason\": \"Both comment blocks are ADDED lines in C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Temp\\\\secure-pairing-desktop.diff (diff lines 1943-1949 and 3349-3358, all '+'), so they are in scope and normative-by-placement. Both are factually false against the implementation in the same diff. http.ts header claims the pairing paths 'accept ONLY the second, pairing-scoped Access identity (and every other path accepts only the main one — fail closed in both directions)'. No such identity exists: config.ts:171-188 states outright the second Cloudflare Access application was REMOVED ('So the token is gone and the host is public'), replaced by SRP-6a plus a two-host split; routeByHost (http.ts:652-680) and classifyRequestHost (http.ts:624-633) gate solely on the Host header vs PAIRING_PUBLIC_HOST/BRIDGE_PUBLIC_HOST, handleRemotePairRequest's own documented gate order (http.ts:766-779) is host -&gt; CF-Connecting-IP -&gt; open window -&gt; rate, with no JWT/aud check anywhere, and loadPublicPairHost (config.ts:817-826) reads only public-pair-host.json's `host` field. Same header also says 'TWO PATHS' and names only /v1/pair/kex and /v1/pair/confirm while REMOTE_PAIR_PATHS (http.ts:545-549) has three, including /v1/pair/begin. index.ts:254 doc claims openRemotePairing throws 'when the pairing-scoped Cloudflare Access application is not configured'; the implementation (index.ts:1443-1449) throws only when state.publicPairHost is null, with an error message about DNS/tunnel ingress and public-pair-host.json — no Access application. Concrete cost is real, not stylistic: the file-head comment is the entry point a future security review reads, and it asserts an authentication control on the ONLY unauthenticated internet-reachable surface in the bridge, inviting a reviewer or later editor to assume token-based protection that does not exist and to weaken the host isolation / SRP / window gates that actually carry the boundary. Fix: rewrite both blocks to describe the real controls (dedicated public pairing hostname, three POST-only paths, SRP-6a PAKE, CF-Connecting-IP per-source caps, bare 404 outside an operator-opened window) and restate the index.ts throw condition as missing public-pair-host.json / unconfigured public pairing hostname.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>69388</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>25</tool_uses><duration_ms>371542</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:39:44.355Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a5885efb646cc54d0</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_bOrMN1YOuHYtZ215m2gNp3pN</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a5885efb646cc54d0.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify candidate eviction failure\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"PLAUSIBLE\",\n  \"severity\": \"MINOR\",\n  \"reason\": \"Corrected with the client sources now read. SERVER (C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\\\packages\\\\host-service\\\\src\\\\companion\\\\pairing.ts): eviction race is real - evictOldestKexSessionsIfFull:1329 drops strictly-oldest createdAtMs; handleRemoteKex:1039 -&gt; requireRemoteCandidate:1111 answers 400 pair_wrong_peer for an evicted deviceId. Bounds: MAX_KEX_SESSIONS=16 (l.228), MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE=4 new deviceIds per cf-connecting-ip per window (l.236; source key is http.ts:738, one bucket per client IP, unforgeable through the tunnel). Phone's candidate is the NEWEST after begin, so evicting it needs 16 introductions landing AFTER its begin and BEFORE its kex (sub-second gap: RemotePairingHandshake.run does begin -&gt; Srp.startClient -&gt; kex with no user interaction between), i.e. &gt;=4 unspent attacker IPs firing inside that gap at a known public pairing host. CLIENT (C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\pairing\\\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:110-263): confirmed strictly linear, one begin/kex/confirm, OkHttp retryOnConnectionFailure(false), no re-begin; non-200 -&gt; remoteFailureOf:339 -&gt; FAILURE_BY_WIRE maps pair_wrong_peer -&gt; PairingFailure.WRONG_PEER, thrown out of run(). BridgeCompanionRepository.pairFromCode:948-988 has no retry loop: PairingException -&gt; PairingState.Failed(remotePairingError(...)) (RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt:30 -&gt; PairingError.WRONG_PEER). So 'no automatic re-begin' is CONFIRMED. But 'terminally fails' is REFUTED: PairingScreen.kt:173-192 renders Failed on the CODE route with a 'Try again' button that clears digits and calls onRetry(), and each pairFromCode mints a fresh deviceId (RemotePairingHandshake.kt:123), so a user-driven retry is a clean new begin - allowed up to the phone IP's 4 introductions per 120s window, after which its own source gets 429 pair_rate_limited. Residual real defect is UX/diagnostic, not availability-critical: PairingState.kt:117 shows WRONG_PEER as 'Something else answered instead of the desktop. Refused.' - an on-path-attacker accusation for a benign eviction, which is the wrong instruction to the user (correct advice is 'try again'), and repeated evictions can burn the phone's 4 begins and close the window. No key, code or secret exposure on this path. Net: mechanism verified, client no-auto-retry verified, terminal-failure claim wrong, impact = timing-precise multi-IP DoS of one 120s window plus misleading error copy. MINOR.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>97082</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>25</tool_uses><duration_ms>933278</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:39:44.355Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a22cbc0a1e74b5f1f</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a22cbc0a1e74b5f1f.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify pairing error shape\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"ACCEPT_AS_MINOR\",\n  \"severity\": \"MINOR\",\n  \"reason\": \"Mechanism confirmed in the worktree, damage overstated. Real: C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\\\packages\\\\host-service\\\\src\\\\companion\\\\http.ts:794 handleRemotePairRequest (public, tokenless pairing host) calls readBoundedBody at :835; that reader (:910) throws CleartextError(400,'envelope_invalid') on a non-numeric content-length (:917), CleartextError(413,'body_too_large') on declared-oversize (:920) and on stream overflow (:935); the catch at :885-886 returns cleartextResponse(), i.e. writeCleartextError (:519) which sets ONLY 'content-type: application/json; charset=utf-8'. So this is the one pairing-host answer that skips pairingJsonResponse (:698) and therefore omits 'cache-control: no-store', directly contradicting that constructor's stated invariant ('EVERY JSON answer the pairing hop gives, success or refusal, built here', :690-697), and it emits main sealed-protocol §3.6 vocabulary plus serverTimeMs on an unauthenticated internet-facing path where the contract is {code: PairingErrorCode}. The doc comment at :906-908 ('the content-length policy ... stay HERE and are not shared') is now stale: the reader has two callers with different error contracts. Overstated: phone mapping is NOT divergent. C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\pairing\\\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:339 remoteFailureOf decodes PairErrorBody (Pairing.kt:146, `code: String? = null`) under CompanionJson ignoreUnknownKeys=true, so the extra serverTimeMs/retryAfterMs keys are ignored and code='envelope_invalid'/'body_too_large' parses fine; FAILURE_BY_WIRE (:376) misses, FAILURE_BY_STATUS (:399) has no 400 and no 413, so it falls to PairingFailure.BAD_MAC — but the canonical pairingErrorResponse(400,'unknown') and the LAN listener's PairingError(413,'unknown') map to BAD_MAC through exactly the same fallback, so the user-visible outcome and message ('the pairing endpoint rejected the exchange with &lt;status&gt;') are identical. No cache exposure demonstrable either: these are POST 400/413 responses, not cached by default. Net: contract/invariant violation plus minor protocol-fingerprint and desktop-clock disclosure, no functional or security failure. Fix: catch CleartextError inside handleRemotePairRequest around the readBoundedBody call (or give the pairing hop its own reader) and re-emit as pairingErrorResponse(400,'unknown') / pairingErrorResponse(413,'unknown'), drop the :885-886 branch, and correct the stale comment at :906-908.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>66006</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>22</tool_uses><duration_ms>402791</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:39:44.355Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a50f761cbf25bb64c</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a50f761cbf25bb64c.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify pairing countdown deadline\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"CONFIRMED\",\n  \"severity\": \"MINOR\",\n  \"reason\": \"Data flow verified end to end. Phone: C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\app-phone\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\phone\\\\ui\\\\pairing\\\\PairingScreen.kt:228 sets submittedAtMs = System.currentTimeMillis() at code submit; PairingCountdown (same file, 320-329) renders ((CompanionConstants.PAIRING_WINDOW_MS - (now - submittedAtMs))/1000) with the label \\\"${remainingSeconds}s left in the pairing window\\\", and PAIRING_WINDOW_MS = 120_000L (core\\\\...\\\\CompanionConstants.kt:83). Server-owned truth: window is 120 s from the moment the DESKTOP displays the code (PROTOCOL.md:846 §4.9.2; LAN analogue packages\\\\host-service\\\\src\\\\companion\\\\pairing.ts:971 expiresAtMs = openedAtMs + PAIRING_WINDOW_MS, hard close timer pairing.ts:1085). No expiry ever reaches the phone: PairRemoteBeginResponse (core\\\\...\\\\protocol\\\\Pairing.kt:49-54) carries only v/pid/pairSalt/serverTimeMs — no expiresAtMs/remainingMs — matching PairKexResponse in packages\\\\host-service\\\\src\\\\companion\\\\types.ts:311-319; RemotePairingHandshake.kt:131-152 consumes only pid/pairSalt and never reads a deadline, and nothing else in the exchange feeds a corrected deadline back — a stale window surfaces only as an HTTP failure at the moment the phone posts (PairingFailure.WINDOW_CLOSED, RemotePairingHandshake.kt:358). withTimeout half also confirmed: BridgeCompanionRepository.kt:961 wraps pairWithCode in withTimeout(PAIRING_WINDOW_MS) started at submit, so its backstop likewise over-runs the server window by the scan-to-submit offset. Correction to the candidate's severity/framing: (a) the countdown renders ONLY on the typed-code route (PairingScreen.kt:205, entry == CODE), not the QR route; (b) the skew is a misleading label, not a functional break — the server still refuses on time and the UI shows PairingError.WINDOW_CLOSED, so no wrong pairing is accepted and no state is corrupted; (c) the divergence is deliberate and documented in-place (PairingScreen.kt:115-118: 'the phone cannot see that moment ... it counts its own deadline'), so it is a knowingly accepted UX gap, not an oversight. Real defect worth reporting: the string asserts server-window semantics the number does not have. Closing it requires the begin response to carry expiresAtMs (or remainingMs) — a wire/protocol change on both sides — or the label to be reworded to a client-side timeout.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>55640</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>30</tool_uses><duration_ms>408308</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:39:44.355Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a32195e6d847dd5b5</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_vYh1ErDBmY2MlO4a1zc4cn1U</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a32195e6d847dd5b5.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify KDF duplication risk\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"PLAUSIBLE\",\n  \"severity\": \"MINOR\",\n  \"duplication_verified\": true,\n  \"claimed_failure_mode_verified\": false,\n  \"observable_current_effect\": \"none\",\n  \"reason\": \"Duplication is real but the stated contrast with desktop is half wrong, and the damage claim does not hold. VERIFIED DUPLICATION (C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\superset-companion\\\\core\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\family\\\\khaira\\\\supersetcompanion\\\\core\\\\crypto\\\\KeyDerivation.kt): derivePairingKeys (L67-92) and deriveRemotePairingKeys (L138-159) each open-code salt-prefix concat + Hkdf.extract + three Hkdf.expand calls with the v1/v3-srp constant sets swapped (PAIR_SALT_PREFIX/INFO_DEVICE/INFO_CONFIRM_* vs REMOTE_*, L39-57); pairingTranscript (L98-121) and remotePairingTranscript (L166-189) likewise. No profile object, no shared expand helper. DESKTOP COMPARISON (…\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\\\packages\\\\host-service\\\\src\\\\companion\\\\pairing.ts): PairingProfile (L539-564) carries only the 4 differing HKDF strings + confirmPath, and only the salt build (buildHkdfSalt L783) and the three Expands (expandPairingKeys L800) are shared. The KEX halves are still two separate functions — derivePairingKeys (L711, IKM = Z || pairingCode) and deriveSrpPairingKeys (L754, IKM = PAD(S)) — and the TRANSCRIPTS are deliberately two builders (buildPairingTranscript L584, buildSrpPairingTranscript L622) with the rationale written in: widening would relax the fixed-width assertions that make separator-free concatenation safe, and the prefix is hardcoded 'so the domain separation between the two flows is a property of these two functions, not of whoever calls them'. So the Kotlin transcript duplication MIRRORS a deliberate desktop decision, not a divergence; the only genuine asymmetry is the 3-expand + salt block (~8 lines, one extra edit site). DAMAGE CLAIM FAILS: (a) both key schedules are wire-frozen protocol (v1 pinned by golden vectors, v3-srp a distinct domain), so a schedule change is a cross-repo, cross-spec version change that a Kotlin-local helper could not have kept in sync with TypeScript anyway; (b) the flows cannot be conflated — every constant is prefixed v1 vs v3-srp, giving full domain separation, and the SRP path has a different IKM and different required sizes (SRP_GROUP_BYTES 384 vs X25519 32), so a shared Kotlin function would need the same branch desktop keeps; (c) 'silently break interoperability' is wrong — drift surfaces as a failed key confirmation (pair_bad_mac / pair_code_wrong) plus explicit size checks (requireSize, PAIRING_TRANSCRIPT_BYTES 117 / REMOTE_PAIRING_TRANSCRIPT_BYTES 825, both asserted). NO CURRENT EFFECT: every constant matches the desktop byte-for-byte — 'superset-companion/pair/v1'/'superset-companion/pair/v3-srp' (config.ts L64/L130-131), 'sc/v1 device '/'sc/v3-srp device ' (L65/L134), confirm-phone/confirm-desktop pairs (L66-67/L135-136), transcript prefixes 'sc/v1'/'sc/v3-srp' (L71/L132), and 825 = 9 + 816 matches SRP_TRANSCRIPT_BODY_BYTES (pairing.ts L216). Net: a real but small maintenance cost (one duplicated 8-line expand block on the client), correctly reported as MINOR/cleanup, not a defect and not a ship blocker.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>138434</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>21</tool_uses><duration_ms>403971</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:39:44.355Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ac4c450d8c25f346e</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_RsU7y9N042UjSG3AuaoRFUxg</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\ac4c450d8c25f346e.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify unknown error mapping\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"REJECT\",\n  \"reason\": \"Mechanism verified, characterization wrong — behavior is deliberate, documented and tested. Confirmed facts: C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/.claude/worktrees/agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f/packages/host-service/src/companion/http.ts:802-808 returns pairingErrorResponse(400,'unknown') when cf-connecting-ip is absent/comma-joined/non-IP-literal, and pairing.ts:682/1140 also emit 400 'unknown'; PairErrorBody.code is `String? = null` (core/protocol/Pairing.kt:146) so 'unknown' decodes; FAILURE_BY_WIRE (RemotePairingHandshake.kt:376-387) lacks 'unknown' and FAILURE_BY_STATUS (399-407) has no 400 entry → line 346 falls back to PairingFailure.BAD_MAC. Reachability and map are exactly as claimed. But the harm claim fails: messageFor() sends BAD_MAC to the `else` arm (line 367) = 'the pairing endpoint rejected the exchange with 400' (no crypto claim), and the user-facing string via BridgeCompanionRepository.kt:1565 → PairingState.kt:118 is 'Pairing failed to verify. Nothing was stored.' — deliberately non-cryptographic, non-code-blaming. It is spec'd and pinned by tests: RemotePairingErrorMappingTest.kt:63-74 ('BAD_MAC is left to mean an unnamed rejection') and RemotePairingHandshakeTest.kt:368-403, which asserts 400 {\\\"code\\\":\\\"unknown\\\"}, 500 {\\\"code\\\":\\\"unknown\\\"} and an unknown future code all land on BAD_MAC for forward compatibility. The LAN asymmetry is also intentional, not an oversight: PairingClient.kt:384-398 falls back to UNREACHABLE, and RemotePairingErrorMappingTest.kt:55-61 documents that the LAN 'join the same Wi-Fi' advice is wrong on the remote route. At most a MINOR naming nit (enum reuse of BAD_MAC for 'unnamed rejection'), not a defect.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>86174</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>34</tool_uses><duration_ms>412151</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:39:44.355Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a0770e41a4e692136</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_SNR2aXQyq7H6sT6a3EckQqzZ</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a0770e41a4e692136.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify burn status oracle\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"REJECT\",\n  \"candidate\": \"http.ts:821 (worktree agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f) — 60s unauthenticated, uncharged 403 pair_code_burned vs 404 burn oracle\",\n  \"premise_confirmed\": true,\n  \"reason\": \"Re-verified in C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/.claude/worktrees/agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f/packages/host-service/src/companion/. The mechanism exists exactly as described — http.ts:819-825 does `const endpoint = (deps.lookup ?? currentRemotePairing)(); if (endpoint === null) { if ((deps.burned ?? remoteWindowWasBurned)()) return pairingErrorResponse(403, \\\"pair_code_burned\\\"); return notFound(); }`, i.e. before `endpoint.admit(sourceKey)` (line 832), before `readBoundedBody` (835), on the unauthenticated public pairing host (no Access JWT; identity is CF-Connecting-IP only), so the branch is genuinely uncharged and unauthenticated for PAIRING_REMOTE_BURN_MEMO_MS = 60_000 (config.ts:144). But it is not a defect: (1) INTENDED AND DOCUMENTED — pairing.ts:1891-1923 states the memo's purpose (tell a user who mistyped three times WHY, instead of a bare 404), it is process-global, holds only `forgetAtMs` (no code, no verifier — both zeroed at burn, pairing.ts:2223-2227), self-expires on read, and is cleared when a new window opens (pairing.ts:2237); rememberRemoteBurn() fires only on the remote confirm path at failedMacAttempts &gt;= PAIRING_MAX_BAD_MACS (pairing.ts:2342-2343). (2) NO INCREMENTAL SECRET — the same unauthenticated audience already learns far more from the open-window state: during the 120s window POST /v1/pair/begin returns 200 with salt/B. Learning 'a window burned in the last 60s' after it is gone grants no grinding surface: window closed, code zeroed, verifier discarded, so no guess can be spent. (3) NO RESOURCE GAP — the 403 path and the 404 path are both O(1), pre-body, allocation-free; the 403 costs a boolean read of a single module-level object, so polling it is exactly as cheap as polling for the catch-all 404. No amplification, no table growth (unlike admit(), which is why the charge sits behind a live window). (4) NO CACHE ISSUE — pairingErrorResponse routes through pairingJsonResponse (http.ts:698-714) which sets `cache-control: no-store`; a 403 with no-store is not stored by Cloudflare or the phone, so the memo cannot be replayed past its deadline. Only observation worth passing back is cosmetic doc drift, not a finding: the comment at pairing.ts:1898-1899 and config.ts:141 say the memo 'holds the pairing REFERENCE and a deadline', but `interface RemoteBurnMemo` (pairing.ts:1903-1905) holds only `forgetAtMs` — the code leaks strictly less than the comment claims.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>63325</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>26</tool_uses><duration_ms>435175</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:39:44.355Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a83dd3475ea376ad7</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_xNkBP3Jd3BoyAMPqf44GfU1i</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a83dd3475ea376ad7.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify schema duplication mismatch\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"REJECT\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"worktree_verified\": \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/.claude/worktrees/agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\",\n  \"reason\": \"Re-verified against the correct worktree. The `/v1/pair/*` routes and the `parsed as RemotePair*Request` casts do exist (http.ts:838-861), but neither claimed defect holds. (1) NOT a zod-layer bypass: the sealed zod ROUTES table is keyed on `SealedPath` and runs only after envelope decrypt + paired-device auth; remote pairing is by construction unsealed and unauthenticated (the caller holds no device key yet — http.ts:760-793), so no sealed route entry could serve it. `routeByHost` (http.ts:652-677) keeps the surfaces provably disjoint: pair host serves EXACTLY the three paths by POST only, everything else 404; main host explicitly 404s `isRemotePairPath` so a future `app.post` cannot expose them. Pre-body gates run first (source key, open window, per-source charge, 4 KiB `readBoundedBody` with content-length pre-check), then JSON.parse with `fatal:true` UTF-8 and an object/non-null check before any cast. (2) NO unvalidated field reaches logic. Full field coverage against types.ts:325-406: `v` -&gt; assertWireVersion (strict `!==`), `deviceId` -&gt; decodeExact (typeof string + base64url decode + exact WIRE_ID_BYTES) in parseKexMetadata before it is used as a map key, and requireRemoteCandidate (typeof string) on kex/confirm, `label`/`surface`/`appVersion`/`protocol` -&gt; assertLabel/assertSurface/assertAppVersion/assertProtocolRange (pairing.ts:1817-1860), `pid` -&gt; assertPidMatches (decode + constantTimeEquals), `A` -&gt; decodeClientPublic (typeof + width check) before the modexp, `macPhone` -&gt; decodeExact(MAC_BYTES) before chargeConfirmAttempt and the constant-time MAC compare. The casts are unchecked assertions, but every consumer validates from `unknown`-typed params; there is no path where a lying type is trusted. (3) protocol.max asymmetry is real but cost-free: pairing.ts:1846-1860 rejects `max &gt; 0xff` while http.ts:970-973 protocolRangeSchema bounds only `min&gt;=0`/`min&lt;=max` — however read-api.ts:592-595 clamps `hi = Math.min(clientRange.max, BRIDGE_PROTOCOL_RANGE.max)` and emits `protocol = degraded ? 0 : hi`, so an oversized hello max negotiates to the bridge max, is never stored, and never widens the AAD protocol byte. No observable behavioural or security cost; a schema-tidiness nit at most, not the claimed inconsistent-boundary defect. Separately noted and NOT part of this candidate: pairing's decodeExact accepts non-canonical base64url deviceIds where the sealed layer's `id22` requires `isCanonicalWireId` — pre-existing in both LAN and remote flows, out of scope here.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>75091</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>40</tool_uses><duration_ms>449520</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:39:44.355Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ad58eca6042dd6449</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\ad58eca6042dd6449.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify repository flow duplication\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"PLAUSIBLE\",\n  \"reason\": \"Duplication verified, not a current defect. pairFromCode (948-994) and pairFromQr (891-933) share a verbatim 4-line tail (pairingState=Exchanging(STORING); publishPairing(record); sessionMutex.withLock { discardSession() }; refresh()) and three byte-identical-in-behavior catch arms (DeviceLockedException -&gt; KEYSTORE_UNAVAILABLE, SecureStoreException -&gt; KEYSTORE_UNAVAILABLE, CompanionException -&gt; BAD_MAC; only log text differs). No shared helper exists: the only extracted abstractions in the file are publishPairing (1288), pairedRecordOrNull (1003), and the per-flow mappers pairingError (1558) / remotePairingError (RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt:20) — i.e. the codebase already factored out the part that legitimately differs, but left the identical part copied. Drift surface is nameable and concrete: (a) SecureStoreException today aborts with Failed(KEYSTORE_UNAVAILABLE) and no publishPairing, so any future change that must clear a half-written record or emit a distinct error has two edit sites and one can be missed, leaving one flow stuck in Exchanging/stale Paired; (b) the tail's ordering (publish before discardSession before refresh) is load-bearing — publishPairing reads wireSession for writeEnabled and refresh() re-establishes the session — so a future reorder or added step applied to one flow only diverges silently, since neither path has a test-visible shared seam. Real observable effect today: none — the two paths are currently equivalent, both mappers handle their failure sets, and PairingFailure.WINDOW_CLOSED/CODE_* are correctly routed per flow. So this is maintenance risk with a named divergence vector, not a live bug and not pure formatting; MINOR at best, fix by extracting a private suspend finishPairing(record) plus a catch ladder parameterized by the failure mapper and log prefix.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>26451</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>6</tool_uses><duration_ms>53950</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:39:44.355Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a21a0d7c687a2d25d</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_tgS8pi92Kt8i1lCdELnB8A3w</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a21a0d7c687a2d25d.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify dialog timer overhead\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"candidate\": \"PairDeviceDialog.tsx:313 — 250ms countdown setInterval + separate 1s pairingState poll cause ~480 renders per 120s window though display rounds to seconds; collapse to one 1s tick/poll\",\n  \"file\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\\\apps\\\\desktop\\\\src\\\\renderer\\\\components\\\\PairDeviceDialog\\\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx\",\n  \"line\": 313,\n  \"verdict\": \"REFUTED\",\n  \"severity\": \"NONE\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"summary\": \"Pure negligible micro-optimization, and its stated mechanism is partly false. Render count is right (480) but per-render cost is ~nothing, blast radius is a modal subtree bounded to &lt;=120s, and the proposed 1s tick regresses countdown smoothness and expiry latency.\",\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"claim_part\": \"1s pairingState poll contributes renders\",\n      \"status\": \"false\",\n      \"detail\": \"Poll callback (lines 332-391) only calls setPhase on terminal transitions. Steady state hits `if (state.kind === 'open') return;` (line 364) — zero setState, zero renders. All ~480 renders come from the 250ms tick alone. Poll cost is IPC/tRPC round-trips, unchanged by the candidate's premise.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_part\": \"~480 renders per window\",\n      \"status\": \"true\",\n      \"detail\": \"120000/250 = 480. setRemainingMs(left) at line 307 always receives a new number → no React bailout ever.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_part\": \"renders are expensive\",\n      \"status\": \"false\",\n      \"detail\": \"QrCode (components/QrCode/QrCode.tsx:33) memoizes encodeQrMatrix + the ~30KB `d` path string on [value], which is stable for the whole window. Re-render walks ~10 elements, all props reference-identical → React writes zero DOM mutations. Rest of the tree is Buttons/DialogFooter/text; only per-render arithmetic is Math.ceil/floor + groupCode. No layout thrash, no observers, no allocation of size.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_part\": \"blast radius\",\n      \"status\": \"bounded\",\n      \"detail\": \"State lives in PairingFlow; PairDeviceDialog and the app tree do not re-render. Dialog is modal, unmounts on close (parent gates on `open &amp;&amp;` at line 78), and the interval is cleared on unmount and on leaving phase 'open' (dep [expiresAtMs], line 316). Max lifetime 120s of a user-initiated pairing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_part\": \"can tick once per second\",\n      \"status\": \"regression\",\n      \"detail\": \"Tick phase is not aligned to second boundaries (first tick fires at open time, line 314). At 1000ms the displayed second can visibly hold ~2s then jump, and transition to phase 'closing' at left&lt;=0 (line 311) is detected up to 1s late instead of up to 250ms late, delaying the 'Finishing up…' handover and the verdict poll. 250ms is a deliberate 4x-oversample of a 1Hz display, the standard fix for that jitter.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_part\": \"complexity cost\",\n      \"status\": \"inverted\",\n      \"detail\": \"The two effects have different lifetimes and deps: countdown keys on phase 'open' only ([expiresAtMs]), poll keys on 'open'|'closing' plus [hostUrl, mode] (lines 302, 328-331, 391). Merging them couples a local-clock concern to a network-verdict concern and widens deps → more complex, not less.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_part\": \"a11y spam via aria-live\",\n      \"status\": \"false\",\n      \"detail\": \"aria-live='polite' text (line 510 equivalent) changes value only once per second regardless of tick rate; React updates the text node only on change, so screen-reader announcements are identical at 250ms and 1000ms.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"if_anyone_still_wants_it\": \"The only non-regressing variant is storing seconds (not ms) in state so setState bails on equal values — ~120 renders, same 250ms sampling. Still zero observable benefit; not worth churn on security-sensitive code.\",\n  \"recommendation\": \"DROP\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>46039</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>6</tool_uses><duration_ms>82630</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
