{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T02:40:16.382Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ada76cb0329fa8d19</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_yn88leoc1rCKKXWBrEbHajyx</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\ada76cb0329fa8d19.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify synchronous SRP cost\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"REFUTED\",\n  \"reason\": \"Mechanics confirmed in the review worktree, harm claim not. srp.ts srpServerHandshake does exactly 3 exponentiations mod the RFC 5054 3072-bit N per NEW candidate: srpSecretModPow(g,b), srpModPow(v,u) (public exponent), srpSecretModPow(A*v^u,b); each secret ladder is a fixed 64 windows x (4 squarings + 1 multiply) = 320 BigInt modmuls. pairing.ts:1057-1066 calls it synchronously inside handleRemoteKex (no worker, no await, no yield) on the host-service event loop, reached from the unauthenticated public path POST /v1/pair/kex (http.ts handleRemotePairRequest). Cost is bounded exactly as the code documents: MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW=64 is charged before the handshake and is monotone for the window's life; a candidate derives once; an exact retry replays the cached B; a different A is refused; MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE=4 distinct deviceIds per cf-connecting-ip (absence refused, comma-lists refused) means reaching 64 requires &gt;=16 distinct client IPs; the window is user-initiated, process-wide-singleton, and lives PAIRING_WINDOW_MS. Using the module's own measured figures (~2.8-3.3 ms per exponentiation, ~10 ms per kex on Electron 41 / win-arm64), worst case is ~10 ms contiguous block per request and ~640 ms aggregate CPU across an entire 120 s window. Nothing in-process is sensitive at that scale: LIVENESS_REFRESH_TIMEOUT_MS=2000, LOCK_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS=5000, HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_FOREGROUND_MS=60000, tunnel INBOUND_SILENCE_TIMEOUT_MS=75000, FCM_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=10000 — two to four orders of magnitude above the stall. Candidate's specific damage claim (heartbeat/lease/push stalls) is unsupported; the design was measured, bounded twice over, and documented as accepted cost, with the native-engine alternative rejected for a measured ~2.4 s constructor. This is a micro-optimization argument, not a defect.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>66869</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>30</tool_uses><duration_ms>684543</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
