{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:14:38.247Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ab4a6c4b0a4d62b40</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_LswitkhG17flx3LonR27vFkZ</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\ab4a6c4b0a4d62b40.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Audit removed pairing behavior\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Coverage limitation (read first)\n\nBash tool is dead in this environment: every invocation — including `echo probe` — fails with `/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching '`, i.e. the shell init preamble aborts before my command runs. Tried 5 shapes (`cd &amp;&amp;`, `git -C`, bare `echo`, `true`, multiline). So `git diff HEAD` / `git status --short` were **unobtainable**, and `Glob` mtime ordering came back alphabetical, so there was no substitute for the change surface.\n\nConsequence: I could not read a single deleted line. Everything below is derived from the **current** tree only. None of it is asserted as \"removed\" — these are defects/gaps verifiable against present code, per the recall-biased brief. A true Angle-B removed-behavior pass still needs re-running once bash works.\n\n## Candidates\n\n**1. [MAJOR] `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1616`**\n- summary: Post-persist failure inside `sealPairedResponse` surfaces to the phone as `BAD_MAC` → \"Nothing was stored\", but the device record is already on disk.\n- failure_scenario: `deps.onPaired()` (line 1604) succeeds and writes the device record + `K_dev`; `deps.loadAccessToken()` (1616) then throws because `~/.superset/companion/` holds no/unreadable/corrupt token file. Not a `PairingError` → `http.ts:917` returns `500 {\"code\":\"unknown\"}`. Phone: `FAILURE_BY_WIRE[\"unknown\"]` absent, `FAILURE_BY_STATUS[500]` absent → default `PairingFailure.BAD_MAC` (`RemotePairingHandshake.kt:408`) → `PairingError.BAD_MAC` → user reads \"Pairing failed to verify. **Nothing was stored.**\" The window is closed, a stale paired-device record with a live key remains on the desktop, and the user is told the exact opposite — the case `CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED` (\"remove this phone if it's listed\") exists for. `RemotePairingErrorMappingTest.onlyTheFailuresThatCanKnowItClaimNothingWasStored` actively pins the false claim onto `BAD_MAC`.\n\n**2. [MAJOR] `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:352`**\n- summary: Phone binds the moving `PROTOCOL_CURRENT` into the step-4 pairing AAD where the desktop pins a frozen `PROTOCOL_VERSION_AT_PAIRING = 1`.\n- failure_scenario: `CompanionConstants.PROTOCOL_CURRENT` is `1` today (`CompanionConstants.kt:70`) and also feeds `CLIENT_PROTOCOL_MAX`, so it moves the day protocol 2 ships. Desktop hardcodes `PROTOCOL_VERSION_AT_PAIRING = 1` (`pairing.ts:262`, used at `pairing.ts:1666`). Bump `PROTOCOL_CURRENT` to `2` → the two AADs differ by one byte → GCM tag fails on every remote pairing → `CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED` for 100% of users, indistinguishable from a MITM. This is the same class of outage the desktop module header records (\"the two AADs differed by 16 bytes and every pairing failed the GCM tag\"). Same pattern at `PairingClient.kt:273` for the QR flow.\n\n**3. [MINOR] `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:470`**\n- summary: `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` maps 503 but not Cloudflare's 502/504/52x, so a tunnel outage reports a crypto failure.\n- failure_scenario: `cloudflared` is down or the origin is unreachable → Cloudflare answers `502` (or `521`/`523`) with an HTML body. `PairErrorBody` decode throws `ProtocolException` → `code = null`; `FAILURE_BY_STATUS[502]` absent → default `BAD_MAC` → \"Pairing failed to verify. Nothing was stored.\" User is sent hunting a verification failure during a plain connectivity outage, while the adjacent `503` correctly yields `UNREACHABLE`/`NETWORK`.\n\n**4. [MINOR] `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:2219`**\n- summary: Opening a QR/LAN window does not clear `remoteBurnMemo`; only the remote opener does (line 2369).\n- failure_scenario: User mistypes the code 3× → remote window burns → `rememberRemoteBurn()` arms a 60 s memo. User immediately opens a **QR** window instead. `currentRemotePairing()` returns `null` (kind `\"lan\"`), so `http.ts:833` sees the live memo and answers `/v1/pair/*` with `403 pair_code_burned` for up to 60 s — instead of the bare `404` the design mandates whenever no remote window exists (\"byte-identical to an unknown path… outside a window a human deliberately opened this surface is not discoverable\"). A phone or scanner therefore learns a burn happened during a window of an entirely different kind.\n\n**5. [MINOR] `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt:113`**\n- summary: The typed 8-digit code is held in `rememberSaveable`, i.e. written into Android saved-instance-state, which can outlive the 120 s window.\n- failure_scenario: User types `40718352`, hits `RESTART_REQUIRED`, then backgrounds the app. Compose's `rememberSaveable` puts `digits` in the Activity `Bundle`, which the framework hands to system_server and may persist to disk for process-death restore. A ~26.6-bit secret that the desktop half refuses to log or persist for exactly this reason (\"a copy that outlives its window in a log file is a code someone can simply try\") now has a copy in system-managed storage with no window-linked erasure. Tradeoff against retained-code retry across rotation may well be deliberate — flagging because I could not check whether it was previously plain `remember`.\n\n**6. [MINOR] `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\http.ts:820`**\n- summary: The missing-source refusal returns `400 {\"code\":\"unknown\"}` before the window check, distinguishable from the bare 404 the no-window path returns.\n- failure_scenario: A caller reaches the pairing hostname without a single valid `cf-connecting-ip` and gets a JSON `400`, whereas an unknown path/unknown Host gets `notFound()` = `new Response(null, {status:404})`. That difference confirms \"this hostname is a Superset pairing host\" even with no window open, weakening the discoverability property claimed in the gate list at `http.ts:785-788`. Practically hard to reach — Cloudflare always sets the header and the origin is loopback+tunnel only — so this is the weakest of the six; passing it through per the recall bias.\n\nChecked and found clean: SRP server arithmetic and boundary checks (`srp.ts` — `A` range/degenerate/width, `u == 0`, `B mod N == 0`, fixed-window secret ladder, `PAD()` refusal-not-truncation); phone-side SRP `B` validation (`Srp.decodeGroupElement` enforces 384 bytes, `0 &lt; B &lt; N`, `B != 1, N-1`, `u != 0` — so the unlengthed `Base64Url.decode(kex.bPublic, \"B\")` is safe); identity/prefix/salt-prefix domain separation between LAN and remote profiles (`sc/v3 remote-pair`, `sc/v3-srp` match across runtimes); Unicode digit normalization (`PairingCode.asciiDigitOrNull` via `Character.digit` normalizes Arabic-Indic/fullwidth to ASCII, `passwordBytes` fails loud on anything else); host/Access isolation (`routeByHost` + the single `app.use(\"*\")` at `http.ts:1600` — pairing paths 404 on the main host, everything non-pairing 404s on the pair host, Access still enforced per-route); caps/eviction and monotone ceilings (`MAX_KEX_SESSIONS` evict-not-refuse, `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE`, `MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW`, `REMOTE_PAIR_MAX_REQUESTS_PER_WINDOW`); fixed-window/one-window-atomic slot (`reserveWindowSlot`/`claimOrTearDown`); key zeroization paths (`wipeAllPairingKeys` via `zeroKexSession`/`clearKexSessions`/`completePairing`'s outermost `finally`); the frozen wire contract itself (`400` + `pair_unknown_candidate` on both remote kex and confirm, no alias, no status-only mapping, `retryOnConnectionFailure(false)`, `resubmitsSameCode` gated on the user's tap only) and its docs (`PROTOCOL.md` §4.9) and tests (`RemotePairingErrorMappingTest`, `PairingWaitTextTest`).\n\n**FIX-FIRST**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>157702</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>45</tool_uses><duration_ms>466470</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
