{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:15:28.959Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ab4a6c4b0a4d62b40</task-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\ab4a6c4b0a4d62b40.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Audit removed pairing behavior\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Report already concluded — exactly 6 candidates, scope unchanged. No `SendMessage` tool in this session, so replying here instead of to `code-review`.\n\n## Coverage caveat (load-bearing)\n\nBash unusable: every invocation, including `echo probe`, dies at `/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching '` — shell init aborts before my command. 5 shapes tried. `git diff HEAD` / `git status --short` never ran; `Glob` mtime order came back alphabetical, so no substitute change surface. **Zero deleted lines were read.** Nothing below is claimed as removed behavior — these are defects verifiable in the current tree. Angle B proper needs a re-run once bash works.\n\n## 6 candidates\n\n**1. [MAJOR]** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1616`\n- summary: Failure after `onPaired` persists reaches the phone as `BAD_MAC` → \"Nothing was stored\", though the record is on disk.\n- failure_scenario: `onPaired()` (1604) writes device record + `K_dev`; `loadAccessToken()` (1616) throws on missing/corrupt token file → `http.ts:917` → `500 {\"code\":\"unknown\"}` → phone has no wire/status mapping → default `BAD_MAC` (`RemotePairingHandshake.kt:408`) → \"Pairing failed to verify. Nothing was stored.\" Window closed, stale live-key record left on desktop, user told the opposite. `RemotePairingErrorMappingTest.onlyTheFailuresThatCanKnowItClaimNothingWasStored` pins that false claim.\n\n**2. [MAJOR]** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:352`\n- summary: Phone binds moving `PROTOCOL_CURRENT` into step-4 AAD; desktop pins frozen `PROTOCOL_VERSION_AT_PAIRING = 1`.\n- failure_scenario: `PROTOCOL_CURRENT` is 1 today (`CompanionConstants.kt:70`) and also feeds `CLIENT_PROTOCOL_MAX`, so it moves when protocol 2 ships. Desktop stays at `pairing.ts:262`/`:1666`. Bump → AADs differ one byte → GCM tag fails on every remote pairing → `CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED` for all users, indistinguishable from MITM. Same class as the outage the desktop header records. Mirrored at `PairingClient.kt:273`.\n\n**3. [MINOR]** `RemotePairingHandshake.kt:470`\n- summary: `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` maps 503 but not 502/504/52x.\n- failure_scenario: `cloudflared` down → Cloudflare `502` + HTML body → `PairErrorBody` decode throws → `code = null` → no status entry → `BAD_MAC` \"Pairing failed to verify. Nothing was stored.\" Connectivity outage reported as a crypto failure, while 503 correctly gives `NETWORK`.\n\n**4. [MINOR]** `pairing.ts:2219`\n- summary: QR/LAN opener never clears `remoteBurnMemo`; only the remote opener does (2369).\n- failure_scenario: 3 mistypes burn the remote window (60 s memo armed) → user opens a **QR** window → `currentRemotePairing()` returns null (kind `lan`) → `http.ts:833` sees the live memo → `/v1/pair/*` answers `403 pair_code_burned` for up to 60 s instead of the mandated bare 404, so the host stays discoverable and leaks a burn during a different window kind.\n\n**5. [MINOR]** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt:113`\n- summary: Typed 8-digit code held in `rememberSaveable` → written to Android saved-instance-state, can outlive the 120 s window.\n- failure_scenario: User types the code, hits `RESTART_REQUIRED`, backgrounds the app; `digits` goes into the Activity `Bundle`, handed to system_server and possibly persisted for process-death restore. A ~26.6-bit secret the desktop refuses to persist now has a copy with no window-linked erasure. May be a deliberate tradeoff for retained-code retry across rotation — could not check the prior value.\n\n**6. [MINOR]** `packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\http.ts:820`\n- summary: Missing-source refusal returns `400 {\"code\":\"unknown\"}` before the window check, distinguishable from the no-window bare 404.\n- failure_scenario: Caller without a single valid `cf-connecting-ip` gets JSON `400`; unknown path/Host gets `notFound()` = `Response(null, 404)`. Confirms \"this is a Superset pairing host\" with no window open, denting the discoverability claim at `http.ts:785-788`. Weakest of the six — Cloudflare always sets the header and the origin is loopback+tunnel only; passed through per recall bias.\n\nChecked clean: SRP server arithmetic and boundary checks (`srp.ts` — `A` range/degenerate/width, `u == 0`, `B mod N == 0`, fixed-window secret ladder, `PAD()` refuses rather than truncates); phone-side `B` validation (`Srp.decodeGroupElement` enforces 384 bytes, `0 &lt; B &lt; N`, `B != 1, N-1`, `u != 0`, so the unlengthed `Base64Url.decode(kex.bPublic, \"B\")` is safe); LAN/remote domain separation (`sc/v3 remote-pair`, `sc/v3-srp`, salt prefixes match across runtimes); Unicode digit normalization (`PairingCode.asciiDigitOrNull` normalizes Arabic-Indic/fullwidth, `passwordBytes` fails loud otherwise); host/Access isolation (`routeByHost` + single `app.use(\"*\")` at `http.ts:1600`); caps/eviction and monotone ceilings; one-window-atomic slot (`reserveWindowSlot`/`claimOrTearDown`); zeroization paths (`wipeAllPairingKeys` via `zeroKexSession`/`clearKexSessions`/`completePairing` outermost `finally`); the frozen wire contract itself (400 + `pair_unknown_candidate` on remote kex and confirm, no alias, no status-only mapping, `retryOnConnectionFailure(false)`, `resubmitsSameCode` gated on the user's tap) plus `PROTOCOL.md` §4.9 and its tests.\n\n**FIX-FIRST**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>153066</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>45</tool_uses><duration_ms>500776</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:15:30.244Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a3a37a69b4b12483f</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_RkKr6a71UNeklYsJh0ijJl2u</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a3a37a69b4b12483f.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Find pairing simplifications\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Bash tool unusable in this session (shell init parse error: `/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF`), so no `git diff HEAD` — scope reconstructed by reading the `(REMOTE-CODE-PAIRING)` / remote-pairing surface in both repos (host-service `pairing.ts`, `srp.ts`, `http.ts`, trpc `companion.ts`, `PairDeviceDialog.tsx`; companion `RemotePairingHandshake.kt`, `PairingClient.kt`, `Srp.kt`, `KeyDerivation.kt`, `PairingCode.kt`, `PairingScreen.kt`, `PairingState.kt`, `RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt`, `BridgeCompanionRepository.kt`). Untracked-vs-modified split not verifiable without git.\n\n6 candidates:\n\n---\n1. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\nline 408-416 (`RemoteKexSession`), enforced at 1049-1061 and 1506-1520\nsummary: Three parallel nullable fields (`clientPublic`, `serverPublic`, `keys`) encode one boolean (\"kex done\") → an impossible-state `500` branch in `handleRemoteKex` plus a triple null-check in `handleRemoteConfirm`; simpler form is one nullable sub-object `kex: { clientPublic; serverPublic; keys } | null` assigned once.\nfailure_scenario: Any future edit that assigns the fields separately (e.g. caching `B` before deriving keys, or wiping `keys` on a failed MAC) re-creates the half-committed candidate the comment says cannot exist: the retry short-circuit at 1060 replays a `B` with no keys behind it, the phone fails at step 3b as `pair_code_wrong` while typing the right digits, and the desktop answers `500 unknown` from 1058 with no log line. Three call sites must be kept in lockstep by hand; one object makes it unrepresentable.\n\n---\n2. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\components\\PairDeviceDialog\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx`\nlines 302-304 (with `CompanionPairingState` at `packages\\host-service\\src\\trpc\\router\\companion\\companion.ts:163-170`)\nsummary: The dialog identifies \"is this verdict about my window?\" from two incidental fields (`expiresAtMs` + `pairingKind`) and silently `return`s on mismatch, even though `pairingRef` exists precisely as the non-secret window identity and is simply not in the response shape; simpler form is `pairingRef` in `CompanionPairingState` and mismatch → the existing \"window is gone\" failure.\nfailure_scenario: Window replaced under the dialog (second dialog instance / anything else calling `openPairing`|`openRemotePairing`): local countdown hits 0 → `phase = \"closing\"`, poll returns `kind:\"open\"` with the new window's `expiresAtMs`, both guards `return`, and the `closing` render (363-370) is a spinner with no footer buttons — permanent \"Finishing up…\" with no verdict and no in-panel way out. Two-field identity also means a same-mode window that happens to share `expiresAtMs` reads another window's verdict as its own.\n\n---\n3. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\nline 1356 (`evictOldestKexSessionsIfFull`)\nsummary: Oldest-candidate eviction does an O(n) `createdAtMs` scan with a `POSITIVE_INFINITY` sentinel, a dead `oldestKey === null` return, a `while` that can only ever iterate once and a redundant re-`get`, while the sibling helper `chargePerSource` (1340-1343) evicts the oldest by relying on Map insertion order; simpler form is the same one-liner (`kexSessions.keys().next()`), giving one eviction idiom in the file.\nfailure_scenario: Two idioms for one invariant drift the moment a candidate is re-inserted or its `createdAtMs` is touched (e.g. \"refresh on retry\"): insertion-order eviction and timestamp eviction then disagree, and the `(PAIR-JUNK-SURVIVAL)` argument that eviction is fair to the real phone becomes untrue in one of the two tables with no test able to see it. The dead null branch also hides a genuine \"table full but nothing evictable\" bug behind a silent `return`.\n\n---\n4. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\nlines 2499-2509 (burn memo set in `currentRemotePairing().confirm`'s catch)\nsummary: \"The window burned\" is decided in two places — `burnMac` (1797-1802) flips `consumed` at `&gt;= PAIRING_MAX_BAD_MACS`, and the endpoint's catch re-derives the same predicate to call `rememberRemoteBurn()`; simpler form is to memo inside `burnMac` where the burn actually happens, leaving the endpoint to just close.\nfailure_scenario: A burn reached from any path other than that one catch (a strike charged elsewhere, a future \"burn on N unattributed attempts\", or the confirm result being consumed before the catch) sets `consumed` without a memo → `handleRemotePairRequest` (http.ts:833) sees no memo and answers a bare `404`, so the user who mistyped three times is told \"the desktop has no pairing window open\" instead of `pair_code_burned` / \"ask for a new code\" — the exact ambiguity `PAIRING_REMOTE_BURN_MEMO_MS` was added to remove, failing silently.\n\n---\n5. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt`\nline 113 (`var digits by rememberSaveable { mutableStateOf(\"\") }`, retained-retry read at 185-190)\nsummary: The typed 8-digit code is held in `rememberSaveable`, i.e. written into the Activity saved-state Bundle (system-persisted for process-death restore), when the retained-code retry only needs it across recomposition/rotation; simpler form is `remember` (or clearing `digits` in `onStop`), keeping only `submittedAtMs` saveable.\nfailure_scenario: Android saves instance state on backgrounding: a ~26.6-bit secret that the design bounds to 120 s is copied into system-held state that can be written to disk and restored minutes or hours later — and the restored value is useless anyway, because the desktop window it belonged to is long gone, so the restored \"Try again\" resubmits a dead code and reports `CODE_WRONG` (\"check the code\") for a code that was correct.\n\n---\n6. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\data\\BridgeCompanionRepository.kt`\nline 950 (`PairingCode.digitsOrNull(code)`; repeated at `core\\...\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:119-125`, then `PairingCode.passwordBytes` re-checks at `PairingCode.kt:81`)\nsummary: The same code string is normalized/validated at three layers (UI `takeDigits` → repository `digitsOrNull` → core `digitsOrNull` → `passwordBytes` ASCII re-check), each with its own failure object; simpler form is one normalization at the UI boundary plus the core's `passwordBytes` guard, dropping the repository copy.\nfailure_scenario: The repository branch is unreachable in practice (the Pair button is enabled only at exactly 8 digits and `takeDigits` already strips non-digits), so it is untested dead code that nonetheless owns a user-visible verdict: a future entry point that submits raw text gets `CODE_WRONG` from the repository, the same input gets `PairingException(CODE_WRONG)` from core, and the two paths must be kept saying the same thing by hand while only one of them can ever be exercised.\n\nChecked and clean: frozen wire behavior (`pair_unknown_candidate` = HTTP 400 + parsed-body code at kex/confirm, no status-only fallback for 400, no automatic retry — `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` has no 400 entry, retry is user-triggered only); one-window atomic reservation (`reserveWindowSlot`/`claimOrTearDown`/`releaseWindowSlot`) and both `close()` paths' zeroization; monotone caps (`remoteRequests` 1024, `kexComputations` 64, per-source 32/32 and 4/32) are each independently reachable and none is derivable from the others; host isolation (`routeByHost`, `normaliseHostHeader`, `cf-connecting-ip` single-literal rule); SRP fixed-window ladder, `PAD()` widths and 825-byte transcript agreement between `pairing.ts`/`KeyDerivation.kt`; sealed step-4 AAD path binding and counter-0 nonce; `admit()` charge-once ordering ahead of body read.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>177941</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>47</tool_uses><duration_ms>518453</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
