. Those are `PairingException`, not `EnvelopeException`, so they bypass the `catch (e: EnvelopeException) -&gt; CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED` wrapper at :177 and reach `remotePairingError` (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\data\\RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt:22) -&gt; `PairingError.CODE_WRONG`. A desktop (or on-path edge) that seals a step-4 envelope of the wrong kind, or one addressed to a different deviceId, tells the user \"the desktop did not accept that code\" and offers a resubmit of the same digits, hiding a wrong-peer/protocol condition behind a typo message. The duplication also means the QR twin and the remote copy now label identical envelope defects differently, and a fix to one silently misses the other. Reuse: hoist PairingClient's implementation into one helper parameterised by confirm path + failure code.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:439\nsummary: `FAILURE_BY_WIRE` / `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` / `messageFor` (:439, :470, :416) hand-build a second wire-string-&gt;failure resolver that `PairingFailure`'s `WireEnum` machinery (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\protocol\\WireEnum.kt) already provides and that `PairingClient.pairingFailureOf` (PairingClient.kt:343) already implements.\nfailure_scenario: The two tables have already drifted on the same wire vocabulary: `FAILURE_BY_WIRE` omits `BAD_MAC.wire`, `CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED`, `UNREACHABLE`, `HOST_NOT_PRIVATE`, `QR_INVALID`; `PairingClient` has no 429 branch at all (429 falls to `UNREACHABLE`) while the new table maps 429 -&gt; `RATE_LIMITED`; `PairingClient` collapses `pair_wrong_peer` -&gt; `WINDOW_CLOSED` while the new table preserves `WRONG_PEER`; unmatched codes default to `BAD_MAC` here and `UNREACHABLE` there. `pair_unknown_candidate` exists only in the new table. Adding one wire code to `PairingFailure` therefore requires two hand edits in two files or one flow silently swallows it into its own default. Same duplication in the payload type: `PairErrorBody` (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\protocol\\Pairing.kt:146) and the private `PairingErrorBody` (PairingClient.kt:409) are the identical `{code}` serializer. Reuse: one `PairingFailure.fromWire()` over the WireEnum entries plus the shared `PairErrorBody`; delete `PairingErrorBody`.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\crypto\\KeyDerivation.kt:138\nsummary: `deriveRemotePairingKeys` (:138) and `remotePairingTranscript` (:166) are line-for-line copies of `derivePairingKeys` (:67) and `pairingTranscript` (:98) with five renamed prefix constants — the exact duplication the desktop side removed via `PairingProfile` + shared `buildHkdfSalt` / `expandPairingKeys` / `concatFixed`.\nfailure_scenario: Cross-runtime differential vectors pin these bytes to the desktop's `LAN_PAIRING_PROFILE` / `REMOTE_PAIRING_PROFILE` table (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:556-570 and :788-826). Because the Kotlin side keeps two independent bodies, any change to the key schedule — a fourth Expand, a label fix, a prefix rename — has to be applied in two Kotlin functions and one TS profile row, and applying it to only the LAN copy leaves the remote flow deriving a different PRK. The failure surfaces as `pair_code_wrong` on every remote pairing with no diagnostic, indistinguishable from a mistyped code. Encoding is already inconsistent inside the copies: salt prefixes go through `Charsets.UTF_8` (:81, :149) while transcript prefixes use `US_ASCII` (:112, :180), and the desktop encodes both as `\"ascii\"` — so the first non-ASCII byte anyone puts in a label breaks half the flows silently. Reuse: parameterise the existing `derivePairingKeys`/`pairingTranscript` with a profile object mirroring desktop's `PairingProfile`.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:86\nsummary: The hardened OkHttp builder at :86-100 plus `connectionSpecsFor` (:486) re-derive the TLS/redirect/retry policy that `SealedTransport.secureBase()` (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\transport\\SealedTransport.kt:110) already owns.\nfailure_scenario: Both copies currently pin `followRedirects(false)`, `followSslRedirects(false)`, `RESTRICTED_TLS + MODERN_TLS`, `retryOnConnectionFailure(false)` — so no drift today. But the pairing hop is the ONE unauthenticated hop in the protocol and its own comment (:501) states that TLS to the pinned hostname is what authenticates the desktop. A future tightening (a cipher removal, a pin, HTTP/1.1-only) applied to `secureBase()` leaves exactly that hop on the old policy, and nothing in the build fails. Reuse: expose `secureBase()` from the transport module and have `RemotePairingHandshake` layer only its own timeouts and the localhost-cleartext exception on top.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1135\nsummary: `requireRemoteCandidate` validates the incoming deviceId with a bare `typeof === \"string\"` and then uses the raw client string as a Map key, instead of `isCanonicalWireId` / `decodeWireId` (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\crypto.ts:782 and :802).\nfailure_scenario: `crypto.ts:764-793` documents `isCanonicalWireId` as THE canonical §0.1 wire-id test, written specifically because the 22-char id shape had been checked four different ways with three strictnesses and \"ids that pass an outer gate and then fail an inner one is the failure mode this protocol can least afford\". This is now a fifth way. `begin` validates the id canonically (via `parseKexMetadata` -&gt; `decodeExact`, :1190) and refuses a bad one as `400 unknown`; `kex`/`confirm` at :1146 do not, so the same non-canonical 22-char variant of a real deviceId (base64url's last char carries 4 significant bits, so four strings decode to identical bytes) misses the map and gets `400 pair_unknown_candidate` — the frozen contract's \"repeat begin\" advice — plus an `unattributedAttempts` warn line. One id string, two different verdicts across three steps of one exchange, and the phone is told to restart a window that was never wrong.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt:388\nsummary: `GroupedCodeTransformation` re-derives the dash position (`PAIRING_CODE_DIGITS / 2`, :390) and its `OffsetMapping` arithmetic (:392-394) instead of taking it from `PairingCode.grouped`, which already owns where the dash goes (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\PairingCode.kt:63).\nfailure_scenario: The transformation calls `PairingCode.grouped(text.text)` for the visible string but computes the cursor mapping from its own local `half`. `grouped` additionally suppresses the dash entirely when `digits.length &lt;= half` (:65), which the mapping does not model; today the two agree only because `half` is 4 and `originalToTransformed` shifts only above `half`. Change `PAIRING_CODE_DIGITS` to any odd value, or change `grouped`'s dash rule, and the offset mapping is off by one against the rendered text — cursor jumps, selection lands in the wrong place, and Compose can throw on a transformed offset past the end of the transformed string. The desktop has a third, independent grouping (`groupCode`, `Math.ceil(len/2)`, C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\components\\PairDeviceDialog\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx:151) which would then disagree with what the phone shows the user typing. Reuse: derive the mapping offset from `PairingCode.grouped`'s own output length rather than a second copy of the rule.\n```\n\n---\n\nChecked and found clean (no duplication worth reporting): `srp.ts` (its parameterised `digest` is required by the SHA-1 test group, and `srpBytesToBigInt`/`srpPad` have no pre-existing equivalent); `chargePerSource` / `chargeKexIntroduction` / `chargeRemoteRequest` (correctly one shared bounded counter); `handleRemotePairRequest`'s reuse of `readBoundedBody`, `pairingJsonResponse` as the single no-store constructor, and `routeByHost`/`classifyRequestHost` host isolation; `wipeAllPairingKeys`/`zeroKexSession`/`clearKexSessions` as the single wipe path; `pairing-modes.ts` as the single qr/code &lt;-&gt; lan/remote table; `PairingCode.digitsOrNull`/`takeDigits`/`passwordBytes` as the single Unicode-digit normalisation point (correctly reused by both `PairingScreen` and `RemotePairingHandshake.run`); the `MIN_SEALED_BODY_BYTES` guard I initially suspected was dropped (it is enforced inside `EnvelopeHeader.parse`, SealedEnvelope.kt:69, so the omission is safe); the frozen `HTTP 400 + pair_unknown_candidate` contract itself, which I found no reason to change.\n\n**FIX-FIRST** (findings 1-3 are duplications with live behavioural divergence, not style).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>190141</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>61</tool_uses><duration_ms>552109</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:16:40.390Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a8eaf6c26af20d34c</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_d426tbVZ2pSt02E8Tn8pZPVA</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a8eaf6c26af20d34c.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Scan pairing diff linewise\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Scope caveat (must read)\n\nBash tool non-functional this session — every invocation (`echo hello`, `git --version`) died in the shell preamble: `/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching `''`. So **no `git diff HEAD` / `git status --short` was obtainable**. Scope was reconstructed by reading the remote-pairing surface directly (desktop `companion/{pairing,srp,http,config,index}.ts`, `PairDeviceDialog/*`; companion `core/crypto/Srp.kt`, `core/crypto/KeyDerivation.kt`, `core/pairing/{PairingCode,RemotePairingHandshake}.kt`, `app-phone/.../{PairingScreen,PairingState,RemotePairingErrorMapping,BridgeCompanionRepository}.kt`, `PROTOCOL.md §4.9`). Untracked-file enumeration and hunk boundaries are therefore unverified.\n\n## Candidate findings (6, recall-biased)\n\n**1.** `file`: packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts `line`: 1063\n`summary`: `chargeKexComputation` is charged before `A` is range-checked (validation lives inside `srpServerHandshake` at srp.ts:497), and the monotone ceiling is never refunded on `SrpError`.\n`failure_scenario`: 16 distinct `CF-Connecting-IP` values × 4 `begin`+`kex` pairs (= `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE`), each `kex` carrying a canonical 384-byte `A` of all zeros → `decodeClientPublic` passes, `chargeKexComputation` charges, `srpAssertClientPublic` throws → 64 charges, zero exponentiations. `state.kexComputations` now hits `MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW`. The real phone's first `kex` gets `429 pair_rate_limited`; phone maps 429 → `RATE_LIMITED`, terminal per PROTOCOL.md §4.9. The user-opened window is unusable for its remaining life — the exact \"junk rate-limits the real phone out\" outcome the docblock at 1740 says cannot happen.\n\n**2.** `file`: packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts `line`: 2547\n`summary`: `state.remoteRequests += 1` runs *before* `chargePerSource`, so requests already over a source's own cap still burn the never-refunded window-wide aggregate.\n`failure_scenario`: One IP sends 1024 `POST /v1/pair/begin`. First 32 admit; requests 33-1024 each increment `remoteRequests` and *then* throw the per-source 429. `remoteRequests` reaches `REMOTE_PAIR_MAX_REQUESTS_PER_WINDOW` (32×32). Every subsequent request from *any* source, including the real phone's `begin`, is refused at 2541 with `429 pair_rate_limited`. Contradicts the stated invariant at 352-357 (\"refuses THAT source and only that source; the real phone keeps its full allowance\") and the aggregate-bound rationale at 2522-2534.\n\n**3.** `file`: app-phone/src/main/java/family/khaira/supersetcompanion/phone/ui/pairing/PairingScreen.kt `line`: 113\n`summary`: The typed 8-digit pairing secret is held in `rememberSaveable`, so it is serialised into Android saved-instance-state and escapes the app process.\n`failure_scenario`: User types `12345678`, then backgrounds the app / rotates / the process is killed while the Failed(RESTART_REQUIRED) screen is up. `digits` is written into the Activity `Bundle` handed to `system_server` for task restoration, outliving the desktop's 120 s window and the app process. ~26.6 bits of authenticator now sits outside the process boundary the desktop side goes to great lengths to keep it inside of (pairing.ts:488-497 \"MUST NOT be logged, persisted...\"). `submittedAtMs` (line 119) is legitimately saveable; `digits` is not.\n\n**4.** `file`: app-phone/src/main/java/family/khaira/supersetcompanion/phone/data/BridgeCompanionRepository.kt `line`: 961\n`summary`: `withTimeout(PAIRING_WINDOW_MS)` can cancel *after* the desktop has persisted the device record, and the resulting `TimeoutCancellationException` maps to `WINDOW_CLOSED` — advice that omits the orphan record.\n`failure_scenario`: Confirm reaches the desktop; `sealPairedResponse` sets `consumed`, `deps.onPaired` writes the device record with `K_dev`, then the sealed 200 is slow (tunnel latency) and the phone's 120 s timeout fires. Phone stores nothing and shows \"The pairing window closed. Start it again on the desktop.\" The desktop now holds a paired device whose key no phone has. Repeat attempts mint fresh `deviceId`s, accumulating orphan records. Contrast `CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED` (PairingState.kt:166-169), which *does* tell the user to check and remove the desktop record — this path needs the same honesty.\n\n**5.** `file`: core/src/main/java/family/khaira/supersetcompanion/core/pairing/RemotePairingHandshake.kt `line`: 382\n`summary`: `response.body.bytes()` reads the pairing hop's response with no size bound, on both success and error paths.\n`failure_scenario`: The public pairing host is unauthenticated and TLS-terminated at Cloudflare; a hostile edge, a captive portal, or a misrouted DNS answer returns a 400 with a multi-hundred-MB body. `bytes()` buffers it all before `remoteFailureOf` parses, OOM-killing the companion app. Desktop-side bodies are bounded at `MAX_PAIR_BODY_BYTES` (http.ts:936-948); the phone has no counterpart — `B` (384 bytes), `pid`/`pairSalt` and the sealed envelope have known ceilings the reader could enforce.\n\n**6.** `file`: packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts `line`: 2369\n`summary`: `remoteBurnMemo` is cleared only by `openRemotePairingReserved`; opening a QR/LAN window leaves a stale burn memo live.\n`failure_scenario`: Remote window burns on three wrong codes → `rememberRemoteBurn()`. Within 60 s the user opens a **QR** window (`openPairingWindowReserved`, ~line 2219, never touches `remoteBurnMemo`). `currentRemotePairing()` returns `null` (state.kind is `\"lan\"`), so `handleRemotePairRequest` at http.ts:833 consults `remoteWindowWasBurned()` → `true` → answers every internet caller `403 pair_code_burned` instead of the bare 404 that \"no remote window\" is supposed to be indistinguishable from. Also mis-advises a phone that a *current* code is dead.\n\n## Areas checked and found clean\nSRP-6a arithmetic and RFC 5054 padding (`srpPad`/`srpBigIntToBytes` refuse-not-truncate, `PAD(N)||PAD(g)` for `k`, `u==0` and degenerate-`A` rejection); the fixed 4-bit-window secret ladder's constant operation count and `table[0] = N+1`; desktop↔Android label/salt/identity/transcript-prefix agreement (`sc/v3-srp`, `superset-companion/pair/v3-srp`, `\"sc/v3 remote-pair\"`, 825-byte transcript, `PAD(S)` IKM); `base64UrlDecode` canonicality (kills the non-canonical-`deviceId` map-key hazard); `pair_unknown_candidate` = HTTP 400 + parsed-body-only mapping with no alias, no status fallback, no automatic retry (`FAILURE_BY_STATUS` has no 400 entry; `resubmitsSameCode` gated on user tap); `assertCandidateSource` pinning on both `begin` and `kex`; candidate key wiping on every drop path (`zeroKexSession`/`wipeAllPairingKeys`/`clearKexSessions`) and `completePairing`'s outermost `finally`; `(PAIR-ONE-WINDOW-ATOMIC)` reserve/claim/release with `claimOrTearDown`; host isolation (`classifyRequestHost`, duplicate-`Host` and root-dot handling, load-time two-host assertion); `cf-connecting-ip` single-value/IP-literal gate; step-4 AAD request-form + reserved nonce counter 0; `PairingCode` Arabic-Indic/fullwidth→ASCII normalisation and `passwordBytes` ASCII guard; QR/LAN `sc/v1` path bytes unchanged.\n\n**FIX-FIRST**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>182712</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>55</tool_uses><duration_ms>588588</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:16:42.261Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>acc432574a787ddc1</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\acc432574a787ddc1.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review pairing implementation altitude\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Tooling note (affects coverage):** Bash unusable this session — every invocation dies in shell init (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching '''`), including `echo hi`. So no `git diff HEAD`, no untracked-file listing, no test/lint run. Scope reconstructed by reading the remote-pairing surface in both repos (host `companion/pairing.ts`, `srp.ts`, `http.ts`, `config.ts`, `types.ts`, trpc `companion.ts`, `PairDeviceDialog.tsx`; Android `RemotePairingHandshake.kt`, `Srp.kt`, `PairingCode.kt`, `PairingClient.kt`, `RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt`, `PairingState.kt`, `PairingScreen.kt`, `BridgeCompanionRepository.kt`, `CompanionConstants.kt`, `Pairing.kt`, `SealedEnvelope.kt`). Untracked-only files may be unreviewed.\n\n```\nCANDIDATES (6, recall-biased, finder only)\n```\n\n**1. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1063**\nsummary: `chargeKexComputation(state)` is charged BEFORE `A` is range-validated, so refusals that perform zero modexp still burn the monotone 64-derivation ceiling — inverting this module's own (PAIR-ATTEMPT-ORDER) \"decode/validate before charging\" rule.\nfailure_scenario: Attacker rotates ≥16 source IPs (per-source intro cap 4, source table evicts → refunds), does 64 `begin`+`kex` pairs with `A = PAD(1)` — 384 correct-width bytes, so `decodeClientPublic` passes, charge lands, then `srpAssertClientPublic` throws `SrpError` → `400 pair_bad_key_agreement`, no exponentiation performed. 128 of the window's 1024 admitted requests spent. Real phone's single legitimate `kex` then hits `kexComputations &gt;= 64` → `429 pair_rate_limited` → \"Your desktop is turning away attempts\". Attacker rate-limits a legitimate device out of a window the user is standing in front of, which §4.7/(PAIR-JUNK-SURVIVAL) says is exactly what must be impossible. Deeper mechanism: the CPU-cost ceiling belongs after the free validation gate (validate → charge → derive), same shape as `chargeConfirmAttempt` after `decodeExact`.\n\n**2. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt:113**\nsummary: The typed 8-digit code lives in `rememberSaveable`, i.e. Compose SavedStateRegistry → Activity saved-instance-state Bundle — a copy of the secret outside every zeroization/one-place discipline the rest of the batch enforces.\nfailure_scenario: User types `1234-5678`; `digits` is written into the saved-state Bundle held by system_server. Rotate the phone / background it / let the process be killed and restored from Recents: the code is restored from that Bundle, so it survives the 120 s window, process death and config change, and cannot be overwritten (immutable `String`, framework-owned storage). Meanwhile `PairingCode.passwordBytes` is zeroized, `Srp.startClient` zeroizes the password, and the desktop's own dialog documents \"no path to a store, to disk, or to a later render\". On success (`Paired`) and on non-resubmittable failures other than the explicit clear paths, the entry is never cleared. Deeper mechanism: retained-code retry (a frozen requirement) should hold the digits in the same one-place, non-persisted, explicitly-cleared secret holder the flow already implies — plain `remember`/ViewModel field cleared on terminal state — never framework saved state.\n\n**3. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:341**\nsummary: Post-confirm sealed-reply defects (`kind != RESPONSE`, header `deviceId` mismatch) are labelled `CODE_WRONG`, but they can only occur AFTER the desktop persisted the device record and consumed the window.\nfailure_scenario: Phone POSTs `/v1/pair/confirm`; desktop verifies `macPhone`, sets `consumed = true`, writes the device record + audit line, seals and returns. Reply arrives mis-addressed or with the wrong envelope kind (desktop bug, edge rewrite, a `deviceId` collision in the header path) → `PairingFailure.CODE_WRONG` → phone shows \"That code wasn't accepted. Check the code … then type it again\", and `resubmitsSameCode` is false so the field is cleared and a retype is invited. The window is already burned, so the retype gets `404 → WINDOW_CLOSED`; the desktop keeps an orphan device record holding a `K_dev` the phone never stored, and the user is never told to remove it. The neighbouring `CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED` text exists precisely for this state and is bypassed. Deeper mechanism: one lifecycle predicate — \"the confirm request left this phone\" — should classify every subsequent failure as may-have-paired, in the handshake, instead of per-check labels chosen at each throw site.\n\n**4. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:352**\nsummary: Step-4 AAD binds `CompanionConstants.PROTOCOL_CURRENT` (the moving negotiation ceiling, also `CLIENT_PROTOCOL_MAX`) where the host binds a deliberately frozen `PROTOCOL_VERSION_AT_PAIRING = 1`; same drift copied at `PairingClient.kt:273`.\nfailure_scenario: A future protocol bump sets `PROTOCOL_CURRENT = 2` (its documented purpose — it is the negotiation max). The host still builds the pairing AAD with `1`. The two AADs then differ by one byte, every GCM tag fails, `SealedEnvelope.open` throws → `CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED`/`CODE_WRONG`, i.e. \"your code was wrong\" for every user on both flows, with no diagnostic — byte-for-byte the failure mode the host header records an earlier revision already shipped (\"the two AADs differed by 16 bytes and every pairing failed the GCM tag and reported `pair_bad_mac`\"). Deeper mechanism: a frozen `PROTOCOL_AT_PAIRING` constant on the Android side, one per flow-independent pairing AAD, so the pinned value cannot be reached by a negotiation edit; the remote wire version is already versioned separately (`REMOTE_PAIRING_PAYLOAD_VERSION = 2`), the AAD version is not.\n\n**5. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1242**\nsummary: `assertCandidateSource` puts transport identity (`CF-Connecting-IP`) inside the candidate state machine, so a source change mid-exchange is reported as `pair_wrong_peer` — a hostile-peer verdict with no retry path — rather than as a throttling concern.\nfailure_scenario: Phone pairs over mobile data / on a Wi-Fi↔LTE boundary. `begin` arrives from IP a; the OkHttp pooled connection drops between the three requests and `kex` (or `confirm`) arrives from IP b (carrier CGNAT rebalance, network handover). `assertCandidateSource` → `refuseCandidateRewrite(\"a different source address\")` → `400 pair_wrong_peer` → phone renders \"Something else answered instead of the desktop. Refused.\" and `resubmitsSameCode` is false, so the still-valid on-screen code is cleared and the user is pushed to mint a new window. The candidate's own key material (`A`, then `macPhone`) already proves ownership cryptographically. Deeper mechanism: IP belongs to the admission/rate layer (`chargeRemoteRequest`, `kexSourceCounts`); candidate ownership should be proven by the candidate's committed `A`/MAC, and the eviction log's own promise (\"it can simply begin again\") should hold for this path too.\n\n**6. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1970` (consumed at `http.ts:833`)**\nsummary: `remoteWindowWasBurned()` is a process-global, source-agnostic memo, so for 60 s after a burn the pairing host answers `403 pair_code_burned` to any caller on the internet instead of the bare 404 the design leans on for indistinguishability.\nfailure_scenario: Attacker (or any scanner) POSTs `/v1/pair/begin` to the public pairing host with no window open. Normally: bodyless 404, identical to an unknown path — \"outside a window a human deliberately opened, this surface is not discoverable at all\". Within 60 s of a burn it instead gets `403 {\"code\":\"pair_code_burned\"}`, confirming that this machine runs the bridge, that a human opened a code window recently, and that three MACs failed against it. An attacker who deliberately burns a window (it holds a candidate, so it can spend three strikes — the accepted residual) gets a positive acknowledgement of that success, and repeated probing yields an occupancy oracle on the desktop. Deeper mechanism: the honest-error memo needs the same scoping as the rest of the hop — remembered against the candidate/source that actually burned it (or delivered only to a caller that can name the burned `pid`) — so honesty to the real phone does not become discoverability for everyone else.\n\nChecked and clean: SRP arithmetic and padding (host `srpPad`/`srpSecretModPow` fixed-window ladder vs BouncyCastle `rfc5054_3072` + `SRP6Util.calculateU`, identity `\"sc/v3 remote-pair\"` and `x = H(salt || H(I \":\" P))` agree byte-for-byte); `B` width/range/degenerate + `u != 0` validation on both sides; transcript shapes (117 vs 825 bytes) and profile domain separation (`sc/v1` literals untouched); atomic one-window reservation (`reserveWindowSlot`/`claimOrTearDown`/`releaseWindowSlot`); candidate key wipe on every exit path (`zeroKexSession`/`wipeAllPairingKeys`/`clearKexSessions`); nonce counter 0 reservation; persist-before-seal ordering and `onPaired` uniqueness/keyStore rollback; two-host isolation (`classifyRequestHost`/`routeByHost`/`normaliseHostHeader`, load-time host-collision assert); `cf-connecting-ip` single-value/IP-literal validation; body caps and `no-store` on every pairing response; Unicode digit normalisation (`Character.digit` → ASCII, `passwordBytes` guard, host `assertAsciiDigitCode`); wire error vocabulary parity host↔Android including the frozen `400 pair_unknown_candidate` contract and bodyless-404 fallback; desktop dialog secret containment, serialized window ops, unmount close, and server-owned verdict.\n\n**FIX-FIRST**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>206549</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>63</tool_uses><duration_ms>590453</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:18:41.391Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>add2c8840ff906cd5</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_xXzNoM2twyKfB2xw520uCrHq</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\add2c8840ff906cd5.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Trace pairing cross-file contracts\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Tooling caveat\n\nBash tool nonfunctional this session (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching `''` on every invocation, incl. `echo ok`). No `git diff HEAD`/untracked listing obtained → candidates derived by reading both working trees directly (Read/Grep/Glob) around the frozen-contract surface. Change-attribution to the diff is therefore inferred, not proven. Finder phase only; nothing verified.\n\n## Candidates (6)\n\n**1.**\n- `file`: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts\n- `line`: 2547\n- `summary`: `chargeRemoteRequest` increments the monotone window total *before* `chargePerSource` can refuse, so per-source-refused requests still spend the global cap.\n- `failure_scenario`: One IP sends 1024 `POST /v1/pair/begin` inside the 120 s window. Requests 1-32 are admitted; 33-1024 throw `429 pair_rate_limited` from `chargePerSource` — but each already did `state.remoteRequests += 1`. `remoteRequests` reaches `REMOTE_PAIR_MAX_REQUESTS_PER_WINDOW` (32×32=1024). The real phone's first `begin` now hits the ceiling at line 2541 → `429 pair_rate_limited`, and PROTOCOL.md §4.9 forbids the phone retrying. Junk with no crypto rate-limits the legitimate device out of its own window — the exact invariant config.ts:212-215 and the `remoteRequestCounts` doc (pairing.ts:348-357) claim is held.\n\n**2.**\n- `file`: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\SessionViewModel.kt\n- `line`: 360\n- `summary`: `pairFromCode` launches into `viewModelScope` with no in-flight guard; the new retained-code \"Try again\" button makes a double-fire trivial.\n- `failure_scenario`: User double-taps \"Try again\" (PairingScreen.kt:186, plain `Button`, no enabled-gate, state only flips to `Exchanging` asynchronously). Two concurrent `begin→kex→confirm` exchanges with two different deviceIds. Desktop is single-use: the first `confirm` sets `state.consumed`, the second gets `410 pair_window_closed` → `pairingState` (single MutableStateFlow, last writer wins) ends as `Failed(WINDOW_CLOSED)` overwriting `Paired`, and `pushRegistrar.onPairingSucceeded()` is skipped for that branch. Phone is paired and stored but shows \"The pairing window closed. Start it again on the desktop.\" with FCM token never registered → no push ever arrives.\n\n**3.**\n- `file`: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt\n- `line`: 382\n- `summary`: `response.body.bytes()` reads every pairing response fully into memory with no size cap, on an unauthenticated public endpoint.\n- `failure_scenario`: The pairing origin (public Cloudflare host, or anything terminating that hostname) answers `POST /v1/pair/kex` with `200` and a multi-hundred-MB body. `bytes()` buffers all of it before `Base64Url.decode(kex.bPublic, \"B\")` (line 312, no expected-length arg) and `Srp.decodeGroupElement` get a chance to reject → `OutOfMemoryError` on the phone, killing the app mid-pairing. Asymmetric with the host, which caps at `MAX_PAIR_BODY_BYTES` (4 KiB) via `readBoundedBody` before parsing.\n\n**4.**\n- `file`: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt\n- `line`: 113\n- `summary`: The typed 8-digit pairing code is held in `rememberSaveable`, i.e. written to Android saved-instance-state, so a ~26.6-bit shared secret outlives its 120 s window.\n- `failure_scenario`: User types the code, pairing fails with `RESTART_REQUIRED`, the app is backgrounded. Compose writes `digits` into the Activity `Bundle`; Android persists saved state (and may surface it via `onSaveInstanceState` dumps / bugreports / heap capture) with no expiry tied to the desktop window. An attacker with post-hoc device access reads a code the desktop deliberately never persists (pairing.ts:490-496 forbids logging/persisting it, and the desktop zeroes the code bytes at open). Retained-code retry needs the digits in memory only, not in saved state.\n\n**5.**\n- `file`: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\shell\\CompanionShell.kt\n- `line`: 185\n- `summary`: `onRetry = session::cancelPairing` republishes the existing paired record instead of resetting to entry, so re-pairing an already-paired phone cannot be retried.\n- `failure_scenario`: A phone that is already paired opens PAIRING to re-pair, submits a code, gets a non-resubmittable failure (e.g. `CODE_WRONG`). \"Try again\" (PairingScreen.kt:191-195) clears `digits` and calls `onRetry` → `repository.cancelPairing()` → `publishPairing(pairedRecordOrNull())` → state becomes `PairingState.Paired` with the OLD record. The screen renders \"Paired with &lt;old origin&gt;…\" + a Done button; the code field never returns. The user is told they are paired when the attempt they just made failed, and has no in-place path back to entry.\n\n**6.**\n- `file`: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt\n- `line`: 203\n- `summary`: In the QR entry the `Failed` branch offers only \"Start again\" wired to `onCancel`, which navigates off the pairing route rather than resetting the scanner.\n- `failure_scenario`: QR pairing fails with `UNREACHABLE` (wrong Wi-Fi) — the single most common recoverable QR failure. The only control is \"Start again\" → `onCancel` → `session.cancelPairing()` + `route = CompanionRoute.AGENTS` (CompanionShell.kt:181-184). The user is thrown out of pairing entirely and must re-navigate Diagnostics → Pair, and the camera permission/scanner is torn down and rebuilt. The CODE entry got a reset-in-place path; QR did not.\n\n## Checked and found consistent\n\nSRP domain separation and identity/label/transcript-prefix parity across repos (`sc/v3 remote-pair`, `sc/v3-srp*`, salt prefix); fixed-window ladder guards (`srpSecretModPow` exponent width, `table[0] = N+1`, `srpAssertClientPublic`, `u == 0`, `B mod N == 0`); `srpBigIntToBytes`/`Srp.pad` exact-width refusal on both sides; `base64UrlDecode`/`decodeWireId` canonicality vs `decodeExact` (no outer-accepts/inner-refuses drift with device-store); the frozen wire contract itself (`400` + body literal `pair_unknown_candidate` on kex and confirm, no `pair_restart_required`, no alias, no 400 entry in `FAILURE_BY_STATUS`, no automatic re-begin/re-confirm, `retryOnConnectionFailure(false)`); `requireRemoteCandidate` charging nothing (no confirm attempt, no MAC strike) and its position after window/version/source/pid; `pair_wrong_peer` retained for rewrite/wrong-source/wrong-pid; LAN v1 vocabulary unchanged; `evictOldestKexSessionsIfFull` oldest-first + `zeroKexSession` wipe; monotone `kexComputations` charged only for genuinely new derivations; sealed step-4 AAD path/protocol-version parity (`/v1/pair/confirm`, protocol 1) and `PROTOCOL_CURRENT == PAIRING_REMOTE_WIRE_VERSION` alignment; host/Access isolation (`routeByHost` matrix, `normaliseHostHeader`, `PAIRING_PUBLIC_HOST !== BRIDGE_PUBLIC_HOST` startup assert); window-slot reserve/claim/release and both `close()` paths (handle preservation, timer clear, key/salt/verifier destruction, burn memo); Unicode digit normalisation (`Character.digit` → ASCII, `passwordBytes` guard, `assertAsciiDigitCode`); `GroupedCodeTransformation` offset mapping; docs (PROTOCOL.md §4.9.3 normative text matches implementation; FEATURES.md marker table consistent with sibling `(PAIR-*)` markers).\n\n**FIX-FIRST**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>188562</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>68</tool_uses><duration_ms>709596</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
