{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:21:18.643Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a6fd47450b7f4ff77</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_wbsTnEXkEoVUXx2JoMbXA0uW</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a6fd47450b7f4ff77.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify countdown rerenders\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>CONFIRMED (low magnitude).\n\n**Corrected location:** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\components\\PairDeviceDialog\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx:189` (write) and `:195` (`setInterval(tick, 250)`). Candidate's `:253` is wrong — that line is the pairingState poll's error handler.\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\components\\PairDeviceDialog\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx:189\nDefect: `setRemainingMs(left)` fires every 250 ms while the only consumer quantises to whole seconds (`Math.ceil(remainingMs / 1000)`, :359) → ~4x the renders needed for identical visible output.\nFailure: dialog left open for a full 120 s window → 480 state commits / PairingFlow renders where 120 change pixels; the other 360 re-run PairingFlow + QrCode + DialogFooter/Button and reconcile the SVG subtree for no visual delta. Multiplies per \"Show a new code\" click.\nFix: keep the 250 ms interval (its `left &lt;= 0` check is what promptly hands off to `closing`) but make the state write quantised — store seconds, e.g. `setRemainingMs(prev =&gt; Math.ceil(prev/1000) === Math.ceil(left/1000) ? prev : left)` or `setSecondsLeft(Math.max(0, Math.ceil(left/1000)))` — so identical visible values hit React's Object.is bailout.\n```\n\nEvidence:\n- Only two `remainingMs` reads in this file: declaration :118 and `secondsLeft` :359. The other `remainingMs` grep hits are unrelated (`resyncAgentStatus.ts`).\n- No subsecond consumption of the *state*: the subsecond value that matters (`left &lt;= 0` → `{kind:\"closing\"}`, :193) is a local from `expiresAtMs - Date.now()`, not from state. So interval frequency is functionally justified; the state write cadence is not.\n- No React bailout: `left` decreases by ~250 ms each tick, so `Object.is(prev, next)` is always false → full render + reconcile every tick. Bailout would only apply to identical values.\n- QrCode (`...\\components\\QrCode\\QrCode.tsx:32`) is **not** `React.memo`, so its body re-runs on every parent render; `useMemo([value])` with a stable `phase.qrUri` does spare the ~2800-module encode and returns the same `path` string reference, so DOM attrs aren't rewritten. That caps the cost at React work, not layout/paint — which is why this is MINOR, not MAJOR. The comment at QrCode.tsx:28-30 (\"a visible cost every time the countdown re-renders the dialog\") shows the author already recognised countdown-driven re-render pressure.\n- `aria-live=\"polite\"` (:373-378): identical text → React writes nothing → no spurious screen-reader announcements from the extra renders.\n- Interval lifecycle clean: keyed on `expiresAtMs` only, immediate `tick()`, `clearInterval` on cleanup (:185-198); no leak or double-timer.\n\nChecked and clean: interval lifecycle/cleanup, `expiresAtMs` effect keying, QrCode memoization correctness, poll effect in-flight guard, absence of other `remainingMs` consumers.\n\n**Verdict: CONFIRMED** — real redundant-render inefficiency, ~360 wasted renders per 120 s window, bounded impact (memoized QR path, no DOM writes).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>31598</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>9</tool_uses><duration_ms>133115</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:22:13.987Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>addb9326abc49c7ec</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_UzrcGcDvGKA3mf71na9ntJ6W</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\addb9326abc49c7ec.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify HTTP client leak\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## CONFIRMED\n\n**Corrected location:** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\PairingClient.kt:223` (line number exact as claimed; method is `pairWithCode`, not `pairRemote`)\n\n**Summary:** `pairWithCode`'s default arg `handshake: RemotePairingHandshake = RemotePairingHandshake()` is re-evaluated on every invocation and falls through to `RemotePairingHandshake`'s own `baseClient: OkHttpClient = OkHttpClient()`, so the remote-pairing path is the one place in the codebase that does not derive from `CompanionCore`'s single process-lifetime `httpClient`.\n\n**Concrete retry/resource scenario:** User mistypes the 8-digit code. `BridgeCompanionRepository.pairFromCode` (line 962) calls `core.pairingClient().pairWithCode(...)` with no `handshake` → attempt 1 builds `OkHttpClient()` #1 with its own `ConnectionPool`, derives the pinned-TLS client from it, runs begin/kex/confirm, fails `CODE_WRONG`. User retries twice more inside the desktop's 120 s window (which burns after 3 strikes) → clients #2 and #3, each with a fresh pool. No attempt can reuse the previous attempt's connection to `REMOTE_PAIRING_ORIGIN`: every retry pays a full TCP + TLS handshake on a mobile link, in a flow already racing a 120 s window, and each attempt's idle socket is retained by its orphaned pool until the 5-minute keepAlive eviction instead of being returned to the shared pool. Same for any future base-client configuration (proxy, CT/logging interceptor, socket factory) — remote pairing would silently not receive it.\n\n**Impact correction (the candidate overstates two parts):**\n- **Not** an unbounded leak. Nothing needs closing — no `Cache` is configured, and no `evictAll`/`shutdown` exists anywhere in the repo (grep: only `PairingScreen.kt:444`, an unrelated camera `Executor`). Retention is bounded by idle eviction.\n- **Not** \"separate dispatchers\" in any thread-cost sense. `RemotePairingHandshake.post` (line 381) uses synchronous `client.newCall(request).execute()`, which never touches `Dispatcher.executorService`, and okhttp 5.4.0 (`gradle/libs.versions.toml:14`) backs connection-pool cleanup with the shared `TaskRunner`. No per-client threads are created.\n\nSo: real convention break plus real per-retry connection/handshake waste — **MINOR**, not a leak.\n\n**Evidence**\n- `PairingClient.kt:223` — default arg, evaluated per call by Kotlin's `$default` bridge; no caller ever supplies it (`pairWithCode` has exactly two hits repo-wide: the declaration and the one caller).\n- `RemotePairingHandshake.kt:80` — `baseClient: OkHttpClient = OkHttpClient()`; line 86 derives the real client via `baseClient.newBuilder()`, i.e. the class is built to inherit a shared pool but is handed a fresh one.\n- `CompanionCore.kt:31,49,96,98` — one `httpClient` field threaded into `PairingClient`, `SealedTransport`, `EventStreamClient`; `PairingClient.kt:52-63` and `SealedTransport.kt:89,110-111` both derive with `baseClient.newBuilder()`, establishing the shared-base convention that line 223 breaks.\n- `CompanionContainer.kt:90-106` — the core is built once in a `by lazy` repository, so that shared client lives for the process.\n- `BridgeCompanionRepository.kt:948-1002` — user-invokable, retryable entry point; failures set `PairingState.Failed` and return, leaving retry entirely to the user.\n- No reuse/cleanup invariant exists that would excuse this: `PairingClient` does not even retain `baseClient` (only the derived `client`), so there is currently no path by which the handshake could inherit the shared pool.\n\n**Fix direction (not applied):** retain `baseClient` in `PairingClient` and construct the handshake from it (`RemotePairingHandshake(baseClient = baseClient)`), or make the parameter non-defaulted and have `CompanionCore` build the handshake with `httpClient`.\n\n**Verdict: FIX-FIRST** (MINOR — mechanism confirmed, impact bounded)</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>54443</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>21</tool_uses><duration_ms>188336</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:22:38.041Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>aa60a2b4b066b592e</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_LpAiIAEJevAFAZuwaNcM4D0a</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\aa60a2b4b066b592e.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify synchronous SRP blocking\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**REFUTED**\n\nCorrected path/line: candidate file does not exist. No `packages/host-service/src/companion/srp.ts` (nothing at `:321`), no SRP implementation anywhere in `packages/` or `apps/`. Actual code under the described role: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts` (kex handler `handleKex` at line 417; confirm handler `handleConfirm` at line 615) plus `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\crypto.ts:326` (`x25519`).\n\nSummary: every load-bearing premise fails.\n\n1. **No SRP, no 3072-bit exponentiation.** Pairing is X25519 ECDH. `crypto.ts:326-351` calls Node's native `diffieHellman()` on `KeyObject`s built from raw RFC 8410 DER prefixes; key gen is `generateKeyPairSync(\"x25519\")` (`crypto.ts:353-368`). No bigint math, no `modPow`, no `createDiffieHellman`, no MODP/ffdhe group constant, no fixed-window exponentiation code anywhere in the tree. Cost per op is native curve25519 (tens of microseconds), not a multi-millisecond modexp.\n2. **Zero asymmetric ops per kex.** `handleKex` (`pairing.ts:417-487`) does: window check, `v` check, pid constant-time compare, base64url decodes (`deviceId` 16 B, `pPub` 32 B), string/enum/range validation, a `Map` lookup, and a bookkeeping insert plus O(n≤16) eviction scan. It never calls `x25519`, never derives keys. The \"twice per unauthenticated kex\" claim has no code behind it.\n3. **The single ECDH sits behind the authenticated-path caps.** `x25519` is reached only via `derivePairingKeys` (`pairing.ts:367`) from `handleConfirm`, which requires a matching `pid` (`pairing.ts:626`) AND an existing candidate session (`:628`), then charges `chargeConfirmAttempt` (`:642`, cap `PAIRING_ATTEMPTS_PER_WINDOW`), with `burnMac` (`:841`) consuming the window after `PAIRING_MAX_BAD_MACS`. Upper bound is a handful of ECDH ops per 120 s window, not 64+.\n4. **The \"64-computation flood\" number is arithmetic over caps that gate storage, not crypto.** `MAX_KEX_SESSIONS = 16` (`:148`), `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE = 4` (`:156`), `MAX_KEX_SOURCES = 32` (`:158`). Even the true worst case (32 sources x 4 = 128 introductions) buys only 128 x (4 KB-bounded body parse + 48 B of base64 decode). Body size is capped at `MAX_PAIR_BODY_BYTES = 4 * 1024` (`:139`, enforced in `readBody` `:1229-1245`), and `headersTimeout`/`requestTimeout` are set (`:1066-1067`).\n5. **Async boundaries are intact where they matter.** `route` is async; the only awaits on the confirm path are genuine I/O (`deps.onPaired`, `deps.loadAccessToken`, `flushed`). The listener is a distinct socket (`0.0.0.0:47611`, 120 s lifetime, `:1076`) but shares the event loop — irrelevant given per-request work is microsecond-scale byte handling.\n\nConcrete load -&gt; observable failure: none reproducible. Saturating the documented caps (32 distinct sources x 4 deviceIds x 4 KB bodies inside one 120 s window) yields ~128 requests of pure parse/validate work with no asymmetric crypto — well under a millisecond aggregate of CPU. Bridge/event/answer traffic on the host-service loop sees no measurable contiguous stall. The claimed damage requires an exponentiation that does not exist in this codebase.\n\nEvidence: full read of `pairing.ts` (1293 lines) and the `x25519`/`generateX25519KeyPair`/HKDF region of `crypto.ts`; content searches for `modPow|3072|kex|powmod|modexp` across `packages/host-service/src` (hits only in `pairing.ts` comments, `limits.ts:93`, `types.ts:297`, and an unrelated `terminal.seq-catchup.node-test.ts:719` `\"blob 3072\"`), for `srp` across `packages/` (no files) and `apps/` (only base64 inside `cursor.svg`), and for `MODP|ffdhe|srpVerifier|SRP_|modPow|createDiffieHellman` across both trees (no code hits). Note: `Bash` was unusable this session (shell init failure, `\"/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching `''\"`), so `git diff`/`git log` could not be run — findings rest on the checked-out file contents only.\n\nSecurity invariants/caps unchanged; nothing modified (read-only throughout).\n\nClean on inspection: kex/confirm cap layering, per-source throttle and eviction bounds, pid constant-time compare ordering, MAC-strike burn ordering, body-size and timeout limits, secret zeroing in `close()`/`handleConfirm` `finally`.\n\n**SHIP**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>50503</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>19</tool_uses><duration_ms>212397</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:22:40.957Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a099f92dbfd042d82</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_yaHdsgSqGqLwdUAABluBz6et</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a099f92dbfd042d82.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify no-window source shape\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>REFUTED\n\n**Corrected location:** candidate names `C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/packages/host-service/src/companion/http.ts:820`; that line is `export const ROUTES: Record&lt;SealedPath, SealedRoute&gt; = {` — the sealed route table. Nearest real construct with the described *shape* is `C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:1134-1138`.\n\n**Summary:** Every load-bearing identifier in the candidate is absent from the repo. `cf-connecting-ip` appears exactly once, in unrelated marketing code (`C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/apps/marketing/src/lib/email-rate-limit.ts:25`); `routeByHost`, `pair_unknown_candidate`, and any Cloudflare-IP-header read in host-service do not exist. The bridge's own caller identity comes from Cloudflare Access JWT (`cf-access-jwt-assertion`, `packages/host-service/src/companion/access-jwt.ts:55`), never from a client IP header, and no host-based routing or \"remote window exists\" branch exists on this listener. The missing-source state the candidate needs is unreachable because no code path reads that header at all.\n\n**The real analog, and why it is also not the described defect:**\n```ts\n// pairing.ts:1130-1138\nif (path === PAIR_PATH_KEX) {\n    const sourceKey = req.socket.remoteAddress;\n    if (sourceKey === undefined || sourceKey.length === 0) {\n        writeJson(res, 400, { code: \"unknown\" satisfies PairingErrorCode });\n        return;\n    }\n```\n- Different socket, different server: pairing is a separate `createServer` bound to `PAIRING_BIND_HOST:PAIRING_PORT` (LAN, 120 s lifetime, `pairing.ts:1063-1087`), not the Hono loopback bridge that owns the bodyless catch-all `app.all(\"*\", (c) =&gt; c.body(null, 404))` (`http.ts:1188`). No Access/tunnel hop, so no proxy IP header is in play.\n- No no-window 404 to be distinguishable *from*: when no window is open the pairing server is closed (`close()` at `pairing.ts:1016-1061`) → TCP refusal, not a 404 body. Comparison premise does not exist.\n- Code is `{\"code\":\"unknown\"}`, not `pair_unknown_candidate`, and the check is on `req.socket.remoteAddress`, not a header — so the \"frozen literal\" constraint is not touched either way.\n- Not observable: `req.socket.remoteAddress` is `undefined` only once the socket is destroyed; the handler runs after `readBody` resolves on `'end'` with a live socket. In the destroyed case `writeJson` writes to a dead socket and no caller reads the 400. Every other rejection on this listener (`405`, `404`, malformed-JSON `400`, `pair_version_unsupported`) already returns the same JSON envelope, so the branch adds no fingerprint.\n\n**Failure scenario:** none constructible. To reach the described state a caller would have to make host-service read `cf-connecting-ip`; nothing in `packages/host-service/src/` reads it, and the pairing listener never consults request headers for source identity.\n\n**Evidence:** repo-wide search for `routeByHost|pair_unknown|connecting.ip|cf_connecting` → single hit in `apps/marketing`; repo-wide `pair_unknown_candidate` → zero hits; `http.ts:700-960` read (line 820 is `ROUTES`); `pairing.ts:1099-1183` route handler and `pairing.ts:409-545` `handleKex`/`chargeKexIntroduction` read in full; `http.ts:1110-1208` shows Access-JWT-only auth plus the bodyless 404 catch-all on the loopback app; `handleKex`/`handleConfirm` have exactly one call site each (`pairing.ts:1142`, `1147`).\n\nChecked clean: pairing kex/confirm ordering and rate-limit charging, candidate-table eviction bounds, sealed route table coverage, bridge auth boundary and 404 catch-all.\n\n**Verdict: SHIP** (candidate refuted; nothing to fix)</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>47708</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>23</tool_uses><duration_ms>209924</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:22:42.756Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a408e9c5ecb977ada</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_33S6TIzpt8z70fDDrlZUUux1</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a408e9c5ecb977ada.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify SRP constant recompute\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**REFUTED**\n\nCandidate target does not exist. No SRP implementation anywhere in the repo.\n\nClosest real code: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts` (companion handshake), current lines 355–396 (`derivePairingKeys`) and 148 (`MAX_KEX_SESSIONS`).\n\nSummary\n- No file `packages/host-service/src/companion/srp.ts`. Companion dir holds 53 `.ts` files; `srp.ts` is not among them.\n- Repo-wide search for `srp.ts`, `secure-remote-password`, `srpVerifier`, `modPow`, `PAD(`, `H(PAD` across `.ts/.tsx/.md/.json/.kt/.swift` → zero matches. No SRP group constants, no `k = H(PAD(N)||PAD(g))`, no modular exponentiation.\n- Actual handshake is X25519 + HKDF, not SRP: `derivePairingKeys` does `x25519(dPriv, pPub)` → `IKM = Z || pairingCode` → `hkdfExtract` → `hkdfExpand` for `deviceKey`/`confirmPhone`/`confirmDesktop`. No group parameters exist to cache.\n- No BigInt modular arithmetic in the handshake path. Only `BigInt` uses in `companion/` are 64-bit counters/timestamps (`crypto.ts:426,558,676`, `device-store.ts` seq/epoch, `keys.ts` highWater) — unrelated to key exchange.\n- Stated \"64-kex cap\" also wrong: caps are `MAX_KEX_SESSIONS = 16` (pairing.ts:148) and `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE = 4` (pairing.ts:156).\n\nCost scenario: none. Nothing recomputes a group constant → zero per-handshake waste to eliminate. Both the file and the alleged hot computation are fabricated.\n\nEvidence: directory enumeration of `packages/host-service/src/companion` (no `srp.ts`); Read of `pairing.ts:286-405, 489-567`; four negative pattern searches (`\\bsrp\\b|kex` scoped to `host-service/src` returned only `limits.ts:93` doc text and pairing.ts kex references; `modPow|PAD\\(|H\\(PAD|srp` over `packages` → no matches).\n\nAreas checked clean: companion pairing/kex path, key-schedule helpers, kex session caps and rate limiting, BigInt usage in `companion/`.\n\nNote: Bash tool is nonfunctional in this environment (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching '` on every command, including `echo ok`), and Glob times out at 20s repo-wide; verification used Read plus path-scoped Grep. This limited git-history inspection — I could not run `git log` to check whether an `srp.ts` ever existed, but no reference to one survives in any tracked source, doc, or client file.\n\n**FIX-FIRST** — not applicable to code; the candidate itself should be dropped from the efficiency list.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>30777</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>23</tool_uses><duration_ms>217283</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:22:44.322Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a2a39361eb60fba36</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_F8ncuNMgYB1EwPnGE7KjQBMO</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a2a39361eb60fba36.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify PID decode churn\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**PLAUSIBLE**\n\n**Corrected location.** Store site: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:959-965` — `pidBytes = randomBytes(WIRE_ID_BYTES)` minted at :959, only `pid: base64UrlEncode(pidBytes)` retained at :965, raw bytes dropped. Cited :1097 is the closing brace of `openPairingWindow`, not the store.\n\nRedundant decode sites of trusted state:\n- `pairing.ts:857` — `constantTimeEquals(given, base64UrlDecode(state.pid))` inside `assertPidMatches`, reached from `handleKex` (:432) and `handleConfirm` (:626).\n- `pairing.ts:646` — `const pidBytes = base64UrlDecode(state.pid);` feeding `derivePairingKeys` (:653) and `buildPairingTranscript` (:658).\n\n**Summary.** Mechanism real. `state.pid` typed `PairingId = Base64Url` (`types.ts:33`, `types.ts:367`) → wire string only; every raw-byte consumer re-decodes. Redundant decodes per request: kex = 1, confirm = 2. Counting the necessary attacker-supplied decode, 2-3 total per request — candidate's per-request arithmetic holds.\n\n**Concrete cost — candidate's figures wrong.**\n- \"1024/window\" unsupported. `PAIRING_ATTEMPTS_PER_WINDOW = 5` (`config.ts:148`), burn at `PAIRING_MAX_BAD_MACS = 3` (`config.ts:63`), kex introductions capped `MAX_KEX_SOURCES 32 * MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE 4 = 128`.\n- Unbounded surface exists but isn't the one claimed: wrong-pid requests only increment/log (`noteUnattributedAttempt`, :870 — never limited), and a repeat kex with identical material returns at :468 before `chargeKexIntroduction` → both replayable for the full 120 s window at socket rate.\n- Per decode: 22-char input through `crypto.ts:750-762` = regex alphabet test + length check + `Buffer.from` + full re-encode + string compare ≈ 3 small allocations, sub-microsecond.\n- Same-path dominators: `JSON.parse` + Node HTTP socket/headers per request; on confirm additionally X25519 + HKDF (`derivePairingKeys`), 2x HMAC-SHA256 (:665, :674), sealing, plus awaited `deps.onPaired` (:681) and `deps.loadAccessToken` (:693) I/O. Redundant decode work is &lt;10 ms even at 10k flood requests/window. No observable effect at any reachable rate.\n\n**Ownership/security downsides — none blocking, one caveat.** `state.pid` is not secret (emitted in `kexResponse` :492, QR URI :986, log :1094), so a cached `pidBytes` needs no zeroization and `close()` (:1036-1037 zeroes only `pairingCode`/`dPriv`) stays correct. Caveat: it adds a mutable `Uint8Array` to session state against a module convention where byte fields carry \"zeroed on close\" semantics. The string form is required at :492, :986, :1001/:1008, :1094 → pid must remain a string; only an additive `pidBytes` companion is viable, never a replacement.\n\n**Maintenance angle is the only real value.** Precedent already exists: `KexSession` stores both `deviceId` (string) and `deviceIdBytes` (raw), consumed at :654/:659. A `pidBytes` companion matches that convention and deletes 2 decode sites.\n\n**Evidence.** Read `pairing.ts:182-199, 410-497, 610-709, 779-875, 930-1097`; `crypto.ts:750-762`; `types.ts:33-39, 286-378`; `config.ts:62-63, 148`; `limits.ts:38-43`. Bash unusable in this session (shell profile init error `unexpected EOF while looking for matching '`) → static read-only verification only, no runtime measurement.\n\nVote: accept as MINOR consistency cleanup; reject the efficiency framing and the 1024/window cost figure.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>39308</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>22</tool_uses><duration_ms>215996</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:22:45.120Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>acb422cff09a3381f</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\acb422cff09a3381f.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify protocol AAD pinning\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**CONFIRMED** (latent; no present-day breakage — `PROTOCOL_CURRENT == 1` today, so wire matches).\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:352\nDefect: step-6/step-4 pairing-confirm AAD binds the MOVING constant CompanionConstants.PROTOCOL_CURRENT where PROTOCOL.md and the host both freeze the byte at 0x01; nothing pins the two together.\nFailure: protocol 2 ships → PROTOCOL_CURRENT = 2 (its doc comment is \"What PROTOCOL.md describes\", and CLIENT_PROTOCOL_MAX derives from it, so a bump is the natural single edit). Phone then builds AAD ...||0x00||0x02 while host seals with PROTOCOL_VERSION_AT_PAIRING = 1 → GCM tag failure in SealedEnvelope.open on EVERY pairing, remote and LAN. Remote surfaces PairingFailure.CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED (\"the desktop's sealed reply could not be opened\"), LAN surfaces BAD_MAC — i.e. indistinguishable from MITM, exactly the regression the host comment at pairing.ts:1652-1662 records as already having happened once. 100% of new pairings dead; existing paired devices unaffected. Fails closed (no data loss), and no test catches it: RemotePairingHandshakeTest.kt:902 seals the fake confirm with PROTOCOL_CURRENT too (self-consistent → stays green), and the frozen differential vectors (CrossRuntimeDifferentialVectors20260729Test.kt:218, :443) hand-build AAD with literal 1 and never call openConfirmEnvelope, so they stay green as well.\nFix: add a dedicated frozen constant (e.g. PROTOCOL_VERSION_AT_PAIRING = 1, mirroring the host name, with a \"pairing precedes negotiation — never track PROTOCOL_CURRENT\" comment) and use it at both AAD sites; assert it in the frozen vector test so a future bump fails loudly at build time.\n```\n\nSame defect, second site: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\PairingClient.kt:273` (LAN `/pair/confirm`) — identical `PROTOCOL_CURRENT` use, same host constant on the other end.\n\nEvidence chain\n- Spec, `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\PROTOCOL.md:399` — `|| 0x00 || protocolVersion  1 byte, the negotiated protocol (§6); 0x01 before negotiation`; `:968` (remote step 6) — `METHOD \"POST\", PATH \"/v1/pair/confirm\", protocolVersion 0x01`. Pairing precedes `hello`, so the value is a literal, not \"current\".\n- Android constant, `...\\core\\CompanionConstants.kt:67-73` — `PROTOCOL_BASELINE = 0`, `PROTOCOL_CURRENT = 1`, `CLIENT_PROTOCOL_MAX = PROTOCOL_CURRENT`. Nothing marks `PROTOCOL_CURRENT` frozen; `PROTOCOL.md:3114` enumerates protocol versions as `0`, `1`, ... i.e. designed to grow.\n- Host frozen literal, `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:262` → `const PROTOCOL_VERSION_AT_PAIRING = 1;`, sole confirm-seal site at `:1666` (shared by both flows via `state.profile.confirmPath`) → no lockstep coupling to any Android constant.\n- No pin: only `PROTOCOL_CURRENT` occurrences are the two AAD sites, `CLIENT_PROTOCOL_MAX`, `BridgeClient.kt:428`, `SealedTransport.kt:63`, and tests that re-use the same constant.\n\nRelated, not the candidate: `...\\core\\transport\\SealedTransport.kt:57-63` documents \"`1` before `hello` (§3.3)\" yet initializes `protocolVersion = PROTOCOL_CURRENT` — same \"literal expressed as a moving constant\" shape on the pre-hello default, though that axis is legitimately negotiated afterwards.\n\nChecked clean: host confirm-seal path and its single AAD constant; `EnvelopeAad.forRequest/forResponse/forEventFrame` field order and separators vs §3.3; remote payload `v` checks (`requireRemoteVersion`, remote `v=2` per PROTOCOL.md:984); `pair_unknown_candidate` wire literal left untouched (no unsafety demonstrated); negotiation §6/§6.1 baseline handling. Not checked: git diff membership of these hunks — the Bash tool aborts on a broken shell profile (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching '`), so no `git diff`/test run was possible; findings are from source and docs only.\n\n**FIX-FIRST**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>67235</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>29</tool_uses><duration_ms>214966</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:22:49.145Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>aa97907abca83ce65</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\aa97907abca83ce65.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify sealed-response honesty\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## CONFIRMED\n\n**File:** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\n**Current line:** 1616 (`const access = await deps.loadAccessToken();`), inside `sealPairedResponse` — candidate line accurate, no drift.\n\n**Summary:** `loadAccessToken` runs *after* `onPaired` has already persisted the device record + K_dev; its throw is a plain `Error` → HTTP 500 `{\"code\":\"unknown\"}` → phone's fallback maps to `BAD_MAC` → user is told \"Nothing was stored.\" while the desktop holds a live paired device.\n\n**Failure scenario:** Remote code pairing, correct code, MAC verifies. `~/.superset/companion/cloudflare-access.json` is unreadable/torn/rotated-mismatched at that instant (deleted or rotated since bridge start, AV/backup lock → `EACCES`, OneDrive placeholder eviction, partial write during rotation). Sequence:\n1. `pairing.ts:1601` `state.consumed = true`.\n2. `pairing.ts:1604` `onPaired` → `deviceStore.create` + `keyStore.put` committed to disk.\n3. `pairing.ts:1616` throws (`config.ts:786` `readJsonFile` / `requireString` / `requireEqual`, all bare `throw new Error`).\n4. `pairing.ts:2498-2510` — `state.consumed` true → window closed quietly, error rethrown.\n5. `http.ts:891-918` — not `PairingError`/`CleartextError` → `pairingErrorResponse(500, \"unknown\")`.\n6. Phone `RemotePairingHandshake.kt:408` — `\"unknown\"` absent from `FAILURE_BY_WIRE`, `500` absent from `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` → `?: PairingFailure.BAD_MAC`.\n7. `RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt:28` → `PairingError.BAD_MAC` → `PairingState.kt:145` `\"Pairing failed to verify. Nothing was stored.\"`\n\nDamage: orphan paired device + live K_dev on desktop, phone stored nothing, and the message actively tells the user no cleanup is needed → stale device record persists unnoticed. Honesty/consistency defect, not corruption or lockout (§4.8 new-deviceId-per-pairing → re-pair still works).\n\n**Evidence:**\n- `pairing.ts:1577-1583` — codebase itself states \"`onPaired` writes to disk and `loadAccessToken` reads from it, so **either can throw** for reasons that have nothing to do with this exchange\". Reachability acknowledged in-repo; the wipe is handled, the *reporting* is not.\n- `pairing.ts:1560-1564` accepts \"desktop holding a key the phone never received — recoverable by re-pairing\" as the crash ordering trade-off, but nothing propagates that fact to the phone as a distinct code.\n- `config.ts:782-796` + `888-896` — sync `readFileSync` + strict field/identity validation, every failure a plain `Error`.\n- `index.ts:470` validates the same file at bridge start → failure at pairing time requires post-start change/transient, i.e. rare, not impossible.\n- Counter-evidence *against* intentionality: `BridgeCompanionRepository.kt:984-991` deliberately routes `ProtocolException` to `PROTOCOL_MISMATCH` rather than BAD_MAC precisely because \"by the time most of these can fire the desktop has already paired this device, so the claim would be false\". Same invariant, gap unpatched for the 500 path.\n- `RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt:17-18` reserves BAD_MAC for \"the exchange was rejected and **nothing was stored**\" — the 500 fallback violates that stated contract.\n- `PROTOCOL.md` §4.9.7 error table carries no code for a post-persist host failure; nothing to map.\n\n**Frozen contract untouched:** missing-candidate remains `pairing.ts:1149` `throw new PairingError(400, \"pair_unknown_candidate\")` → `FAILURE_BY_WIRE` → `UNKNOWN_CANDIDATE` → `RESTART_REQUIRED`. Not implicated, no change proposed.\n\n**Fix direction (not applied):** load the Access token *before* `onPaired` (nothing is persisted yet if it throws), or give post-persist failures their own `PairingError` code meaning \"paired on the desktop, reply not delivered\" so the phone stops asserting nothing was stored.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>63450</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>28</tool_uses><duration_ms>219070</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
