{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:07.861Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a156cda089b7159f4</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a156cda089b7159f4.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify gateway status mapping\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>VERDICT: **PLAUSIBLE**\n\nLocation (current): `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:470` (`FAILURE_BY_STATUS`, entries 471-478; default at line 408).\n\nSummary: Cloudflare gateway statuses (502/504/520-524/530) have no entry in `FAILURE_BY_STATUS`, their HTML bodies fail `PairErrorBody` decode, so they fall to the `PairingFailure.BAD_MAC` default → user text \"Pairing failed to verify. Nothing was stored.\" — verification/no-storage language for a tunnel/origin outage, while 503 alone routes to NETWORK.\n\nFailure scenario: desktop shows a pairing code, `cloudflared` service is down or the pairing listener isn't bound (530 / 502 — both empirically observed on this exact tunnel, `SETUP.md:53-65`). Phone posts `/v1/pair/begin`; edge returns 530 with an HTML page. Result: \"Pairing failed to verify. Nothing was stored.\" Sharper variant: a 504/502 on `/v1/pair/confirm` after the desktop already processed confirm and stored the device record → phone asserts \"Nothing was stored\" while the desktop holds a paired device, with no \"check your desktop's paired devices\" advice (the sentence `CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED` exists to give). Same physical situation arriving as an `IOException` correctly yields UNREACHABLE (`RemotePairingHandshake.kt:389-397`, per `PROTOCOL.md:1005-1009`); arriving as a status does not.\n\nEvidence:\n- `RemotePairingHandshake.kt:401-410` — `remoteFailureOf`: wire code null on unparsable body, `FAILURE_BY_STATUS[502]` null, `?: PairingFailure.BAD_MAC`.\n- `CompanionJson.kt:51-55` — HTML body → `ProtocolException`, the only exception `remoteFailureOf` catches → `code == null`. No crash, silent default.\n- `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` has 401/403/404/410/429/503 only; no 5xx besides 503.\n- `RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt:28` → `PairingState.kt:145` = \"Pairing failed to verify. Nothing was stored.\"; reached via `BridgeCompanionRepository.kt:973-975`. No interception in `PairingClient.pairWithCode` (`PairingClient.kt:218-226`).\n- Convention divergence in-repo: `ConnectionClassifier.kt:47-50` calls the 530-vs-502 distinction \"load-bearing\" (530 = DesktopUnreachable, 502 = SupersetNotRunning) for the same Cloudflare architecture.\n- Honest-claim rule the codebase enforces: `RemotePairingErrorMappingTest.kt:135-152` (`onlyTheFailuresThatCanKnowItClaimNothingWasStored`) — BAD_MAC is allowed the claim only because \"in the flow that raises it, nothing was\".\n\nCounter-evidence (why PLAUSIBLE, not CONFIRMED): `PROTOCOL.md:1248-1252` normatively mandates this exact default — \"Any other non-2xx is a rejection the phone has no name for: it reports 'nothing was stored' and must neither call it unreachable (the endpoint answered) nor blame the code.\" Code matches spec; the defect is in the spec's blanket clause, whose \"the endpoint answered\" premise is false for 502/530/504 (the edge answered, the desktop did not).\n\nFix direction (not applied): add gateway statuses to `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` — 502/504/520-524/530 → `UNREACHABLE` (\"the edge could not reach the desktop right now\", same class as `edge_unverifiable`/503) — and amend `PROTOCOL.md` §4.9.7 accordingly; separately, stop claiming \"Nothing was stored\" for any failure after `confirm` was transmitted. Leave HTTP-400 / `pair_unknown_candidate` wire-code-only handling untouched; add no automatic retry.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>68879</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>30</tool_uses><duration_ms>242225</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:21.901Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a86072aa40e0253a9</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_bBedYuB7FaYvODm3BHO6X86o</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a86072aa40e0253a9.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify saved pairing code\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## REFUTED\n\n**File:** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt:113` (line number correct as cited: `var digits by rememberSaveable { mutableStateOf(\"\") }`)\n\n**Summary.** Saved-state copy of the 8 digits is real, but it is not a *persisted* secret and gains an attacker nothing. Three invariants kill the claim: no disk serialization, no lower-privileged reader than the heap copy the protocol already accepts, and zero residual value once the desktop window closes.\n\n**Evidence**\n\n1. **No disk copy — platform invariant.** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\AndroidManifest.xml:31-41`: `MainActivity` declares no `android:persistableMode`. Plain `onSaveInstanceState(Bundle)` state lives only in system_server RAM (`ActivityRecord` saved state); only `persistableMode` + `PersistableBundle` reaches `recent_tasks/*_task.xml` on disk. Plus `allowBackup=\"false\"`, `fullBackupContent=\"false\"` (manifest:22-23) → no backup/adb-pull path.\n2. **Already-accepted exposure class, same adversary.** `PROTOCOL.md:1102-1105` (§4.9.4, \"What is *not* claimed\"): \"The typed code is not zeroizable the way `pc` is. It arrives from a text field as a `String` and lives on the heap until the collector gets to it… Its 120-second, single-use lifetime is what bounds that, not memory hygiene.\" Reading the system_server Bundle requires root/system — strictly ≥ the privilege needed to read the app heap copy the doc accepts. No new reader.\n3. **Retained value is dead by normative desktop behaviour.** `PROTOCOL.md:847-853`: 120 000 ms lifetime, exactly one successful use then window closes, 3 bad confirm MACs burn it, \"On close/burn the code, the SRP verifier, `b`, `S` and `PRK` are zeroed\"; each window mints a fresh CSPRNG code (`PROTOCOL.md:901`). A restored stale code authenticates to nothing. Window-linked erasure exists — on the side that holds the verifier, which is where it decides the outcome.\n4. **Copy stops being saved once the screen leaves.** `CompanionShell.kt:177-190` renders `PairingScreen` from a bare `when (route)` — no `SaveableStateHolder`/`SaveableStateProvider`, so the `rememberSaveable` entry is unregistered on leaving composition (`onCancel`/`onDone` → `route = AGENTS`). No post-pairing retention.\n5. **Retry stays user-triggered (unchanged).** Submits exist only in `Button onClick` at `PairingScreen.kt:187-196` and `238-242`; `resubmitsSameCode` (`:341-343`) gates resubmit to `RESTART_REQUIRED` + 8 digits, matching `PROTOCOL.md:1027-1036` (\"recovery is user-initiated only\", \"`confirm` is NEVER re-sent automatically\"). Wire literal untouched.\n6. **Restored state cannot mislead the countdown.** `submittedAtMs` (`:119`) only renders under `PairingState.Exchanging` (`:217-220`); process death drops the ViewModel state, so a restored `submittedAtMs` is never displayed.\n\n**Residual, not the claimed defect (MINOR, informational).** After process-death restore on the PAIRING route the field is pre-filled with a stale code and `Pair` is enabled (`CodeEntry` `complete` at `:307`). A user returning later, typing nothing, and tapping `Pair` against a *new* desktop window spends one of that window's three confirm MAC strikes (`PROTOCOL.md:851`) on digits that were never the current code. Recoverable, user-visible, no secret disclosure — a UX wart, not the persisted-secret exposure alleged.\n\n**Checked clean:** saved-state lifecycle and unregistration path, manifest persistence/backup posture, `PROTOCOL.md` §4.2/§4.9.2/§4.9.3/§4.9.4/§4.9.5 threat model, retry/auto-submit call graph, countdown restore behaviour, error mapping for `RESTART_REQUIRED`.\n\n**Tooling note:** the Bash tool was non-functional this session (every invocation, including `pwd`, returned `/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching `''`), so `git log`/`git blame` history for this hunk was not consulted; findings rest on working-tree contents read directly.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>62668</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>29</tool_uses><duration_ms>251587</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:22.003Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a79f34ba96e503957</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a79f34ba96e503957.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify burn memo isolation\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**REFUTED**\n\nCorrected location: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:2219` (LAN claim, no memo clear) vs `:2367-2369` (remote claim + `remoteBurnMemo = null`). Consumer: `...\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\http.ts:831-837`.\n\nSummary: behavior described is real — a LAN/QR open leaves `remoteBurnMemo` set, so `/v1/pair/*` answers 403 `pair_code_burned` for the memo remainder — but it is the memo's deliberate contract, remote-scoped, with no observable consequence beyond the answer the memo exists to give.\n\nEvidence:\n- Route reachable, so \"unreachable\" is not the refutation. `routeByHost` (http.ts:658-686) keys only on `Host`/method/path + `publicPairHost`; window state irrelevant. Gate 3's documented bare-404 (http.ts:785-788) proves the path is served with no remote window.\n- Memo intent is remote-flow-only, not window-slot-only. pairing.ts:1945-1959: memo distinguishes \"you burned it\" from \"never existed\"; it holds only `forgetAtMs`. The clear-on-open comment at 2367-2368 scopes clearing to a **remote** successor: \"the phone asking about THIS window must not be told about the last one.\" A LAN window is not reachable on `/v1/pair/*` (LAN flow is its own 47611 listener, `route()` at pairing.ts:2189-2190), so no phone can be \"asking about THIS window\" through the remote paths. `FEATURES.md:86` states the contract flatly: \"A burned window is remembered for 60 s so the phone can be told 'you burned it' instead of the bare 404 a never-opened window produces\" — no LAN exception, and the remote window genuinely was burned.\n- Ordering is safe. http.ts:831-837 checks `currentRemotePairing()` FIRST and only consults `burned()` when it is null → memo can never mask a live remote window. `currentRemotePairing` (pairing.ts:2462-2468) returns null for a LAN slot, so the fallthrough is the same \"no live remote window\" state as an empty slot.\n- No incremental leak. The 403-vs-404 distinguishability exists for the full 60 s with **no** window open; opening a LAN window adds no information about the LAN window, changes no crypto/rate state, and the memo self-forgets on read (pairing.ts:1971-1976). No LAN-side consumer of `remoteWindowWasBurned` exists (only http.ts:833 repo-wide).\n- Tests encode the same scope: `...\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\remote-pairing.test.ts:1817-1826` asserts 403 after burn with the real memo; :177-179 pins the memo off elsewhere. No test asserts a bare 404 while a LAN window is open, and no doc requires one.\n- Worst realistic sequence: burn a code window → user switches dialog to QR (`PairDeviceDialog.tsx:189-201` closes then opens) → phone retries `/v1/pair/begin` → 403 \"burned, get a new code.\" Truthful (that code is dead, no live remote window), self-clearing ≤60 s, no data or authz effect. Residual is a cosmetic wording nit, below MINOR — not a defect worth reporting.\n\nFrozen missing-candidate behavior (`pair_unknown_candidate`) untouched by this question; unchanged.\n\nChecked clean: memo lifecycle (set/read/expire/clear), burn→close teardown and key zeroing (pairing.ts:2337-2356, 2498-2509), 403-before/after-endpoint ordering, host/method route isolation, LAN-vs-remote path separation, one-window slot reserve/claim/release, tests and FEATURES.md contract text. Bash tool is non-functional in this environment (`unexpected EOF` on every invocation), so no `git`/test execution — findings are from source, tests, and manifest text only.\n\n**SHIP**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>77248</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>27</tool_uses><duration_ms>256466</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a3a37a69b4b12483f</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_RkKr6a71UNeklYsJh0ijJl2u</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a3a37a69b4b12483f.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Find pairing simplifications\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Bash tool unusable in this session (shell init parse error: `/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF`), so no `git diff HEAD` — scope reconstructed by reading the `(REMOTE-CODE-PAIRING)` / remote-pairing surface in both repos (host-service `pairing.ts`, `srp.ts`, `http.ts`, trpc `companion.ts`, `PairDeviceDialog.tsx`; companion `RemotePairingHandshake.kt`, `PairingClient.kt`, `Srp.kt`, `KeyDerivation.kt`, `PairingCode.kt`, `PairingScreen.kt`, `PairingState.kt`, `RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt`, `BridgeCompanionRepository.kt`). Untracked-vs-modified split not verifiable without git.\n\n6 candidates:\n\n---\n1. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\nline 408-416 (`RemoteKexSession`), enforced at 1049-1061 and 1506-1520\nsummary: Three parallel nullable fields (`clientPublic`, `serverPublic`, `keys`) encode one boolean (\"kex done\") → an impossible-state `500` branch in `handleRemoteKex` plus a triple null-check in `handleRemoteConfirm`; simpler form is one nullable sub-object `kex: { clientPublic; serverPublic; keys } | null` assigned once.\nfailure_scenario: Any future edit that assigns the fields separately (e.g. caching `B` before deriving keys, or wiping `keys` on a failed MAC) re-creates the half-committed candidate the comment says cannot exist: the retry short-circuit at 1060 replays a `B` with no keys behind it, the phone fails at step 3b as `pair_code_wrong` while typing the right digits, and the desktop answers `500 unknown` from 1058 with no log line. Three call sites must be kept in lockstep by hand; one object makes it unrepresentable.\n\n---\n2. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\components\\PairDeviceDialog\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx`\nlines 302-304 (with `CompanionPairingState` at `packages\\host-service\\src\\trpc\\router\\companion\\companion.ts:163-170`)\nsummary: The dialog identifies \"is this verdict about my window?\" from two incidental fields (`expiresAtMs` + `pairingKind`) and silently `return`s on mismatch, even though `pairingRef` exists precisely as the non-secret window identity and is simply not in the response shape; simpler form is `pairingRef` in `CompanionPairingState` and mismatch → the existing \"window is gone\" failure.\nfailure_scenario: Window replaced under the dialog (second dialog instance / anything else calling `openPairing`|`openRemotePairing`): local countdown hits 0 → `phase = \"closing\"`, poll returns `kind:\"open\"` with the new window's `expiresAtMs`, both guards `return`, and the `closing` render (363-370) is a spinner with no footer buttons — permanent \"Finishing up…\" with no verdict and no in-panel way out. Two-field identity also means a same-mode window that happens to share `expiresAtMs` reads another window's verdict as its own.\n\n---\n3. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\nline 1356 (`evictOldestKexSessionsIfFull`)\nsummary: Oldest-candidate eviction does an O(n) `createdAtMs` scan with a `POSITIVE_INFINITY` sentinel, a dead `oldestKey === null` return, a `while` that can only ever iterate once and a redundant re-`get`, while the sibling helper `chargePerSource` (1340-1343) evicts the oldest by relying on Map insertion order; simpler form is the same one-liner (`kexSessions.keys().next()`), giving one eviction idiom in the file.\nfailure_scenario: Two idioms for one invariant drift the moment a candidate is re-inserted or its `createdAtMs` is touched (e.g. \"refresh on retry\"): insertion-order eviction and timestamp eviction then disagree, and the `(PAIR-JUNK-SURVIVAL)` argument that eviction is fair to the real phone becomes untrue in one of the two tables with no test able to see it. The dead null branch also hides a genuine \"table full but nothing evictable\" bug behind a silent `return`.\n\n---\n4. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\nlines 2499-2509 (burn memo set in `currentRemotePairing().confirm`'s catch)\nsummary: \"The window burned\" is decided in two places — `burnMac` (1797-1802) flips `consumed` at `&gt;= PAIRING_MAX_BAD_MACS`, and the endpoint's catch re-derives the same predicate to call `rememberRemoteBurn()`; simpler form is to memo inside `burnMac` where the burn actually happens, leaving the endpoint to just close.\nfailure_scenario: A burn reached from any path other than that one catch (a strike charged elsewhere, a future \"burn on N unattributed attempts\", or the confirm result being consumed before the catch) sets `consumed` without a memo → `handleRemotePairRequest` (http.ts:833) sees no memo and answers a bare `404`, so the user who mistyped three times is told \"the desktop has no pairing window open\" instead of `pair_code_burned` / \"ask for a new code\" — the exact ambiguity `PAIRING_REMOTE_BURN_MEMO_MS` was added to remove, failing silently.\n\n---\n5. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt`\nline 113 (`var digits by rememberSaveable { mutableStateOf(\"\") }`, retained-retry read at 185-190)\nsummary: The typed 8-digit code is held in `rememberSaveable`, i.e. written into the Activity saved-state Bundle (system-persisted for process-death restore), when the retained-code retry only needs it across recomposition/rotation; simpler form is `remember` (or clearing `digits` in `onStop`), keeping only `submittedAtMs` saveable.\nfailure_scenario: Android saves instance state on backgrounding: a ~26.6-bit secret that the design bounds to 120 s is copied into system-held state that can be written to disk and restored minutes or hours later — and the restored value is useless anyway, because the desktop window it belonged to is long gone, so the restored \"Try again\" resubmits a dead code and reports `CODE_WRONG` (\"check the code\") for a code that was correct.\n\n---\n6. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\data\\BridgeCompanionRepository.kt`\nline 950 (`PairingCode.digitsOrNull(code)`; repeated at `core\\...\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:119-125`, then `PairingCode.passwordBytes` re-checks at `PairingCode.kt:81`)\nsummary: The same code string is normalized/validated at three layers (UI `takeDigits` → repository `digitsOrNull` → core `digitsOrNull` → `passwordBytes` ASCII re-check), each with its own failure object; simpler form is one normalization at the UI boundary plus the core's `passwordBytes` guard, dropping the repository copy.\nfailure_scenario: The repository branch is unreachable in practice (the Pair button is enabled only at exactly 8 digits and `takeDigits` already strips non-digits), so it is untested dead code that nonetheless owns a user-visible verdict: a future entry point that submits raw text gets `CODE_WRONG` from the repository, the same input gets `PairingException(CODE_WRONG)` from core, and the two paths must be kept saying the same thing by hand while only one of them can ever be exercised.\n\nChecked and clean: frozen wire behavior (`pair_unknown_candidate` = HTTP 400 + parsed-body code at kex/confirm, no status-only fallback for 400, no automatic retry — `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` has no 400 entry, retry is user-triggered only); one-window atomic reservation (`reserveWindowSlot`/`claimOrTearDown`/`releaseWindowSlot`) and both `close()` paths' zeroization; monotone caps (`remoteRequests` 1024, `kexComputations` 64, per-source 32/32 and 4/32) are each independently reachable and none is derivable from the others; host isolation (`routeByHost`, `normaliseHostHeader`, `cf-connecting-ip` single-literal rule); SRP fixed-window ladder, `PAD()` widths and 825-byte transcript agreement between `pairing.ts`/`KeyDerivation.kt`; sealed step-4 AAD path binding and counter-0 nonce; `admit()` charge-once ordering ahead of body read.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>177941</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>47</tool_uses><duration_ms>518453</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a3a37a69b4b12483f</task-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a3a37a69b4b12483f.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Find pairing simplifications\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Report already concluded within scope — 6 candidates, no broadening. No `SendMessage` tool is available in this session, so the acknowledgement to `code-review` is inlined here rather than sent.\n\nNote on coverage: Bash was unusable for the whole session (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching '` on every command, including `echo ok`), so `git diff HEAD` and untracked-file enumeration never ran. Scope was reconstructed by reading the remote-pairing surface directly; modified-vs-untracked split is unverified.\n\nFinal finder output (unchanged):\n\n1. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts` — line 408-416, enforced 1049-1061 / 1506-1520. Three parallel nullable fields (`clientPublic`, `serverPublic`, `keys`) encode one boolean \"kex done\" → impossible-state `500` branch + triple null-check. Simpler: one nullable `kex: { clientPublic; serverPublic; keys } | null` assigned once. Failure: any edit assigning them separately recreates the half-committed candidate — retry short-circuit replays a `B` with no keys, phone reports `pair_code_wrong` on correct digits, desktop returns `500 unknown` with no log.\n\n2. `...\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\components\\PairDeviceDialog\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx` — lines 302-304 (+ `CompanionPairingState`, `packages\\host-service\\src\\trpc\\router\\companion\\companion.ts:163-170`). Window identity inferred from `expiresAtMs` + `pairingKind` with silent-ignore on mismatch, while `pairingRef` exists for exactly this and is absent from the response shape. Simpler: carry `pairingRef`, mismatch → existing \"window is gone\" failure. Failure: window replaced under the dialog → phase `closing` never resolves; render at 363-370 is a spinner with no footer buttons.\n\n3. `...\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts` — line 1356. Oldest-eviction uses an O(n) `createdAtMs` scan, `POSITIVE_INFINITY` sentinel, dead `oldestKey === null` return, once-only `while`, redundant re-`get`; sibling `chargePerSource` (1340-1343) evicts by Map insertion order. Simpler: `kexSessions.keys().next()`. Failure: two idioms for one invariant drift on any re-insert/timestamp touch; `(PAIR-JUNK-SURVIVAL)` fairness silently untrue in one table.\n\n4. `...\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts` — lines 2499-2509. Burn fact owned twice: `burnMac` (1797-1802) flips `consumed`; the endpoint catch re-derives `failedMacAttempts &gt;= PAIRING_MAX_BAD_MACS` to memo it. Simpler: memo inside `burnMac`. Failure: a burn reached any other way leaves no memo → `http.ts:833` answers bare `404`, user told \"no pairing window open\" instead of `pair_code_burned`.\n\n5. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt` — line 113 (retry read 185-190). Typed code in `rememberSaveable` → 120 s secret written to system-persisted saved-instance state. Simpler: `remember`, or clear `digits` in `onStop`; keep only `submittedAtMs` saveable. Failure: secret persists past its window and restores after process death, where resubmitting yields `CODE_WRONG` for a correct code.\n\n6. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\data\\BridgeCompanionRepository.kt` — line 950 (+ `RemotePairingHandshake.kt:119-125`, `PairingCode.kt:81`). Four-layer normalize/validate chain with two different failure objects for one input. Simpler: normalize once at the UI boundary plus core's `passwordBytes` guard. Failure: repository branch is unreachable dead code that still owns a user-visible verdict; two paths must be kept in agreement by hand.\n\nChecked clean: frozen wire behavior (400 + parsed-body `pair_unknown_candidate`, no 400 status-only fallback, user-triggered retry only); one-window reservation/teardown and both `close()` zeroization paths; caps 1024/64/32-32/4-32 each independently reachable; host isolation and `cf-connecting-ip` single-literal rule; SRP ladder, `PAD()` widths, 825-byte transcript cross-runtime agreement; sealed step-4 AAD path binding and counter-0 nonce; `admit()` charge-before-body ordering.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>172526</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>47</tool_uses><duration_ms>546206</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a596254e3f4f2c589</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_TL4OI0aryXmY1uVFp6OnmLYA</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a596254e3f4f2c589.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Find pairing code reuse\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Caveat on method\n\nThe Bash tool is non-functional in this environment — every invocation (including `true` and `pwd`) dies in shell init: `/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching ''`. I could not run `git diff HEAD`, `git status`, or list untracked files in either repo. Findings below are read from current file contents on the `(REMOTE-CODE-PAIRING)` / `(PAIR-EVICTION-HONEST)` surface and are verified against the existing helper they duplicate, but I cannot certify each hunk is in the uncommitted diff rather than already committed. Finder only.\n\n---\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:334\nsummary: `openConfirmEnvelope` is a second copy of `PairingClient.openConfirmEnvelope` (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\PairingClient.kt:256) with the raised failure swapped from BAD_MAC to CODE_WRONG.\nfailure_scenario: Lines 340-345 throw `PairingException(PairingFailure.CODE_WRONG, ...)` for \"not a response envelope\" and \"addressed to another device\". Those are `PairingException`, not `EnvelopeException`, so they bypass the `catch (e: EnvelopeException) -&gt; CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED` wrapper at :177 and reach `remotePairingError` (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\data\\RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt:22) -&gt; `PairingError.CODE_WRONG`. A desktop (or on-path edge) that seals a step-4 envelope of the wrong kind, or one addressed to a different deviceId, tells the user \"the desktop did not accept that code\" and offers a resubmit of the same digits, hiding a wrong-peer/protocol condition behind a typo message. The duplication also means the QR twin and the remote copy now label identical envelope defects differently, and a fix to one silently misses the other. Reuse: hoist PairingClient's implementation into one helper parameterised by confirm path + failure code.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:439\nsummary: `FAILURE_BY_WIRE` / `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` / `messageFor` (:439, :470, :416) hand-build a second wire-string-&gt;failure resolver that `PairingFailure`'s `WireEnum` machinery (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\protocol\\WireEnum.kt) already provides and that `PairingClient.pairingFailureOf` (PairingClient.kt:343) already implements.\nfailure_scenario: The two tables have already drifted on the same wire vocabulary: `FAILURE_BY_WIRE` omits `BAD_MAC.wire`, `CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED`, `UNREACHABLE`, `HOST_NOT_PRIVATE`, `QR_INVALID`; `PairingClient` has no 429 branch at all (429 falls to `UNREACHABLE`) while the new table maps 429 -&gt; `RATE_LIMITED`; `PairingClient` collapses `pair_wrong_peer` -&gt; `WINDOW_CLOSED` while the new table preserves `WRONG_PEER`; unmatched codes default to `BAD_MAC` here and `UNREACHABLE` there. `pair_unknown_candidate` exists only in the new table. Adding one wire code to `PairingFailure` therefore requires two hand edits in two files or one flow silently swallows it into its own default. Same duplication in the payload type: `PairErrorBody` (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\protocol\\Pairing.kt:146) and the private `PairingErrorBody` (PairingClient.kt:409) are the identical `{code}` serializer. Reuse: one `PairingFailure.fromWire()` over the WireEnum entries plus the shared `PairErrorBody`; delete `PairingErrorBody`.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\crypto\\KeyDerivation.kt:138\nsummary: `deriveRemotePairingKeys` (:138) and `remotePairingTranscript` (:166) are line-for-line copies of `derivePairingKeys` (:67) and `pairingTranscript` (:98) with five renamed prefix constants — the exact duplication the desktop side removed via `PairingProfile` + shared `buildHkdfSalt` / `expandPairingKeys` / `concatFixed`.\nfailure_scenario: Cross-runtime differential vectors pin these bytes to the desktop's `LAN_PAIRING_PROFILE` / `REMOTE_PAIRING_PROFILE` table (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:556-570 and :788-826). Because the Kotlin side keeps two independent bodies, any change to the key schedule — a fourth Expand, a label fix, a prefix rename — has to be applied in two Kotlin functions and one TS profile row, and applying it to only the LAN copy leaves the remote flow deriving a different PRK. The failure surfaces as `pair_code_wrong` on every remote pairing with no diagnostic, indistinguishable from a mistyped code. Encoding is already inconsistent inside the copies: salt prefixes go through `Charsets.UTF_8` (:81, :149) while transcript prefixes use `US_ASCII` (:112, :180), and the desktop encodes both as `\"ascii\"` — so the first non-ASCII byte anyone puts in a label breaks half the flows silently. Reuse: parameterise the existing `derivePairingKeys`/`pairingTranscript` with a profile object mirroring desktop's `PairingProfile`.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:86\nsummary: The hardened OkHttp builder at :86-100 plus `connectionSpecsFor` (:486) re-derive the TLS/redirect/retry policy that `SealedTransport.secureBase()` (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\transport\\SealedTransport.kt:110) already owns.\nfailure_scenario: Both copies currently pin `followRedirects(false)`, `followSslRedirects(false)`, `RESTRICTED_TLS + MODERN_TLS`, `retryOnConnectionFailure(false)` — so no drift today. But the pairing hop is the ONE unauthenticated hop in the protocol and its own comment (:501) states that TLS to the pinned hostname is what authenticates the desktop. A future tightening (a cipher removal, a pin, HTTP/1.1-only) applied to `secureBase()` leaves exactly that hop on the old policy, and nothing in the build fails. Reuse: expose `secureBase()` from the transport module and have `RemotePairingHandshake` layer only its own timeouts and the localhost-cleartext exception on top.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1135\nsummary: `requireRemoteCandidate` validates the incoming deviceId with a bare `typeof === \"string\"` and then uses the raw client string as a Map key, instead of `isCanonicalWireId` / `decodeWireId` (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\crypto.ts:782 and :802).\nfailure_scenario: `crypto.ts:764-793` documents `isCanonicalWireId` as THE canonical §0.1 wire-id test, written specifically because the 22-char id shape had been checked four different ways with three strictnesses and \"ids that pass an outer gate and then fail an inner one is the failure mode this protocol can least afford\". This is now a fifth way. `begin` validates the id canonically (via `parseKexMetadata` -&gt; `decodeExact`, :1190) and refuses a bad one as `400 unknown`; `kex`/`confirm` at :1146 do not, so the same non-canonical 22-char variant of a real deviceId (base64url's last char carries 4 significant bits, so four strings decode to identical bytes) misses the map and gets `400 pair_unknown_candidate` — the frozen contract's \"repeat begin\" advice — plus an `unattributedAttempts` warn line. One id string, two different verdicts across three steps of one exchange, and the phone is told to restart a window that was never wrong.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt:388\nsummary: `GroupedCodeTransformation` re-derives the dash position (`PAIRING_CODE_DIGITS / 2`, :390) and its `OffsetMapping` arithmetic (:392-394) instead of taking it from `PairingCode.grouped`, which already owns where the dash goes (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\PairingCode.kt:63).\nfailure_scenario: The transformation calls `PairingCode.grouped(text.text)` for the visible string but computes the cursor mapping from its own local `half`. `grouped` additionally suppresses the dash entirely when `digits.length &lt;= half` (:65), which the mapping does not model; today the two agree only because `half` is 4 and `originalToTransformed` shifts only above `half`. Change `PAIRING_CODE_DIGITS` to any odd value, or change `grouped`'s dash rule, and the offset mapping is off by one against the rendered text — cursor jumps, selection lands in the wrong place, and Compose can throw on a transformed offset past the end of the transformed string. The desktop has a third, independent grouping (`groupCode`, `Math.ceil(len/2)`, C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\components\\PairDeviceDialog\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx:151) which would then disagree with what the phone shows the user typing. Reuse: derive the mapping offset from `PairingCode.grouped`'s own output length rather than a second copy of the rule.\n```\n\n---\n\nChecked and found clean (no duplication worth reporting): `srp.ts` (its parameterised `digest` is required by the SHA-1 test group, and `srpBytesToBigInt`/`srpPad` have no pre-existing equivalent); `chargePerSource` / `chargeKexIntroduction` / `chargeRemoteRequest` (correctly one shared bounded counter); `handleRemotePairRequest`'s reuse of `readBoundedBody`, `pairingJsonResponse` as the single no-store constructor, and `routeByHost`/`classifyRequestHost` host isolation; `wipeAllPairingKeys`/`zeroKexSession`/`clearKexSessions` as the single wipe path; `pairing-modes.ts` as the single qr/code &lt;-&gt; lan/remote table; `PairingCode.digitsOrNull`/`takeDigits`/`passwordBytes` as the single Unicode-digit normalisation point (correctly reused by both `PairingScreen` and `RemotePairingHandshake.run`); the `MIN_SEALED_BODY_BYTES` guard I initially suspected was dropped (it is enforced inside `EnvelopeHeader.parse`, SealedEnvelope.kt:69, so the omission is safe); the frozen `HTTP 400 + pair_unknown_candidate` contract itself, which I found no reason to change.\n\n**FIX-FIRST** (findings 1-3 are duplications with live behavioural divergence, not style).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>190141</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>61</tool_uses><duration_ms>552109</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a8eaf6c26af20d34c</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_d426tbVZ2pSt02E8Tn8pZPVA</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a8eaf6c26af20d34c.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Scan pairing diff linewise\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Scope caveat (must read)\n\nBash tool non-functional this session — every invocation (`echo hello`, `git --version`) died in the shell preamble: `/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching `''`. So **no `git diff HEAD` / `git status --short` was obtainable**. Scope was reconstructed by reading the remote-pairing surface directly (desktop `companion/{pairing,srp,http,config,index}.ts`, `PairDeviceDialog/*`; companion `core/crypto/Srp.kt`, `core/crypto/KeyDerivation.kt`, `core/pairing/{PairingCode,RemotePairingHandshake}.kt`, `app-phone/.../{PairingScreen,PairingState,RemotePairingErrorMapping,BridgeCompanionRepository}.kt`, `PROTOCOL.md §4.9`). Untracked-file enumeration and hunk boundaries are therefore unverified.\n\n## Candidate findings (6, recall-biased)\n\n**1.** `file`: packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts `line`: 1063\n`summary`: `chargeKexComputation` is charged before `A` is range-checked (validation lives inside `srpServerHandshake` at srp.ts:497), and the monotone ceiling is never refunded on `SrpError`.\n`failure_scenario`: 16 distinct `CF-Connecting-IP` values × 4 `begin`+`kex` pairs (= `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE`), each `kex` carrying a canonical 384-byte `A` of all zeros → `decodeClientPublic` passes, `chargeKexComputation` charges, `srpAssertClientPublic` throws → 64 charges, zero exponentiations. `state.kexComputations` now hits `MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW`. The real phone's first `kex` gets `429 pair_rate_limited`; phone maps 429 → `RATE_LIMITED`, terminal per PROTOCOL.md §4.9. The user-opened window is unusable for its remaining life — the exact \"junk rate-limits the real phone out\" outcome the docblock at 1740 says cannot happen.\n\n**2.** `file`: packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts `line`: 2547\n`summary`: `state.remoteRequests += 1` runs *before* `chargePerSource`, so requests already over a source's own cap still burn the never-refunded window-wide aggregate.\n`failure_scenario`: One IP sends 1024 `POST /v1/pair/begin`. First 32 admit; requests 33-1024 each increment `remoteRequests` and *then* throw the per-source 429. `remoteRequests` reaches `REMOTE_PAIR_MAX_REQUESTS_PER_WINDOW` (32×32). Every subsequent request from *any* source, including the real phone's `begin`, is refused at 2541 with `429 pair_rate_limited`. Contradicts the stated invariant at 352-357 (\"refuses THAT source and only that source; the real phone keeps its full allowance\") and the aggregate-bound rationale at 2522-2534.\n\n**3.** `file`: app-phone/src/main/java/family/khaira/supersetcompanion/phone/ui/pairing/PairingScreen.kt `line`: 113\n`summary`: The typed 8-digit pairing secret is held in `rememberSaveable`, so it is serialised into Android saved-instance-state and escapes the app process.\n`failure_scenario`: User types `12345678`, then backgrounds the app / rotates / the process is killed while the Failed(RESTART_REQUIRED) screen is up. `digits` is written into the Activity `Bundle` handed to `system_server` for task restoration, outliving the desktop's 120 s window and the app process. ~26.6 bits of authenticator now sits outside the process boundary the desktop side goes to great lengths to keep it inside of (pairing.ts:488-497 \"MUST NOT be logged, persisted...\"). `submittedAtMs` (line 119) is legitimately saveable; `digits` is not.\n\n**4.** `file`: app-phone/src/main/java/family/khaira/supersetcompanion/phone/data/BridgeCompanionRepository.kt `line`: 961\n`summary`: `withTimeout(PAIRING_WINDOW_MS)` can cancel *after* the desktop has persisted the device record, and the resulting `TimeoutCancellationException` maps to `WINDOW_CLOSED` — advice that omits the orphan record.\n`failure_scenario`: Confirm reaches the desktop; `sealPairedResponse` sets `consumed`, `deps.onPaired` writes the device record with `K_dev`, then the sealed 200 is slow (tunnel latency) and the phone's 120 s timeout fires. Phone stores nothing and shows \"The pairing window closed. Start it again on the desktop.\" The desktop now holds a paired device whose key no phone has. Repeat attempts mint fresh `deviceId`s, accumulating orphan records. Contrast `CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED` (PairingState.kt:166-169), which *does* tell the user to check and remove the desktop record — this path needs the same honesty.\n\n**5.** `file`: core/src/main/java/family/khaira/supersetcompanion/core/pairing/RemotePairingHandshake.kt `line`: 382\n`summary`: `response.body.bytes()` reads the pairing hop's response with no size bound, on both success and error paths.\n`failure_scenario`: The public pairing host is unauthenticated and TLS-terminated at Cloudflare; a hostile edge, a captive portal, or a misrouted DNS answer returns a 400 with a multi-hundred-MB body. `bytes()` buffers it all before `remoteFailureOf` parses, OOM-killing the companion app. Desktop-side bodies are bounded at `MAX_PAIR_BODY_BYTES` (http.ts:936-948); the phone has no counterpart — `B` (384 bytes), `pid`/`pairSalt` and the sealed envelope have known ceilings the reader could enforce.\n\n**6.** `file`: packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts `line`: 2369\n`summary`: `remoteBurnMemo` is cleared only by `openRemotePairingReserved`; opening a QR/LAN window leaves a stale burn memo live.\n`failure_scenario`: Remote window burns on three wrong codes → `rememberRemoteBurn()`. Within 60 s the user opens a **QR** window (`openPairingWindowReserved`, ~line 2219, never touches `remoteBurnMemo`). `currentRemotePairing()` returns `null` (state.kind is `\"lan\"`), so `handleRemotePairRequest` at http.ts:833 consults `remoteWindowWasBurned()` → `true` → answers every internet caller `403 pair_code_burned` instead of the bare 404 that \"no remote window\" is supposed to be indistinguishable from. Also mis-advises a phone that a *current* code is dead.\n\n## Areas checked and found clean\nSRP-6a arithmetic and RFC 5054 padding (`srpPad`/`srpBigIntToBytes` refuse-not-truncate, `PAD(N)||PAD(g)` for `k`, `u==0` and degenerate-`A` rejection); the fixed 4-bit-window secret ladder's constant operation count and `table[0] = N+1`; desktop↔Android label/salt/identity/transcript-prefix agreement (`sc/v3-srp`, `superset-companion/pair/v3-srp`, `\"sc/v3 remote-pair\"`, 825-byte transcript, `PAD(S)` IKM); `base64UrlDecode` canonicality (kills the non-canonical-`deviceId` map-key hazard); `pair_unknown_candidate` = HTTP 400 + parsed-body-only mapping with no alias, no status fallback, no automatic retry (`FAILURE_BY_STATUS` has no 400 entry; `resubmitsSameCode` gated on user tap); `assertCandidateSource` pinning on both `begin` and `kex`; candidate key wiping on every drop path (`zeroKexSession`/`wipeAllPairingKeys`/`clearKexSessions`) and `completePairing`'s outermost `finally`; `(PAIR-ONE-WINDOW-ATOMIC)` reserve/claim/release with `claimOrTearDown`; host isolation (`classifyRequestHost`, duplicate-`Host` and root-dot handling, load-time two-host assertion); `cf-connecting-ip` single-value/IP-literal gate; step-4 AAD request-form + reserved nonce counter 0; `PairingCode` Arabic-Indic/fullwidth→ASCII normalisation and `passwordBytes` ASCII guard; QR/LAN `sc/v1` path bytes unchanged.\n\n**FIX-FIRST**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>182712</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>55</tool_uses><duration_ms>588588</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>acc432574a787ddc1</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\acc432574a787ddc1.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review pairing implementation altitude\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Tooling note (affects coverage):** Bash unusable this session — every invocation dies in shell init (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching '''`), including `echo hi`. So no `git diff HEAD`, no untracked-file listing, no test/lint run. Scope reconstructed by reading the remote-pairing surface in both repos (host `companion/pairing.ts`, `srp.ts`, `http.ts`, `config.ts`, `types.ts`, trpc `companion.ts`, `PairDeviceDialog.tsx`; Android `RemotePairingHandshake.kt`, `Srp.kt`, `PairingCode.kt`, `PairingClient.kt`, `RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt`, `PairingState.kt`, `PairingScreen.kt`, `BridgeCompanionRepository.kt`, `CompanionConstants.kt`, `Pairing.kt`, `SealedEnvelope.kt`). Untracked-only files may be unreviewed.\n\n```\nCANDIDATES (6, recall-biased, finder only)\n```\n\n**1. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1063**\nsummary: `chargeKexComputation(state)` is charged BEFORE `A` is range-validated, so refusals that perform zero modexp still burn the monotone 64-derivation ceiling — inverting this module's own (PAIR-ATTEMPT-ORDER) \"decode/validate before charging\" rule.\nfailure_scenario: Attacker rotates ≥16 source IPs (per-source intro cap 4, source table evicts → refunds), does 64 `begin`+`kex` pairs with `A = PAD(1)` — 384 correct-width bytes, so `decodeClientPublic` passes, charge lands, then `srpAssertClientPublic` throws `SrpError` → `400 pair_bad_key_agreement`, no exponentiation performed. 128 of the window's 1024 admitted requests spent. Real phone's single legitimate `kex` then hits `kexComputations &gt;= 64` → `429 pair_rate_limited` → \"Your desktop is turning away attempts\". Attacker rate-limits a legitimate device out of a window the user is standing in front of, which §4.7/(PAIR-JUNK-SURVIVAL) says is exactly what must be impossible. Deeper mechanism: the CPU-cost ceiling belongs after the free validation gate (validate → charge → derive), same shape as `chargeConfirmAttempt` after `decodeExact`.\n\n**2. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt:113**\nsummary: The typed 8-digit code lives in `rememberSaveable`, i.e. Compose SavedStateRegistry → Activity saved-instance-state Bundle — a copy of the secret outside every zeroization/one-place discipline the rest of the batch enforces.\nfailure_scenario: User types `1234-5678`; `digits` is written into the saved-state Bundle held by system_server. Rotate the phone / background it / let the process be killed and restored from Recents: the code is restored from that Bundle, so it survives the 120 s window, process death and config change, and cannot be overwritten (immutable `String`, framework-owned storage). Meanwhile `PairingCode.passwordBytes` is zeroized, `Srp.startClient` zeroizes the password, and the desktop's own dialog documents \"no path to a store, to disk, or to a later render\". On success (`Paired`) and on non-resubmittable failures other than the explicit clear paths, the entry is never cleared. Deeper mechanism: retained-code retry (a frozen requirement) should hold the digits in the same one-place, non-persisted, explicitly-cleared secret holder the flow already implies — plain `remember`/ViewModel field cleared on terminal state — never framework saved state.\n\n**3. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:341**\nsummary: Post-confirm sealed-reply defects (`kind != RESPONSE`, header `deviceId` mismatch) are labelled `CODE_WRONG`, but they can only occur AFTER the desktop persisted the device record and consumed the window.\nfailure_scenario: Phone POSTs `/v1/pair/confirm`; desktop verifies `macPhone`, sets `consumed = true`, writes the device record + audit line, seals and returns. Reply arrives mis-addressed or with the wrong envelope kind (desktop bug, edge rewrite, a `deviceId` collision in the header path) → `PairingFailure.CODE_WRONG` → phone shows \"That code wasn't accepted. Check the code … then type it again\", and `resubmitsSameCode` is false so the field is cleared and a retype is invited. The window is already burned, so the retype gets `404 → WINDOW_CLOSED`; the desktop keeps an orphan device record holding a `K_dev` the phone never stored, and the user is never told to remove it. The neighbouring `CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED` text exists precisely for this state and is bypassed. Deeper mechanism: one lifecycle predicate — \"the confirm request left this phone\" — should classify every subsequent failure as may-have-paired, in the handshake, instead of per-check labels chosen at each throw site.\n\n**4. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:352**\nsummary: Step-4 AAD binds `CompanionConstants.PROTOCOL_CURRENT` (the moving negotiation ceiling, also `CLIENT_PROTOCOL_MAX`) where the host binds a deliberately frozen `PROTOCOL_VERSION_AT_PAIRING = 1`; same drift copied at `PairingClient.kt:273`.\nfailure_scenario: A future protocol bump sets `PROTOCOL_CURRENT = 2` (its documented purpose — it is the negotiation max). The host still builds the pairing AAD with `1`. The two AADs then differ by one byte, every GCM tag fails, `SealedEnvelope.open` throws → `CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED`/`CODE_WRONG`, i.e. \"your code was wrong\" for every user on both flows, with no diagnostic — byte-for-byte the failure mode the host header records an earlier revision already shipped (\"the two AADs differed by 16 bytes and every pairing failed the GCM tag and reported `pair_bad_mac`\"). Deeper mechanism: a frozen `PROTOCOL_AT_PAIRING` constant on the Android side, one per flow-independent pairing AAD, so the pinned value cannot be reached by a negotiation edit; the remote wire version is already versioned separately (`REMOTE_PAIRING_PAYLOAD_VERSION = 2`), the AAD version is not.\n\n**5. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1242**\nsummary: `assertCandidateSource` puts transport identity (`CF-Connecting-IP`) inside the candidate state machine, so a source change mid-exchange is reported as `pair_wrong_peer` — a hostile-peer verdict with no retry path — rather than as a throttling concern.\nfailure_scenario: Phone pairs over mobile data / on a Wi-Fi↔LTE boundary. `begin` arrives from IP a; the OkHttp pooled connection drops between the three requests and `kex` (or `confirm`) arrives from IP b (carrier CGNAT rebalance, network handover). `assertCandidateSource` → `refuseCandidateRewrite(\"a different source address\")` → `400 pair_wrong_peer` → phone renders \"Something else answered instead of the desktop. Refused.\" and `resubmitsSameCode` is false, so the still-valid on-screen code is cleared and the user is pushed to mint a new window. The candidate's own key material (`A`, then `macPhone`) already proves ownership cryptographically. Deeper mechanism: IP belongs to the admission/rate layer (`chargeRemoteRequest`, `kexSourceCounts`); candidate ownership should be proven by the candidate's committed `A`/MAC, and the eviction log's own promise (\"it can simply begin again\") should hold for this path too.\n\n**6. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1970` (consumed at `http.ts:833`)**\nsummary: `remoteWindowWasBurned()` is a process-global, source-agnostic memo, so for 60 s after a burn the pairing host answers `403 pair_code_burned` to any caller on the internet instead of the bare 404 the design leans on for indistinguishability.\nfailure_scenario: Attacker (or any scanner) POSTs `/v1/pair/begin` to the public pairing host with no window open. Normally: bodyless 404, identical to an unknown path — \"outside a window a human deliberately opened, this surface is not discoverable at all\". Within 60 s of a burn it instead gets `403 {\"code\":\"pair_code_burned\"}`, confirming that this machine runs the bridge, that a human opened a code window recently, and that three MACs failed against it. An attacker who deliberately burns a window (it holds a candidate, so it can spend three strikes — the accepted residual) gets a positive acknowledgement of that success, and repeated probing yields an occupancy oracle on the desktop. Deeper mechanism: the honest-error memo needs the same scoping as the rest of the hop — remembered against the candidate/source that actually burned it (or delivered only to a caller that can name the burned `pid`) — so honesty to the real phone does not become discoverability for everyone else.\n\nChecked and clean: SRP arithmetic and padding (host `srpPad`/`srpSecretModPow` fixed-window ladder vs BouncyCastle `rfc5054_3072` + `SRP6Util.calculateU`, identity `\"sc/v3 remote-pair\"` and `x = H(salt || H(I \":\" P))` agree byte-for-byte); `B` width/range/degenerate + `u != 0` validation on both sides; transcript shapes (117 vs 825 bytes) and profile domain separation (`sc/v1` literals untouched); atomic one-window reservation (`reserveWindowSlot`/`claimOrTearDown`/`releaseWindowSlot`); candidate key wipe on every exit path (`zeroKexSession`/`wipeAllPairingKeys`/`clearKexSessions`); nonce counter 0 reservation; persist-before-seal ordering and `onPaired` uniqueness/keyStore rollback; two-host isolation (`classifyRequestHost`/`routeByHost`/`normaliseHostHeader`, load-time host-collision assert); `cf-connecting-ip` single-value/IP-literal validation; body caps and `no-store` on every pairing response; Unicode digit normalisation (`Character.digit` → ASCII, `passwordBytes` guard, host `assertAsciiDigitCode`); wire error vocabulary parity host↔Android including the frozen `400 pair_unknown_candidate` contract and bodyless-404 fallback; desktop dialog secret containment, serialized window ops, unmount close, and server-owned verdict.\n\n**FIX-FIRST**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>206549</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>63</tool_uses><duration_ms>590453</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>add2c8840ff906cd5</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_xXzNoM2twyKfB2xw520uCrHq</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\add2c8840ff906cd5.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Trace pairing cross-file contracts\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Tooling caveat\n\nBash tool nonfunctional this session (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching `''` on every invocation, incl. `echo ok`). No `git diff HEAD`/untracked listing obtained → candidates derived by reading both working trees directly (Read/Grep/Glob) around the frozen-contract surface. Change-attribution to the diff is therefore inferred, not proven. Finder phase only; nothing verified.\n\n## Candidates (6)\n\n**1.**\n- `file`: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts\n- `line`: 2547\n- `summary`: `chargeRemoteRequest` increments the monotone window total *before* `chargePerSource` can refuse, so per-source-refused requests still spend the global cap.\n- `failure_scenario`: One IP sends 1024 `POST /v1/pair/begin` inside the 120 s window. Requests 1-32 are admitted; 33-1024 throw `429 pair_rate_limited` from `chargePerSource` — but each already did `state.remoteRequests += 1`. `remoteRequests` reaches `REMOTE_PAIR_MAX_REQUESTS_PER_WINDOW` (32×32=1024). The real phone's first `begin` now hits the ceiling at line 2541 → `429 pair_rate_limited`, and PROTOCOL.md §4.9 forbids the phone retrying. Junk with no crypto rate-limits the legitimate device out of its own window — the exact invariant config.ts:212-215 and the `remoteRequestCounts` doc (pairing.ts:348-357) claim is held.\n\n**2.**\n- `file`: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\SessionViewModel.kt\n- `line`: 360\n- `summary`: `pairFromCode` launches into `viewModelScope` with no in-flight guard; the new retained-code \"Try again\" button makes a double-fire trivial.\n- `failure_scenario`: User double-taps \"Try again\" (PairingScreen.kt:186, plain `Button`, no enabled-gate, state only flips to `Exchanging` asynchronously). Two concurrent `begin→kex→confirm` exchanges with two different deviceIds. Desktop is single-use: the first `confirm` sets `state.consumed`, the second gets `410 pair_window_closed` → `pairingState` (single MutableStateFlow, last writer wins) ends as `Failed(WINDOW_CLOSED)` overwriting `Paired`, and `pushRegistrar.onPairingSucceeded()` is skipped for that branch. Phone is paired and stored but shows \"The pairing window closed. Start it again on the desktop.\" with FCM token never registered → no push ever arrives.\n\n**3.**\n- `file`: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt\n- `line`: 382\n- `summary`: `response.body.bytes()` reads every pairing response fully into memory with no size cap, on an unauthenticated public endpoint.\n- `failure_scenario`: The pairing origin (public Cloudflare host, or anything terminating that hostname) answers `POST /v1/pair/kex` with `200` and a multi-hundred-MB body. `bytes()` buffers all of it before `Base64Url.decode(kex.bPublic, \"B\")` (line 312, no expected-length arg) and `Srp.decodeGroupElement` get a chance to reject → `OutOfMemoryError` on the phone, killing the app mid-pairing. Asymmetric with the host, which caps at `MAX_PAIR_BODY_BYTES` (4 KiB) via `readBoundedBody` before parsing.\n\n**4.**\n- `file`: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt\n- `line`: 113\n- `summary`: The typed 8-digit pairing code is held in `rememberSaveable`, i.e. written to Android saved-instance-state, so a ~26.6-bit shared secret outlives its 120 s window.\n- `failure_scenario`: User types the code, pairing fails with `RESTART_REQUIRED`, the app is backgrounded. Compose writes `digits` into the Activity `Bundle`; Android persists saved state (and may surface it via `onSaveInstanceState` dumps / bugreports / heap capture) with no expiry tied to the desktop window. An attacker with post-hoc device access reads a code the desktop deliberately never persists (pairing.ts:490-496 forbids logging/persisting it, and the desktop zeroes the code bytes at open). Retained-code retry needs the digits in memory only, not in saved state.\n\n**5.**\n- `file`: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\shell\\CompanionShell.kt\n- `line`: 185\n- `summary`: `onRetry = session::cancelPairing` republishes the existing paired record instead of resetting to entry, so re-pairing an already-paired phone cannot be retried.\n- `failure_scenario`: A phone that is already paired opens PAIRING to re-pair, submits a code, gets a non-resubmittable failure (e.g. `CODE_WRONG`). \"Try again\" (PairingScreen.kt:191-195) clears `digits` and calls `onRetry` → `repository.cancelPairing()` → `publishPairing(pairedRecordOrNull())` → state becomes `PairingState.Paired` with the OLD record. The screen renders \"Paired with &lt;old origin&gt;…\" + a Done button; the code field never returns. The user is told they are paired when the attempt they just made failed, and has no in-place path back to entry.\n\n**6.**\n- `file`: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt\n- `line`: 203\n- `summary`: In the QR entry the `Failed` branch offers only \"Start again\" wired to `onCancel`, which navigates off the pairing route rather than resetting the scanner.\n- `failure_scenario`: QR pairing fails with `UNREACHABLE` (wrong Wi-Fi) — the single most common recoverable QR failure. The only control is \"Start again\" → `onCancel` → `session.cancelPairing()` + `route = CompanionRoute.AGENTS` (CompanionShell.kt:181-184). The user is thrown out of pairing entirely and must re-navigate Diagnostics → Pair, and the camera permission/scanner is torn down and rebuilt. The CODE entry got a reset-in-place path; QR did not.\n\n## Checked and found consistent\n\nSRP domain separation and identity/label/transcript-prefix parity across repos (`sc/v3 remote-pair`, `sc/v3-srp*`, salt prefix); fixed-window ladder guards (`srpSecretModPow` exponent width, `table[0] = N+1`, `srpAssertClientPublic`, `u == 0`, `B mod N == 0`); `srpBigIntToBytes`/`Srp.pad` exact-width refusal on both sides; `base64UrlDecode`/`decodeWireId` canonicality vs `decodeExact` (no outer-accepts/inner-refuses drift with device-store); the frozen wire contract itself (`400` + body literal `pair_unknown_candidate` on kex and confirm, no `pair_restart_required`, no alias, no 400 entry in `FAILURE_BY_STATUS`, no automatic re-begin/re-confirm, `retryOnConnectionFailure(false)`); `requireRemoteCandidate` charging nothing (no confirm attempt, no MAC strike) and its position after window/version/source/pid; `pair_wrong_peer` retained for rewrite/wrong-source/wrong-pid; LAN v1 vocabulary unchanged; `evictOldestKexSessionsIfFull` oldest-first + `zeroKexSession` wipe; monotone `kexComputations` charged only for genuinely new derivations; sealed step-4 AAD path/protocol-version parity (`/v1/pair/confirm`, protocol 1) and `PROTOCOL_CURRENT == PAIRING_REMOTE_WIRE_VERSION` alignment; host/Access isolation (`routeByHost` matrix, `normaliseHostHeader`, `PAIRING_PUBLIC_HOST !== BRIDGE_PUBLIC_HOST` startup assert); window-slot reserve/claim/release and both `close()` paths (handle preservation, timer clear, key/salt/verifier destruction, burn memo); Unicode digit normalisation (`Character.digit` → ASCII, `passwordBytes` guard, `assertAsciiDigitCode`); `GroupedCodeTransformation` offset mapping; docs (PROTOCOL.md §4.9.3 normative text matches implementation; FEATURES.md marker table consistent with sibling `(PAIR-*)` markers).\n\n**FIX-FIRST**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>188562</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>68</tool_uses><duration_ms>709596</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a6fd47450b7f4ff77</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_wbsTnEXkEoVUXx2JoMbXA0uW</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a6fd47450b7f4ff77.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify countdown rerenders\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>CONFIRMED (low magnitude).\n\n**Corrected location:** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\components\\PairDeviceDialog\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx:189` (write) and `:195` (`setInterval(tick, 250)`). Candidate's `:253` is wrong — that line is the pairingState poll's error handler.\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\components\\PairDeviceDialog\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx:189\nDefect: `setRemainingMs(left)` fires every 250 ms while the only consumer quantises to whole seconds (`Math.ceil(remainingMs / 1000)`, :359) → ~4x the renders needed for identical visible output.\nFailure: dialog left open for a full 120 s window → 480 state commits / PairingFlow renders where 120 change pixels; the other 360 re-run PairingFlow + QrCode + DialogFooter/Button and reconcile the SVG subtree for no visual delta. Multiplies per \"Show a new code\" click.\nFix: keep the 250 ms interval (its `left &lt;= 0` check is what promptly hands off to `closing`) but make the state write quantised — store seconds, e.g. `setRemainingMs(prev =&gt; Math.ceil(prev/1000) === Math.ceil(left/1000) ? prev : left)` or `setSecondsLeft(Math.max(0, Math.ceil(left/1000)))` — so identical visible values hit React's Object.is bailout.\n```\n\nEvidence:\n- Only two `remainingMs` reads in this file: declaration :118 and `secondsLeft` :359. The other `remainingMs` grep hits are unrelated (`resyncAgentStatus.ts`).\n- No subsecond consumption of the *state*: the subsecond value that matters (`left &lt;= 0` → `{kind:\"closing\"}`, :193) is a local from `expiresAtMs - Date.now()`, not from state. So interval frequency is functionally justified; the state write cadence is not.\n- No React bailout: `left` decreases by ~250 ms each tick, so `Object.is(prev, next)` is always false → full render + reconcile every tick. Bailout would only apply to identical values.\n- QrCode (`...\\components\\QrCode\\QrCode.tsx:32`) is **not** `React.memo`, so its body re-runs on every parent render; `useMemo([value])` with a stable `phase.qrUri` does spare the ~2800-module encode and returns the same `path` string reference, so DOM attrs aren't rewritten. That caps the cost at React work, not layout/paint — which is why this is MINOR, not MAJOR. The comment at QrCode.tsx:28-30 (\"a visible cost every time the countdown re-renders the dialog\") shows the author already recognised countdown-driven re-render pressure.\n- `aria-live=\"polite\"` (:373-378): identical text → React writes nothing → no spurious screen-reader announcements from the extra renders.\n- Interval lifecycle clean: keyed on `expiresAtMs` only, immediate `tick()`, `clearInterval` on cleanup (:185-198); no leak or double-timer.\n\nChecked and clean: interval lifecycle/cleanup, `expiresAtMs` effect keying, QrCode memoization correctness, poll effect in-flight guard, absence of other `remainingMs` consumers.\n\n**Verdict: CONFIRMED** — real redundant-render inefficiency, ~360 wasted renders per 120 s window, bounded impact (memoized QR path, no DOM writes).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>31598</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>9</tool_uses><duration_ms>133115</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>addb9326abc49c7ec</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_UzrcGcDvGKA3mf71na9ntJ6W</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\addb9326abc49c7ec.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify HTTP client leak\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## CONFIRMED\n\n**Corrected location:** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\PairingClient.kt:223` (line number exact as claimed; method is `pairWithCode`, not `pairRemote`)\n\n**Summary:** `pairWithCode`'s default arg `handshake: RemotePairingHandshake = RemotePairingHandshake()` is re-evaluated on every invocation and falls through to `RemotePairingHandshake`'s own `baseClient: OkHttpClient = OkHttpClient()`, so the remote-pairing path is the one place in the codebase that does not derive from `CompanionCore`'s single process-lifetime `httpClient`.\n\n**Concrete retry/resource scenario:** User mistypes the 8-digit code. `BridgeCompanionRepository.pairFromCode` (line 962) calls `core.pairingClient().pairWithCode(...)` with no `handshake` → attempt 1 builds `OkHttpClient()` #1 with its own `ConnectionPool`, derives the pinned-TLS client from it, runs begin/kex/confirm, fails `CODE_WRONG`. User retries twice more inside the desktop's 120 s window (which burns after 3 strikes) → clients #2 and #3, each with a fresh pool. No attempt can reuse the previous attempt's connection to `REMOTE_PAIRING_ORIGIN`: every retry pays a full TCP + TLS handshake on a mobile link, in a flow already racing a 120 s window, and each attempt's idle socket is retained by its orphaned pool until the 5-minute keepAlive eviction instead of being returned to the shared pool. Same for any future base-client configuration (proxy, CT/logging interceptor, socket factory) — remote pairing would silently not receive it.\n\n**Impact correction (the candidate overstates two parts):**\n- **Not** an unbounded leak. Nothing needs closing — no `Cache` is configured, and no `evictAll`/`shutdown` exists anywhere in the repo (grep: only `PairingScreen.kt:444`, an unrelated camera `Executor`). Retention is bounded by idle eviction.\n- **Not** \"separate dispatchers\" in any thread-cost sense. `RemotePairingHandshake.post` (line 381) uses synchronous `client.newCall(request).execute()`, which never touches `Dispatcher.executorService`, and okhttp 5.4.0 (`gradle/libs.versions.toml:14`) backs connection-pool cleanup with the shared `TaskRunner`. No per-client threads are created.\n\nSo: real convention break plus real per-retry connection/handshake waste — **MINOR**, not a leak.\n\n**Evidence**\n- `PairingClient.kt:223` — default arg, evaluated per call by Kotlin's `$default` bridge; no caller ever supplies it (`pairWithCode` has exactly two hits repo-wide: the declaration and the one caller).\n- `RemotePairingHandshake.kt:80` — `baseClient: OkHttpClient = OkHttpClient()`; line 86 derives the real client via `baseClient.newBuilder()`, i.e. the class is built to inherit a shared pool but is handed a fresh one.\n- `CompanionCore.kt:31,49,96,98` — one `httpClient` field threaded into `PairingClient`, `SealedTransport`, `EventStreamClient`; `PairingClient.kt:52-63` and `SealedTransport.kt:89,110-111` both derive with `baseClient.newBuilder()`, establishing the shared-base convention that line 223 breaks.\n- `CompanionContainer.kt:90-106` — the core is built once in a `by lazy` repository, so that shared client lives for the process.\n- `BridgeCompanionRepository.kt:948-1002` — user-invokable, retryable entry point; failures set `PairingState.Failed` and return, leaving retry entirely to the user.\n- No reuse/cleanup invariant exists that would excuse this: `PairingClient` does not even retain `baseClient` (only the derived `client`), so there is currently no path by which the handshake could inherit the shared pool.\n\n**Fix direction (not applied):** retain `baseClient` in `PairingClient` and construct the handshake from it (`RemotePairingHandshake(baseClient = baseClient)`), or make the parameter non-defaulted and have `CompanionCore` build the handshake with `httpClient`.\n\n**Verdict: FIX-FIRST** (MINOR — mechanism confirmed, impact bounded)</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>54443</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>21</tool_uses><duration_ms>188336</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>aa60a2b4b066b592e</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_LpAiIAEJevAFAZuwaNcM4D0a</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\aa60a2b4b066b592e.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify synchronous SRP blocking\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**REFUTED**\n\nCorrected path/line: candidate file does not exist. No `packages/host-service/src/companion/srp.ts` (nothing at `:321`), no SRP implementation anywhere in `packages/` or `apps/`. Actual code under the described role: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts` (kex handler `handleKex` at line 417; confirm handler `handleConfirm` at line 615) plus `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\crypto.ts:326` (`x25519`).\n\nSummary: every load-bearing premise fails.\n\n1. **No SRP, no 3072-bit exponentiation.** Pairing is X25519 ECDH. `crypto.ts:326-351` calls Node's native `diffieHellman()` on `KeyObject`s built from raw RFC 8410 DER prefixes; key gen is `generateKeyPairSync(\"x25519\")` (`crypto.ts:353-368`). No bigint math, no `modPow`, no `createDiffieHellman`, no MODP/ffdhe group constant, no fixed-window exponentiation code anywhere in the tree. Cost per op is native curve25519 (tens of microseconds), not a multi-millisecond modexp.\n2. **Zero asymmetric ops per kex.** `handleKex` (`pairing.ts:417-487`) does: window check, `v` check, pid constant-time compare, base64url decodes (`deviceId` 16 B, `pPub` 32 B), string/enum/range validation, a `Map` lookup, and a bookkeeping insert plus O(n≤16) eviction scan. It never calls `x25519`, never derives keys. The \"twice per unauthenticated kex\" claim has no code behind it.\n3. **The single ECDH sits behind the authenticated-path caps.** `x25519` is reached only via `derivePairingKeys` (`pairing.ts:367`) from `handleConfirm`, which requires a matching `pid` (`pairing.ts:626`) AND an existing candidate session (`:628`), then charges `chargeConfirmAttempt` (`:642`, cap `PAIRING_ATTEMPTS_PER_WINDOW`), with `burnMac` (`:841`) consuming the window after `PAIRING_MAX_BAD_MACS`. Upper bound is a handful of ECDH ops per 120 s window, not 64+.\n4. **The \"64-computation flood\" number is arithmetic over caps that gate storage, not crypto.** `MAX_KEX_SESSIONS = 16` (`:148`), `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE = 4` (`:156`), `MAX_KEX_SOURCES = 32` (`:158`). Even the true worst case (32 sources x 4 = 128 introductions) buys only 128 x (4 KB-bounded body parse + 48 B of base64 decode). Body size is capped at `MAX_PAIR_BODY_BYTES = 4 * 1024` (`:139`, enforced in `readBody` `:1229-1245`), and `headersTimeout`/`requestTimeout` are set (`:1066-1067`).\n5. **Async boundaries are intact where they matter.** `route` is async; the only awaits on the confirm path are genuine I/O (`deps.onPaired`, `deps.loadAccessToken`, `flushed`). The listener is a distinct socket (`0.0.0.0:47611`, 120 s lifetime, `:1076`) but shares the event loop — irrelevant given per-request work is microsecond-scale byte handling.\n\nConcrete load -&gt; observable failure: none reproducible. Saturating the documented caps (32 distinct sources x 4 deviceIds x 4 KB bodies inside one 120 s window) yields ~128 requests of pure parse/validate work with no asymmetric crypto — well under a millisecond aggregate of CPU. Bridge/event/answer traffic on the host-service loop sees no measurable contiguous stall. The claimed damage requires an exponentiation that does not exist in this codebase.\n\nEvidence: full read of `pairing.ts` (1293 lines) and the `x25519`/`generateX25519KeyPair`/HKDF region of `crypto.ts`; content searches for `modPow|3072|kex|powmod|modexp` across `packages/host-service/src` (hits only in `pairing.ts` comments, `limits.ts:93`, `types.ts:297`, and an unrelated `terminal.seq-catchup.node-test.ts:719` `\"blob 3072\"`), for `srp` across `packages/` (no files) and `apps/` (only base64 inside `cursor.svg`), and for `MODP|ffdhe|srpVerifier|SRP_|modPow|createDiffieHellman` across both trees (no code hits). Note: `Bash` was unusable this session (shell init failure, `\"/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching `''\"`), so `git diff`/`git log` could not be run — findings rest on the checked-out file contents only.\n\nSecurity invariants/caps unchanged; nothing modified (read-only throughout).\n\nClean on inspection: kex/confirm cap layering, per-source throttle and eviction bounds, pid constant-time compare ordering, MAC-strike burn ordering, body-size and timeout limits, secret zeroing in `close()`/`handleConfirm` `finally`.\n\n**SHIP**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>50503</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>19</tool_uses><duration_ms>212397</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a099f92dbfd042d82</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_yaHdsgSqGqLwdUAABluBz6et</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a099f92dbfd042d82.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify no-window source shape\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>REFUTED\n\n**Corrected location:** candidate names `C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/packages/host-service/src/companion/http.ts:820`; that line is `export const ROUTES: Record&lt;SealedPath, SealedRoute&gt; = {` — the sealed route table. Nearest real construct with the described *shape* is `C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:1134-1138`.\n\n**Summary:** Every load-bearing identifier in the candidate is absent from the repo. `cf-connecting-ip` appears exactly once, in unrelated marketing code (`C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/apps/marketing/src/lib/email-rate-limit.ts:25`); `routeByHost`, `pair_unknown_candidate`, and any Cloudflare-IP-header read in host-service do not exist. The bridge's own caller identity comes from Cloudflare Access JWT (`cf-access-jwt-assertion`, `packages/host-service/src/companion/access-jwt.ts:55`), never from a client IP header, and no host-based routing or \"remote window exists\" branch exists on this listener. The missing-source state the candidate needs is unreachable because no code path reads that header at all.\n\n**The real analog, and why it is also not the described defect:**\n```ts\n// pairing.ts:1130-1138\nif (path === PAIR_PATH_KEX) {\n    const sourceKey = req.socket.remoteAddress;\n    if (sourceKey === undefined || sourceKey.length === 0) {\n        writeJson(res, 400, { code: \"unknown\" satisfies PairingErrorCode });\n        return;\n    }\n```\n- Different socket, different server: pairing is a separate `createServer` bound to `PAIRING_BIND_HOST:PAIRING_PORT` (LAN, 120 s lifetime, `pairing.ts:1063-1087`), not the Hono loopback bridge that owns the bodyless catch-all `app.all(\"*\", (c) =&gt; c.body(null, 404))` (`http.ts:1188`). No Access/tunnel hop, so no proxy IP header is in play.\n- No no-window 404 to be distinguishable *from*: when no window is open the pairing server is closed (`close()` at `pairing.ts:1016-1061`) → TCP refusal, not a 404 body. Comparison premise does not exist.\n- Code is `{\"code\":\"unknown\"}`, not `pair_unknown_candidate`, and the check is on `req.socket.remoteAddress`, not a header — so the \"frozen literal\" constraint is not touched either way.\n- Not observable: `req.socket.remoteAddress` is `undefined` only once the socket is destroyed; the handler runs after `readBody` resolves on `'end'` with a live socket. In the destroyed case `writeJson` writes to a dead socket and no caller reads the 400. Every other rejection on this listener (`405`, `404`, malformed-JSON `400`, `pair_version_unsupported`) already returns the same JSON envelope, so the branch adds no fingerprint.\n\n**Failure scenario:** none constructible. To reach the described state a caller would have to make host-service read `cf-connecting-ip`; nothing in `packages/host-service/src/` reads it, and the pairing listener never consults request headers for source identity.\n\n**Evidence:** repo-wide search for `routeByHost|pair_unknown|connecting.ip|cf_connecting` → single hit in `apps/marketing`; repo-wide `pair_unknown_candidate` → zero hits; `http.ts:700-960` read (line 820 is `ROUTES`); `pairing.ts:1099-1183` route handler and `pairing.ts:409-545` `handleKex`/`chargeKexIntroduction` read in full; `http.ts:1110-1208` shows Access-JWT-only auth plus the bodyless 404 catch-all on the loopback app; `handleKex`/`handleConfirm` have exactly one call site each (`pairing.ts:1142`, `1147`).\n\nChecked clean: pairing kex/confirm ordering and rate-limit charging, candidate-table eviction bounds, sealed route table coverage, bridge auth boundary and 404 catch-all.\n\n**Verdict: SHIP** (candidate refuted; nothing to fix)</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>47708</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>23</tool_uses><duration_ms>209924</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a408e9c5ecb977ada</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_33S6TIzpt8z70fDDrlZUUux1</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a408e9c5ecb977ada.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify SRP constant recompute\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**REFUTED**\n\nCandidate target does not exist. No SRP implementation anywhere in the repo.\n\nClosest real code: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts` (companion handshake), current lines 355–396 (`derivePairingKeys`) and 148 (`MAX_KEX_SESSIONS`).\n\nSummary\n- No file `packages/host-service/src/companion/srp.ts`. Companion dir holds 53 `.ts` files; `srp.ts` is not among them.\n- Repo-wide search for `srp.ts`, `secure-remote-password`, `srpVerifier`, `modPow`, `PAD(`, `H(PAD` across `.ts/.tsx/.md/.json/.kt/.swift` → zero matches. No SRP group constants, no `k = H(PAD(N)||PAD(g))`, no modular exponentiation.\n- Actual handshake is X25519 + HKDF, not SRP: `derivePairingKeys` does `x25519(dPriv, pPub)` → `IKM = Z || pairingCode` → `hkdfExtract` → `hkdfExpand` for `deviceKey`/`confirmPhone`/`confirmDesktop`. No group parameters exist to cache.\n- No BigInt modular arithmetic in the handshake path. Only `BigInt` uses in `companion/` are 64-bit counters/timestamps (`crypto.ts:426,558,676`, `device-store.ts` seq/epoch, `keys.ts` highWater) — unrelated to key exchange.\n- Stated \"64-kex cap\" also wrong: caps are `MAX_KEX_SESSIONS = 16` (pairing.ts:148) and `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE = 4` (pairing.ts:156).\n\nCost scenario: none. Nothing recomputes a group constant → zero per-handshake waste to eliminate. Both the file and the alleged hot computation are fabricated.\n\nEvidence: directory enumeration of `packages/host-service/src/companion` (no `srp.ts`); Read of `pairing.ts:286-405, 489-567`; four negative pattern searches (`\\bsrp\\b|kex` scoped to `host-service/src` returned only `limits.ts:93` doc text and pairing.ts kex references; `modPow|PAD\\(|H\\(PAD|srp` over `packages` → no matches).\n\nAreas checked clean: companion pairing/kex path, key-schedule helpers, kex session caps and rate limiting, BigInt usage in `companion/`.\n\nNote: Bash tool is nonfunctional in this environment (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching '` on every command, including `echo ok`), and Glob times out at 20s repo-wide; verification used Read plus path-scoped Grep. This limited git-history inspection — I could not run `git log` to check whether an `srp.ts` ever existed, but no reference to one survives in any tracked source, doc, or client file.\n\n**FIX-FIRST** — not applicable to code; the candidate itself should be dropped from the efficiency list.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>30777</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>23</tool_uses><duration_ms>217283</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a2a39361eb60fba36</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_F8ncuNMgYB1EwPnGE7KjQBMO</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a2a39361eb60fba36.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify PID decode churn\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**PLAUSIBLE**\n\n**Corrected location.** Store site: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:959-965` — `pidBytes = randomBytes(WIRE_ID_BYTES)` minted at :959, only `pid: base64UrlEncode(pidBytes)` retained at :965, raw bytes dropped. Cited :1097 is the closing brace of `openPairingWindow`, not the store.\n\nRedundant decode sites of trusted state:\n- `pairing.ts:857` — `constantTimeEquals(given, base64UrlDecode(state.pid))` inside `assertPidMatches`, reached from `handleKex` (:432) and `handleConfirm` (:626).\n- `pairing.ts:646` — `const pidBytes = base64UrlDecode(state.pid);` feeding `derivePairingKeys` (:653) and `buildPairingTranscript` (:658).\n\n**Summary.** Mechanism real. `state.pid` typed `PairingId = Base64Url` (`types.ts:33`, `types.ts:367`) → wire string only; every raw-byte consumer re-decodes. Redundant decodes per request: kex = 1, confirm = 2. Counting the necessary attacker-supplied decode, 2-3 total per request — candidate's per-request arithmetic holds.\n\n**Concrete cost — candidate's figures wrong.**\n- \"1024/window\" unsupported. `PAIRING_ATTEMPTS_PER_WINDOW = 5` (`config.ts:148`), burn at `PAIRING_MAX_BAD_MACS = 3` (`config.ts:63`), kex introductions capped `MAX_KEX_SOURCES 32 * MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE 4 = 128`.\n- Unbounded surface exists but isn't the one claimed: wrong-pid requests only increment/log (`noteUnattributedAttempt`, :870 — never limited), and a repeat kex with identical material returns at :468 before `chargeKexIntroduction` → both replayable for the full 120 s window at socket rate.\n- Per decode: 22-char input through `crypto.ts:750-762` = regex alphabet test + length check + `Buffer.from` + full re-encode + string compare ≈ 3 small allocations, sub-microsecond.\n- Same-path dominators: `JSON.parse` + Node HTTP socket/headers per request; on confirm additionally X25519 + HKDF (`derivePairingKeys`), 2x HMAC-SHA256 (:665, :674), sealing, plus awaited `deps.onPaired` (:681) and `deps.loadAccessToken` (:693) I/O. Redundant decode work is &lt;10 ms even at 10k flood requests/window. No observable effect at any reachable rate.\n\n**Ownership/security downsides — none blocking, one caveat.** `state.pid` is not secret (emitted in `kexResponse` :492, QR URI :986, log :1094), so a cached `pidBytes` needs no zeroization and `close()` (:1036-1037 zeroes only `pairingCode`/`dPriv`) stays correct. Caveat: it adds a mutable `Uint8Array` to session state against a module convention where byte fields carry \"zeroed on close\" semantics. The string form is required at :492, :986, :1001/:1008, :1094 → pid must remain a string; only an additive `pidBytes` companion is viable, never a replacement.\n\n**Maintenance angle is the only real value.** Precedent already exists: `KexSession` stores both `deviceId` (string) and `deviceIdBytes` (raw), consumed at :654/:659. A `pidBytes` companion matches that convention and deletes 2 decode sites.\n\n**Evidence.** Read `pairing.ts:182-199, 410-497, 610-709, 779-875, 930-1097`; `crypto.ts:750-762`; `types.ts:33-39, 286-378`; `config.ts:62-63, 148`; `limits.ts:38-43`. Bash unusable in this session (shell profile init error `unexpected EOF while looking for matching '`) → static read-only verification only, no runtime measurement.\n\nVote: accept as MINOR consistency cleanup; reject the efficiency framing and the 1024/window cost figure.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>39308</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>22</tool_uses><duration_ms>215996</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>acb422cff09a3381f</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\acb422cff09a3381f.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify protocol AAD pinning\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**CONFIRMED** (latent; no present-day breakage — `PROTOCOL_CURRENT == 1` today, so wire matches).\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:352\nDefect: step-6/step-4 pairing-confirm AAD binds the MOVING constant CompanionConstants.PROTOCOL_CURRENT where PROTOCOL.md and the host both freeze the byte at 0x01; nothing pins the two together.\nFailure: protocol 2 ships → PROTOCOL_CURRENT = 2 (its doc comment is \"What PROTOCOL.md describes\", and CLIENT_PROTOCOL_MAX derives from it, so a bump is the natural single edit). Phone then builds AAD ...||0x00||0x02 while host seals with PROTOCOL_VERSION_AT_PAIRING = 1 → GCM tag failure in SealedEnvelope.open on EVERY pairing, remote and LAN. Remote surfaces PairingFailure.CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED (\"the desktop's sealed reply could not be opened\"), LAN surfaces BAD_MAC — i.e. indistinguishable from MITM, exactly the regression the host comment at pairing.ts:1652-1662 records as already having happened once. 100% of new pairings dead; existing paired devices unaffected. Fails closed (no data loss), and no test catches it: RemotePairingHandshakeTest.kt:902 seals the fake confirm with PROTOCOL_CURRENT too (self-consistent → stays green), and the frozen differential vectors (CrossRuntimeDifferentialVectors20260729Test.kt:218, :443) hand-build AAD with literal 1 and never call openConfirmEnvelope, so they stay green as well.\nFix: add a dedicated frozen constant (e.g. PROTOCOL_VERSION_AT_PAIRING = 1, mirroring the host name, with a \"pairing precedes negotiation — never track PROTOCOL_CURRENT\" comment) and use it at both AAD sites; assert it in the frozen vector test so a future bump fails loudly at build time.\n```\n\nSame defect, second site: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\PairingClient.kt:273` (LAN `/pair/confirm`) — identical `PROTOCOL_CURRENT` use, same host constant on the other end.\n\nEvidence chain\n- Spec, `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\PROTOCOL.md:399` — `|| 0x00 || protocolVersion  1 byte, the negotiated protocol (§6); 0x01 before negotiation`; `:968` (remote step 6) — `METHOD \"POST\", PATH \"/v1/pair/confirm\", protocolVersion 0x01`. Pairing precedes `hello`, so the value is a literal, not \"current\".\n- Android constant, `...\\core\\CompanionConstants.kt:67-73` — `PROTOCOL_BASELINE = 0`, `PROTOCOL_CURRENT = 1`, `CLIENT_PROTOCOL_MAX = PROTOCOL_CURRENT`. Nothing marks `PROTOCOL_CURRENT` frozen; `PROTOCOL.md:3114` enumerates protocol versions as `0`, `1`, ... i.e. designed to grow.\n- Host frozen literal, `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:262` → `const PROTOCOL_VERSION_AT_PAIRING = 1;`, sole confirm-seal site at `:1666` (shared by both flows via `state.profile.confirmPath`) → no lockstep coupling to any Android constant.\n- No pin: only `PROTOCOL_CURRENT` occurrences are the two AAD sites, `CLIENT_PROTOCOL_MAX`, `BridgeClient.kt:428`, `SealedTransport.kt:63`, and tests that re-use the same constant.\n\nRelated, not the candidate: `...\\core\\transport\\SealedTransport.kt:57-63` documents \"`1` before `hello` (§3.3)\" yet initializes `protocolVersion = PROTOCOL_CURRENT` — same \"literal expressed as a moving constant\" shape on the pre-hello default, though that axis is legitimately negotiated afterwards.\n\nChecked clean: host confirm-seal path and its single AAD constant; `EnvelopeAad.forRequest/forResponse/forEventFrame` field order and separators vs §3.3; remote payload `v` checks (`requireRemoteVersion`, remote `v=2` per PROTOCOL.md:984); `pair_unknown_candidate` wire literal left untouched (no unsafety demonstrated); negotiation §6/§6.1 baseline handling. Not checked: git diff membership of these hunks — the Bash tool aborts on a broken shell profile (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching '`), so no `git diff`/test run was possible; findings are from source and docs only.\n\n**FIX-FIRST**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>67235</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>29</tool_uses><duration_ms>214966</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>aa97907abca83ce65</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\aa97907abca83ce65.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify sealed-response honesty\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## CONFIRMED\n\n**File:** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts`\n**Current line:** 1616 (`const access = await deps.loadAccessToken();`), inside `sealPairedResponse` — candidate line accurate, no drift.\n\n**Summary:** `loadAccessToken` runs *after* `onPaired` has already persisted the device record + K_dev; its throw is a plain `Error` → HTTP 500 `{\"code\":\"unknown\"}` → phone's fallback maps to `BAD_MAC` → user is told \"Nothing was stored.\" while the desktop holds a live paired device.\n\n**Failure scenario:** Remote code pairing, correct code, MAC verifies. `~/.superset/companion/cloudflare-access.json` is unreadable/torn/rotated-mismatched at that instant (deleted or rotated since bridge start, AV/backup lock → `EACCES`, OneDrive placeholder eviction, partial write during rotation). Sequence:\n1. `pairing.ts:1601` `state.consumed = true`.\n2. `pairing.ts:1604` `onPaired` → `deviceStore.create` + `keyStore.put` committed to disk.\n3. `pairing.ts:1616` throws (`config.ts:786` `readJsonFile` / `requireString` / `requireEqual`, all bare `throw new Error`).\n4. `pairing.ts:2498-2510` — `state.consumed` true → window closed quietly, error rethrown.\n5. `http.ts:891-918` — not `PairingError`/`CleartextError` → `pairingErrorResponse(500, \"unknown\")`.\n6. Phone `RemotePairingHandshake.kt:408` — `\"unknown\"` absent from `FAILURE_BY_WIRE`, `500` absent from `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` → `?: PairingFailure.BAD_MAC`.\n7. `RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt:28` → `PairingError.BAD_MAC` → `PairingState.kt:145` `\"Pairing failed to verify. Nothing was stored.\"`\n\nDamage: orphan paired device + live K_dev on desktop, phone stored nothing, and the message actively tells the user no cleanup is needed → stale device record persists unnoticed. Honesty/consistency defect, not corruption or lockout (§4.8 new-deviceId-per-pairing → re-pair still works).\n\n**Evidence:**\n- `pairing.ts:1577-1583` — codebase itself states \"`onPaired` writes to disk and `loadAccessToken` reads from it, so **either can throw** for reasons that have nothing to do with this exchange\". Reachability acknowledged in-repo; the wipe is handled, the *reporting* is not.\n- `pairing.ts:1560-1564` accepts \"desktop holding a key the phone never received — recoverable by re-pairing\" as the crash ordering trade-off, but nothing propagates that fact to the phone as a distinct code.\n- `config.ts:782-796` + `888-896` — sync `readFileSync` + strict field/identity validation, every failure a plain `Error`.\n- `index.ts:470` validates the same file at bridge start → failure at pairing time requires post-start change/transient, i.e. rare, not impossible.\n- Counter-evidence *against* intentionality: `BridgeCompanionRepository.kt:984-991` deliberately routes `ProtocolException` to `PROTOCOL_MISMATCH` rather than BAD_MAC precisely because \"by the time most of these can fire the desktop has already paired this device, so the claim would be false\". Same invariant, gap unpatched for the 500 path.\n- `RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt:17-18` reserves BAD_MAC for \"the exchange was rejected and **nothing was stored**\" — the 500 fallback violates that stated contract.\n- `PROTOCOL.md` §4.9.7 error table carries no code for a post-persist host failure; nothing to map.\n\n**Frozen contract untouched:** missing-candidate remains `pairing.ts:1149` `throw new PairingError(400, \"pair_unknown_candidate\")` → `FAILURE_BY_WIRE` → `UNKNOWN_CANDIDATE` → `RESTART_REQUIRED`. Not implicated, no change proposed.\n\n**Fix direction (not applied):** load the Access token *before* `onPaired` (nothing is persisted yet if it throws), or give post-persist failures their own `PairingError` code meaning \"paired on the desktop, reply not delivered\" so the phone stops asserting nothing was stored.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>63450</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>28</tool_uses><duration_ms>219070</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a156cda089b7159f4</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a156cda089b7159f4.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify gateway status mapping\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>VERDICT: **PLAUSIBLE**\n\nLocation (current): `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:470` (`FAILURE_BY_STATUS`, entries 471-478; default at line 408).\n\nSummary: Cloudflare gateway statuses (502/504/520-524/530) have no entry in `FAILURE_BY_STATUS`, their HTML bodies fail `PairErrorBody` decode, so they fall to the `PairingFailure.BAD_MAC` default → user text \"Pairing failed to verify. Nothing was stored.\" — verification/no-storage language for a tunnel/origin outage, while 503 alone routes to NETWORK.\n\nFailure scenario: desktop shows a pairing code, `cloudflared` service is down or the pairing listener isn't bound (530 / 502 — both empirically observed on this exact tunnel, `SETUP.md:53-65`). Phone posts `/v1/pair/begin`; edge returns 530 with an HTML page. Result: \"Pairing failed to verify. Nothing was stored.\" Sharper variant: a 504/502 on `/v1/pair/confirm` after the desktop already processed confirm and stored the device record → phone asserts \"Nothing was stored\" while the desktop holds a paired device, with no \"check your desktop's paired devices\" advice (the sentence `CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED` exists to give). Same physical situation arriving as an `IOException` correctly yields UNREACHABLE (`RemotePairingHandshake.kt:389-397`, per `PROTOCOL.md:1005-1009`); arriving as a status does not.\n\nEvidence:\n- `RemotePairingHandshake.kt:401-410` — `remoteFailureOf`: wire code null on unparsable body, `FAILURE_BY_STATUS[502]` null, `?: PairingFailure.BAD_MAC`.\n- `CompanionJson.kt:51-55` — HTML body → `ProtocolException`, the only exception `remoteFailureOf` catches → `code == null`. No crash, silent default.\n- `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` has 401/403/404/410/429/503 only; no 5xx besides 503.\n- `RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt:28` → `PairingState.kt:145` = \"Pairing failed to verify. Nothing was stored.\"; reached via `BridgeCompanionRepository.kt:973-975`. No interception in `PairingClient.pairWithCode` (`PairingClient.kt:218-226`).\n- Convention divergence in-repo: `ConnectionClassifier.kt:47-50` calls the 530-vs-502 distinction \"load-bearing\" (530 = DesktopUnreachable, 502 = SupersetNotRunning) for the same Cloudflare architecture.\n- Honest-claim rule the codebase enforces: `RemotePairingErrorMappingTest.kt:135-152` (`onlyTheFailuresThatCanKnowItClaimNothingWasStored`) — BAD_MAC is allowed the claim only because \"in the flow that raises it, nothing was\".\n\nCounter-evidence (why PLAUSIBLE, not CONFIRMED): `PROTOCOL.md:1248-1252` normatively mandates this exact default — \"Any other non-2xx is a rejection the phone has no name for: it reports 'nothing was stored' and must neither call it unreachable (the endpoint answered) nor blame the code.\" Code matches spec; the defect is in the spec's blanket clause, whose \"the endpoint answered\" premise is false for 502/530/504 (the edge answered, the desktop did not).\n\nFix direction (not applied): add gateway statuses to `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` — 502/504/520-524/530 → `UNREACHABLE` (\"the edge could not reach the desktop right now\", same class as `edge_unverifiable`/503) — and amend `PROTOCOL.md` §4.9.7 accordingly; separately, stop claiming \"Nothing was stored\" for any failure after `confirm` was transmitted. Leave HTTP-400 / `pair_unknown_candidate` wire-code-only handling untouched; add no automatic retry.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>68879</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>30</tool_uses><duration_ms>242225</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a86072aa40e0253a9</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_bBedYuB7FaYvODm3BHO6X86o</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a86072aa40e0253a9.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify saved pairing code\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## REFUTED\n\n**File:** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt:113` (line number correct as cited: `var digits by rememberSaveable { mutableStateOf(\"\") }`)\n\n**Summary.** Saved-state copy of the 8 digits is real, but it is not a *persisted* secret and gains an attacker nothing. Three invariants kill the claim: no disk serialization, no lower-privileged reader than the heap copy the protocol already accepts, and zero residual value once the desktop window closes.\n\n**Evidence**\n\n1. **No disk copy — platform invariant.** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\AndroidManifest.xml:31-41`: `MainActivity` declares no `android:persistableMode`. Plain `onSaveInstanceState(Bundle)` state lives only in system_server RAM (`ActivityRecord` saved state); only `persistableMode` + `PersistableBundle` reaches `recent_tasks/*_task.xml` on disk. Plus `allowBackup=\"false\"`, `fullBackupContent=\"false\"` (manifest:22-23) → no backup/adb-pull path.\n2. **Already-accepted exposure class, same adversary.** `PROTOCOL.md:1102-1105` (§4.9.4, \"What is *not* claimed\"): \"The typed code is not zeroizable the way `pc` is. It arrives from a text field as a `String` and lives on the heap until the collector gets to it… Its 120-second, single-use lifetime is what bounds that, not memory hygiene.\" Reading the system_server Bundle requires root/system — strictly ≥ the privilege needed to read the app heap copy the doc accepts. No new reader.\n3. **Retained value is dead by normative desktop behaviour.** `PROTOCOL.md:847-853`: 120 000 ms lifetime, exactly one successful use then window closes, 3 bad confirm MACs burn it, \"On close/burn the code, the SRP verifier, `b`, `S` and `PRK` are zeroed\"; each window mints a fresh CSPRNG code (`PROTOCOL.md:901`). A restored stale code authenticates to nothing. Window-linked erasure exists — on the side that holds the verifier, which is where it decides the outcome.\n4. **Copy stops being saved once the screen leaves.** `CompanionShell.kt:177-190` renders `PairingScreen` from a bare `when (route)` — no `SaveableStateHolder`/`SaveableStateProvider`, so the `rememberSaveable` entry is unregistered on leaving composition (`onCancel`/`onDone` → `route = AGENTS`). No post-pairing retention.\n5. **Retry stays user-triggered (unchanged).** Submits exist only in `Button onClick` at `PairingScreen.kt:187-196` and `238-242`; `resubmitsSameCode` (`:341-343`) gates resubmit to `RESTART_REQUIRED` + 8 digits, matching `PROTOCOL.md:1027-1036` (\"recovery is user-initiated only\", \"`confirm` is NEVER re-sent automatically\"). Wire literal untouched.\n6. **Restored state cannot mislead the countdown.** `submittedAtMs` (`:119`) only renders under `PairingState.Exchanging` (`:217-220`); process death drops the ViewModel state, so a restored `submittedAtMs` is never displayed.\n\n**Residual, not the claimed defect (MINOR, informational).** After process-death restore on the PAIRING route the field is pre-filled with a stale code and `Pair` is enabled (`CodeEntry` `complete` at `:307`). A user returning later, typing nothing, and tapping `Pair` against a *new* desktop window spends one of that window's three confirm MAC strikes (`PROTOCOL.md:851`) on digits that were never the current code. Recoverable, user-visible, no secret disclosure — a UX wart, not the persisted-secret exposure alleged.\n\n**Checked clean:** saved-state lifecycle and unregistration path, manifest persistence/backup posture, `PROTOCOL.md` §4.2/§4.9.2/§4.9.3/§4.9.4/§4.9.5 threat model, retry/auto-submit call graph, countdown restore behaviour, error mapping for `RESTART_REQUIRED`.\n\n**Tooling note:** the Bash tool was non-functional this session (every invocation, including `pwd`, returned `/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching `''`), so `git log`/`git blame` history for this hunk was not consulted; findings rest on working-tree contents read directly.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>62668</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>29</tool_uses><duration_ms>251587</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:23:42.810Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a79f34ba96e503957</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a79f34ba96e503957.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify burn memo isolation\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**REFUTED**\n\nCorrected location: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:2219` (LAN claim, no memo clear) vs `:2367-2369` (remote claim + `remoteBurnMemo = null`). Consumer: `...\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\http.ts:831-837`.\n\nSummary: behavior described is real — a LAN/QR open leaves `remoteBurnMemo` set, so `/v1/pair/*` answers 403 `pair_code_burned` for the memo remainder — but it is the memo's deliberate contract, remote-scoped, with no observable consequence beyond the answer the memo exists to give.\n\nEvidence:\n- Route reachable, so \"unreachable\" is not the refutation. `routeByHost` (http.ts:658-686) keys only on `Host`/method/path + `publicPairHost`; window state irrelevant. Gate 3's documented bare-404 (http.ts:785-788) proves the path is served with no remote window.\n- Memo intent is remote-flow-only, not window-slot-only. pairing.ts:1945-1959: memo distinguishes \"you burned it\" from \"never existed\"; it holds only `forgetAtMs`. The clear-on-open comment at 2367-2368 scopes clearing to a **remote** successor: \"the phone asking about THIS window must not be told about the last one.\" A LAN window is not reachable on `/v1/pair/*` (LAN flow is its own 47611 listener, `route()` at pairing.ts:2189-2190), so no phone can be \"asking about THIS window\" through the remote paths. `FEATURES.md:86` states the contract flatly: \"A burned window is remembered for 60 s so the phone can be told 'you burned it' instead of the bare 404 a never-opened window produces\" — no LAN exception, and the remote window genuinely was burned.\n- Ordering is safe. http.ts:831-837 checks `currentRemotePairing()` FIRST and only consults `burned()` when it is null → memo can never mask a live remote window. `currentRemotePairing` (pairing.ts:2462-2468) returns null for a LAN slot, so the fallthrough is the same \"no live remote window\" state as an empty slot.\n- No incremental leak. The 403-vs-404 distinguishability exists for the full 60 s with **no** window open; opening a LAN window adds no information about the LAN window, changes no crypto/rate state, and the memo self-forgets on read (pairing.ts:1971-1976). No LAN-side consumer of `remoteWindowWasBurned` exists (only http.ts:833 repo-wide).\n- Tests encode the same scope: `...\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\remote-pairing.test.ts:1817-1826` asserts 403 after burn with the real memo; :177-179 pins the memo off elsewhere. No test asserts a bare 404 while a LAN window is open, and no doc requires one.\n- Worst realistic sequence: burn a code window → user switches dialog to QR (`PairDeviceDialog.tsx:189-201` closes then opens) → phone retries `/v1/pair/begin` → 403 \"burned, get a new code.\" Truthful (that code is dead, no live remote window), self-clearing ≤60 s, no data or authz effect. Residual is a cosmetic wording nit, below MINOR — not a defect worth reporting.\n\nFrozen missing-candidate behavior (`pair_unknown_candidate`) untouched by this question; unchanged.\n\nChecked clean: memo lifecycle (set/read/expire/clear), burn→close teardown and key zeroing (pairing.ts:2337-2356, 2498-2509), 403-before/after-endpoint ordering, host/method route isolation, LAN-vs-remote path separation, one-window slot reserve/claim/release, tests and FEATURES.md contract text. Bash tool is non-functional in this environment (`unexpected EOF` on every invocation), so no `git`/test execution — findings are from source, tests, and manifest text only.\n\n**SHIP**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>77248</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>27</tool_uses><duration_ms>256466</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
