 No recomputation exists → zero per-handshake work to remove. Both file and hot computation fabricated.\n\nEvidence: directory enumeration of companion dir; Read `pairing.ts:286-405` and `489-567`; scoped searches — `\\bsrp\\b|kex` over `host-service/src` returned only `limits.ts:93` doc text plus pairing kex identifiers; `modPow|PAD\\(|H\\(PAD|srp` over `packages` → no matches.\n\nCoverage: companion pairing/kex path, key schedule, kex caps/rate limiting, BigInt usage in `companion/`.\n\nTooling caveat: Bash nonfunctional this session (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching `''` on every command incl. `echo ok`); repo-wide Glob times out at 20s. Verification done via Read + path-scoped Grep; `git log` unavailable, so a historically deleted `srp.ts` could not be ruled out — no reference survives in any tracked source, doc, or client file.\n\n**FIX-FIRST** on the candidate list itself: drop this entry (not a code change).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>31502</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>0</tool_uses><duration_ms>15392</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:26:43.803Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a79f34ba96e503957</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_GETa7dLwGgW0ejG1Vv05fFFm</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a79f34ba96e503957.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify burn memo isolation\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**REFUTED**\n\nLocation (corrected): `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:2219` (LAN claim, no memo clear); contrast `:2367-2369` (remote claim → `remoteBurnMemo = null`). Consumer: `...\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\http.ts:831-837`.\n\nSummary: described behavior is real (LAN open leaves `remoteBurnMemo` set → `/v1/pair/*` returns 403 `pair_code_burned` for the memo remainder instead of bare 404), but it is the memo's deliberate remote-scoped contract with no observable consequence. Not a defect.\n\nEvidence:\n- Route reachable — `routeByHost` (http.ts:658-686) keys on `Host`/method/path + `publicPairHost` only; window state irrelevant. So \"unreachable\" is not the basis.\n- Memo is remote-flow state, not slot state. pairing.ts:1945-1959 (memo = \"burned\" vs \"never existed\", holds only `forgetAtMs`); clear-comment at 2367-2368 scopes clearing to a **remote** successor (\"the phone asking about THIS window\"). A LAN window is unreachable on `/v1/pair/*` (LAN flow = own 47611 listener, pairing.ts:2189-2190), so no phone asks about it there. `FEATURES.md:86`: \"A burned window is remembered for 60 s so the phone can be told 'you burned it' instead of the bare 404 a never-opened window produces\" — no LAN carve-out; the remote window genuinely was burned.\n- Ordering safe — http.ts:831-837 checks `currentRemotePairing()` first, `burned()` only when null → memo can never mask a live remote window. `currentRemotePairing` (pairing.ts:2462-2468) returns null for a LAN slot = same state as empty slot.\n- No incremental leak/contamination — 403-vs-404 distinguishability exists for the full 60 s with no window open; a LAN window adds no info about itself, no crypto/rate/state change, memo self-forgets on read (pairing.ts:1971-1976). Only consumer repo-wide is http.ts:833; no LAN-side consumer.\n- Tests match the scope — `remote-pairing.test.ts:1817-1826` (403 after burn, real memo), `:177-179` (memo pinned off elsewhere). No test or doc requires bare 404 during a LAN window.\n- Worst sequence: burn code window → switch dialog to QR (`PairDeviceDialog.tsx:189-201` close-then-open) → phone retries `/v1/pair/begin` → 403 \"burned, get a new code.\" Truthful (code dead, no live remote window), clears ≤60 s, no authz/data effect. Cosmetic wording nit, below MINOR.\n\nFrozen missing-candidate behavior (`pair_unknown_candidate`) unchanged/untouched.\n\nCoverage: memo lifecycle, burn→close teardown + key zeroing (pairing.ts:2337-2356, 2498-2509), 403/endpoint ordering, host-method route isolation, LAN-vs-remote path separation, one-window reserve/claim/release, tests, FEATURES.md contract. Bash tool non-functional here (`unexpected EOF` every invocation) → no git/test execution; source+tests+manifest only.\n\n**SHIP**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>66938</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>0</tool_uses><duration_ms>15174</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:26:44.418Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a4117a6364d938610</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_yXo4ETtEAMMktdkRg9OGRUci</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a4117a6364d938610.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Check pairing efficiency\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Sweep already complete — no re-inspection. Final candidates below. (No `SendMessage` tool in this session, so answering inline rather than to `from=code-review`.)\n\n**Tooling caveat:** Bash was dead all session (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching `''` — broken shell-init preamble, not my commands). No `git diff HEAD`, no untracked enumeration. Candidates derived by reading the remote-pairing surface directly via Grep/Read. Under-coverage risk on untracked files I could not list.\n\n---\n\n**1. [MAJOR]**\n- `file`: `C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/superset-companion/core/src/main/java/family/khaira/supersetcompanion/core/pairing/PairingClient.kt`\n- `line`: 223 (`handshake: RemotePairingHandshake = RemotePairingHandshake()`), with `RemotePairingHandshake.kt:80` `baseClient: OkHttpClient = OkHttpClient()`\n- `summary`: Default arg re-evaluated per call → fresh OkHttpClient per remote pairing attempt instead of `CompanionCore.httpClient`.\n- `failure_scenario`: `BridgeCompanionRepository.pairFromCode` (line 962) passes no handshake. User mistypes digits → `pair_code_wrong` → user-triggered retained-code retry. Each attempt allocates a new Dispatcher ExecutorService (threads idle 60 s), ConnectionPool (5 idle TLS conns, 5 min keep-alive), TaskRunner thread; nothing calls `executorService.shutdown()` / `connectionPool.evictAll()`. 3 retries + a mode switch → 4 leaked pools holding live TLS sockets to the pairing host. Every sibling (`PairingClient`, `SealedTransport` CompanionCore.kt:96, `EventStreamClient` :98) gets the shared client — this is the sole fork.\n- cheaper safe alternative: thread the shared `httpClient` into the handshake (field on PairingClient, or construct the handshake once in CompanionCore). Hoist `SecureRandom()` off the per-call path.\n\n**2. [MAJOR]**\n- `file`: `C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/.claude/worktrees/agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f/packages/host-service/src/companion/srp.ts`\n- `line`: 321 (`srpSecretModPow`), called at 508 and 525; charged at `pairing.ts:1063`\n- `summary`: 64-window ladder = 320 BigInt mulmods of 3072-bit operands, run synchronously on the host-service event loop, twice per kex, from unauthenticated public input.\n- `failure_scenario`: Module header's own measurement ~2.8-3.3 ms/exponentiation, ~10 ms/kex on win-arm64. `MAX_KEX_COMPUTATIONS_PER_WINDOW = 64` (pairing.ts:256) with `REMOTE_PAIR_MAX_REQUESTS_PER_WINDOW = 1024` (config.ts:234) lets a flood deliver all 64 back-to-back → ~640 ms contiguous event-loop block inside the 120 s window. Same loop serves `/v1/events` WS, `/v1/answer`, push → phone answering a blocked question stalls, and the real phone's kex queues behind the flood.\n- cheaper safe alternative: worker_threads pool for the two exponentiations, or at minimum `setImmediate` yields between B and S and between queued kex requests. Bounds stay as-is.\n\n**3. [MINOR]**\n- `file`: `.../packages/host-service/src/companion/srp.ts`\n- `line`: 505 (`const k = srpComputeK(group)`)\n- `summary`: `k = H(PAD(N) || PAD(g))` is a per-group constant, recomputed on every handshake.\n- `failure_scenario`: Each kex pays two `srpPad` round-trips (`BigInt.toString(16)` → `padStart` → `Buffer.from(hex,\"hex\")`, srp.ts:205-219) on the 3072-bit modulus plus a SHA-256 and a hex→BigInt parse. Under flood: 64× per window, on the already-blocked loop.\n- cheaper safe alternative: compute `k` (and `PAD(N)`/`PAD(g)`) in `defineGroup`, expose as readonly fields.\n\n**4. [MINOR]**\n- `file`: `.../packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts`\n- `line`: 1097 (also 1528, and 1808/1813 in `assertPidMatches`)\n- `summary`: `pid` kept only as base64url text and re-decoded 2-3× per request, though raw `pidBytes` existed at open (line 2275) and was discarded.\n- `failure_scenario`: Up to 1024 admitted requests/window each pay 2-3 base64 decodes + allocations for 16 bytes already in hand; `assertPidMatches` re-decodes the *server's own* pid on every hostile packet.\n- cheaper safe alternative: store `pidBytes` on `PairingSessionCommon`; decode only the caller-supplied value.\n\n**5. [MINOR]**\n- `file`: `.../packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts`\n- `line`: 1356 (`evictOldestKexSessionsIfFull`)\n- `summary`: Full O(n) scan on `createdAtMs` to find the oldest candidate, but the Map is append-only so insertion order already yields it — `chargePerSource` 15 lines earlier (:1341) uses the cheap `keys().next()` idiom.\n- `failure_scenario`: Every introduction during a `begin` flood pays an O(17) scan plus `POSITIVE_INFINITY` bookkeeping instead of one `keys().next()`. Small absolute cost, but redundant work on the public flood path and inconsistent with the file's own eviction pattern.\n- cheaper safe alternative: `state.kexSessions.keys().next()` as victim; drop the scan.\n\n**6. [MINOR]**\n- `file`: `.../apps/desktop/src/renderer/components/PairDeviceDialog/PairDeviceDialog.tsx`\n- `line`: 253 (`setInterval(tick, 250)`)\n- `summary`: 250 ms tick stores raw `remainingMs`, but the only consumer renders `Math.ceil(remainingMs/1000)` (line 419) → 3 of 4 renders are identical output.\n- `failure_scenario`: ~480 `PairingFlow` renders per 120 s window instead of ~120. `QrCode` memoizes on `value` so the encode is skipped, but React still reconciles the ~2 800-module `&lt;path&gt;` and the whole dialog tree 4×/s, on top of the 1 s `pairingState` poll (line 325).\n- cheaper safe alternative: store integer `secondsLeft` so `setState` no-ops when unchanged, or tick once per second aligned to the next boundary.\n\n---\n\nChecked clean: window-slot reservation lifecycle (`reserveWindowSlot`/`claimOrTearDown`/`releaseWindowSlot` release on every failure path, timer cleared in `close()`, bridge teardown closes via `index.ts:1142`); all caps bounded (`MAX_KEX_SESSIONS` 16 with wipe-on-evict, `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE`/`MAX_KEX_SOURCES` and `remoteRequestCounts` table-bounded at 32, `remoteRequests`/`kexComputations` monotone); candidate key zeroization on every drop path; `remoteBurnMemo` single lazily-expired slot; no-window 404 allocates nothing; `QrCode` encode correctly `useMemo`'d; Android `produceState` countdown stops emitting once clamped and cancels on leave; frozen `pair_unknown_candidate` + HTTP 400 behaviour untouched, no alias/status-only/auto-retry proposed.\n\n**FIX-FIRST**</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>169301</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>0</tool_uses><duration_ms>41257</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:26:44.461Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>addb9326abc49c7ec</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_zC6micxNfH9xz54jDGrDr9hi</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\addb9326abc49c7ec.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify HTTP client leak\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## CONFIRMED\n\n**Location:** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\PairingClient.kt:223` (line exact; method is `pairWithCode`, not `pairRemote`)\n\n**Summary:** Default arg `handshake: RemotePairingHandshake = RemotePairingHandshake()` re-evaluates per invocation and falls through to `RemotePairingHandshake`'s own `baseClient: OkHttpClient = OkHttpClient()` — remote pairing is the only path in the repo that does not derive from `CompanionCore`'s single process-lifetime `httpClient`.\n\n**Retry/resource scenario:** Mistyped 8-digit code. `BridgeCompanionRepository.pairFromCode` (line 962) calls `core.pairingClient().pairWithCode(...)` with no `handshake` → attempt 1 builds `OkHttpClient()` #1 with its own `ConnectionPool`, runs begin/kex/confirm, fails `CODE_WRONG`. Two more retries inside the desktop's 120 s window (burns at 3 strikes) → clients #2, #3, each with a fresh pool. No retry reuses the prior connection to `REMOTE_PAIRING_ORIGIN`: full TCP+TLS handshake per attempt on a mobile link, inside a flow already racing 120 s; each orphaned pool retains its idle socket until 5-min keepAlive eviction. Any future base-client config (proxy, CT/logging interceptor, socket factory) would silently skip remote pairing.\n\n**Impact correction — candidate overstates two parts:**\n- Not an unbounded leak. No `Cache` configured; no `evictAll`/`shutdown` anywhere (grep: only `PairingScreen.kt:444`, unrelated camera `Executor`). Retention bounded by idle eviction.\n- Not \"separate dispatchers\" in thread-cost terms. `RemotePairingHandshake.post` (line 381) uses synchronous `execute()` → `Dispatcher.executorService` never touched; okhttp 5.4.0 (`gradle/libs.versions.toml:14`) backs pool cleanup with shared `TaskRunner`. Zero per-client threads.\n\nNet: real convention break + real per-retry handshake/connection waste. **MINOR**, not a leak.\n\n**Evidence**\n- `PairingClient.kt:223` — per-call default via Kotlin `$default` bridge; repo-wide `pairWithCode` hits = declaration + one caller, neither supplying it.\n- `RemotePairingHandshake.kt:80` — `baseClient: OkHttpClient = OkHttpClient()`; line 86 `baseClient.newBuilder()` → class is built to inherit a shared pool, handed a fresh one.\n- `CompanionCore.kt:31,49,96,98` — one `httpClient` threaded into `PairingClient`/`SealedTransport`/`EventStreamClient`; `PairingClient.kt:52-63`, `SealedTransport.kt:89,110-111` both derive via `baseClient.newBuilder()` — the convention line 223 breaks.\n- `CompanionContainer.kt:90-106` — core built once in `by lazy` repository → shared client lives for process.\n- `BridgeCompanionRepository.kt:948-1002` — user-invokable retryable entry; failures set `PairingState.Failed` and return, retry left to user.\n- No reuse/cleanup invariant excuses it: `PairingClient` does not retain `baseClient` (only derived `client`), so no existing path lets the handshake inherit the shared pool.\n\n**Fix (not applied):** retain `baseClient` in `PairingClient` and pass `RemotePairingHandshake(baseClient = baseClient)`, or drop the default and have `CompanionCore` build the handshake with `httpClient`.\n\nChecked clean: Kotlin default-arg evaluation semantics, OkHttp 5.4.0 ownership/thread behavior, caller lifetime, close/share paths repo-wide.\n\n**Verdict: FIX-FIRST** (MINOR — mechanism confirmed, impact bounded)</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>56235</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>0</tool_uses><duration_ms>18712</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
