{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:26:48.355Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a2a39361eb60fba36</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_Cg1rmwAUSpIEj9wXPIWXlKKS</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a2a39361eb60fba36.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify PID decode churn\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Verification already complete before the interrupt. Re-emitting final verdict unchanged; no restart, no broadening. (No `SendMessage` tool in this session — returning to parent via output.)\n\n**PLAUSIBLE**\n\n**Corrected location.** Store site: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:959-965` — `pidBytes = randomBytes(WIRE_ID_BYTES)` at :959, only `pid: base64UrlEncode(pidBytes)` retained at :965, raw bytes dropped. Cited :1097 is the closing brace of `openPairingWindow`, not the store site.\n\nRedundant decodes of trusted state:\n- `pairing.ts:857` — `constantTimeEquals(given, base64UrlDecode(state.pid))` in `assertPidMatches`; reached from `handleKex` (:432) and `handleConfirm` (:626).\n- `pairing.ts:646` — `const pidBytes = base64UrlDecode(state.pid);` → `derivePairingKeys` (:653), `buildPairingTranscript` (:658).\n\n**Summary.** Mechanism real. `state.pid` typed `PairingId = Base64Url` (`types.ts:33`, `types.ts:367`) → wire string only; every raw-byte consumer re-decodes. Redundant decodes/request: kex = 1, confirm = 2. Including the necessary attacker-supplied decode: 2-3 total per request — candidate's per-request arithmetic holds.\n\n**Concrete cost — candidate's magnitudes wrong.**\n- \"1024/window\" unsupported. `PAIRING_ATTEMPTS_PER_WINDOW = 5` (`config.ts:148`), burn at `PAIRING_MAX_BAD_MACS = 3` (`config.ts:63`), kex introductions capped `MAX_KEX_SOURCES 32 * MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE 4 = 128`.\n- Unbounded surface exists, but not the claimed one: wrong-pid requests only increment/log (`noteUnattributedAttempt` :870, deliberately unlimited), and a repeat kex with identical material returns at :468 before `chargeKexIntroduction` → both replayable at socket rate for the 120 s window.\n- Per decode: 22-char input through `crypto.ts:750-762` = regex alphabet test + length check + `Buffer.from` + full re-encode + string compare ≈ 3 small allocations, sub-microsecond.\n- Same-path dominators: `JSON.parse` + Node HTTP socket/headers every request; on confirm also X25519 + HKDF (`derivePairingKeys`), 2x HMAC-SHA256 (:665, :674), sealing, plus awaited `deps.onPaired` (:681) and `deps.loadAccessToken` (:693) I/O. Redundant decode work &lt;10 ms even at 10k flood requests/window → no observable effect at any reachable rate.\n\n**Ownership/security downsides — none blocking, one caveat.** `state.pid` is not secret (emitted in `kexResponse` :492, QR URI :986, log :1094) → cached `pidBytes` needs no zeroization; `close()` (:1036-1037, zeroes only `pairingCode`/`dPriv`) stays correct. Caveat: adds a mutable `Uint8Array` to session state against the module's \"zeroed on close\" byte-field convention. String form still required at :492, :986, :1001/:1008, :1094 → pid must stay a string; only an additive `pidBytes` companion is viable, never a replacement.\n\n**Maintenance angle is the only real value.** Precedent exists: `KexSession` stores both `deviceId` (string) and `deviceIdBytes` (raw), consumed at :654/:659. A `pidBytes` companion matches that convention and deletes 2 decode sites.\n\n**Evidence.** Read `pairing.ts:182-199, 410-497, 610-709, 779-875, 930-1097`; `crypto.ts:750-762`; `types.ts:33-39, 286-378`; `config.ts:62-63, 148`; `limits.ts:38-43`. Bash unusable this session (profile init: `unexpected EOF while looking for matching '`) → static read-only verification only, no runtime measurement.\n\nVote: accept as MINOR consistency cleanup; reject the efficiency framing and the 1024/window figure.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>41610</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>0</tool_uses><duration_ms>21148</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:26:49.825Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a86072aa40e0253a9</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_WlTwRmS2riyiNNHJr38qI28N</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a86072aa40e0253a9.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify saved pairing code\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## REFUTED\n\n**File:** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt:113` — line as cited is correct: `var digits by rememberSaveable { mutableStateOf(\"\") }`\n\n**Summary.** Saved-state copy of the 8 digits exists, but it is not a persisted secret and buys an attacker nothing. Three invariants kill the claim: no disk serialization, no reader less privileged than the heap copy the protocol already accepts, and zero residual value once the desktop window closes.\n\n**Evidence**\n\n1. **No disk copy — platform invariant.** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\AndroidManifest.xml:31-41`: `MainActivity` declares no `android:persistableMode`. Plain `onSaveInstanceState(Bundle)` state lives only in system_server RAM; only `persistableMode` + `PersistableBundle` reaches `recent_tasks/*_task.xml`. Also `allowBackup=\"false\"`, `fullBackupContent=\"false\"` (manifest:22-23) → no backup/extraction path.\n2. **Already-accepted exposure class, same adversary.** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\PROTOCOL.md:1102-1105` (§4.9.4, \"What is *not* claimed\"): \"The typed code is not zeroizable the way `pc` is. It arrives from a text field as a `String` and lives on the heap until the collector gets to it… Its 120-second, single-use lifetime is what bounds that, not memory hygiene.\" Reading the system_server Bundle needs root/system — ≥ the privilege to read that accepted heap copy. No new reader.\n3. **Retained value is dead by normative desktop behaviour.** `PROTOCOL.md:847-853`: 120 000 ms lifetime; exactly one successful use then close; 3 bad confirm MACs burn it; \"On close/burn the code, the SRP verifier, `b`, `S` and `PRK` are zeroed\". Fresh CSPRNG code per window (`PROTOCOL.md:901`). Window-linked erasure exists on the side holding the verifier — a restored stale code authenticates to nothing.\n4. **Copy stops being saved when the screen leaves.** `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\shell\\CompanionShell.kt:177-190` renders `PairingScreen` from a bare `when (route)` — no `SaveableStateHolder`/`SaveableStateProvider`, so the `rememberSaveable` entry is unregistered on leaving composition (`onCancel`/`onDone` → `route = AGENTS`). No post-pairing retention.\n5. **Retry stays user-triggered; wire literal untouched.** Submits exist only in `Button onClick` (`PairingScreen.kt:187-196`, `:238-242`); `resubmitsSameCode` (`:341-343`) gates resubmit to `RESTART_REQUIRED` + 8 digits, matching `PROTOCOL.md:1027-1036` (\"recovery is user-initiated only\", \"`confirm` is NEVER re-sent automatically\").\n6. **Restored state cannot mislead the countdown.** `submittedAtMs` (`:119`) renders only under `PairingState.Exchanging` (`:217-220`); process death drops ViewModel state, so a restored value is never displayed.\n\n**Failure scenario as alleged, traced to its end:** user types the code on the PAIRING route → app backgrounded → `onSaveInstanceState` copies the 8 digits into system_server RAM → process killed → hours later the task is resumed and `digits` restores pre-filled. Damage at that point: an attacker needs root/system to read the Bundle, and what they read is a burned/expired code against a window whose verifier and code were zeroed. Net exposure over the accepted heap retention: none.\n\n**Residual, not the claimed defect (MINOR, informational).** After process-death restore the field is pre-filled and `Pair` is enabled (`CodeEntry` `complete`, `:307`). A user returning later and tapping `Pair` without re-reading the desktop spends one of the new window's three confirm MAC strikes (`PROTOCOL.md:851`) on digits that are not the current code. Recoverable, visible, no disclosure — UX wart, not a persisted-secret exposure.\n\n**Checked clean:** saved-state lifecycle/unregistration, manifest persistence + backup posture, PROTOCOL.md §4.2 / §4.9.2 / §4.9.3 / §4.9.4 / §4.9.5 threat model, retry + auto-submit call graph, countdown restore path, `RESTART_REQUIRED` error mapping.\n\n**Tooling note:** Bash was non-functional this session (every invocation, including `pwd`, returned `/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching `''`), so `git log`/`git blame` were not consulted; verdict rests on working-tree contents read directly. No SendMessage tool is available in this session, so this text is the reply to `code-review`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>65584</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>0</tool_uses><duration_ms>20411</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:26:50.585Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a596254e3f4f2c589</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a596254e3f4f2c589.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Find pairing code reuse\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Sweep already complete. Final candidates, unchanged.\n\n**Method caveat:** Bash tool non-functional this session — every invocation dies in shell init (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching ''`), including `true`. No `git diff HEAD`, no untracked-file listing. Findings read from current file contents on the `(REMOTE-CODE-PAIRING)` / `(PAIR-EVICTION-HONEST)` surface and verified against the helper each duplicates; I cannot certify each hunk is uncommitted vs already committed. Finder only, not verification.\n\n---\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:334\nsummary: `openConfirmEnvelope` is a second copy of `PairingClient.openConfirmEnvelope` (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\PairingClient.kt:256) with the raised failure swapped BAD_MAC -&gt; CODE_WRONG.\nfailure_scenario: Lines 340-345 throw `PairingException(PairingFailure.CODE_WRONG, ...)` for \"not a response envelope\" and \"addressed to another device\". Those are PairingException, not EnvelopeException, so they bypass the `catch (e: EnvelopeException) -&gt; CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED` wrapper at :177 and land in `remotePairingError` (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\data\\RemotePairingErrorMapping.kt:22) -&gt; PairingError.CODE_WRONG. A desktop or on-path edge sealing a step-4 envelope of the wrong kind, or addressed to another deviceId, tells the user \"the desktop did not accept that code\" and offers a resubmit of the same digits — a wrong-peer condition presented as a typo. Two copies also mean the QR twin and remote copy label identical envelope defects differently forever. Fix: hoist PairingClient's implementation into one helper parameterised by confirm path + failure code.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:439\nsummary: `FAILURE_BY_WIRE` / `FAILURE_BY_STATUS` / `messageFor` (:439, :470, :416) hand-build a second wire-string-&gt;failure resolver that `PairingFailure`'s WireEnum machinery (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\protocol\\WireEnum.kt) already provides and that `PairingClient.pairingFailureOf` (PairingClient.kt:343) already implements.\nfailure_scenario: Already drifted on one vocabulary. FAILURE_BY_WIRE omits BAD_MAC.wire, CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED, UNREACHABLE, HOST_NOT_PRIVATE, QR_INVALID. PairingClient has no 429 branch at all (429 -&gt; UNREACHABLE) while the new table maps 429 -&gt; RATE_LIMITED. PairingClient collapses `pair_wrong_peer` -&gt; WINDOW_CLOSED; the new table preserves WRONG_PEER. Unmatched codes default BAD_MAC here, UNREACHABLE there. `pair_unknown_candidate` exists only in the new table. Adding one code to PairingFailure needs two hand edits in two files or one flow swallows it into its own default. Same duplication in the payload type: shared `PairErrorBody` (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\protocol\\Pairing.kt:146) vs private `PairingErrorBody` (PairingClient.kt:409), identical `{code}` serializer. Fix: one `PairingFailure.fromWire()` over WireEnum entries + shared PairErrorBody; delete PairingErrorBody.\n```\n\n```\n[MAJOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\crypto\\KeyDerivation.kt:138\nsummary: `deriveRemotePairingKeys` (:138) and `remotePairingTranscript` (:166) are line-for-line copies of `derivePairingKeys` (:67) and `pairingTranscript` (:98) with five renamed prefix constants — the exact duplication the desktop removed via `PairingProfile` + shared `buildHkdfSalt` / `expandPairingKeys` / `concatFixed`.\nfailure_scenario: Cross-runtime differential vectors pin these bytes to the desktop profile table (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:556-570, :788-826). With two independent Kotlin bodies, any schedule change — fourth Expand, label fix, prefix rename — needs two Kotlin edits plus one TS profile row; applying it to the LAN copy only leaves the remote flow on a different PRK, surfacing as `pair_code_wrong` on every remote pairing with no diagnostic, indistinguishable from a mistyped code. Encoding is already inconsistent inside the copies: salt prefixes go through Charsets.UTF_8 (:81, :149) while transcript prefixes use US_ASCII (:112, :180), and the desktop encodes both as \"ascii\" — the first non-ASCII byte breaks half the flows silently. Fix: parameterise the existing derivePairingKeys/pairingTranscript with a profile object mirroring desktop's PairingProfile.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:86\nsummary: Hardened OkHttp builder at :86-100 plus `connectionSpecsFor` (:486) re-derive the TLS/redirect/retry policy that `SealedTransport.secureBase()` (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\transport\\SealedTransport.kt:110) already owns.\nfailure_scenario: Both copies currently pin followRedirects(false), followSslRedirects(false), RESTRICTED_TLS + MODERN_TLS, retryOnConnectionFailure(false) — no drift today. But this is the one unauthenticated hop, and its own comment (:501) states TLS to the pinned hostname is what authenticates the desktop. A tightening applied to secureBase() (cipher removal, pin, HTTP/1.1-only) leaves exactly that hop on the old policy, and nothing in the build fails. Fix: expose secureBase() and layer only the pairing timeouts + localhost-cleartext exception on top.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1135\nsummary: `requireRemoteCandidate` validates the incoming deviceId with a bare `typeof === \"string\"` then uses the raw client string as a Map key, instead of `isCanonicalWireId` / `decodeWireId` (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\crypto.ts:782, :802).\nfailure_scenario: crypto.ts:764-793 documents isCanonicalWireId as THE canonical §0.1 wire-id test, written because the 22-char id shape had been checked four ways with three strictnesses — \"ids that pass an outer gate and then fail an inner one is the failure mode this protocol can least afford\". This is a fifth way. `begin` validates canonically (parseKexMetadata -&gt; decodeExact, :1190) and refuses a bad id as `400 unknown`; `kex`/`confirm` at :1146 do not, so a non-canonical 22-char variant of a real deviceId (base64url's last char carries 4 significant bits, so four strings decode to identical bytes) misses the map and gets `400 pair_unknown_candidate` — the frozen contract's \"repeat begin\" advice — plus an unattributedAttempts warn. One id string, two verdicts across three steps of one exchange; the phone is told to restart a window that was never wrong.\n```\n\n```\n[MINOR] C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt:388\nsummary: `GroupedCodeTransformation` re-derives the dash position (`PAIRING_CODE_DIGITS / 2`, :390) and its OffsetMapping arithmetic (:392-394) instead of taking it from `PairingCode.grouped`, which already owns where the dash goes (C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\PairingCode.kt:63).\nfailure_scenario: The transformation calls PairingCode.grouped for the visible string but computes the cursor mapping from its own local `half`. `grouped` additionally suppresses the dash when `digits.length &lt;= half` (:65), which the mapping does not model; the two agree only because half is 4 and originalToTransformed shifts only above half. Change PAIRING_CODE_DIGITS to any odd value, or change grouped's dash rule, and the mapping is off by one against the rendered text — cursor jumps, wrong selection, and Compose can throw on a transformed offset past the end of the transformed string. A third independent grouping exists on the desktop (`groupCode`, Math.ceil(len/2), C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\apps\\desktop\\src\\renderer\\components\\PairDeviceDialog\\PairDeviceDialog.tsx:151) and would then disagree with what the phone shows. Fix: derive the mapping offset from grouped's own output.\n```\n\n---\n\nChecked, no duplication worth reporting: `srp.ts` (parameterised `digest` required by the SHA-1 test group; `srpBytesToBigInt`/`srpPad` have no pre-existing equivalent); `chargePerSource` / `chargeKexIntroduction` / `chargeRemoteRequest` (correctly one shared bounded counter); `handleRemotePairRequest`'s reuse of `readBoundedBody`, `pairingJsonResponse` as single no-store constructor, `routeByHost`/`classifyRequestHost` host isolation; `wipeAllPairingKeys`/`zeroKexSession`/`clearKexSessions` as single wipe path; `pairing-modes.ts` as single qr/code &lt;-&gt; lan/remote table; `PairingCode.digitsOrNull`/`takeDigits`/`passwordBytes` as single Unicode-digit normalisation point, correctly reused by both PairingScreen and RemotePairingHandshake.run; the `MIN_SEALED_BODY_BYTES` guard I first suspected dropped (enforced inside `EnvelopeHeader.parse`, SealedEnvelope.kt:69 — safe); the frozen `HTTP 400 + pair_unknown_candidate` contract, no reason to change it.\n\n**FIX-FIRST** — findings 1-3 are duplications with live behavioural divergence, not style.\n\nNo SendMessage tool in this session's toolset; reply to `code-review` is this message.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>182735</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>0</tool_uses><duration_ms>46169</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:26:58.280Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>acc432574a787ddc1</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\acc432574a787ddc1.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review pairing implementation altitude\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Resuming — no re-sweep. Final finder candidates, unchanged.\n\n**Tooling limit (coverage caveat):** Bash dead all session — every invocation, including `echo hi`, fails in shell init (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: line 104: unexpected EOF while looking for matching '''`). No `git diff HEAD`, no untracked-file enumeration, no test/lint run. Scope reconstructed by reading the remote-pairing surface in both repos. Untracked-only files may be unreviewed.\n\n```\nCANDIDATES (6, recall-biased, finder only)\n```\n\n**1. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1063**\nsummary: `chargeKexComputation(state)` charged BEFORE `A` is range-validated — refusals costing zero modexp burn the monotone 64-derivation ceiling, inverting this module's own (PAIR-ATTEMPT-ORDER) validate-then-charge rule.\nfailure_scenario: Attacker rotates ≥16 source IPs (per-source intro cap 4; source table evicts → refunds), sends 64 `begin`+`kex` pairs with `A = PAD(1)`. Correct 384-byte width, so `decodeClientPublic` passes and the charge lands; `srpAssertClientPublic` then throws `SrpError` → `400 pair_bad_key_agreement` with no exponentiation performed. 128 of the window's 1024 admitted requests spent. Real phone's single legitimate `kex` hits `kexComputations &gt;= 64` → `429 pair_rate_limited` → \"Your desktop is turning away attempts\". A flood rate-limits a legitimate device out of a window the user is standing in front of — precisely what §4.7/(PAIR-JUNK-SURVIVAL) promises is impossible. Deeper mechanism: the CPU-cost ceiling belongs after the free validation gate (validate → charge → derive), same shape as `chargeConfirmAttempt` after `decodeExact`.\n\n**2. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\app-phone\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\phone\\ui\\pairing\\PairingScreen.kt:113**\nsummary: Typed 8-digit code held in `rememberSaveable` → Compose SavedStateRegistry → Activity saved-instance-state Bundle: a copy of the secret outside every zeroization/one-place discipline in the batch.\nfailure_scenario: User types `1234-5678`; `digits` is written to the saved-state Bundle held by system_server. Rotate / background / process-death-and-restore-from-Recents: the code is restored, so it outlives the 120 s window, process death and config change, and cannot be overwritten (immutable `String` in framework-owned storage). Meanwhile `PairingCode.passwordBytes` and `Srp.startClient` zeroize the password bytes, and the desktop dialog documents \"no path to a store, to disk, or to a later render\". On `Paired`, and on failures outside the explicit clear paths, the entry is never cleared. Deeper mechanism: retained-code retry (frozen requirement) should hold digits in one non-persisted, explicitly-cleared secret holder — plain `remember`/ViewModel field cleared on terminal state — never framework saved state.\n\n**3. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:341**\nsummary: Post-confirm sealed-reply defects (`kind != RESPONSE`, header `deviceId` mismatch) labelled `CODE_WRONG`, but they are reachable only AFTER the desktop persisted the device record and consumed the window.\nfailure_scenario: Phone POSTs `/v1/pair/confirm`; desktop verifies `macPhone`, sets `consumed = true`, writes device record + audit line, seals, returns. Reply arrives mis-addressed or with wrong envelope kind (desktop bug, edge rewrite) → `PairingFailure.CODE_WRONG` → \"That code wasn't accepted. Check the code … type it again\", and `resubmitsSameCode` is false so the field clears and a retype is invited. Window is burned: retype gets `404 → WINDOW_CLOSED`. Desktop keeps an orphan record holding a `K_dev` the phone never stored, and the user is never told to remove it — the exact state the adjacent `CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED` text exists to describe, bypassed. Deeper mechanism: one lifecycle predicate — \"the confirm request left this phone\" — should classify every later failure as may-have-paired, in the handshake, not per-throw-site labels.\n\n**4. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\core\\src\\main\\java\\family\\khaira\\supersetcompanion\\core\\pairing\\RemotePairingHandshake.kt:352**\nsummary: Step-4 AAD binds `CompanionConstants.PROTOCOL_CURRENT` (the moving negotiation ceiling, also `CLIENT_PROTOCOL_MAX`) where the host binds a deliberately frozen `PROTOCOL_VERSION_AT_PAIRING = 1`; drift duplicated at `PairingClient.kt:273`.\nfailure_scenario: A future bump sets `PROTOCOL_CURRENT = 2` — its documented purpose. Host still binds `1`. AADs differ by one byte, every GCM tag fails, `SealedEnvelope.open` throws → `CONFIRM_UNVERIFIED`/`CODE_WRONG`: \"your code was wrong\" for every user on BOTH flows, no diagnostic. Byte-for-byte the failure the host header records an earlier revision already shipped (\"the two AADs differed by 16 bytes and every pairing failed the GCM tag and reported `pair_bad_mac`\"). Deeper mechanism: a frozen `PROTOCOL_AT_PAIRING` constant on the Android side so the pinned pairing value is unreachable from a negotiation edit; the remote wire version is already separately versioned (`REMOTE_PAIRING_PAYLOAD_VERSION = 2`), the AAD version is not.\n\n**5. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1242**\nsummary: `assertCandidateSource` puts transport identity (`CF-Connecting-IP`) inside the candidate state machine, so a mid-exchange source change is reported as `pair_wrong_peer` — a hostile-peer verdict with no retry path.\nfailure_scenario: Phone pairs over mobile data or across a Wi-Fi↔LTE boundary. `begin` arrives from IP a; the pooled OkHttp connection drops and `kex`/`confirm` arrives from IP b (handover, CGNAT rebalance). `refuseCandidateRewrite(\"a different source address\")` → `400 pair_wrong_peer` → \"Something else answered instead of the desktop. Refused.\"; `resubmitsSameCode` false, so a still-valid on-screen code is cleared and the user must mint a new window. The candidate's own `A`/`macPhone` already prove ownership cryptographically. Deeper mechanism: IP belongs to the admission/rate layer (`chargeRemoteRequest`, `kexSourceCounts`); candidate ownership belongs to key material — and the eviction log's own promise (\"it can simply begin again\") should hold on this path too.\n\n**6. `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a6c0cf0431904f59f\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\pairing.ts:1970` (consumed at `http.ts:833`)**\nsummary: `remoteWindowWasBurned()` is a process-global, source-agnostic memo, so for 60 s after a burn the pairing host answers `403 pair_code_burned` to any internet caller instead of the bare 404 the design's indistinguishability rests on.\nfailure_scenario: Any scanner POSTs `/v1/pair/begin` with no window open. Normally a bodyless 404, identical to an unknown path — \"outside a window a human deliberately opened, this surface is not discoverable at all\". Within 60 s of a burn it gets `403 {\"code\":\"pair_code_burned\"}`, confirming this machine runs the bridge, a human opened a code window recently, and three MACs failed. An attacker who deliberately burns a window (it holds a candidate, so it can spend three strikes — the accepted residual) receives positive acknowledgement of the burn, and repeated probing becomes an occupancy oracle. Deeper mechanism: scope the honest-error memo like the rest of the hop — against the candidate/source that burned it, or delivered only to a caller that can name the burned `pid`.\n\nChecked and clean: SRP arithmetic/padding (host `srpPad`, fixed-window `srpSecretModPow` vs BouncyCastle `rfc5054_3072` + `SRP6Util.calculateU`; identity `\"sc/v3 remote-pair\"`, `x = H(salt || H(I \":\" P))` agree byte-for-byte); `B` width/range/degenerate and `u != 0` checks both sides; transcript shapes (117 / 825 bytes) and profile domain separation with `sc/v1` literals untouched; atomic one-window reservation (`reserveWindowSlot`/`claimOrTearDown`/`releaseWindowSlot`); candidate key wipe on every exit path; nonce counter-0 reservation; persist-before-seal ordering plus `onPaired` uniqueness and keyStore rollback; two-host isolation (`classifyRequestHost`/`routeByHost`/`normaliseHostHeader`, load-time host-collision assert); `cf-connecting-ip` single-value/IP-literal validation; body caps and `no-store` on every pairing response; Unicode digit normalisation (`Character.digit` → ASCII, `passwordBytes` guard, host `assertAsciiDigitCode`); wire-error vocabulary parity including the frozen `400 pair_unknown_candidate` contract and bodyless-404 fallback; desktop dialog secret containment, serialized window operations, unmount close, server-owned verdict.\n\n**FIX-FIRST**\n\nNote for `code-review`: no SendMessage tool is exposed in this session, so this reply is the delivery channel. Findings 1–4 are the ones I would gate on; 5–6 are altitude/honesty issues. All are finder-stage — nothing verified by execution, since the