{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T12:37:23.873Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a371c34da940b2ad0</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\db223e0d-1ca5-4ab0-8b87-291b28fd63ce\\tasks\\a371c34da940b2ad0.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify kex eviction scan\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**REFUTED**\n\n**Location:** `C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/fork-superset-windows-arm64/packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:551-567` (`evictOldestKexSessionsIfFull`). Candidate's cited line 1356 does not exist — file is 1293 lines.\n\n**Summary:** Factual claim half-right, efficiency claim empty. Insertion order does track oldest (verified all mutation paths), and adjacent `chargeKexIntroduction` does use Map-first eviction at :541-542 — so the inconsistency is real. But the scan is bounded at 17 entries, runs at most once per insert, only inside a flood of a 120 s single-use pairing window. Zero observable effect. Pure style.\n\n**Cost scenario:** Worst case = attacker floods distinct deviceIds. Each insert past the cap → one loop over ≤17 Map entries (~17 integer compares). `while` can never iterate more than once: eviction is called immediately after each single `set` at :484, so `size` exceeds `MAX_KEX_SESSIONS` (16) by exactly 1. Per-source cap `MAX_KEX_PER_SOURCE` plus `MAX_KEX_SOURCES` bound total introductions per window. Aggregate saved work over an entire window is sub-microsecond, on a socket doing X25519 + HKDF + AEAD per packet. Nothing observable.\n\n**Evidence — all `kexSessions` mutation paths (exhaustive grep):**\n- :186 type decl; :974 `new Map()`; :1038 `clear()`\n- :441 `get` — existing deviceId returns early at :468 (`kexResponse`) without re-`set`; the differing-material branch throws `pair_wrong_peer` at :466. No re-set path.\n- :473 `set` — new keys only, `createdAtMs: Date.now()`\n- :562 `delete` — only site\n- :628 `get` — confirm path, no delete/re-add\n\nSo no key is ever re-inserted, and JS `Map.set` on an existing key preserves its original position regardless — insertion order = first-insert order, permanently.\n\n**Why the scan still isn't wrong to keep:**\n1. Tie behaviour is identical, not merely similar. Under a flood many inserts share one millisecond; strict `&lt;` at :556 never replaces on equality, so the scan selects the earliest-inserted among equal `createdAtMs` — exactly what `keys().next()` returns.\n2. The one divergence is a backwards wall-clock step (`Date.now()` is not monotonic; NTP step / manual clock change). There the scan evicts by wall clock and Map-first evicts by arrival. Neither is more correct — and the eviction is explicitly documented as best-effort at :142-153 and :564 (\"if the real phone was evicted it can simply kex again\"), so no invariant rests on the choice.\n3. `createdAtMs` is load-bearing elsewhere (window/candidate age), so switching eviction to `keys().next()` removes the read but not the field — net complexity reduction is one loop, and it silently couples eviction order to Map internals that the current code states explicitly.\n\nAreas checked clean: all `kexSessions` reads/writes/clears, early-return semantics of the duplicate-deviceId branch at :441-469, `while`-loop iteration bound vs. call site at :484, tie-break semantics of `&lt;` at :556, per-source and per-window flood caps feeding the eviction, and the adjacent `kexSourceCounts` Map-first eviction at :535-544 used as the comparison baseline.\n\n**Verdict: SHIP** (no defect; do not spend a change on this)</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>25554</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>12</tool_uses><duration_ms>1097553</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
