{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T16:13:20.197Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<agent-message from=\"update-gate-fable\">\nArchitecture review, read-only, all claims verified against tree.\n\n## 1. Correct choke point: the shared filter, `filterApplicableNotices` in `packages/shared/src/desktop-notices.ts:83`\n\nEvery applicable notice — remote `notices[]` AND the synthetic legacy `minimumVersion` gate — flows through this one function: the hook pushes the synthetic blocking notice into the array BEFORE filtering (`apps/desktop/src/renderer/hooks/useDesktopNotices/useDesktopNotices.ts:78-96`). Neuter here: map `severity: \"blocking\"` → `\"warning\"` and force `dismissible: true` on every output notice. Forcing dismissible also re-enables the dismissal check at `desktop-notices.ts:57`, so users can permanently dismiss what was a gate.\n\nWhy the others leak:\n- **Render site** (`apps/desktop/src/renderer/components/DesktopNotices/DesktopNotices.tsx:29-37`): only kills UpdateRequiredPage. Misses the second blocking surface — `NoticeDialog` with `dismissible: false` is an undismissable modal (no close button, Escape and outside-click prevented at `NoticeDialog.tsx:48-49`, no Dismiss button per line 61). A remote `warning + dismissible:false` still bricks the UI. Also misses any future upstream render site.\n- **Hook** (`useDesktopNotices.ts`): covers today's consumers (DesktopNotices, UpdatesPill are the only two — verified by grep), but a future upstream caller of `filterApplicableNotices` directly (e.g. a main-process boot gate) bypasses it. The filter is the definitional waist: nothing is \"applicable\" without passing through it.\n\nRecommend defense in depth: (a) filter downgrade as the primary invariant, (b) delete/neuter the `severity === \"blocking\"` branch at `DesktopNotices.tsx:29` as a second layer (it becomes dead code but catches a refactor that moves minimumVersion synthesis AFTER filtering).\n\n## 2. Every path to a blocking/undismissable prompt\n\nIN SCOPE (remote-driven, must be neutered):\n1. `DesktopNoticesGate` → `UpdateRequiredPage` on `current.severity === \"blocking\"` (`DesktopNotices.tsx:29`). Sole render site of UpdateRequiredPage (grep-verified). Fed by (i) remote notice with `severity:\"blocking\"`, (ii) synthetic minimum-version notice (`useDesktopNotices.ts:79-87`, hardcoded blocking + dismissible:false).\n2. `NoticeDialog` with remote `dismissible: false` — undismissable modal (`NoticeDialog.tsx:48-49, 59-69`). This is the one people miss.\n\nOUT OF SCOPE (legitimately ours, keep):\n3. Host-service min-version gate: `useRemoteHostStatus.ts:73-80` compares the remote host's reported version against `MIN_HOST_SERVICE_VERSION` — a compile-time constant from `@superset/shared/host-version` that WE ship. Rendered as `WorkspaceHostIncompatibleState` (v2-workspace `layout.tsx:196`, `V2WorkspaceMount.tsx:135`). Per-workspace only, has a \"Browse workspaces\" escape (`WorkspaceHostIncompatibleState.tsx:85`). Not remote-controllable. Keep untouched.\n4. Dev-only blocking preview (`useDesktopNotices.ts:40`, Escape hatch at `DesktopNotices.tsx:20-27`). Keep.\n5. UpdatesPill / PreUpdateConfirmPopover: only appears in auto-update READY state; on Windows auto-update is entirely disabled — `IS_AUTO_UPDATE_PLATFORM = PLATFORM.IS_MAC || PLATFORM.IS_LINUX` (`apps/desktop/src/main/lib/auto-updater.ts:52`), `checkForUpdates` early-returns (`:203`), `setupAutoUpdater` early-returns (`:335`). Unreachable on the fork.\n6. Main process: no consumer of `/api/desktop/version` outside the renderer hook (grep-verified). Tray/menu \"Check for Updates\" shows a dismissible \"only macOS/Linux\" dialog on Windows (`auto-updater.ts:231-237`).\n\nScope statement for the override: \"no data returned by GET /api/desktop/version may produce a non-dismissible surface.\" Host gate and dev preview are explicitly exempt.\n\n## 3. Marker design — what a merge must not silently break\n\nFailure modes to protect against:\n- Upstream rewrites/renames `filterApplicableNotices` or inlines filtering into the hook → merge resolves clean, fork downgrade sits in dead code.\n- Upstream moves the minimumVersion synthesis to after filtering, or to the main process → bypasses the filter entirely.\n- Upstream adds a new blocking mechanism the downgrade map doesn't cover: a new severity enum value (\"critical\"), a new field (`forceUpdate: true`), or changes the gate condition from severity to something else.\n\nAssertions (SCREENREADER-GUARD-DRIFT lesson — invariant over the whole surface, zero incidental upstream names):\n- **Behavioral invariant test** in `packages/shared/src/desktop-notices.test.ts` (existing suite, `desktop-notices.test.ts:37`): parse a maximally hostile raw server payload through the REAL `desktopVersionResponseSchema.parse` + `filterApplicableNotices` pipeline and assert for EVERY output notice: `severity !== \"blocking\" && dismissible === true`. Property over the output set, survives renames of internals.\n- **Marker tokens** (e.g. `(REMOTE-NO-BLOCK)`) placed in BOTH `packages/shared/src/desktop-notices.ts` and its test, with two `\\t`-rooted lines in the FEATURES.md ```markers``` block (format per `scripts/check-feature-markers.mjs:104-105`; multi-root precedent: `(WATCHER-BLUE-STOMP)` at FEATURES.md:119-120). If upstream rewrites the file and the AI resolver drops the fork hunk, the marker grep fails the merge before anything ships.\n- **FEATURES.md table row stating the invariant in behavioral terms** (\"remote data can never render a non-dismissible surface; blocking severity is downgraded at the shared filter\") — this text is direct input to the `(MERGE-SEMANTIC-GATE)` Opus review, which is the only layer that can catch a semantically new upstream blocking mechanism (new field, new render site) that no grep token covers.\n- Do NOT assert on `UpdateRequiredPage`/`DesktopNoticesGate` names or the schema's severity enum shape — those are upstream incidentals that rename freely.\n\n## 4. Legitimate fork blocking?\n\nYes, but it already exists in the correct form and needs nothing new: the host-service version mismatch (item 3 above). The distinguishing rule is **provenance of the threshold**: blocking is legitimate only when the condition compares against a constant compiled into the fork's own binary (`MIN_HOST_SERVICE_VERSION`), scoped to the affected workspace, with an escape route. Remote-sourced data from upstream's API never qualifies. An app-wide forced-update gate has no purpose on this fork at all — it ships its own releases (\"one version, ever\") and upstream's updater can't deliver our build. If a genuinely corrupting protocol break ever needs an app-level gate, implement it as a fork-local constant check, never via the desktop-notices severity channel.\n\n## 5. CTAs to neuter\n\n- `UpdateRequiredPage` \"Download Manually\" → `openUrl.mutate(COMPANY.CHANGELOG_URL)` (`UpdateRequiredPage.tsx:52-54, 119-121`) — sends the user to upstream's download page to install upstream's build over the fork. Moot once the page is unreachable; if the component is kept at all, retarget to the fork's releases URL.\n- Notice CTA `action: \"install-update\"` (`NoticeDialog.tsx:35-39` → `autoUpdate.check/install`): inert on Windows today (`auto-updater.ts:203, 186`), so a hard neuter is optional — but note the feed URL points at `superset-sh/superset` releases (`auto-updater.ts:58-60`) and the only thing keeping it off is the one-line `IS_AUTO_UPDATE_PLATFORM` constant, which an upstream merge could extend to Windows cleanly. Worth either neutering `install-update` CTAs to a no-op/fork-URL, or flagging the feed URL as a standing risk deserving its own override marker. My pick: neuter the CTA action in the fork (cheap, closes the class), and leave `open-url` CTAs alone — post-override every dialog is dismissible and opening a browser is the user's choice.\n\nOne implementation caveat for the Opus agent: forcing `dismissible: true` inside `filterApplicableNotices` before the dismissal check means the synthetic minimum-version notice becomes permanently dismissible via the dismissals store — that is the intended behavior (fork can never be version-gated by upstream's API), but state it in the feature row so the semantic review doesn't \"fix\" it.\n</agent-message>"}
