— PostHog, Segment, Amplitude, GA, Mixpanel, or any custom event POST. Include renderer AND main process AND host-service.\n2. Crash/error reporting — Sentry (look for @sentry/*, sentry init, dsn, tracesSampleRate, replay), Electron crashReporter, breakpad/minidump uploads.\n3. Auto-update — electron-updater / update feed URLs / `install-update` actions / `checkForUpdates`. Note which feed our fork's releases actually use vs upstream's.\n4. The desktop notices/version endpoint (`/api/desktop/version`) and anything else polling their API on a timer.\n5. Auth / session / organization — better-auth or equivalent, `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` usage, token refresh, `/api/auth/*`. CRITICAL: determine whether the app can boot, sign in, and open a workspace with NO reachable Superset API. Is there any local/offline/self-host mode? Read the `_authenticated` route guards and session restore path and say plainly what happens when the API is unreachable.\n6. Electric / workspace sync (`packages/db`, TanStack DB, electric-sql) — is the sync origin their cloud, our own, or local? What breaks if severed?\n7. AI/model providers reached directly by the app (Anthropic/OpenAI/etc.) and any proxying through their cloud — the user's coding agents legitimately need model access; distinguish \"model API the user pays for directly\" from \"routed through Superset's cloud\".\n8. Any remaining phone-home: license checks, feature flags, remote config, changelog fetch, avatar/CDN fetches, font/CDN loads, marketing links opened at boot.\n9. Env plumbing: how NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL and friends are baked at build time (apps/desktop build config, .env handling, electron-builder config) — i.e. what a build-time override could change.\n\nDELIVERABLE — three clearly separated lists:\nA. SAFE TO SEVER with no functional loss for a local-only user (with the mechanism you'd use for each: build-time env, code no-op, dependency drop).\nB. SEVERABLE BUT WITH A COST — name the exact feature lost and how visible it is