{"parentUuid":"92df002a-fd4a-4bd0-a644-95a8782fc2b4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAFGGk2cg5T3ab6WVhrq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KrDUUd2PJqJCjM4yQQcRPQ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Opus review of CI-integrity diffs","name":"ci-trust-review-opus","prompt":"Repo: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64 (owner's own private fork of superset-sh/superset). REPOSITORY FILES ONLY — never read user home-directory files, shell profiles, or credentials; explicitly out of scope per owner.\n\nIMPORTANT ENV NOTE: the Bash tool is broken in this session (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: unexpected EOF`). Use the PowerShell tool for all commands. Some gate scripts need Git-for-Windows coreutils on PATH: `$env:PATH = \"C:\\Program Files\\Git\\usr\\bin;$env:PATH\"`.\n\nGet the diff:\n  git diff -- .github/workflows/build-arm64.yml scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs scripts/ci-repair.sh\n(run from the repo root, which is already the working directory)\n\nCONTEXT — what this code is for. This fork's CI has a bounded automated self-repair loop ((BUILD-REPAIR)): when a build fails, an AI agent edits the checked-out tree and pushes to a candidate branch; the next attempt builds that exact sha. That agent reads build logs, which are untrusted input. The design goal of the code under review is straightforward release-integrity engineering: make it impossible for an automated repair step to publish a GitHub Release that does not satisfy the fork's cloud-severance gate. Think of it as the same class of control as \"required status checks\" or a signed-artifact policy — defensive supply-chain hardening on the owner's own repository.\n\nAlready committed at HEAD: publishing was moved out of the repairable composite action into the frozen workflow, behind an enforcement step. That step deletes any pre-existing stamp, re-runs scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs itself, and requires mode=full + exact assertion count (11) + a stamp sha equal to the sha that was built. It unsets NODE_OPTIONS/NODE_PATH and resolves an absolute node path so an earlier repairable step cannot poison the interpreter.\n\nTHE THREE UNCOMMITTED CHANGES UNDER REVIEW (working tree vs HEAD):\n1. scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs — strengthened assertion set; writes .fork/cloud-severance-stamp.json only on success, with sha from `git rev-parse HEAD`, mode, and assertion count.\n2. scripts/ci-repair.sh — (D1) trusted-validator extraction: the validator is taken from a trusted ref rather than the agent-edited working tree; (D2) YAML-aware step-block comparison replacing a line-based awk reader. The line-based reader was previously fooled by a look-alike step embedded inside another step's `run: |` block scalar.\n3. .github/workflows/build-arm64.yml — corresponding wiring.\n\nThe implementer reports an 18-shape tamper matrix, all failing loud. Verify that claim skeptically and independently. Specifically:\n- Does the trusted-validator extraction genuinely source the validator from a ref the repair step cannot have modified during this run (workflow-pinned sha / pre-repair tip), or does it read from the agent-edited tree at any point? Look for TOCTOU windows between extraction, verification, and use.\n- Is the YAML-aware extraction real YAML parsing (yq / node+js-yaml / python) or a smarter regex? Try to construct a bypass: anchors and aliases, merge keys, flow-style mappings, duplicate keys, nested block scalars, CRLF, unicode look-alike characters in step names/ids.\n- Stamp integrity: can a repair pre-place a stamp file, or win a race between the delete and the re-check? Is the sha compared against the sha actually built?\n- Workflow YAML is frozen at run start, so build-arm64.yml edits only take effect on the NEXT run. Does anything in the diff wrongly assume same-run effect?\n- Shell correctness in ci-repair.sh: quoting (the repo path contains a space), `set -euo pipefail` discipline, error paths that silently succeed, exit-code propagation through pipes.\n- Can the exact-count assertion (11) be satisfied by a degenerate run — empty artifact set, skipped packaging, missing release dir?\n\nRank findings BLOCKER / MAJOR / MINOR, each with file:line and a concrete failure scenario. Verify every finding against the real code before reporting it — no speculative findings. If the batch is clean, say so plainly; do not manufacture issues. Your final message is the entire report returned to the orchestrator.","subagent_type":"code-reviewer-opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":72147,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1900,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":181},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":72147,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1900,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":72147,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":72147},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"65d32d8b-21ee-4633-b37a-74702a592fe1","timestamp":"2026-08-18T12:53:43.385Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
