{"parentUuid":"f9117ac0-c647-4918-9dd9-1db57ef3154e","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAFg8tuey8Jd2xMC9ifm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fable's second instance did complete — verdict **FIX-FIRST**: 2 BLOCKERs, 2 MAJORs, 3 MINORs. Both blockers share one root cause and both are real.\n\nKey correction to my earlier deadline framing: the nightly builds from `origin/main`, so these uncommitted files don't affect tonight's run at all. No forced deadline — HEAD's protections stand either way. Doing it right beats doing it by 02:13.\n\nSplitting the work: cheap independent fixes to Opus now, structural design to Fable for audit in parallel."}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4796,"cache_read_input_tokens":78320,"output_tokens":8105,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3750},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4796,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8105,"cache_read_input_tokens":78320,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4796,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4796},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"1c16a4c2-f61f-4e28-9436-c61e4279eef8","timestamp":"2026-08-18T12:59:39.543Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"1c16a4c2-f61f-4e28-9436-c61e4279eef8","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAFg8tuey8Jd2xMC9ifm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Krquqy1weNDgi63srB49Pk","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Implement 3 low-risk CI gate fixes","name":"ci-cheap-fixes-opus","prompt":"Repo: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64 (owner's own private fork). REPOSITORY FILES ONLY — never read user home-directory files, shell profiles, or credentials; out of scope per owner.\n\nENV: the Bash tool is BROKEN in this session (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: unexpected EOF`). Use the PowerShell tool for everything. Gate scripts need Git-for-Windows coreutils: `$env:PATH = \"C:\\Program Files\\Git\\usr\\bin;$env:PATH\"`.\n\nThere are already uncommitted working-tree changes to `.github/workflows/build-arm64.yml`, `scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs`, `scripts/ci-repair.sh`. DO NOT revert or restructure them. You are adding three targeted fixes on top, found by an adversarial review.\n\nA separate, larger structural refactor (splitting enforcement/publish and validate/push into fresh-runner jobs) is being designed in parallel by someone else and will touch build-arm64.yml heavily. To avoid conflicts: keep your edits MINIMAL and confined to the specific lines below. Prefer `scripts/ci-repair.sh` edits; touch `build-arm64.yml` only for fix 3, which is a one-line comparison operator or comment.\n\nFIX 1 (MAJOR) — scripts/ci-repair.sh around lines 108, 219, 273-277.\nThe \"one version, ever\" freeze compares post-agent package versions against `$STATE_DIR/base-versions`, a file under $RUNNER_TEMP written before the agent runs. The workflow's own comment (build-arm64.yml:214-217) concedes the agent can rewrite files under RUNNER_TEMP. Only base-sha is anchored to a step output; base-versions is not. A hostile repair can bump versions and rewrite base-versions to match, defeating the freeze — which matters because the publish script does `gh release delete \"$TAG\" --yes`, so a version bump enables clobbering an existing Release.\nFix: recompute the base side from trusted git objects instead of the runner-temp file — `git show \"$TRUSTED_BASE_SHA:<pkg>/package.json\"` for each versioned package. TRUSTED_BASE_SHA already exists in this script's flow (it comes from a pre-agent step output). Keep the existing failure messages loud. If base-versions is still used elsewhere for non-security bookkeeping, leave that alone but make the SECURITY comparison use git objects.\n\nFIX 2 (MINOR) — scripts/ci-repair.sh around line 336, the python3 `yaml.safe_load` used by the YAML-aware step-block comparison.\nPyYAML resolves duplicate mapping keys last-wins with no error. A step rewritten as `{run: EVIL, ..., run: <original>}` therefore compares EQUAL to base. Today the Actions runner rejects duplicate keys so the practical worst case is a next-run parse failure rather than a bypass — but that safety rests on the runner's parser, not on this gate.\nFix: reject duplicate mapping keys explicitly so the comparison itself fails loud. Either subclass yaml.SafeLoader and override construct_mapping to raise on a repeated key, or use ruamel with allow_duplicate_keys=False. Keep the existing fail-loud exit-code convention (the script already exits 3 when PyYAML is missing — follow that pattern).\n\nFIX 3 (MINOR) — .github/workflows/build-arm64.yml around lines 163-169.\nThe comment and design intent say the assertion count must be EXACT, but the code enforces `>= 11`. Harmless today (the stamp only exists when every assertion passed, and a full packaged run yields exactly 11) but the semantics diverge from the documented intent. Make them agree — prefer tightening the code to an equality check, since \"exactly the expected assertion set ran\" is the stronger and intended property. Keep the message loud and self-explaining on mismatch.\n\nAFTER EDITING, verify:\n- `node scripts/check-feature-markers.mjs` → must end \"All 201 feature markers present.\"\n- `node scripts/check-dangerous-diagnostics.mjs` → must end \"(REFERR-GATE) clean\".\n- Syntax-check the shell script: `bash -n scripts/ci-repair.sh` via `& \"C:\\Program Files\\Git\\bin\\bash.exe\" -n scripts/ci-repair.sh`\n- Exercise your duplicate-key rejection with a small throwaway YAML fixture under `tmp/` proving it now fails loud, and a normal step block proving it still passes. Delete the fixtures after, or leave them under tmp/ only.\n- Do NOT commit. Do NOT push. Leave changes in the working tree and report exactly what you changed with file:line.\n\nYour final message is the entire report returned to the orchestrator: what you changed, verification output, anything you chose not to do and why.","subagent_type":"claude","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4796,"cache_read_input_tokens":78320,"output_tokens":8105,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3750},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4796,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8105,"cache_read_input_tokens":78320,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4796,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4796},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"1f7b3259-7067-461a-95f5-06b21a8add9d","timestamp":"2026-08-18T13:00:02.972Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"1f7b3259-7067-461a-95f5-06b21a8add9d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAFg8tuey8Jd2xMC9ifm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VaHR5XmnwcFA6FNEoEMdKr","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Audit CI job-split design","name":"jobsplit-audit-fable","prompt":"Repo: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64 (owner's own private fork of an open-source desktop app). REPOSITORY FILES ONLY — never read user home-directory files, shell profiles, or credentials; out of scope per owner.\n\nENV: the Bash tool is BROKEN in this session (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: unexpected EOF`). Use the PowerShell tool for everything. Gate scripts need Git-for-Windows coreutils on PATH: `$env:PATH = \"C:\\Program Files\\Git\\usr\\bin;$env:PATH\"`.\n\nYou are auditing a DESIGN, not code. Read-only. This is ordinary release-integrity engineering on the owner's own repository — the same class of control as required status checks or a signed-artifact policy.\n\nBACKGROUND. This fork's CI has a bounded automated self-repair loop: when a build fails, an AI agent edits the checked-out tree and pushes to a candidate branch, and the next attempt builds that sha. The agent reads build logs, which are untrusted input, so the design treats the agent as potentially hostile. The property we want: an automated repair must never be able to publish a GitHub Release that violates the fork's cloud-severance gate (scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs).\n\nAn adversarial review just found two BLOCKERs with a common root cause: the \"frozen\" enforcement step and the trusted-validator push step both execute INSIDE jobs whose earlier steps are attacker-controlled. So even though their BYTES are trusted (extracted from a pre-agent sha), their execution environment is not — the agent can poison BASH_ENV or PATH via $GITHUB_ENV/$GITHUB_PATH (the runner applies these to subsequent steps), swap files from a background process, or replace binaries on the writable runner VM.\n\nMY PROPOSED STRUCTURAL FIX — audit this:\n\n(1) New job `enforce-and-publish`, `needs:` the build job, fresh runner.\n    - Checks out the RUN'S TRIGGERING SHA (github.sha — frozen at run start, same trust level as the workflow YAML itself, and critically NOT the repair agent's commit) into a clean directory. This is the source of TRUSTED CODE.\n    - Downloads the build job's artifacts (dist/, release/) via upload-artifact/download-artifact. These are UNTRUSTED DATA — scanning them is the gate's whole job.\n    - Runs scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs FROM THE TRUSTED CHECKOUT against the downloaded artifacts.\n    - Requires mode=full, the exact assertion count, and stamp sha == BUILT_SHA (passed between jobs as a job output).\n    - Publishes using a publish script FROM THE TRUSTED CHECKOUT, uploading only files whose hashes the gate recorded.\n    - ONLY this job gets `contents: write`. The build job's token becomes read-only.\n\n    Key insight I want you to stress-test: the gate SCRIPT must come from the triggering sha, NOT from BUILT_SHA. BUILT_SHA is the repair agent's own commit. Today the agent is stopped from editing the gate script by the push-time validator's FROZEN_GATE_PATHS check — but BLOCKER 2 says that validator is itself subvertible, so BUILT_SHA's copy of the gate cannot be trusted.\n\n    Complication I have already spotted and want your judgement on: if any assertion in check-cloud-severance.mjs reads the SOURCE tree rather than built artifacts, the trusted checkout is the PRE-repair source and will not match a legitimately repaired build. Possible resolution: check out BUILT_SHA into a separate directory for source-reading assertions while still executing the gate SCRIPT from the trusted checkout — trusted code, untrusted data. Verify which assertions actually read source vs artifacts and tell me whether this split is necessary and whether it is sound.\n\n(2) Split validate+push the same way. The agent job holds NO token and cannot push; it uploads its resulting tree (git bundle or patch) as an artifact. A separate `validate-and-push` job on a fresh runner checks out the trusted sha, applies the patch onto a fresh checkout of BASE_SHA, runs the trusted validator, and pushes. The push credential exists only in that job.\n    Side benefit I am claiming: this also removes the write-scoped token from the agent's runner entirely, which closes a previously-deferred item.\n\nAUDIT THESE QUESTIONS CONCRETELY, citing file:line from the current repo:\n- Is the triggering-sha-as-trust-anchor claim actually correct for BOTH entry points (the nightly merge workflow and a manual build dispatch)? For a nightly the candidate branch is derived during the run — does github.sha still point at something pre-repair and trusted in every path? What about a dispatch whose input IS a repair branch?\n- Does artifact upload/download preserve what the gate needs (file modes, symlinks, the NSIS .exe intact, nested dist/ layout)? Any assertion that would break when run against downloaded rather than freshly-built artifacts?\n- Which assertions in scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs read source vs built artifacts? Enumerate them.\n- Does the stamp mechanism still make sense across a job boundary, or is it now redundant (the gate runs in the same job that publishes)? Simpler is better if it is genuinely equivalent.\n- Version-freeze and marker checks: where do they need to run under the new split?\n- What NEW attack surface does the split introduce? Artifact poisoning between jobs, job-output injection (BUILT_SHA is a string crossing a job boundary — can it be forged?), `needs` bypass, workflow_run trust pitfalls, re-run semantics.\n- Practical cost: does the split materially slow the build (artifact upload/download of a ~200MB installer and a >1GB asar)? Is there a cheaper variant that gets most of the benefit?\n- Anything in AGENTS.md's documented non-negotiables or accepted limitations that this design contradicts or should update.\n\nDELIVER: a verdict (SOUND / SOUND-WITH-CHANGES / REDESIGN) plus a concrete corrected design an implementer can follow, and an honest list of what remains unfixable within GitHub Actions' model. Push back hard if the design is wrong or over-engineered. Your final message is the entire report.","subagent_type":"architect-fable"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4796,"cache_read_input_tokens":78320,"output_tokens":8105,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3750},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4796,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8105,"cache_read_input_tokens":78320,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4796,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4796},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"8026f4e2-081c-42d3-82cb-25b79ffad7a0","timestamp":"2026-08-18T13:00:30.701Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
