{"parentUuid":"b59a49d9-0b38-4a43-bd03-2282be74569d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAFtC4zfmj9P6rF2rc47","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Four agents running. Summary of where the CI work landed:\n\n**Fable's verdict: FIX-FIRST.** Both BLOCKERs are the same root cause — the \"frozen\" enforcement step and the trusted-validator push step run *inside* jobs whose earlier steps are attacker-controlled. Trusted bytes, untrusted environment: a repair can poison `BASH_ENV`/`PATH` through `$GITHUB_ENV`/`$GITHUB_PATH`, leave a background process to swap files mid-step, or replace binaries on the writable runner. Either chain ends in a published Release that violates the gate.\n\nFable also confirmed the parts that hold: the YAML-aware comparison is genuine PyYAML parsing (anchors, aliases, merge keys and homoglyph renames all caught), stamps can't be pre-placed, and no degenerate run reaches a full pass.\n\n**In flight now:**\n- Opus implementing three low-risk fixes (version-freeze base read from git objects instead of runner-temp; duplicate-key rejection in the YAML compare; assertion-count equality to match documented intent).\n- Fable auditing my structural fix — move enforcement+publish and validate+push to fresh-runner jobs, with the gate script sourced from the run's *triggering* sha rather than the repair agent's commit. Side benefit if it holds: the write-scoped token leaves the agent's runner entirely, closing the item I'd deferred and owed you.\n- Opus second-opinion review of the same three files.\n- Opus architect still mapping the terminal detach/reattach design.\n\nNothing commits until reviewed."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8399,"cache_read_input_tokens":83116,"output_tokens":669,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":127},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":