{"parentUuid":"027da82c-5e96-4d50-aca5-89e13faa7cd7","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAG1z2BCANEkPjwFr3k1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014yRoBf8o1dhS4onoe2vitm","name":"SendMessage","input":{"message":"You went idle without sending me your report. I received no findings from you.\n\nSend the full terminal detach/reattach architecture blueprint now, as a single message. It must cover, with file:line citations:\n1. What pty-daemon.js is, its spawn site and options (detached? stdio? job object?), and the named-pipe naming scheme (is the hex suffix stable across restarts or random per boot?).\n2. Division of labour between terminal-host.js, host-service.js and pty-daemon.js — which one actually owns the node-pty handles.\n3. Whether pty-daemon already survives its parent dying, and what kills it today at app quit (explicit kill, process group, stdio, job object, idle timer, pipe-disconnect exit).\n4. Port map: host-service listens on 127.0.0.1:47610; renderer dials 127.0.0.1:48056. Which process owns which, and which carries terminal I/O.\n5. Whether the NSIS one-click installer force-kills the app and its children.\n6. Ranked candidate designs with concrete deltas (files to modify, protocol changes), version-skew handling between an old surviving daemon and a new app, and failure modes (orphan leak, auth, two apps contending for one daemon).\n\nIf you did not finish the investigation, say exactly what you did and did not establish rather than guessing. Partial findings with clear gaps are far more useful to me than a confident summary that papers over what you did not check. Repository files and the installed app's own artifacts only.","summary":"Request missing architecture report","to":"term-persist-explore-opus","type":"message","recipient":"term-persist-explore-opus","content":"You went idle without sending me your report. I r…"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_u