quit path signals it. Reattach-by-stable-pipe and session adoption are already built. The ONLY thing blocking \"install without losing terminals\" is that the daemon's process image is `Superset.exe` from the INSTALL DIRECTORY — so the installer must terminate it to replace files it holds open.\n\nSpecific claims to verify:\n- packages/host-service/src/daemon/DaemonSupervisor.ts:1190-1207 — daemon spawned by host-service from `process.execPath` with `--socket=<pipe>`; `detached: !isDev` at :1203; isDev = NODE_ENV===\"development\" at :1159; desktop sets NODE_ENV=production when app.isPackaged (apps/desktop/src/main/lib/host-service-coordinator.ts:787-789); `windowsHide:true` :1206; stdio ignore+logfd; `child.unref()` :1261.\n- NO job object anywhere (searched JobObject/AssignProcessToJobObject/nsProcess/taskkill).\n- Pipe name STABLE not random: DaemonSupervisor.ts:149-164, `\\\\.\\pipe\\superset-ptyd-${sha256(organizationId).slice(0,12)}`; SUPERSET_HOME_DIR namespacing gated on dev only at :158-160. Manifest with pid+socketPath at ~/.superset/host/<org>/pty-daemon-manifest.json (packages/host-service/src/daemon/manifest.ts:37-55).\n- pty-daemon owns the node-pty handles (packages/pty-daemon/src/Pty/Pty.ts, Server.ts); 64 KiB in-memory ring buffer per session, memory-only, never on disk (SessionStore.ts:4,:36); spawn passes no --buffer-bytes.\n- host-service is a CLIENT; no in-process PTY fallback (packages/host-service/src/terminal/terminal.ts:889 always uses getDaemonClient()).\n- terminal-host.js is the VESTIGIAL v1 stack, still spawned unconditionally at boot via prewarmTerminalRuntime (apps/desktop/src/main/index.ts:528-530, apps/desktop/src/main/lib/terminal/index.ts:118).\n- Quit path kills ONLY host-service's single pid: quit-sequence.ts:29-75 → stopHostServices → killProcess(hostServicePid,\"SIGTERM\") → process.kill on one pid (host-service-manifest.ts:91-100). tree-kill imported only by v1 stack + port-manager, never the quit path.\n- Ports: BOTH 48056 (tRPC + terminal WebSocket, renderer-facing, STABLE_PORT_BASE=48000 + org hash, host-service-coordinator.ts:110-131) and 47610 (companion bridge, packages/host-service/src/companion/config.ts:24) belong to host-service.js. No PTY bytes on 47610.\n- Version skew already handled: drift judged on pty-daemon PACKAGE version not app version (packages/host-service/src/daemon/expected-version.ts:12-16); tryAdopt at :965-1051; fd-handoff update ALWAYS fails on Windows non-destructively (DaemonSupervisor.ts:351-357, packages/pty-daemon/src/Pty/Pty.ts:17 SUPPORTS_FD_HANDOFF).\n\nRECOMMENDED FIX (Rank 1) — \"sidecar runtime\": at app boot on Windows, copy the packaged app directory to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Superset\\pty-runtime\\<appVersion>\\, renaming Superset.exe to superset-ptyd.exe. host-service spawns the daemon from that copy. Installer's name-based kill misses it (different image name), path-based kill misses it (outside $INSTDIR), and file replacement is unaffected since no handle points into the install dir. Claims NO new protocol needed because the pipe name is already version-independent and adoption already exists.\nNew file apps/desktop/src/main/lib/pty-runtime/pty-runtime.ts (atomic copy: tmp dir + rename + .ready marker, plus GC of stale version dirs). Env var SUPERSET_PTY_DAEMON_RUNTIME_ROOT added in host-service-coordinator buildEnv (:762-841) only when ready. DaemonSupervisor.spawn() uses sidecar exe + path-relative remap of scriptPath, failing loud if missing. Optional runtimeRoot field in manifest.ts. FEATURES.md marker (PTY-RUNTIME-SIDECAR).\n\nARCHITECT'S OWN UNVERIFIED ITEMS (it could not check these — you should try):\n(a) Whether a RENAMED Electron binary still works under ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1. Called \"the single assumption that could invalidate Rank 1\".\n(b) Whether NSIS actually force-kills by name/path (node_modules absent so the electron-builder template was unreadable). Config: oneClick:true, no custom include/script (apps/desktop/electron-builder.ts:191-199).\n(c) Whether the daemon in fact survives a plain quit TODAY. The owner reported \"terminals die when I close superset (tested)\", which CONTRADICTS the code analysis. Architect says gate everything on this.\n(d) Whether @lydell/node-pty-win32-arm64 ships a Node-ABI prebuild (would make a slim standalone-node sidecar viable instead of a ~300MB Electron copy, flipping the ranking).\n\nKNOWN RISKS THE ARCHITECT RAISED: Windows orphan-shell leak because packages/pty-daemon/src/process-tree.ts is POSIX-only (ps -axo at :130-142, process.kill(-pgid) at :300) so terminateProcessTreeAndGroups (DaemonSupervisor.ts:1523-1532) orphans shell trees on Windows; named-pipe auth is only same-user (README.md:85-87 claims 0600 socket, false on Windows); ~300MB per retained runtime version + GC correctness; reaper false positives during the install window (reaper.ts:136-176); uninstall residue; nightly-merge conflicts on upstream-owned files.\n\n=== YOUR TASK ===\n1. Verify or refute the headline claim and each cited line. The architect could not run anything — you have PowerShell, so you can inspect the INSTALLED app too (C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Programs\\@supersetdesktop\\) and the daemon's own state/logs under the .superset host dir. Read-only: do NOT quit, kill, or restart anything, and do not run the installer.\n2. Resolve (c) if you can WITHOUT disrupting the running app — e.g. evidence from pty-daemon.log rotation, manifest history, process start times, or host.db session rows showing whether a daemon ever outlived an app restart. The owner's report and the code disagree; find out which is right, or state precisely what test would settle it.\n3. Attack the sidecar design: what breaks it? Consider Defender/SmartScreen on a copied unsigned exe, Electron's dependence on sibling resources when renamed, code-signing/ASAR integrity checks, `app.getAppPath()` assumptions inside the daemon, the daemon writing into the sidecar tree, GC deleting a live runtime, two app versions racing the copy, the .ready marker being non-atomic, disk exhaustion, and the case where the sidecar copy is CORRUPT or half-written.\n4. Given the hard-kill constraint: does anything in this design depend on a graceful path? Is rediscovery genuinely handoff-free and crash-consistent (atomic writes, tolerant reads, stale-record and lost-record cases both)? What is the daemon's orphan policy with NO client at all — does it hold PTYs forever, and is that acceptable?\n5. Scrollback honesty: with a 64 KiB memory-only ring buffer, what does the user ACTUALLY see after reattaching post-install? State the real UX.\n6. Is there a materially cheaper design that gets the same outcome? Push back hard if the sidecar is over-engineered.\n\nDELIVER: verdict (SOUND / SOUND-WITH-CHANGES / REDESIGN), a corrected implementable design, an ordered build plan whose steps are independently verifiable, and an honest list of what stays unsolved. Your final message is the entire report.","teammate_id":"term-plan-audit-fable@session-92794baa","agent_id":"term-plan-audit-fable@session-92794baa","agent_type":"architect-fable","model":"fable","name":"term-plan-audit-fable","color":"red","tmux_session_name":"in-process","tmux_window_name":"in-process","tmux_pane_id":"in-process","team_name":"session-92794baa","is_splitpane":false,"plan_mode_required":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7ca66aca-13da-4613-8a69-2f7a0d6bfaf8","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
