{"parentUuid":"367e16c8-a699-48b6-acc4-eccb2f5526ec","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAGusopU8ayyQiVtyH1e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01S3fHSsfRzTYBwCsQK4iD89","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Fix converged CI blockers and majors","model":"opus","name":"ci-blockers-impl-opus","prompt":"Repo: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64 (owner's own private fork). REPOSITORY FILES ONLY — never read user home-directory files, shell profiles, or credentials; out of scope per owner.\n\nENV: the Bash tool is BROKEN in this session (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: unexpected EOF`). Use the PowerShell tool for everything. Coreutils: `$env:PATH = \"C:\\Program Files\\Git\\usr\\bin;$env:PATH\"`. Git bash binary for syntax checks: `& \"C:\\Program Files\\Git\\bin\\bash.exe\" -n <script>`.\n\nCONTEXT. This fork's CI has a bounded automated self-repair loop: on build failure an AI agent edits the checked-out tree and pushes to a candidate branch, and the next attempt builds that sha. The agent reads build logs, which are untrusted input, so the design treats it as potentially hostile. The property required: an automated repair must never be able to publish a GitHub Release that violates the cloud-severance gate (scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs). This is ordinary release-integrity engineering on the owner's own repo — the same class of control as required status checks.\n\nThere are existing uncommitted working-tree changes to `.github/workflows/build-arm64.yml`, `scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs`, `scripts/ci-repair.sh`. DO NOT revert or restructure them; three targeted fixes already landed in them (git-object-sourced version freeze, PyYAML duplicate-key rejection via NoDuplicateKeyLoader, exact assertion-count equality). You are adding the fixes below on top.\n\nTWO INDEPENDENT ADVERSARIAL REVIEWS CONVERGED ON THESE. I independently confirmed finding M2 myself. Implement all six.\n\n--- BLOCKER B1: BASH_ENV is not sanitized in the frozen enforcement steps ---\nFile: .github/workflows/build-arm64.yml, the \"Enforce cloud severance\" steps (there are THREE near-identical copies, around lines 118/130, 305/317, 489/501 — fix ALL of them).\nDefect: the step unsets NODE_OPTIONS and NODE_PATH but not BASH_ENV. Bash sources $BASH_ENV for every non-interactive shell, including the runner's `bash --noprofile --norc -eo pipefail {0}`. An earlier repairable step (in .github/actions/arm64-build/action.yml, which IS repairable and takes effect on the next attempt of the same run) can write `BASH_ENV=<path>` to $GITHUB_ENV. The enforcement step then sources attacker code BEFORE its own first line and can `exit 0`, so the gate never runs and the Release publishes. Note NODE_OPTIONS is already in the runner's own _setEnvBlockList, so the one variable explicitly handled is the one that could not be set anyway; NODE_PATH and BASH_ENV are not blocked.\nFix: at the very top of each enforcement step's run block, `unset BASH_ENV ENV NODE_OPTIONS NODE_PATH`, set PATH to a fixed absolute value, and additionally ASSERT that BASH_ENV was empty on entry and fail loud if not — a set BASH_ENV at that point is itself evidence of tampering and should be reported as such, not silently cleaned. Keep the existing absolute-node resolution.\n\n--- BLOCKER B2: same class in the validate+push step ---\nFile: .github/workflows/build-arm64.yml, \"Validate + push repair\" steps around lines 258 and 442.\nDefect: the trusted-validator extraction pins the validator's BYTES but not the environment it executes in. This step runs immediately after the agent step in the same job with no env/PATH sanitization, and the agent runs with unrestricted shell access (scripts/ai-run.sh:182). A poisoned $GITHUB_PATH or BASH_ENV means bash, git, python3, node, grep, diff and base64 are all attacker-resolved — inside the shell that holds PUSH_TOKEN (the WORKFLOW_PUSH_TOKEN PAT with Contents+Workflows write). That shell can push arbitrary content, write repaired=true/sha= to $GITHUB_OUTPUT, exit 0, and exfiltrate the PAT. TRUSTED_BASE_SHA being a runner-owned step output does not help, because the code that reads it never runs.\nFix: same sanitization at the top of the run block (unset BASH_ENV ENV NODE_OPTIONS NODE_PATH, fixed absolute PATH, fail loud if BASH_ENV was set), and invoke bash/git by absolute path. Inside scripts/ci-repair.sh, resolve python3/node/git to absolute paths too (or verify they resolve under the fixed PATH before use, failing loud otherwise).\n\n--- MAJOR M1: step relocation across jobs is invisible to the freeze check ---\nFile: scripts/ci-repair.sh, `collect()` around line 341, comparison around line 393.\nDefect: collect() flattens matching steps from every job into one flat list and discards which job and index each came from. The three enforcement steps in build-arm64.yml are BYTE-IDENTICAL (same name, shell, env, run), so they parse to three identical dicts. A repair deletes the enforcement step from `build-attempt-3` and adds a second identical copy anywhere else: base_steps and head_steps are both a list of three identical objects, so the comparison sees no change. On a later night where attempts 1 and 2 fail, build-attempt-3 builds, skips enforcement entirely, and publishes.\nFix: key extracted steps by location — emit `{\"job\": <job-name-or-\"runs\">, \"index\": i, \"step\": step}` so any relocation changes the serialization. ADDITIONALLY assert placement structurally: every job that contains a \"Publish GitHub Release\" step must contain exactly one enforcement step BEFORE it in that same job. Fail loud with a clear message naming the offending job.\n\n--- MAJOR M2: the gate validates packaging's INPUT, not the artifact that ships (I confirmed this myself) ---\nFiles: scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs (distRoot at :48, bundledCliPath at :51, content assertions read dist/main, dist/renderer, dist/preload at :219-221), and .github/actions/arm64-build/action.yml step ordering.\nDefect: the content assertions (telemetry hostnames, baked key shapes, upstream releases URL, CSP, CORS shim) all scan `apps/desktop/dist`, which is the INPUT to electron-builder, not the packaged app.asar that actually ships. Only the app-update.yml assertion looks at release/. In action.yml, \"Build Windows ARM64 installer\" runs BEFORE \"Verify cloud severance\", so a repair can insert a step after packaging that scrubs the offending strings out of dist/ (e.g. a sed over dist/**/*.js). That inserted step contains no `scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs` substring, so collect() never picks it up, the freeze check sees no change, SEVERANCE_COUNT stays 1, and FROZEN_GATE_PATHS does not cover action.yml. Both gates then pass on scrubbed dist/ while the published installer still contains the telemetry key. Composite-action edits take effect in the same run's next attempt, so this needs no PAT.\nFix: assert over the artifact that is published. Add assertions that scan the packaged `release/*-unpacked/resources/app.asar` (a plain `grep -a -F`-style byte scan over the asar is sufficient and is known to work — the installed app.asar was scanned this way successfully). Keep the existing dist/ assertions as well; they are still useful. IMPORTANT: adding assertions changes the assertion count, and the frozen workflow now checks `s.assertionsPassed !== EXPECTED` in THREE places (build-arm64.yml around :171, :360, :546). Update EXPECTED in all three in lockstep and make the mismatch message explain the lockstep requirement. Also ensure the new assertions fail loud rather than vacuously when the asar is missing or empty.\n\n--- MINOR m1: .claude/ hook persistence ---\nFile: scripts/ci-repair.sh around line 224.\nDefect: only `.claude/settings.local.json` is swept before `git add -A`. `.claude/settings.json` and `.claude/hooks/` are neither removed nor in FROZEN_GATE_PATHS. An agent can write `.claude/settings.json` with a PreToolUse hook; it gets staged, passes every freeze check, and rides onto the candidate branch. The NEXT night's repair agent — in a job holding PUSH_TOKEN — executes that hook on its first tool call.\nFix: sweep all of `.claude/`, or add `.claude/**` to FROZEN_GATE_PATHS so any repair introducing it fails loud. Prefer the FROZEN_GATE_PATHS route so it is loud rather than silent, but make sure a legitimately pre-existing .claude/ in the repo does not trip it spuriously — check whether one exists and handle it correctly.\n\n--- MINOR m2: TRUSTED_BASE_SHA fails open ---\nFile: scripts/ci-repair.sh around line 210.\nDefect: when TRUSTED_BASE_SHA is empty, BASE_SHA falls back to `$STATE_DIR/base-sha`, a RUNNER_TEMP file the agent step can rewrite. Fail-open where the design requires fail-closed. Unreachable in current CI (step-level env always sets it) but goes live the moment someone invokes `ci-repair.sh push` from a step that omits it.\nFix: `: \"${TRUSTED_BASE_SHA:?...}\"` with a clear message, and delete the fallback branch.\n\n--- VERIFICATION REQUIRED ---\n- `node scripts/check-feature-markers.mjs` → must end \"All 201 feature markers present.\"\n- `node scripts/check-dangerous-diagnostics.mjs` → must end \"(REFERR-GATE) clean\".\n- `& \"C:\\Program Files\\Git\\bin\\bash.exe\" -n scripts/ci-repair.sh` → clean.\n- Validate the workflow YAML parses (python3 + PyYAML, or node) — all three enforcement steps and both push steps must still be well-formed.\n- Build a tamper matrix under `tmp/` proving EACH of B1, B2, M1, M2, m1, m2 now fails loud. For M1 specifically, construct the delete-from-attempt-3-and-duplicate-elsewhere shape and show it is now caught. For M2, construct a scrubbed-dist-but-dirty-asar fixture and show the gate catches it. Report the matrix results.\n- Do NOT commit. Do NOT push. Leave everything in the working tree.\n\nYour final message is the entire report: what you changed with file:line, the tamper-matrix results, verification output, and anything you could not do and why. Do not claim a fix works without having exercised it.","subagent_type":"claude"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2220,"cache_read_input_tokens":140047,"output_tokens":4833,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":840},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2220,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4833,"cache_read_input_tokens":140047,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2220,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2220},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"012a786f-69bc-40f9-a45d-d9a975b47393","timestamp":"2026-08-18T13:15:52.706Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
