{"parentUuid":"dfc3d9b3-79d7-4a8c-a9a6-a5fe2d11c6de","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAL2RGBmU39Q2dSpnWnj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The fix is well-reasoned — POSIX path untouched, Windows routed to the no-argument close, with a one-shot guard against double-dispose that goes beyond what I specified. Sending it for adversarial review."}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":709,"cache_read_input_tokens":223297,"output_tokens":2890,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":309},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":709,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2890,"cache_read_input_tokens":223297,"cache_creation_input_tokens":709,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":709},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"c35dd840-3174-4e23-b4f5-88da904c5340","timestamp":"2026-08-18T13:55:51.790Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"c35dd840-3174-4e23-b4f5-88da904c5340","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAL2RGBmU39Q2dSpnWnj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NhfW3ndTwUwVJ9oE6Bc2eF","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Review Windows pty daemon fixes","name":"pty-fix-review-fable","prompt":"Repo: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64 (owner's own Windows ARM64 fork of the Superset desktop app). REPOSITORY FILES ONLY — never read user home-directory shell profiles or credentials; out of scope per owner. Reading the app's own state dir (`~\\.superset\\host\\...`) and the installed app under `C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Programs\\@supersetdesktop\\` IS in scope.\n\nENV: the Bash tool is BROKEN (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: unexpected EOF`). Use the PowerShell tool. `node_modules` is NOT installed, so the test suite cannot run — do not claim you ran tests. Coreutils: `$env:PATH = \"C:\\Program Files\\Git\\usr\\bin;$env:PATH\"`.\n\nDO NOT quit, kill or restart the running Superset app or any of its processes — the owner has live terminals and this session runs inside it.\n\nREVIEW THIS DIFF (uncommitted working tree):\n    git diff -- packages/pty-daemon apps/desktop/src/main/pty-daemon FEATURES.md\nplus the new untracked file `packages/pty-daemon/src/crash-guard.ts` (read it directly).\nIGNORE changes to .github/workflows/build-arm64.yml, scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs and scripts/ci-repair.sh — different workstream, already reviewed.\n\n=== WHY THIS CHANGE EXISTS (confirmed root cause, verified on the live machine) ===\nnode-pty's Windows implementation throws when given a signal:\n    WindowsTerminal.prototype.kill = function (signal) {\n        this._deferNoArgs(function () {\n            if (signal) { throw new Error('Signals not supported on windows.'); }\n            _this._close(); ... });\n    };\n    WindowsTerminal.prototype._deferNoArgs = function (fn) {\n        if (this._isReady) { fn.call(this); return; }      // SYNCHRONOUS\n        this._deferreds.push({ run: () => fn.call(this) }); // DEFERRED -> runs from a socket event\n    };\nThe daemon passed a signal on every kill path (including a default of \"SIGHUP\", always truthy). When the terminal was not yet ready the throw fired later from the conpty agent's socket event as an UNCAUGHT EXCEPTION, killing the daemon process and every other session with it. The owner's `pty-daemon.log` contains two full uncaught-exception dumps with exactly that stack, ending in the `Node.js v24.18.0` process-death footer, plus 287 occurrences of `kill escalation for pid N: final ps failed, survivor state unknown` because `process-tree.ts` was POSIX-only (`ps -axo`, process groups) and cannot work on Windows.\n\n=== WHAT TO VERIFY, SKEPTICALLY ===\n1. POSIX BEHAVIOUR MUST BE PROVABLY UNCHANGED. This is the top priority — the fork runs Windows but the code is shared and upstream-owned. For every touched call site, confirm the non-Windows branch is byte-equivalent in effect to before: the SIGHUP-then-SIGKILL escalation, the verify rounds and backoff (KILL_VERIFY_DELAYS_MS), pgid/tty targeting, knownPgids, includeRoot semantics. Any behavioural drift on POSIX is a BLOCKER.\n2. THE ONE-SHOT GUARD. `killConptyOnce()` fires `term.kill()` at most once, justified by the claim that a second `_agent.kill()` double-disposes the conout worker and calls native kill on a freed handle from inside a promise, producing an unhandled rejection — i.e. another dead daemon. VERIFY that claim against the INSTALLED node-pty source (`...\\app.asar.unpacked\\node_modules\\node-pty\\windowsTerminal.js`, `windowsPtyAgent.js`, `windowsConoutConnection.js`). Then ask the harder question: if the FIRST close fails to actually terminate the shell, the guard now permanently blocks every later attempt in the escalation chain — is the process then leaked forever? Does the Windows tree-kill path independently reap it? Trace the escalation and say plainly whether a stuck shell can now survive indefinitely where it previously would not.\n3. THE WINDOWS TREE-KILL. Read the new code in `process-tree.ts` and any helper. Check: how it enumerates the tree; pid-reuse safety (is a candidate's parentage AND start-time verified before killing? `rootStartedAt` appears to be used — confirm it is actually enforced, not just carried); whether it can ever kill something outside the session's subtree (a catastrophic failure mode — killing the user's other processes); behaviour when the root is already gone; and whether `survivor state unknown` now reports honestly on Windows instead of always claiming failure.\n4. THE UNCAUGHT-EXCEPTION GUARD (`crash-guard.ts`, wired into `packages/pty-daemon/src/main.ts` and `apps/desktop/src/main/pty-daemon/index.ts`). Judge the exit-vs-continue decision. A handler that swallows genuinely fatal state is as dangerous as no handler; one that exits defeats the purpose. Check it logs the FULL stack to the daemon log, that it cannot itself throw, that it does not mask a corrupt-state crash, and that unhandledRejection is covered too (the one-shot-guard rationale above is specifically about an unhandled REJECTION, so if only uncaughtException is handled that is a gap).\n5. CONCURRENCY. Multiple sessions killed at once; a session exiting normally while its kill chain runs; `pendingKills` / `drainPendingKills` interaction with the new guards; the daemon's `process.exit()` shutdown path racing an in-flight Windows tree-kill.\n6. FEATURES.md markers `(WIN-PTY-KILL-NOSIGNAL)`, `(WIN-PROCESS-TREE)`, `(DAEMON-UNCAUGHT-GUARD)` — confirm each token genuinely appears at the code sites claimed and that the descriptions are accurate rather than aspirational. The marker gate reports 205 present; verify they are not vacuous.\n7. Anything that hardcodes an upstream incidental (chunk filenames, node-pty internal field names like `_isReady`/`_deferreds`, path layouts). Depending on node-pty PRIVATE internals is a real risk to flag — note where the fix relies on undocumented behaviour and how it would fail on a node-pty upgrade.\n\nRank findings BLOCKER / MAJOR / MINOR with file:line and a concrete failure scenario each. Verify every finding against the real code before reporting — no speculative findings. If it is clean, say so plainly. Your final message is the entire report.","subagent_type":"code-reviewer-fable"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":709,"cache_read_input_tokens":223297,"output_tokens":2890,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":309},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":709,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2890,"cache_read_input_tokens":223297,"cache_creation_input_tokens":709,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":709},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"bb8ce229-ec2f-408a-aa8d-21b7e8817b78","timestamp":"2026-08-18T13:56:23.261Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"bb8ce229-ec2f-408a-aa8d-21b7e8817b78","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"51b620fc-5d74-4e89-a4b6-b504e385424e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NhfW3ndTwUwVJ9oE6Bc2eF","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Spawned successfully. (This tool result is internal metadata — never quote or paste any part of it, including the ID below, into a user-facing reply.)\nagent_id: pty-fix-review-fable@session-92794baa\nname: pty-fix-review-fable\nThe agent is now running and will receive instructions via mailbox."}]}]},"uuid":"af6cfcfd-2b6c-4c30-b545-b4c82c63fce0","timestamp":"2026-08-18T13:57:25.869Z","toolUseResult":{"status":"teammate_spawned","prompt":"Repo: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64 (owner's own Windows ARM64 fork of the Superset desktop app). REPOSITORY FILES ONLY — never read user home-directory shell profiles or credentials; out of scope per owner. Reading the app's own state dir (`~\\.superset\\host\\...`) and the installed app under `C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Programs\\@supersetdesktop\\` IS in scope.\n\nENV: the Bash tool is BROKEN (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: unexpected EOF`). Use the PowerShell tool. `node_modules` is NOT installed, so the test suite cannot run — do not claim you ran tests. Coreutils: `$env:PATH = \"C:\\Program Files\\Git\\usr\\bin;$env:PATH\"`.\n\nDO NOT quit, kill or restart the running Superset app or any of its processes — the owner has live terminals and this session runs inside it.\n\nREVIEW THIS DIFF (uncommitted working tree):\n    git diff -- packages/pty-daemon apps/desktop/src/main/pty-daemon FEATURES.md\nplus the new untracked file `packages/pty-daemon/src/crash-guard.ts` (read it directly).\nIGNORE changes to .github/workflows/build-arm64.yml, scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs and scripts/ci-repair.sh — different workstream, already reviewed.\n\n=== WHY THIS CHANGE EXISTS (confirmed root cause, verified on the live machine) ===\nnode-pty's Windows implementation throws when given a signal:\n    WindowsTerminal.prototype.kill = function (signal) {\n        this._deferNoArgs(function () {\n            if (signal) { throw new Error('Signals not supported on windows.'); }\n            _this._close(); ... });\n    };\n    WindowsTerminal.prototype._deferNoArgs = function (fn) {\n        if (this._isReady) { fn.call(this); return; }      // SYNCHRONOUS\n        this._deferreds.push({ run: () => fn.call(this) }); // DEFERRED -> runs from a socket event\n    };\nThe daemon passed a signal on every kill path (including a default of \"SIGHUP\", always truthy). When the terminal was not yet ready the throw fired later from the conpty agent's socket event as an UNCAUGHT EXCEPTION, killing the daemon process and every other session with it. The owner's `pty-daemon.log` contains two full uncaught-exception dumps with exactly that stack, ending in the `Node.js v24.18.0` process-death footer, plus 287 occurrences of `kill escalation for pid N: final ps failed, survivor state unknown` because `process-tree.ts` was POSIX-only (`ps -axo`, process groups) and cannot work on Windows.\n\n=== WHAT TO VERIFY, SKEPTICALLY ===\n1. POSIX BEHAVIOUR MUST BE PROVABLY UNCHANGED. This is the top priority — the fork runs Windows but the code is shared and upstream-owned. For every touched call site, confirm the non-Windows branch is byte-equivalent in effect to before: the SIGHUP-then-SIGKILL escalation, the verify rounds and backoff (KILL_VERIFY_DELAYS_MS), pgid/tty targeting, knownPgids, includeRoot semantics. Any behavioural drift on POSIX is a BLOCKER.\n2. THE ONE-SHOT GUARD. `killConptyOnce()` fires `term.kill()` at most once, justified by the claim that a second `_agent.kill()` double-disposes the conout worker and calls native kill on a freed handle from inside a promise, producing an unhandled rejection — i.e. another dead daemon. VERIFY that claim against the INSTALLED node-pty source (`...\\app.asar.unpacked\\node_modules\\node-pty\\windowsTerminal.js`, `windowsPtyAgent.js`, `windowsConoutConnection.js`). Then ask the harder question: if the FIRST close fails to actually terminate the shell, the guard now permanently blocks every later attempt in the escalation chain — is the process then leaked forever? Does the Windows tree-kill path independently reap it? Trace the escalation and say plainly whether a stuck shell can now survive indefinitely where it previously would not.\n3. THE WINDOWS TREE-KILL. Read the new code in `process-tree.ts` and any helper. Check: how it enumerates the tree; pid-reuse safety (is a candidate's parentage AND start-time verified before killing? `rootStartedAt` appears to be used — confirm it is actually enforced, not just carried); whether it can ever kill something outside the session's subtree (a catastrophic failure mode — killing the user's other processes); behaviour when the root is already gone; and whether `survivor state unknown` now reports honestly on Windows instead of always claiming failure.\n4. THE UNCAUGHT-EXCEPTION GUARD (`crash-guard.ts`, wired into `packages/pty-daemon/src/main.ts` and `apps/desktop/src/main/pty-daemon/index.ts`). Judge the exit-vs-continue decision. A handler that swallows genuinely fatal state is as dangerous as no handler; one that exits defeats the purpose. Check it logs the FULL stack to the daemon log, that it cannot itself throw, that it does not mask a corrupt-state crash, and that unhandledRejection is covered too (the one-shot-guard rationale above is specifically about an unhandled REJECTION, so if only uncaughtException is handled that is a gap).\n5. CONCURRENCY. Multiple sessions killed at once; a session exiting normally while its kill chain runs; `pendingKills` / `drainPendingKills` interaction with the new guards; the daemon's `process.exit()` shutdown path racing an in-flight Windows tree-kill.\n6. FEATURES.md markers `(WIN-PTY-KILL-NOSIGNAL)`, `(WIN-PROCESS-TREE)`, `(DAEMON-UNCAUGHT-GUARD)` — confirm each token genuinely appears at the code sites claimed and that the descriptions are accurate rather than aspirational. The marker gate reports 205 present; verify they are not vacuous.\n7. Anything that hardcodes an upstream incidental (chunk filenames, node-pty internal field names like `_isReady`/`_deferreds`, path layouts). Depending on node-pty PRIVATE internals is a real risk to flag — note where the fix relies on undocumented behaviour and how it would fail on a node-pty upgrade.\n\nRank findings BLOCKER / MAJOR / MINOR with file:line and a concrete failure scenario each. Verify every finding against the real code before reporting — no speculative findings. If it is clean, say so plainly. Your final message is the entire report.","teammate_id":"pty-fix-review-fable@session-92794baa","agent_id":"pty-fix-review-fable@session-92794baa","agent_type":"code-reviewer-fable","model":"fable","name":"pty-fix-review-fable","color":"yellow","tmux_session_name":"in-process","tmux_window_name":"in-process","tmux_pane_id":"in-process","team_name":"session-92794baa","is_splitpane":false,"plan_mode_required":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bb8ce229-ec2f-408a-aa8d-21b7e8817b78","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"af6cfcfd-2b6c-4c30-b545-b4c82c63fce0","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14846414 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"3ca48104-7f5e-4e88-8e9b-070d526cb417","timestamp":"2026-08-18T13:57:25.899Z","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"3ca48104-7f5e-4e88-8e9b-070d526cb417","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAL9tbBUTb1mCa6hH4rE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Windows terminal fix is implemented and passing gates. What landed:\n\n- **The kill path now splits by platform.** POSIX keeps its exact signal escalation; Windows routes to node-pty's no-argument close, which is the only form that doesn't throw.\n- **A one-shot guard** the implementer added beyond my brief, with a good rationale: a second close would dispose the console worker twice and call native kill on a freed handle from inside a promise — producing an unhandled rejection and *another* dead daemon. Worth having, though I've asked the reviewer to check the flip side: if the first close fails to kill the shell, does the guard now block every later attempt and leak the process?\n- **A real Windows process-tree implementation**, replacing the POSIX-only version that produced those 287 `survivor state unknown` lines.\n- **A crash guard** in the daemon entry so one bad session can't silently destroy every other session.\n\nGates: **205 feature markers** (four new, all verified present) and **REFERR clean** across all seven packages.\n\nFable is now reviewing it adversarially. The two things I flagged hardest: that POSIX behaviour must be provably unchanged, since this is shared upstream code and only Windows is broken; and that the new tree-kill must never reach outside a session's own subtree, since killing the wrong process tree would be far worse than the bug it fixes. I also asked it to check pid-reuse safety, because a recycled process ID is exactly how that failure would happen.\n\nStill outstanding: the CI hardening implementation, and the daemon-death diagnosis that needs your next app restart."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3022,"cache_read_input_tokens":224006,"output_tokens":521,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3022,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":521,"cache_read_input_tokens":224006,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3022,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3022},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"6c1d0342-96c2-4206-974c-396555648093","timestamp":"2026-08-18T13:57:35.988Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
