{"parentUuid":"9b7604e8-5eb5-4da8-abd0-1268493028fc","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAPDcn8gQh879vPAMYdk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tests running in background. Meanwhile, rather than keep waiting on the CI implementer's self-assessment, I'm having an independent agent try to break the fixes — better evidence than the author grading its own work."}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":465,"cache_read_input_tokens":275756,"output_tokens":2886,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":287},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":465,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2886,"cache_read_input_tokens":275756,"cache_creation_input_tokens":465,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":465},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"c0279299-92c3-4ba1-b8b9-e9f6ff6e9b8a","timestamp":"2026-08-18T14:37:45.449Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"c0279299-92c3-4ba1-b8b9-e9f6ff6e9b8a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAPDcn8gQh879vPAMYdk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01B3z3cTvUwmK9EhrcoxXGWC","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Independently attack CI gate fixes","model":"opus","name":"ci-tamper-matrix-opus","prompt":"Repo: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64 (owner's own private fork). REPOSITORY FILES ONLY — never read user home-directory shell profiles or credentials; out of scope per owner.\n\nENV: the Bash tool is BROKEN (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: unexpected EOF`). Use the PowerShell tool. node_modules IS installed. Coreutils + bash binary: `$env:PATH = \"C:\\Program Files\\Git\\usr\\bin;$env:PATH\"` and `& \"C:\\Program Files\\Git\\bin\\bash.exe\"`. Python with PyYAML is available (`python -c \"import yaml\"`).\n\nDO NOT quit/kill/restart the running Superset app — the owner has live terminals and this session runs inside it. Leave alone: detached watcher pid 38312 and tmp/daemon-death-watch.log. Do NOT commit or push. Do NOT modify tracked files — build all fixtures under `tmp/tamper/` and clean up after.\n\nYOUR JOB: adversarially verify, by EXECUTION rather than reading, that six recently-implemented CI-integrity fixes actually block the attacks they claim to. Someone else implemented them; you are the independent check. Assume they are broken until a test proves otherwise. Report honestly — a fix that does not actually block its attack is the most valuable thing you can find.\n\nCONTEXT. This fork's CI has a bounded automated self-repair loop: on build failure an AI agent edits the checked-out tree and pushes to a candidate branch; the next attempt builds that sha. The agent reads build logs, which are untrusted input, so the design treats it as potentially hostile. Required property: an automated repair must never publish a GitHub Release that violates the cloud-severance gate (scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs). This is ordinary release-integrity engineering on the owner's own repo — the same class of control as a required status check.\n\nTHE SIX FIXES TO ATTACK (all in the working tree, uncommitted):\n\nB1 — .github/workflows/build-arm64.yml, the three \"Enforce cloud severance\" steps (~:142-155, ~:441-454, ~:737-750). They now `unset BASH_ENV ENV NODE_OPTIONS NODE_PATH`, pin PATH, resolve node absolutely, AND fail loud if BASH_ENV/ENV was set on entry (treating it as tampering rather than quietly cleaning).\n   ATTACK: write BASH_ENV to $GITHUB_ENV from an earlier repairable step and verify the enforcement step now dies loudly instead of sourcing your code and exiting 0. Also try ENV, NODE_PATH, and a poisoned PATH supplying a fake `node`. Confirm the absolute-node resolution actually defeats a PATH-supplied node.\n\nB2 — same file, the \"Validate + push repair\" steps (~:353-356, ~:649-652). Same sanitization, in the shell that holds PUSH_TOKEN.\n   ATTACK: same vectors. Critically, verify the tamper check runs BEFORE anything that could leak the token, and that a poisoned PATH cannot substitute git/bash/python3 for the validator.\n\nM1 — scripts/ci-repair.sh: step extraction now keys by job name AND index (~:503) and asserts placement (~:509): every job containing a publish step must contain exactly one enforcement step BEFORE it.\n   ATTACK: the exact shape that defeated the old code — delete the enforcement step from `build-attempt-3` and add a byte-identical duplicate elsewhere. Then: move an enforcement step to AFTER the publish step in the same job; rename a job; reorder jobs; add a fourth identical enforcement step; use YAML anchors/aliases/merge keys/flow style to express a step differently but equivalently; duplicate mapping keys. Each must fail loud.\n\nM2 — scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs now extracts the NSIS installer (7z, resolved from PINNED ABSOLUTE paths ~:355-361) and scans the extracted payload, in addition to dist/.\n   ATTACK: the three scrub windows. (a) telemetry string present in dist/ → must fail. (b) dist/ scrubbed AFTER packaging but the packaged app.asar still dirty → must fail. (c) app.asar scrubbed after the installer was built, installer still dirty → must fail; this is the one only installer extraction can catch. Build synthetic fixtures rather than a real 2GB build — a small NSIS-shaped or 7z-shaped fixture is fine if it exercises the same code path; if you cannot construct a faithful installer fixture, say so plainly rather than claiming coverage. Also verify: missing 7z → fails loud, not silently skipped; empty/corrupt extraction → fails loud; a `7z` planted earlier on PATH is NOT used.\n\nm1 — scripts/ci-repair.sh: `.claude` and `.mcp.json` are in FROZEN_GATE_PATHS (~:104-119), with untracked `.claude/` swept before `git add -A` (~:318-322).\n   ATTACK: have a simulated repair add `.claude/settings.json` with a PreToolUse hook, and separately modify a TRACKED file under `.claude/`. First must be swept, second must fail loud. Verify a legitimately pre-existing `.claude/` in the repo does not trip it spuriously — check whether one exists.\n\nm2 — scripts/ci-repair.sh: `: \"${TRUSTED_BASE_SHA:?...}\"` (~:296) plus a cross-check that fails loud if it disagrees with the RUNNER_TEMP `base-sha` (~:305).\n   ATTACK: invoke the push path with TRUSTED_BASE_SHA unset → must fail loud, never fall back. Then set it to disagree with the state file → must fail loud.\n\nALSO WORTH PROBING (report if you find anything): can any of these checks be satisfied vacuously — an empty artifact set, a skipped packaging step, a zero-length file, a missing directory that reads as \"clean\"? The gate is supposed to refuse to pass rather than pass on absence.\n\nMETHOD: run things. Drive `scripts/ci-repair.sh` directly with a synthetic STATE_DIR and fixture workflow files; run the gate with crafted fixture trees; use `bash -n`, `python -c \"import yaml...\"` and `node` as needed. For each of the ~20 attack shapes report: SHAPE / COMMAND RUN / ACTUAL OUTPUT / BLOCKED or NOT BLOCKED. Do not claim a shape is blocked unless you actually executed it and saw it fail loud. If a shape is untestable locally, mark it UNTESTED and say why — that is a legitimate result and far better than a fabricated pass.\n\nYour final message is the entire report: the matrix, any shape that got through, and your overall verdict on whether this batch is safe to commit.","subagent_type":"claude"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":465,"cache_read_input_tokens":275756,"output_tokens":2886,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":287},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":465,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2886,"cache_read_input_tokens":275756,"cache_creation_input_tokens":465,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":465},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"00e5eeb6-ffce-4d60-bc53-16797889e474","timestamp":"2026-08-18T14:38:17.953Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
