{"parentUuid":"62b6ba3e-8bab-4c8a-9049-3a5bfa6c3d20","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"51b620fc-5d74-4e89-a4b6-b504e385424e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SMNk2RiYtXDZ6W8vyJv7Zh","type":"tool_result","content":"=== FEATURES.md diff (must be pty-only) ===\r\ndiff --git a/FEATURES.md b/FEATURES.md\nindex 112cfd9315..52c98146de 100644\n--- a/FEATURES.md\n+++ b/FEATURES.md\n@@ -102,6 +102,8 @@ in the merge that drops it (the only legitimate way a marker leaves this list).\n | Cloud severance phase 1 (telemetry / update / notices) | this fork does not phone home. FOUR channels are cut at once and each is enforced at build time, not by configuration a merge can flip back. (1) **Telemetry keys are BANNED**: `assertNoTelemetryKeys` runs immediately after the repo-root `.env` load in `electron.vite.config.ts` and THROWS if `NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY`, `SENTRY_DSN_DESKTOP`, `SENTRY_DSN_HOST_SERVICE` or `SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN` is set — a throw rather than a silent force-empty, because quietly rewriting a value the maintainer put in `.env` hides the disagreement instead of surfacing it. Banning `SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN` also disarms the two build-time sourcemap-UPLOAD plugins, themselves a phone-home to upstream's Sentry org. A `.env` carrying ports/URLs/workspace name keeps working. (2) **The bundled CLI's per-command ping is gutted**: `trackCommandInvoked` posted `api.analytics.captureEvent` to `SUPERSET_API_URL` on EVERY command via `commands/middleware.ts` — the one genuinely live per-command sink — and is now an empty body. Its signature, export and call site are unchanged on purpose: the middleware's `resolveAuth` chain is what the bundled `superset-cli` preset and the desktop startup shim depend on. (3) **Auto-update is hard-disabled**, layered: `FORK_AUTO_UPDATE_DISABLED` early-returns from `setupAutoUpdater`, `checkForUpdates` and `checkForUpdatesInteractive` INSIDE `auto-updater.ts` (call sites in `main/index.ts` churn on every merge, so the kill cannot live there); the feed constant is repointed from upstream's releases to the fork's; `electron-builder.ts` sets `publish: null` so no `app-update.yml` naming `superset-sh` is baked into the package even under `--publish never`; and ALL THREE \"Check for Updates\" entries — macOS app menu, tray, and the command palette's `actions.checkUpdates` — now permanent no-ops, are relabelled \"Download Latest Release\" and open the fork's releases page rather than being deleted or left as dead surfaces. They share ONE constant, `RELEASES_URL` in `shared/auto-update.ts`, which upstream pointed at `superset-sh/superset/releases` and which is repointed to the fork: it had no consumer at all before this, so a merge that started using it would have silently sent Windows ARM64 users to upstream's x64 build. Before this, only `IS_AUTO_UPDATE_PLATFORM` (mac/linux) stood between our Windows ARM64 users and upstream's x64 releases: one upstream line flip. (4) **CSP + CORS shim tightened surgically**: `posthog.com`, `sentry.io` and `sentry-ipc:` are gone from both `script-src` and `connect-src`, and the Windows CORS shim's `*.posthog.com` / `*.sentry.io` / `app.outlit.ai` patterns are deleted. `ws:`/`wss:`/`http://127.0.0.1:*` (terminals + host-service — removing these kills every terminal), `blob:`/`data:` (file previews) and the relay/api/electric entries are deliberately UNTOUCHED. `scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs` proves all of it against the BUILT artifact set (whole set, never named chunks — see SCREENREADER-GUARD-DRIFT), and that set deliberately includes the BUNDLED CLI: it ships as a compiled bun binary (`dist/resources/bin/superset.exe`), not a `.js` chunk, so the bundle scans cannot see it and its bytes are scanned separately for the analytics route name — otherwise the CLI channel would be the one phase-1 change enforced by source review alone. (`api.superset.sh` IS still in that binary and must be: the CLI's auth chain legitimately targets it in phase 1.) The gate also asserts the string `/api/desktop/version` appears in NO artifact: the poll-off is the one phase-1 behaviour whose markers live only in comments, so without that assertion a merge restoring the fetch would keep every marker and pass every other gate (api.superset.sh is phase-1-legitimate, so no hostname check can catch it). The gate is phase-scoped via an explicit `PHASE` constant because api/electric/streams/relay are still legitimately baked, and is frozen against build-repair edits alongside its allowlist. ENFORCEMENT does not rest on that freeze: the deciding invocation lives in `.github/workflows/build-arm64.yml`, which GitHub freezes for the whole run and which a mid-run repair therefore cannot reach, and the gate must leave a STAMP recording the sha it validated, the mode it ran in and how many assertions actually executed — the workflow re-checks all three against the sha it built, `mode=full`, and a floor of 11, so \"the gate ran\" is a fact the script has to produce rather than a string in an editable step. The floor is EXACT, not a lower bound with slack: a missing `apps/desktop/release` is a FAILURE rather than a skipped check, because a floor a skipped-packaging run can satisfy is not a floor. `--allow-unpackaged` exists for local post-compile runs and stamps `mode=partial`, which the workflow rejects, so the convenience cannot become a CI bypass. A failing gate writes no stamp at all. Publishing was moved OUT of the composite action to sit after that enforcement, because as its last step a neutered gate would have published before anything frozen could object. `ci-repair.sh` additionally requires the severance step blocks in both files to be byte-identical to the pre-repair blob, which admits none of `|| true`, `continue-on-error`, `if: false`, echo-wrapping or a duplicated weakened copy. Its deliberately-unasserted gap is documented in-file: a blanket `posthog.com`/`sentry.io` artifact scan cannot pass while the SDKs are statically imported (posthog-js by 8 renderer modules incl. a feature flag, `@sentry/electron` by 5, `@sentry/node` by 3), and both are INERT after the key ban — but NOT because they are unreachable, and the distinction is load-bearing: `PostHogProvider` DOES call `initPostHog()` on every boot, and `initSentry()` runs in both main and renderer. What makes them inert is the KEY/DSN GATE each one opens with (`renderer/lib/posthog.ts` returns before `posthog.init()` on an empty `NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY`; both Sentry inits return without `SENTRY_DSN_DESKTOP`), combined with the fact that the value can never be non-empty — the vite `define` block bakes it at build time so no runtime env can arm it, and the build-time ban makes baking one impossible. A later phase must NOT delete those gates believing init is unreachable. Removing the imports is a later phase, not a silent widening of this one | `(CLOUD-SEVERANCE-P1)` |\n | Egress fence (log-only) | a fork-owned `onBeforeRequest` observer on `session.fromPartition(\"persist:superset\")`, installed in the boot path immediately after `app.whenReady()` and BEFORE any window exists — `MainWindow()` calls `assertEgressFenceInstalled()` and THROWS if it did not run, so the ordering is a runtime proof rather than a comment asking the next person to preserve it. It CANCELS NOTHING by design: this phase exists to prove the allowlist is complete before a later phase starts blocking. It logs ORIGIN ONLY (`scheme://host:port`) plus method, resourceType, initiator class and a first-seen timestamp with a count — never a full URL, path, query or fragment, because the companion pairing secret travels in a URL FRAGMENT and other URLs carry bearer tokens; an unparseable URL is recorded as `<unparseable>` rather than echoed. Browser-pane webviews are EXEMPT: `persist:superset` is also the `<webview>` partition (`usePersistentWebview.ts`, `browserRuntimeRegistry.ts`), so arbitrary user browsing arrives on the same listener and is counted only as a bare total — logging those origins would make this a browsing-history file and drown the app's own egress. The exemption is an ALLOWLIST, not a denylist: an origin is logged ONLY for a request positively attributed to a non-webview webContents, and everything unattributable is counted-and-dropped under a second bare total. That is not pedantry — a site in a browser pane that registers a SERVICE WORKER (any PWA) fetches on this partition with NO webContentsId at all, and a webview's in-flight requests can complete after it is destroyed; both would otherwise be classed \"unknown\" and have their origins logged, leaking exactly the browsing the exemption protects and able to flood the entry cap with it. The accepted cost, stated plainly: unattributable APP traffic (a main-process `net.fetch` on this session) is invisible here too, and the counter is the honest measure of what went unseen. Initiator is resolved via `webContents.fromId`, which must tolerate an absent or destroyed id without throwing since the callback runs on every request. Dedupe is in-memory and BOUNDED (512 keys); at the cap it drops and says so loudly rather than evicting silently, because an eviction would make the log lie. Honestly scoped in-file: it sees session webRequests only — main-process fetch, electron-updater, `@sentry/electron`'s main transport (renderer Sentry too, IPCMode.Classic), the host-service child, the bundled CLI, spawned agent CLIs and the pty-daemon ALL bypass it, so a clean fence log proves the RENDERER is quiet, not the app. The build-time gate covers what this listener cannot reach | `(EGRESS-FENCE)` |\n | Network logging is OFF unless asked for | upstream calls `netLog.startLogging` unconditionally at boot with `captureMode: \"includeSensitive\"` and a 1 GB cap, retaining 3 sessions, which records the app's FULL network traffic — URLs with query strings and fragments, request and response headers, cookies — as plain JSON under `%APPDATA%/Superset/network-logs`. On this machine one session file reached 1,014 MB: bearer tokens, the Cloudflare Access cookie and potentially the companion pairing code (which travels in a URL FRAGMENT) sitting on disk in cleartext, which is precisely what `(EGRESS-FENCE)` refuses to write when it logs ORIGIN ONLY. The logger is now OFF by default and starts only when `SUPERSET_NETWORK_LOG` is set to `1`/`true`; otherwise it prints one line naming the variable and returns with `started` false, so `stopNetworkLogger()` stays a safe no-op. The gate lives INSIDE `startNetworkLogger()`, not at the boot call site in `main/index.ts` — that sequence churns on every upstream merge and a kill placed there is one conflict resolution away from vanishing (same reasoning as `FORK_AUTO_UPDATE_DISABLED`). When it IS enabled the blast radius is cut: `captureMode` is `default` (no cookies, no authorization headers, no bodies) and the cap is 64 MB rather than 1 GB. **`includeSensitive` is reachable by NO environment variable** — a second opt-in would rebuild the exact footgun this removes, so restoring it is a code change someone reviews, and a test parses every `captureMode:` value out of the module's source and asserts none of them is `includeSensitive` (it also requires at least one match, so the check cannot pass by finding nothing). Every pre-existing file is deleted at boot whether or not logging is enabled this run, because all of it was written in the sensitive mode: only `current.json` and `session-*.json` inside that directory, never the directory itself, fail-soft (a file the running app holds open is counted and reported, never thrown at boot). A `default`-mode log still contains full request URLs, so the enable path says so and tells the maintainer to delete the file afterwards | `(NETLOG-OFF)` |\n+| Windows PTY kill never passes a signal | node-pty's `WindowsTerminal.kill(signal)` throws `Signals not supported on windows.` for ANY truthy argument, and it throws from inside its deferred queue — which, when the terminal is not yet ready, is drained from the conpty agent's socket `data` event. That throw lands on node-pty's stack, not the caller's, so no `try`/`catch` and no promise `.catch` can see it: it is an UNCAUGHT EXCEPTION that kills the pty-daemon and, with it, every terminal in every workspace on the machine (captured live in the owner's `pty-daemon.log`: two full crash dumps with the `Socket.emit → Object.run → Array.forEach → WindowsTerminal` stack). The daemon passed a signal on every kill path (`kill(signal ?? \"SIGHUP\")` defaults to a truthy one), so this was reachable from an ordinary session close. All node-pty kills now funnel through `NodePtyAdapter.killTerm` / the raw-term branch of `spawn`, where Windows takes the no-argument path (close the conpty + terminate its console process list) and POSIX passes its signal completely unchanged — `UnixTerminal.kill` is a real `process.kill(pid, signal)` and the SIGHUP-then-SIGKILL escalation depends on it. The Windows conpty kill is a CLOSE, not a repeatable signal, so it is one-shot-guarded: `WindowsTerminal.destroy()` is that same deferred no-argument kill, and firing both would dispose the conout worker twice and call the native kill on a freed handle from inside a promise (an unhandled rejection = another dead daemon). `(DAEMON-UNCAUGHT-GUARD)` is the containment behind the fix: the daemon entry (package + desktop bundle mirror) installs `uncaughtException`/`unhandledRejection` handlers that log the full stack to the daemon log and KEEP RUNNING, because this process is the durable owner of PTY master fds and a restart cannot recover them — the realistic fault population is per-session, so surviving costs one broken session while exiting costs all of them. Repeated faults are the exception to the exception: more than 5 in 60s is no longer a per-session fault, so it exits non-zero for a clean supervisor restart | `(WIN-PTY-KILL-NOSIGNAL)`, `(DAEMON-UNCAUGHT-GUARD)` |\n+| Windows process-tree kill | `process-tree.ts` was POSIX-only — it shells out to `ps -axo pid=,ppid=,pgid=,tty=,stat=` and signals process GROUPS, none of which exist on Windows. Killing a session there signalled only the root pid and ORPHANED the entire shell subtree, permanently and invisibly: the owner's `pty-daemon.log` carried 287 `final ps failed, survivor state unknown` lines, one per leaked subtree. Windows now enumerates `Win32_Process` (Windows PowerShell 5.1 + `Get-CimInstance`; `wmic` is removed from current Windows 11 builds) and walks ppid to terminate the real tree. Chosen over `taskkill /PID <pid> /T /F` because taskkill resolves the same ppid field with NO creation-time check, so a recycled pid whose stale `ParentProcessId` names our root is killed as if it were ours, with no way for us to see or report it. Pid reuse is guarded by start times: the root's is captured at session open (the Windows analogue of the pgid/tty capture) and re-checked on every pass, so a root pid that has been recycled targets NOTHING; a candidate child must have started at or after its parent (a process that predates its \"parent\" is an orphan on a recycled pid); and a candidate whose start time is unreadable is never targeted — the kill fails safe and leaks a process, which the survivor report then names. There is deliberately NO synchronous Windows enumerator: a blocking PowerShell spawn freezes the daemon's only event loop and stalls every session's IO, so `readProcessTable()` warns ONCE and returns empty (UNKNOWN, never \"no descendants\") and the whole reap lives in the async escalation chain — which on Windows therefore also runs for a SIGKILL request (immediately, no grace delay), since POSIX's synchronous volley has no counterpart there. The survivor report is accurate instead of always claiming failure | `(WIN-PROCESS-TREE)` |\n \n ## Machine-readable markers (the nightly gate reads this block)\n \n@@ -310,4 +312,8 @@ FORK_AUTO_UPDATE_DISABLED\tapps/desktop/src/main/lib/auto-updater.ts\n (NETLOG-OFF)\tapps/desktop/src/main/network-logger/policy.ts\n (NETLOG-OFF)\tapps/desktop/src/main/network-logger/policy.test.ts\n SUPERSET_NETWORK_LOG\tapps/desktop/src/main/network-logger/policy.ts\n+(WIN-PTY-KILL-NOSIGNAL)\tpackages/pty-daemon\n+(WIN-PROCESS-TREE)\tpackages/pty-daemon\n+(DAEMON-UNCAUGHT-GUARD)\tpackages/pty-daemon\n+(DAEMON-UNCAUGHT-GUARD)\tapps/desktop/src/main/pty-daemon\n ```\n\r\n=== untracked files ===\r\n\r\n?? packages/pty-daemon/src/crash-guard.test.ts\r\n?? packages/pty-daemon/src/crash-guard.ts","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"13920159-01a2-489f-ac6e-f6e5f44057a3","timestamp":"2026-08-18T14:40:05.062Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== FEATURES.md diff (must be pty-only) ===\r\ndiff --git a/FEATURES.md b/FEATURES.md\nindex 112cfd9315..52c98146de 100644\n--- a/FEATURES.md\n+++ b/FEATURES.md\n@@ -102,6 +102,8 @@ in the merge that drops it (the only legitimate way a marker leaves this list).\n | Cloud severance phase 1 (telemetry / update / notices) | this fork does not phone home. FOUR channels are cut at once and each is enforced at build time, not by configuration a merge can flip back. (1) **Telemetry keys are BANNED**: `assertNoTelemetryKeys` runs immediately after the repo-root `.env` load in `electron.vite.config.ts` and THROWS if `NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY`, `SENTRY_DSN_DESKTOP`, `SENTRY_DSN_HOST_SERVICE` or `SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN` is set — a throw rather than a silent force-empty, because quietly rewriting a value the maintainer put in `.env` hides the disagreement instead of surfacing it. Banning `SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN` also disarms the two build-time sourcemap-UPLOAD plugins, themselves a phone-home to upstream's Sentry org. A `.env` carrying ports/URLs/workspace name keeps working. (2) **The bundled CLI's per-command ping is gutted**: `trackCommandInvoked` posted `api.analytics.captureEvent` to `SUPERSET_API_URL` on EVERY command via `commands/middleware.ts` — the one genuinely live per-command sink — and is now an empty body. Its signature, export and call site are unchanged on purpose: the middleware's `resolveAuth` chain is what the bundled `superset-cli` preset and the desktop startup shim depend on. (3) **Auto-update is hard-disabled**, layered: `FORK_AUTO_UPDATE_DISABLED` early-returns from `setupAutoUpdater`, `checkForUpdates` and `checkForUpdatesInteractive` INSIDE `auto-updater.ts` (call sites in `main/index.ts` churn on every merge, so the kill cannot live there); the feed constant is repointed from upstream's releases to the fork's; `electron-builder.ts` sets `publish: null` so no `app-update.yml` naming `superset-sh` is baked into the package even under `--publish never`; and ALL THREE \"Check for Updates\" entries — macOS app menu, tray, and the command palette's `actions.checkUpdates` — now permanent no-ops, are relabelled \"Download Latest Release\" and open the fork's releases page rather than being deleted or left as dead surfaces. They share ONE constant, `RELEASES_URL` in `shared/auto-update.ts`, which upstream pointed at `superset-sh/superset/releases` and which is repointed to the fork: it had no consumer at all before this, so a merge that started using it would have silently sent Windows ARM64 users to upstream's x64 build. Before this, only `IS_AUTO_UPDATE_PLATFORM` (mac/linux) stood between our Windows ARM64 users and upstream's x64 releases: one upstream line flip. (4) **CSP + CORS shim tightened surgically**: `posthog.com`, `sentry.io` and `sentry-ipc:` are gone from both `script-src` and `connect-src`, and the Windows CORS shim's `*.posthog.com` / `*.sentry.io` / `app.outlit.ai` patterns are deleted. `ws:`/`wss:`/`http://127.0.0.1:*` (terminals + host-service — removing these kills every terminal), `blob:`/`data:` (file previews) and the relay/api/electric entries are deliberately UNTOUCHED. `scripts/check-cloud-severance.mjs` proves all of it against the BUILT artifact set (whole set, never named chunks — see SCREENREADER-GUARD-DRIFT), and that set deliberately includes the BUNDLED CLI: it ships as a compiled bun binary (`dist/resources/bin/superset.exe`), not a `.js` chunk, so the bundle scans cannot see it and its bytes are scanned separately for the analytics route name — otherwise the CLI channel would be the one phase-1 change enforced by source review alone. (`api.superset.sh` IS still in that binary and must be: the CLI's auth chain legitimately targets it in phase 1.) The gate also asserts the string `/api/desktop/version` appears in NO artifact: the poll-off is the one phase-1 behaviour whose markers live only in comments, so without that assertion a merge restoring the fetch would keep every marker and pass every other gate (api.superset.sh is phase-1-legitimate, so no hostname check can catch it). The gate is phase-scoped via an explicit `PHASE` constant because api/electric/streams/relay are still legitimately baked, and is frozen against build-repair edits alongside its allowlist. ENFORCEMENT does not rest on that freeze: the deciding invocation lives in `.github/workflows/build-arm64.yml`, which GitHub freezes for the whole run and which a mid-run repair therefore cannot reach, and the gate must leave a STAMP recording the sha it validated, the mode it ran in and how many assertions actually executed — the workflow re-checks all three against the sha it built, `mode=full`, and a floor of 11, so \"the gate ran\" is a fact the script has to produce rather than a string in an editable step. The floor is EXACT, not a lower bound with slack: a missing `apps/desktop/release` is a FAILURE rather than a skipped check, because a floor a skipped-packaging run can satisfy is not a floor. `--allow-unpackaged` exists for local post-compile runs and stamps `mode=partial`, which the workflow rejects, so the convenience cannot become a CI bypass. A failing gate writes no stamp at all. Publishing was moved OUT of the composite action to sit after that enforcement, because as its last step a neutered gate would have published before anything frozen could object. `ci-repair.sh` additionally requires the severance step blocks in both files to be byte-identical to the pre-repair blob, which admits none of `|| true`, `continue-on-error`, `if: false`, echo-wrapping or a duplicated weakened copy. Its deliberately-unasserted gap is documented in-file: a blanket `posthog.com`/`sentry.io` artifact scan cannot pass while the SDKs are statically imported (posthog-js by 8 renderer modules incl. a feature flag, `@sentry/electron` by 5, `@sentry/node` by 3), and both are INERT after the key ban — but NOT because they are unreachable, and the distinction is load-bearing: `PostHogProvider` DOES call `initPostHog()` on every boot, and `initSentry()` runs in both main and renderer. What makes them inert is the KEY/DSN GATE each one opens with (`renderer/lib/posthog.ts` returns before `posthog.init()` on an empty `NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY`; both Sentry inits return without `SENTRY_DSN_DESKTOP`), combined with the fact that the value can never be non-empty — the vite `define` block bakes it at build time so no runtime env can arm it, and the build-time ban makes baking one impossible. A later phase must NOT delete those gates believing init is unreachable. Removing the imports is a later phase, not a silent widening of this one | `(CLOUD-SEVERANCE-P1)` |\n | Egress fence (log-only) | a fork-owned `onBeforeRequest` observer on `session.fromPartition(\"persist:superset\")`, installed in the boot path immediately after `app.whenReady()` and BEFORE any window exists — `MainWindow()` calls `assertEgressFenceInstalled()` and THROWS if it did not run, so the ordering is a runtime proof rather than a comment asking the next person to preserve it. It CANCELS NOTHING by design: this phase exists to prove the allowlist is complete before a later phase starts blocking. It logs ORIGIN ONLY (`scheme://host:port`) plus method, resourceType, initiator class and a first-seen timestamp with a count — never a full URL, path, query or fragment, because the companion pairing secret travels in a URL FRAGMENT and other URLs carry bearer tokens; an unparseable URL is recorded as `<unparseable>` rather than echoed. Browser-pane webviews are EXEMPT: `persist:superset` is also the `<webview>` partition (`usePersistentWebview.ts`, `browserRuntimeRegistry.ts`), so arbitrary user browsing arrives on the same listener and is counted only as a bare total — logging those origins would make this a browsing-history file and drown the app's own egress. The exemption is an ALLOWLIST, not a denylist: an origin is logged ONLY for a request positively attributed to a non-webview webContents, and everything unattributable is counted-and-dropped under a second bare total. That is not pedantry — a site in a browser pane that registers a SERVICE WORKER (any PWA) fetches on this partition with NO webContentsId at all, and a webview's in-flight requests can complete after it is destroyed; both would otherwise be classed \"unknown\" and have their origins logged, leaking exactly the browsing the exemption protects and able to flood the entry cap with it. The accepted cost, stated plainly: unattributable APP traffic (a main-process `net.fetch` on this session) is invisible here too, and the counter is the honest measure of what went unseen. Initiator is resolved via `webContents.fromId`, which must tolerate an absent or destroyed id without throwing since the callback runs on every request. Dedupe is in-memory and BOUNDED (512 keys); at the cap it drops and says so loudly rather than evicting silently, because an eviction would make the log lie. Honestly scoped in-file: it sees session webRequests only — main-process fetch, electron-updater, `@sentry/electron`'s main transport (renderer Sentry too, IPCMode.Classic), the host-service child, the bundled CLI, spawned agent CLIs and the pty-daemon ALL bypass it, so a clean fence log proves the RENDERER is quiet, not the app. The build-time gate covers what this listener cannot reach | `(EGRESS-FENCE)` |\n | Network logging is OFF unless asked for | upstream calls `netLog.startLogging` unconditionally at boot with `captureMode: \"includeSensitive\"` and a 1 GB cap, retaining 3 sessions, which records the app's FULL network traffic — URLs with query strings and fragments, request and response headers, cookies — as plain JSON under `%APPDATA%/Superset/network-logs`. On this machine one session file reached 1,014 MB: bearer tokens, the Cloudflare Access cookie and potentially the companion pairing code (which travels in a URL FRAGMENT) sitting on disk in cleartext, which is precisely what `(EGRESS-FENCE)` refuses to write when it logs ORIGIN ONLY. The logger is now OFF by default and starts only when `SUPERSET_NETWORK_LOG` is set to `1`/`true`; otherwise it prints one line naming the variable and returns with `started` false, so `stopNetworkLogger()` stays a safe no-op. The gate lives INSIDE `startNetworkLogger()`, not at the boot call site in `main/index.ts` — that sequence churns on every upstream merge and a kill placed there is one conflict resolution away from vanishing (same reasoning as `FORK_AUTO_UPDATE_DISABLED`). When it IS enabled the blast radius is cut: `captureMode` is `default` (no cookies, no authorization headers, no bodies) and the cap is 64 MB rather than 1 GB. **`includeSensitive` is reachable by NO environment variable** — a second opt-in would rebuild the exact footgun this removes, so restoring it is a code change someone reviews, and a test parses every `captureMode:` value out of the module's source and asserts none of them is `includeSensitive` (it also requires at least one match, so the check cannot pass by finding nothing). Every pre-existing file is deleted at boot whether or not logging is enabled this run, because all of it was written in the sensitive mode: only `current.json` and `session-*.json` inside that directory, never the directory itself, fail-soft (a file the running app holds open is counted and reported, never thrown at boot). A `default`-mode log still contains full request URLs, so the enable path says so and tells the maintainer to delete the file afterwards | `(NETLOG-OFF)` |\n+| Windows PTY kill never passes a signal | node-pty's `WindowsTerminal.kill(signal)` throws `Signals not supported on windows.` for ANY truthy argument, and it throws from inside its deferred queue — which, when the terminal is not yet ready, is drained from the conpty agent's socket `data` event. That throw lands on node-pty's stack, not the caller's, so no `try`/`catch` and no promise `.catch` can see it: it is an UNCAUGHT EXCEPTION that kills the pty-daemon and, with it, every terminal in every workspace on the machine (captured live in the owner's `pty-daemon.log`: two full crash dumps with the `Socket.emit → Object.run → Array.forEach → WindowsTerminal` stack). The daemon passed a signal on every kill path (`kill(signal ?? \"SIGHUP\")` defaults to a truthy one), so this was reachable from an ordinary session close. All node-pty kills now funnel through `NodePtyAdapter.killTerm` / the raw-term branch of `spawn`, where Windows takes the no-argument path (close the conpty + terminate its console process list) and POSIX passes its signal completely unchanged — `UnixTerminal.kill` is a real `process.kill(pid, signal)` and the SIGHUP-then-SIGKILL escalation depends on it. The Windows conpty kill is a CLOSE, not a repeatable signal, so it is one-shot-guarded: `WindowsTerminal.destroy()` is that same deferred no-argument kill, and firing both would dispose the conout worker twice and call the native kill on a freed handle from inside a promise (an unhandled rejection = another dead daemon). `(DAEMON-UNCAUGHT-GUARD)` is the containment behind the fix: the daemon entry (package + desktop bundle mirror) installs `uncaughtException`/`unhandledRejection` handlers that log the full stack to the daemon log and KEEP RUNNING, because this process is the durable owner of PTY master fds and a restart cannot recover them — the realistic fault population is per-session, so surviving costs one broken session while exiting costs all of them. Repeated faults are the exception to the exception: more than 5 in 60s is no longer a per-session fault, so it exits non-zero for a clean supervisor restart | `(WIN-PTY-KILL-NOSIGNAL)`, `(DAEMON-UNCAUGHT-GUARD)` |\n+| Windows process-tree kill | `process-tree.ts` was POSIX-only — it shells out to `ps -axo pid=,ppid=,pgid=,tty=,stat=` and signals process GROUPS, none of which exist on Windows. Killing a session there signalled only the root pid and ORPHANED the entire shell subtree, permanently and invisibly: the owner's `pty-daemon.log` carried 287 `final ps failed, survivor state unknown` lines, one per leaked subtree. Windows now enumerates `Win32_Process` (Windows PowerShell 5.1 + `Get-CimInstance`; `wmic` is removed from current Windows 11 builds) and walks ppid to terminate the real tree. Chosen over `taskkill /PID <pid> /T /F` because taskkill resolves the same ppid field with NO creation-time check, so a recycled pid whose stale `ParentProcessId` names our root is killed as if it were ours, with no way for us to see or report it. Pid reuse is guarded by start times: the root's is captured at session open (the Windows analogue of the pgid/tty capture) and re-checked on every pass, so a root pid that has been recycled targets NOTHING; a candidate child must have started at or after its parent (a process that predates its \"parent\" is an orphan on a recycled pid); and a candidate whose start time is unreadable is never targeted — the kill fails safe and leaks a process, which the survivor report then names. There is deliberately NO synchronous Windows enumerator: a blocking PowerShell spawn freezes the daemon's only event loop and stalls every session's IO, so `readProcessTable()` warns ONCE and returns empty (UNKNOWN, never \"no descendants\") and the whole reap lives in the async escalation chain — which on Windows therefore also runs for a SIGKILL request (immediately, no grace delay), since POSIX's synchronous volley has no counterpart there. The survivor report is accurate instead of always claiming failure | `(WIN-PROCESS-TREE)` |\n \n ## Machine-readable markers (the nightly gate reads this block)\n \n@@ -310,4 +312,8 @@ FORK_AUTO_UPDATE_DISABLED\tapps/desktop/src/main/lib/auto-updater.ts\n (NETLOG-OFF)\tapps/desktop/src/main/network-logger/policy.ts\n (NETLOG-OFF)\tapps/desktop/src/main/network-logger/policy.test.ts\n SUPERSET_NETWORK_LOG\tapps/desktop/src/main/network-logger/policy.ts\n+(WIN-PTY-KILL-NOSIGNAL)\tpackages/pty-daemon\n+(WIN-PROCESS-TREE)\tpackages/pty-daemon\n+(DAEMON-UNCAUGHT-GUARD)\tpackages/pty-daemon\n+(DAEMON-UNCAUGHT-GUARD)\tapps/desktop/src/main/pty-daemon\n ```\n\r\n=== untracked files ===\r\n\r\n?? packages/pty-daemon/src/crash-guard.test.ts\r\n?? packages/pty-daemon/src/crash-guard.ts","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"62b6ba3e-8bab-4c8a-9049-3a5bfa6c3d20","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
