{"parentUuid":"5bc40c64-e87d-4a86-9e57-fbeef82ecc59","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAPSV9NCXUaCZKahK7Q4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014XSGzg8SNyrKCfkhCHDVEW","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Efficiency review of pty diff","model":"opus","name":"simplify-efficiency","prompt":"Repo: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64. REPOSITORY FILES ONLY — never read user home-directory shell profiles or credentials.\n\nENV: Bash tool is BROKEN (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: unexpected EOF`). Use PowerShell. node_modules IS installed. Coreutils: `$env:PATH = \"C:\\Program Files\\Git\\usr\\bin;$env:PATH\"`. Do NOT quit/kill/restart the running Superset app; leave watcher pid 38312 alone. Do NOT edit any file — you are review-only, report findings.\n\nDIFF UNDER REVIEW: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\tmp\\review\\simplify-scope.diff`\nTwo files are NEW and therefore not in the diff — read them directly:\n  packages\\pty-daemon\\src\\crash-guard.ts\n  packages\\pty-daemon\\src\\crash-guard.test.ts\n\nWHAT THE CHANGE DOES: fixes a Windows crash where node-pty's `kill(signal)` throws asynchronously and kills the whole pty-daemon; adds a Windows process-tree walk to replace POSIX-only `ps -axo`/pgid logic; adds a crash guard.\n\nYOUR ANGLE: **EFFICIENCY**. Flag wasted work the diff introduces: redundant computation or repeated I/O, independent operations run sequentially, blocking work added to startup or hot paths. Also flag long-lived objects built from closures or captured environments — they keep the entire enclosing scope alive for the object's lifetime (a memory leak when that scope holds large values); prefer a class/struct that copies only the fields it needs. Name the cheaper alternative.\n\nKNOWN COST TO ASSESS RIGOROUSLY — this is the headline efficiency question:\nEach Windows process enumeration spawns Windows PowerShell 5.1 and runs `Get-CimInstance Win32_Process`, MEASURED at ~590ms per call. The kill escalation chain runs multiple verify rounds (KILL_VERIFY_DELAYS_MS = [300, 700, 1500, 2500]), and `DaemonSupervisor.terminateProcessTreeAndGroups` does TWO passes (SIGTERM then SIGKILL), each enumerating. So a single session kill can spawn many ~590ms PowerShell processes, and killing N sessions concurrently multiplies that.\nAssess concretely:\n- How many enumerations does one session kill cost, worst case? How many for N concurrent session kills — does each TreeKiller enumerate independently, and could one shared snapshot per round serve all of them?\n- Is there any caching/coalescing of the process table within a single escalation round, or across concurrent kills? Should there be?\n- Is `Get-CimInstance` the cheapest available option? Consider `Get-CimInstance -Filter`, selecting fewer properties, a single long-lived helper process, or the existing tree-kill approach. Note the code deliberately rejected `taskkill /T /F` for a correctness reason (no creation-time check) — do not propose it as a straight swap, but say if a cheaper enumerator exists with the same safety.\n- The daemon is described as having \"only one event loop\" and IO for every terminal session rides it. Does the async enumeration genuinely stay off the critical path, or can a burst of kills starve terminal IO?\n- `windowsDescendants` Map latch in TreeKiller (Pty.ts): does it grow unbounded over a long-lived session? Is it cleared?\n- Does the crash guard add per-event cost or retain references (its burst window keeps timestamps — bounded?)?\n- Any closure-captured large objects (process tables can be hundreds of rows) retained by long-lived objects?\n\nReport each finding with: file, line, one-line summary, the concrete cost (quantified where you can), and the cheaper alternative. If the code is already efficient, say so plainly rather than manufacturing findings. Your final message is the entire report.","subagent_type":"claude"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1111,"cache_read_input_tokens":289281,"output_tokens":5823,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1111,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5823,"cache_read_input_tokens":289281,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1111,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1111},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"attributionSkill":"simplify","type":"assistant","uuid":"d23c0748-0234-4382-ad40-b052dcb835f9","timestamp":"2026-08-18T14:41:20.617Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"d23c0748-0234-4382-ad40-b052dcb835f9","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAPSV9NCXUaCZKahK7Q4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GapGwFSV7kFmBiwh7jK2r2","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Altitude review of pty diff","model":"opus","name":"simplify-altitude","prompt":"Repo: C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64 (a VENDORED FORK of superset-sh/superset — upstream deltas are merged nightly, and fork changes to upstream-owned files are a recurring merge-conflict surface). REPOSITORY FILES ONLY — never read user home-directory shell profiles or credentials.\n\nENV: Bash tool is BROKEN (`/usr/bin/bash: -c: unexpected EOF`). Use PowerShell. node_modules IS installed. Coreutils: `$env:PATH = \"C:\\Program Files\\Git\\usr\\bin;$env:PATH\"`. Do NOT quit/kill/restart the running Superset app; leave watcher pid 38312 alone. Do NOT edit any file — you are review-only, report findings.\n\nDIFF UNDER REVIEW: `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\tmp\\review\\simplify-scope.diff`\nTwo files are NEW and therefore not in the diff — read them directly:\n  packages\\pty-daemon\\src\\crash-guard.ts\n  packages\\pty-daemon\\src\\crash-guard.test.ts\nRead `AGENTS.md` for the fork's architecture, non-negotiables and documented footguns before judging.\n\nWHAT THE CHANGE DOES: fixes a Windows crash where node-pty's `kill(signal)` throws asynchronously (from a deferred queue drained by a socket event) and kills the whole pty-daemon along with every session; adds a Windows process-tree walk replacing POSIX-only `ps -axo`/pgid logic; adds an uncaughtException/unhandledRejection crash guard that logs and keeps running, exiting only on a burst.\n\nYOUR ANGLE: **ALTITUDE**. Is each change implemented at the right DEPTH, or is it a fragile bandaid? Special cases layered on shared infrastructure signal the fix isn't deep enough — prefer generalizing the underlying mechanism over adding special cases.\n\nSpecifically worth judging:\n- The root fix routes Windows to node-pty's no-argument close. Is the RIGHT layer `NodePtyAdapter.killTerm`, or should the platform difference be absorbed lower (a platform-specific Pty adapter/strategy) or higher? Note there are now Windows branches in Pty.ts, process-tree.ts AND DaemonSupervisor.ts — is that one concept leaking across three layers, or three genuinely separate concerns?\n- `process-tree.ts` is fundamentally a POSIX abstraction (process GROUPS, controlling TTYs, pgid signalling). Bolting a ppid walk into it means the module now exports two incompatible models behind one interface. Is that the right shape, or should Windows have its own module behind a narrower shared interface (e.g. \"give me the processes to signal\")?\n- The crash guard is containment for a bug now fixed at source. Is it justified defence-in-depth at the right level, or does it mask future faults? Judge the log-and-continue-except-on-burst policy against the fork's documented \"fail fast and loud\" stance and AGENTS.md's non-negotiables.\n- FORK-MERGE ALTITUDE (important here): every touched file except crash-guard.ts is UPSTREAM-OWNED, so each edit is a permanent nightly-merge conflict surface. Is the fix structured to minimise that — logic concentrated in fork-owned files with thin upstream call-outs — or is it spread thinly across upstream code where merges will erode it? AGENTS.md documents that fork-only behaviour without a marker gets silently dropped by a merge. Assess whether the FEATURES.md markers are placed where they actually protect the load-bearing logic.\n- Is `DaemonSupervisor.signalDaemonTree` (new, ~43 lines in an upstream-owned file) at the right altitude, or should it live in the pty-daemon package / a fork-owned module the supervisor calls?\n- Does the change respect the documented footgun \"no synchronous/blocking fs on the main thread at startup\"? The code deliberately provides NO synchronous Windows enumerator for related reasons — is that consistent everywhere?\n\nReport each finding with: file, line, one-line summary, the concrete cost (what future maintenance or merge pain it creates), and the deeper/more general alternative. Do NOT report correctness bugs — a separate review covers those. If the altitude is right, say so plainly rather than manufacturing findings. Your final message is the entire report.","subagent_type":"claude"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1111,"cache_read_input_tokens":289281,"output_tokens":5823,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1111,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5823,"cache_read_input_tokens":289281,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1111,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1111},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"attributionSkill":"simplify","type":"assistant","uuid":"18595b1e-3df6-40f1-ad03-6650fe57a986","timestamp":"2026-08-18T14:41:39.997Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
