{"parentUuid":"e1155af3-0a06-421f-9c88-38800c44068a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAQLUR9pnf6eDKxiF29h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":337,"cache_read_input_tokens":313630,"output_tokens":1101,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":631},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":337,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1101,"cache_read_input_tokens":313630,"cache_creation_input_tokens":337,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":337},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"878291e8-c2af-412b-8cee-255c7e5d117a","timestamp":"2026-08-18T14:52:27.939Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"878291e8-c2af-412b-8cee-255c7e5d117a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAQLUR9pnf6eDKxiF29h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Efficiency fix landed and verified. Suite is back to **107 tests, 106 passing** and runs in 418 ms rather than 6.25 s.\n\nWhat I found and fixed while the cleanup agents were quiet:\n\n**Six PowerShell enumerations per session kill**, each ~590 ms — five escalation rounds plus a final survivor check — and every session's kill chain runs independently. Closing a workspace with ten terminals meant sixty process spawns.\n\nThe fix is **single-flight coalescing**: callers arriving while a read is in progress join it instead of starting another. I deliberately chose that over a time-to-live cache, and the reasoning matters — a TTL could hand a caller a process table that predates a process it just signalled, which is exactly the \"concluded the kill was complete\" mistake the surrounding code's null-means-unknown rule exists to prevent. Joiners get a snapshot from the moment they asked, so nobody sees a staler view than before.\n\nI also caught myself writing a **flaky test**. My first version awaited a real enumeration; it passed alone in 2.3 s but blew the 5 s limit under full-suite contention. A test that fails depending on machine load is worse than no test, so I rewrote it to assert the actual invariant — that both callers receive the same promise — which is instant and deterministic.\n\nStill waiting on the four cleanup agents, which have all gone idle without reporting, and on the CI tamper matrix."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":337,"cache_read_input_tokens":313630,"output_tokens":1101,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":631},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_