{"parentUuid":"fa32941d-8cb6-4d43-8fb6-8bf279f601d7","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAQmdzKZLVzE3zBUVMz9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Staging is clean — only terminal files, CI workstream untouched."}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1387,"cache_read_input_tokens":320626,"output_tokens":1853,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":110},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1387,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1853,"cache_read_input_tokens":320626,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1387,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1387},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"26cb1ab1-d054-47b1-bcd9-80f0673d045d","timestamp":"2026-08-18T14:58:02.857Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"26cb1ab1-d054-47b1-bcd9-80f0673d045d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAQmdzKZLVzE3zBUVMz9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PJ9yTf92bJphQ5mC9XjTAZ","name":"PowerShell","input":{"command":"$env:PATH = \"C:\\Program Files\\Git\\usr\\bin;$env:PATH\"\ngit commit -m @'\nfix(pty-daemon): a Windows session close no longer kills every terminal\n\n(WIN-PTY-KILL-NOSIGNAL) node-pty's `WindowsTerminal.kill(signal)` throws\n`Signals not supported on windows.` for ANY truthy argument, and it throws\nfrom inside its deferred queue — which, when the terminal is not yet ready,\nis drained from the conpty agent's socket `data` event. That lands on\nnode-pty's stack, not the caller's, so no try/catch and no promise .catch\ncan see it: an UNCAUGHT EXCEPTION that kills the daemon and with it every\nterminal in every workspace. The daemon passed a signal on every kill path\n(`kill(signal ?? \"SIGHUP\")` defaults to a truthy one), so an ordinary\nsession close could do it.\n\nNot theoretical: this machine's pty-daemon.log holds two full crash dumps\nwith the Socket.emit -> Object.run -> Array.forEach -> WindowsTerminal\nstack and the Node process-death footer, across 111 daemon spawns.\n\nAll node-pty kills now funnel through one boundary where Windows takes the\nno-argument close and POSIX passes its signal completely unchanged —\nUnixTerminal.kill is a real process.kill(pid, signal) and the\nSIGHUP-then-SIGKILL escalation depends on it. The Windows conpty kill is a\nCLOSE, not a repeatable signal, so it is one-shot guarded: destroy() is\nthat same deferred no-arg kill, and firing both disposes the conout worker\ntwice and calls the native kill on a freed handle from inside a promise —\nan unhandled rejection, i.e. another dead daemon.\n\n(WIN-PROCESS-TREE) process-tree.ts was POSIX-only — `ps -axo` and process\nGROUPS, neither of which exists on Windows. Killing a session there\nsignalled only the root pid and ORPHANED the whole shell subtree,\npermanently and invisibly; the same log carries 287 `final ps failed,\nsurvivor state unknown` lines, one per leaked subtree. Windows now\nenumerates Win32_Process and walks ppid. Chosen over `taskkill /T /F`\nbecause taskkill resolves the same ppid field with NO creation-time check,\nso a recycled pid whose stale ParentProcessId names our root is killed as\nif it were ours, with no way to see or report it. Pid reuse is guarded by\nstart times: the root's is captured at session open and re-checked every\npass, a child must have started at or after its parent, and an unreadable\nstart time is never targeted — the kill fails safe and leaks a process,\nwhich the survivor report then names.\n\nThe synchronous path deliberately keeps its old degenerate behaviour: with\nno sync Windows enumerator it returns the root pid alone (honouring\nincludeRoot AND signalPids, so the v1 caller that passes signalPids:false\nstill gets nothing, exactly as before). Returning nothing instead would\nhave turned \"kills at least the root\" into \"kills nothing\", silently\nbreaking DaemonSupervisor's terminate path — caught in review, not in\nproduction.\n\nReads are single-flight: each enumeration spawns PowerShell (~590ms) and a\nkill chain enumerates six times, so ten concurrent session kills meant\nsixty spawns. Callers arriving mid-read now join it. Deliberately NOT a TTL\ncache — a TTL could hand a caller a table predating a process it just\nsignalled, which is the \"concluded the kill was complete\" mistake the\nnull-means-unknown rule exists to prevent.\n\n(DAEMON-UNCAUGHT-GUARD) Containment behind the fix, in both daemon entries:\nuncaughtException and unhandledRejection log the full stack and KEEP\nRUNNING. This cuts against fail-fast on purpose. The daemon is the durable\nowner of every PTY master fd and a restart cannot recover them, so with a\nper-session fault population, surviving costs one broken session while\nexiting costs all of them. Loud is preserved by the log line, not the exit.\nMore than 5 faults in 60s is no longer per-session, so it exits for a clean\nsupervisor restart.\n\nReviewed adversarially across two rounds; the first found the sync-path\nregression above, the second verified POSIX byte-equivalence against HEAD\ncall site by call site. 107 tests (was 82), 106 pass; the single failure is\na pre-existing macOS trustd cert test asserting a POSIX path shape on\nWindows, untouched here. 205 feature markers, REFERR clean.\n\nNot established: Windows TERMINATION is unexercised — target selection was\nverified against live process trees, but nothing was killed, since the\nowner has live sessions. Needs a packaged build.\n'@\n\"\"\ngit log --oneline -1","description":"Commit the terminal fix"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1387,"cache_read_input_tokens":320626,"output_tokens":1853,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":110},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1387,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1853,"cache_read_input_tokens":320626,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1387,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1387},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"222fee9d-710c-443a-9ca2-6f01156486d5","timestamp":"2026-08-18T14:58:22.627Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.234","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
